#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     "Stop," I said.\par
\par
     Dlane froze immediately. Two days later we'd traveled farther than I'd expected but not, apparently far enough. My canted ears strained the forest's noisy dusk and heard the faint chuffs of the hunting \'f1edsu, the beasts employed to track by scent what the Jokka cannot see to kill.\par
\par
     "They brought the beasts," I whispered.\par
\par
     Dlane's pupils contracted. "Oh, no, Thenet\'85."\par
\par
     "Ssh," I said. A few minutes later, I said, "They're not far enough for my liking."\par
\par
     "What are we going to do?"\par
\par
     I glanced back the way we'd come; there was no profit in moving deeper into the woods. The \'f1edsu were slender and sure-footed and would find us anywhere on the ground.\par
\par
     My eyes rose up the trunk of the tree beside her. "We climb," I said, grabbing her waist.\par
\par
     "Climb?" Dlane said with a gasp as I swung her up toward the nearest branch. "But I don't know how to climb\'85."\par
\par
     "Now is an excellent time to learn," I answered, deadpan. "Grab the branch and get onto it. I'll be up behind you."\par
\par
     She scrabbled into the tree, leaves rustling. As she searched for her bearings I grabbed a handful of flowers and crushed them, sprinkling them over the ground near the roots\'85 then I crouched down, muscles twitching in my thighs, and sprang straight up into the tree.\par
\par
     Dlane squeaked in surprise as I manhandled myself up beside her. "There. The flowers should confuse them."\par
\par
     "Are we going to hide here all night?" she said.\par
\par
     "No\'85 we're going to resume traveling," I answered, pointing to the north.\par
\par
Her gaze followed my arm and she blinked a few times. "You want to climb from tree to tree?"\par
\par
     "They're tangled together. It will be an ideal way to keep our scent off the ground," I said. Resettling my pack, I reached up to the next branch and pulled myself up. I offered her my hand.\par
\par
     Dubious but trusting, she took it and let me haul her up after me. We repeated the routine until the branches supporting us became small enough our toes and toe-claws could wrap around them. Gently, I pushed apart the foliage and peered down at the forest floor.\par
\par
     "Shouldn't we get out of this tree?" Dlane whispered, her tight grip on my hand betraying her nervousness. From the direction of her ears she too heard the chuffing of the nearby \'f1edsu. I pressed my free hand to her mouth to quiet her, felt her body go rigid against mine.\par
\par
     Through the holes between the shimmering olive green leaves I saw them spill into the clearing beneath our tree: lithe, slender creatures as tall as my calves with thin, long legs and silvery scaled bodies. Their sleek heads looked like extensions of their ropy necks, wedge-shaped with a diamond pattern on the forehead. There were ten, milling in the dirt, nosing the flowers I'd crushed, sniffing and panting.\par
\par
     Dlane's chest jumped and I pulled her closer to me, hand still over her mouth. I could feel her breath, moist and hot against my palm. My eyes did not leave the bodies of the beasts swarming beneath us, and as they sought our scent trail I counted heart-beats: one and a half beats of hers for each of mine, a syncopated pattern that heightened my anxiety.\par
\par
     The \'f1edsu moaned, milling in confusion. Even as my heart finally began to match Dlane's in speed, they turned as one and bound back the way they'd come, wailing their frustration.\par
\par
     A tiny whine escaped Dlane's mouth through my fingers. I let my hand open enough to free her lips, but did not release her chin, listening to make sure the \'f1edsu's masters weren't coming this way to see what had confounded their beasts.\par
\par
     "Thenet," Dlane whispered, quivering against me.\par
\par
     "Hush!" I hissed. I strained the wind: nothing but forest murmurs, trees exchanging caresses, the soft cooing calls of the birds regaining their courage as the breeze swept the scent of the predators away. The white ache in my chest vanished as I let the breath go, the one I hadn't even noticed holding. "They're not coming back. For now, at least."\par
\par
     I never quite knew what to expect from my crazy anadi\'85 but for her to bury her head in my shoulder, shivering violently, acting as normal anadi would\'85 no, not that. I wondered how it was that she could flip my expectations so quickly. Hesitant, I slid my arms around her, tangling them in the clothing she wore so badly. "Hush, ke anadi. They did not find us this time."\par
\par
     "I just want to be left alone," she said into my shoulder. Her mouth rested on my exposed clavicle, her cheek against the bunched fabric of my blouse collar. "Why won't they leave me alone?"\par
\par
     "They want you home," I said, uncomfortable. I had held females before\'85 but never eperu. And while Dlane was undeniably anadi, she wore clothing. It jarred. "They want you back."\par
\par
     "They have no right to want me!" Dlane said against my skin. "I am my own. I have not given myself to them."\par
\par
     She was making no sense. Giving yourself to other people? Could you do that? What did it mean to withhold yourself from others, anyway? "Dlane\'85."\par
\par
     "It's not fair."\par
\par
     "Life isn't, ba anadi."\par
\par
     "No, Thenet," she said, drawing away from me, wary of her balance on the branch. "Jokka have made life that way. It isn't that way on its own." She turned her flame-bright eyes on me. "And I am not a child."\par
\par
     I hadn't even realized I'd been calling her one: ba anadi, youngling female. A rueful smile hiked up one side of my mouth. "No, ke anadi. If you were, they wouldn't be looking so hard for you."\par
\par
     "Let's get off this tree," Dlane said.\par
\par
     "And into the next," I agreed. I scooted around the trunk. "This way. We need to head east, if we can."\par
\par
     "East!" Dlane exclaimed. "Isn't that toward town?"\par
\par
     I smiled grimly as I skirted the branch on the other side of the trunk, the stink of sap cloying in my nostrils as leaves rustled around me. "No, ke anadi\'85 town is to the southeast."\par
\par
     "That's close enough!"\par
\par
     "They're expecting us to head away from town," I said. "We will confuse them by not going precisely where they expect."\par
\par
     She skirted the trunk and joined me on the branch, chest heaving beneath her rumpled blouse. "But we'll still be going toward them. We'll lose time."\par
\par
     "Time!" I sighed, reaching for a limb above me: it was attached to the neighboring tree, and became thick enough to support weight well before the one beneath me gave out. Would that we'd be so lucky in all our junctions. "What's your rush? Do you need to be someplace specific at a certain time?"\par
\par
     Her ears flattened to her skull as she edged out behind me. "No," she said softly. "I just don't want to travel during summer."\par
\par
     "Summer is a ways off," I said, even though it was only a few weeks away. The reminder of her body's fragility bothered me. "And even so, there are ways that anadi can travel in the heat." I stepped onto the next tree and turned, holding out a hand to her. "Come on," I said, noting how round her eyes had become. They quivered minutely in their sockets as she stared at me. "Come on," I said again. "I won't let you fall."\par
\par
     She laughed nervously and took my hand. "See that you don't, please."\par
\par
     Dlane almost lost her balance hopping to my branch. My arm shot around her waist and dragged her close as her feet scrabbled at the bark. Her grip was desperate, and I could hear her heart slamming through her chest against my side. The branch had ceased to bob when she finally said in a small voice, "I hate heights."\par
\par
     "There will be greater ones when we cross the mountains to the Birthwell."\par
\par
     A shudder rippled through her, but she disentangled herself from my grasp and edged up the branch. My tail flicked my ambivalence, but I followed her. She was strong, my anadi, but there were limits. I wasn't certain whether to be disappointed or relieved.\par
\par
     We traveled thus, carefully, in a generally eastward direction until the dusk deepened too much for me to trust our senses. We Jokka are creatures of mostly sight, very highly developed sight; had I been alone I would have continued, for I trusted my vision implicitly, particularly with my hearing to bolster it. But I was not alone, and Dlane's fear and inexperience made her clumsy.\par
\par
     I chose a black pew to spend the rest of the night, a nondescript tree in a cluster of others far from any glades or valleys. It had a splendid split in its trunk above the branches of several of its fellows, two massive black boles separating to form almost a cradle.\par
\par
     "Here," I said, taking my pack off and hanging it on a stunted limb just above the crotch. I turned and helped her into the bowl, divesting her of her pack and hanging it as well. Dlane settled into the black pew's cradle and shivered.\par
\par
     I touched her shoulder, straightened the folds of her blouse. "When I was young, my mother's sister used to brew tea of the black pew for me to help me relax."\par
\par
     "You remember the female who raised you?" Dlane asked.\par
\par
     I canted my head. "Vividly. She was kind and had a beautiful voice. She would sing me through my doze-dreams."\par
\par
     Dlane curled up, back against the wood. "I never knew who my mother was. And even if I had, she probably would have had no mind left\'85." She slid her hand over her belly. I could almost sense her fingertips running over the golden ring through her navel. The eperu wear them in their ears, the emodo at the base of their spines, and the anadi at their navels - those rings, the ones that marked us as special.\par
\par
     "You are safe here with me," I said, touching her knee. "It has been a difficult evening. Why don't you rest?"\par
\par
     "I don't want to rest," she whispered. "I want to hold on to every moment I am aware."\par
\par
     "No one is going to take you," I said. "Not tonight, at least." I glanced up and said, "I'm going to look at what's around. If you need me, cry out."\par
\par
     With that, I scrambled up one of the branches forming the cradle, higher and higher toward the canopy of dark leaves. Pushing aside a few sprigs, I poked my head above the forest's roof. The relief of the open sky sent a few appreciative shudders down my back as I gulped in the cool air, free of the scent of resin and moving constantly in a breeze that ruffled the leaves around me. Locking my legs around the bough just beneath the canopy, I found my balance and let my body sway with the black pew, enjoying the wind.\par
\par
     After a while such indulgences gave way to more practical concerns. I scanned the horizon, trying to triangulate our exact position. We hadn't traveled too far off our chosen course, perhaps half a day. My gaze drifted to the north, to the mountains that barred the path to the Birthwell, gray smudges nearly leeched of the sandy haze they wore in daylight. Even if we kept moving every day, we wouldn't make those crags by summer\rquote s end. I wondered how I was going to keep Dlane safe from the heat. Particularly if she insisted on going clothed the entire way.\par
\par
     A black moth settled on my hand, mistaking it for a leaf. I glanced uneasily at it, a velvety black with satiny black markings and black curlicues that had a distinctly purple cast. Its legs tickled. I thought briefly of eating it, then shook my head and cast it back into the sky. Better the honey bars than an insect, though we would run low on the bars soon enough.\par
\par
     Climbing down I found Dlane in the cradle, sleeping an anadi's sleep. Sitting on a branch radiating from the bole of the tree, I sang her the lullaby in my quiet alto. I had forgotten the words, but such details were immaterial.\par
\par
