\par
 \par
\par
     "Did you read much?" she asked me as we made our perilous way across the roof of the forest the following afternoon. The sun's pale rays drilled through the spaces between the leaves, cutting glittering, eye-mazing patterns on our branches and the matted earth beneath.\par
\par
     "Read?" I asked, perplexed. "Read what?" I leaned over and grabbed the limb of a neighboring tree, pulling it taut so Dlane could use it to cross.\par
\par
     "Well, House histories. Tales. Lyricals. That sort of thing." Hesitantly, she slid her scintillating hand over the branch and hopped over, her feet splaying against the pebbly grey-brown bark.\par
\par
     "Why would I want to do that?" I asked, bemused.\par
\par
     "You do know how to read, don't you?" Dlane asked, looking back at me.\par
\par
     "Of course I do. To leave notes, and read signs and waypost marks."\par
\par
     Dlane's delicate nose wrinkled. "You've never gone to the clays?"\par
\par
     I chuckled, braided tail swaying in amusement. "I never had the time to go to the public clays, and the private ones were too costly. How much spare shell do you think I had as an anadi-guardian in Mated?"\par
\par
     "Surely enough to go to a reading once in a while!" Dlane said.\par
\par
     "There were other things I needed to spend my salary on," I said. "New wood for my spears and staves. Clothing and medicaments. Ornaments for festivals\'85" I saw her incredulous stare and said, "Do you think Mated provided those things? They offered food and board, but those other things\'85 you earned them. Who would want to wear clothing or hunt with a spear they did not choose themselves, anyway?"\par
\par
     "I can't imagine such a thing," she said, more a muse than a comment to me. "Choosing everything for yourself."\par
\par
     "Accustom yourself to it," I said dryly. "Your life ahead is nothing but choices."\par
\par
     A grin seized her mouth as she reached the trunk. "I look forward to-oh!"\par
\par
     Her gasp ran liquid through my body; in a breath I'd snatched my spear from its sheath on my back and stiffened, ears and eyes searching. I saw it only a few heart-beats later: one of the \'f1edsu at the base of our tree. When they lost the scent they hunted alone, and one piping ululation would bring the rest of the pack when that one found its quarry. The creature raised its blank eyes to mine, opening its mouth.\par
\par
     I dropped to the ground and rammed my spear down its throat as the first note escaped its black lips. The bottom of its skull cracked as I thrust it down and pinned it to the earth. It flopped and twitched for a few minutes and then slumped.\par
\par
     "Dlane\'85 climb! Climb!"\par
\par
     "Thenet!"\par
\par
     "Climb, damn it!"\par
\par
     With a squeak she leaped up onto the trunk and began shimmying awkwardly up toward the thickest blankets of leaves. I pulled my spear from the \'f1edsu's head with a grunt and glanced up with narrowed eyes. She wasn't visible, nor were the leaves ruffling.\par
\par
     The second \'f1edsu bashed into my side as I stood there and I turned on it with a snarl, tossing it away with a hand. Three more burst from the knobby shrubs at the base of the tree. The first made the mistake of snuffing too long at the body of its fallen comrade; my spear parted its ribcage and spilled its organs across the sandy earth. The other two backed away from me and howled their eerie song. I leaped and scattered them, trying to break their concentration.\par
\par
     The rest of the pack spilled out of the surrounding brush, overriding the moans of the first two with a symphony of disconcertingly harmonious snickers. I've always hated the growl of the \'f1edsu, like the laughter of the worst kind of empty and vicious anadi.\par
\par
     I lifted my spear.\par
\par
     "Hold!" A voice cried: emodo, too deep for even the largest of male-Turned neuters. Pushing aside a branch, the Jokkad appeared from behind his pack, two eperu at his side. He was well-shaped: scaled skin a respectable palette of crimsons with silver curlicues and a mane of silver and gray and black. His purple eyes matched the sash wrapped around his waist, securing the black trews to his thickly muscled body. The House pendant dangling from his neck did not match Ashoi or Mated's\'85 perhaps he was of the house that had hoped to gain Dlane as a match from Ashoi.\par
\par
     "Call off your beasts," I said. I prayed Dlane did not choose this moment to fall or squeal.\par
\par
     "You're an arrogant one," he said, hands resting on his hips. "In case you had not noticed, ke eperu, you are not in a position to issue commands."\par
\par
     "I have as much right to do so as any traveler unjustly detained," I replied, flattening my ears to accentuate my displeasure.\par
\par
     "A traveler!" He laughed. "One who happens to fit the description of the rogue eperu who kidnapped the Ashoi anadi? Really."\par
\par
     Whatever prompted my next comments would forever remain a mystery to me, but they popped from my mouth before I could stop them. "A rogue eperu? Kidnapping an anadi? Brightness, World and Void, what a story! What's going on? Who was it, do you know its name?"\par
\par
     The male shifted, perplexed. "Your act won't fool us," he said, but there was uncertainty in his voice. The eperu at his shoulder exchanged confused glances.\par
\par
     "What act? Tell me the story, already! I've never heard of such a thing! Was the anadi hurt? What would an eperu do with one of the anadi? Ke emodo, do not leave me in suspense! I will give you a honey bar just for the tale!"\par
\par
     His frown was far more pronounced now. "I\'85 you look just like it, though."\par
\par
     "But where is the anadi?" one of the eperu behind him wondered.\par
\par
     "With the eperu, I guess," I interjected, tail swaying in what I hoped was agitated curiosity. "Where did the kidnapping take place? Do you know where they were going? Perhaps," I paused and lowered my voice, "Perhaps they were in love."\par
\par
     The two eperu behind the male jumped. "Surely not!" one of them said, eyeing me askance. The other shook its head with a delicate shiver.\par
\par
     "If you were that eperu, you could have hidden her," the emodo said, pointing at me with very little conviction. The \'f1edsu milled around him, confused by his lack of confidence.\par
\par
     "The tree, perhaps?" one of the eperu suggested.\par
\par
     I chortled. "More evidence for an eperu-anadi love pair," I said, trying very hard to project an unseemly interest in the forbidden and unspeakable. "Anadi don't climb!"\par
\par
     The leaves above my head chose that moment to rustle.\par
\par
     I froze; the emodo froze\'85 the eperu froze. Then the emodo lifted his hand. "Climb that-"\par
\par
     A cloud of moths erupted from the branches, composed of individuals just like the one that had so lately investigated my hand. They fluttered to the ground, covering the two \'f1edsu corpses completely, so thickly that the bodies seemed to grow a writhing black carpet. I recalled my unease at the sight of the moth and remembered now the reasons: carrion eaters. I shuddered, then proffered in a small voice, "Sorry about those two."\par
\par
     The emodo stared at them, then shook himself. "It happens." To the two eperu, he barked, "Move on!"\par
\par
     As he turned away, I said, "Ke emodo? A honeybar for the tale?"\par
\par
     "Another time, perhaps," he said. A whistle from his lips drew the pack away and they all melted into the dusk. As all signs of them withdrew from my listening ears, I crouched beside the corpses, twin meals for the cloud of moths that crawled and twined above their ruined bodies. To that fluttering mass, I whispered softly, "Thank you."\par
\par
     "Thenet!"\par
\par
     The hushed, urgent tone yanked my eyes upward, but it was the explosion of sound moments later that drew me to my feet in time to catch her. Her spine smacked against my forearms and Dlane rolled toward my chest as my knees flexed. My body recoiled like a giant cushion absorbing the shock of her, the weight of her, the beauty of her face through the mask of her fear.\par
\par
     I registered her trembling before my body had straightened, cradled her to my chest.\par
\par
     "I couldn't hold on any longer, just couldn't, ke eperu, oh Thenet you were wonderful-wonderful!" She buried her face against my breastbone as she quaked. The hot snakes of her tears slithered down the inside of my blouse. Warmed my chest and set fire to my skin, so thin over my ribs, the scales too tiny even to see.\par
\par
     I looked over my shoulder; her fall hadn't even dislodged one of the moths. With a female in my arms and the black moths at my feet, I experienced a peculiar vertigo.\par
\par
     "I am the World," I murmured softly.\par
\par
     "What?" she asked. I looked into her eyes; they were flame-complected, but their slit pupils gave me back my reflection. Not the World, caught between Life and Death\'85 just a Jokkad holding an exhausted and frightened anadi with an effluvious sack of bloodied meat at its feet.\par
\par
     "That was too close for my liking," I said to her instead. "We should move on."\par
\par
     "Yes," Dlane said, curling against my chest.\par
\par
     Carefully I crouched and slung our bags and my spear over my shoulder, balancing her against a knee. By the time I rose her breathing had slowed. The anadi and emodo slept after stress. I wished briefly I could do the same.\par
\par
     The afternoon had lost its brilliance during my encounter with the hunters, and a strange sense of peace buoyed my steps as I resumed our journey. A wind followed me, tangling my patterned long-cloth against my legs. Dlane's beaded tail swung alongside my knee, and her long, slow breaths warmed my throat.\par
\par
     Luminous purple pieces of the sky shone between the black silhouettes of the trees as I picked my way over sandy slopes, claws digging deep into the soft soil. My general heading was northwesterly; for the nonce my fear of discovery had deserted me, leaving only more immediate concerns. Was Dlane well? Was I gripping her too hard? Would taking too great a step over that ditch dislodge the packs on my shoulder?\par
\par
     By true nightfall I'd located a ravine closely guarded by the sentinels of tangled trees. The vines wrapping the branches together had a thick, dark scent enhanced by the green perfume of the fern I crushed beneath one foot as I stood on the bank. It occurred to me that I should be tired\'85 but I was not. After kneeling to re-establish my hold on my sleeping anadi, I stood and followed the smell and sound of the running water. My ears tracked the soft splashes and bubbling of insects and their predators bounding across the wet pebbles.\par
\par
     If I was correct, this ravine would broaden into a stream thick with fish once it left the forest. We might build a permanent camp there while we decided what to do. If Dlane truly wanted to travel to the Birthwell, we would need more supplies than we had now\'85 and it was questionable whether she even had the stamina.\par
\par
     I looked on her sleeping face and found her beautiful\'85 but there was a touch of pity in my observation. The anadi are not meant for punishment, but as delicate ornaments to a Household. I wondered if it would be a kindness for me to turn around right now and deliver her back into the arms of Ashoi, where she would be safe, a precious treasure attended by faithful eperu whose charters were to see to her every need.\par
\par
     But I did not turn.\par
\par
     Truedark found my arms aching. I stopped and closed my useless eyes. The ravine beside me trickled; I could hear it and the wind through the trees. With caution my companion I took my first step, violating a directive old as my people: do not travel when you cannot see.\par
\par
     But anadi didn't run away, either. And eperu did not protest when they were banished from their Houses to the arms of the Void.\par
\par
     I walked. I banged my knees and shins, stumbled several times and stopped frequently to get my bearings\'85 but I did not stop. Every danger sense I'd developed went off and didn't stop ringing; eyes open, eyes closed, it didn't matter. When the first ray of light pulled up the curtain of black from the world, my hungry eyes seized on it and a giddy rapture threatened my balance and my reason. I staggered to a stop and stared at it, shuddering.\par
\par
     Dlane murmured in her sleep, distracting me. I squeezed her tightly and forced the shivers away. Pacing alongside the bank of the ravine, I found a thickset trunk with roots that lurched out of the earth to form a cage around the ground and over the water. Nestling into one of the hollows behind one of the tree's knees, I covered her with my arms, curled into a ball around her and dozed\'85 with my eyes closed, to shut out the world and what I had just done.\par
\par
     The gradual lightening of the veil behind one's eyes is easily ignored, so it was the abrupt shadow thrown over me that roused me the next morning well after dawn. Dlane was leaning over me, great golden eyes trained on my face in open concern.\par
\par
     "I was worried!" she said. "You are usually awake before me."\par
\par
     "Yes," I said. Fire spread over my back when I tried to move my arms. My thighs and calves were in similar shape and moving them apparently caused an appalling set of expressions to contort my face, for Dlane's eyes widened and one hand rose.\par
\par
     "Thenet?"\par
\par
     "It was a long night," I said with a grimace. I rolled awkwardly onto my knees, steadying myself against the tall root.\par
\par
     Dlane watched skeptically as I tried not to groan. "Sit down."\par
\par
     "We have to keep moving."\par
\par
     "Sit down," she said again, knitting words to action as she pushed me back to the ground. Tired indeed I must be for an anadi to be able to do so! "Not that way. On your stomach."\par
\par
     I complied with a grunt, stretching out parallel to the bank of the ravine and watching shafts of sunlight play on its rippled surface. After the horror of the utter darkness last night, just to see again was an unalloyed pleasure.\par
\par
     Cloth dragged across my spine as Dlane straddled my back. As I opened my mouth to protest, her hands gripped my shoulders and pulled. A very undignified squeak escaped me instead. She didn't have very strong hands, but determination: that she had in plenty. And she didn't need strength when she could rest all of her weight on the balls of her palms. Her thumbs pushed down along the doubled ridge of my spine while I sank slowly into the earth, certain that my insides were oozing together.\par
\par
     "You're very tight," she murmured.\par
\par
     "Pardon?" Or I said something that sounded like that.\par
\par
She must have understood because she continued, "Your back. I don't know if it's because you're over-worked or if you're normally that way." Her fingers softly skirted one of the bruises still healing from my expulsion from Mated. "You feel good, though."\par
\par
     I gurgled something incoherent and she leaned forward. The insides of her thighs grew taut against my hips and her face appeared at my shoulder. "Thenet? Are you okay?"\par
\par
     "I am\'85 at your mercy," I said finally.\par
\par
     She giggled. "That's good, I hope."\par
\par
     "If you keep doing what you're doing, I'll be yours forever."\par
\par
     "You already are," she said, satisfied, and returned to kneading my shoulders.\par
\par
     I swallowed all the saliva collecting in my mouth and said, "We should move on soon."\par
\par
     "Soon isn't immediately," Dlane said. "Are you always this tired in the morning?"\par
\par
     I didn't say anything for a while, eyelids drooping as she pushed her fingers into a tight swath of muscle across my shoulder-blades. I wasn't sure whether to tell her about my experiment during truedark. Of all people, she would probably understand\'85 but in the light of day I could hardly believe I'd done it. Traveling through truedark? Madness!\par
\par
     "Having to fool the hunters made me far more tense than I thought," I said at last. "And it was harder going alongside the ravine\'85 the ground is softer, more give."\par
\par
     Dlane leaned back against my rump. "I didn't know there was a stream here."\par
\par
     "We're going to follow it out of Neked Pamari. There'll be fish big enough to feed us once it clears the forest."\par
\par
     "Won't they think of that?"\par
\par
     "They?"\par
\par
     "The searchers," she said. "Won't they assume we'll be following a ready source of water?"\par
\par
     Sharp, my anadi. "There's more than one stream in the forest; this is the least friendly of the three. The others are clearer and wider."\par
\par
     Dlane frowned. "Oh."\par
\par
     Her weight on my rump exerted a pleasant pressure against my tail-plate bone, and she bobbed on it as I chuckled. "Don't sound so skeptical, ke anadi. This stream might be clotted, but it's clotted with edible weeds and crunchy insects we can eat if we're pressed for food."\par
\par
     "Insects!"\par
\par
     I laughed again. "Yes, insects. And now, ke anadi, we are resuming our travels!"\par
\par
     "You can't go anywhere if I sit on you," Dlane said.\par
\par
     I rolled over, spilling her to the ground. She squealed, torn between laughter and indignation. "Not fair!" she said, lying mussed on the sandy earth, sunlight winking off the red and orange beads in her braided hair. "You're stronger than I am!"\par
\par
     "Of course," I said. "I am your eperu, aren't I?" Leaning down sent spears of pain up my back which I steadfastly ignored. Picking up the packs, I slung one over my shoulder and handed the other to her. "Come on."\par
\par
     Dlane pulled the pack awkwardly over her dirty blouse, pausing to examine a small hole in the fabric with a tiny frown. "Is everything so fragile?"\par
\par
     "Not if you treat it well," I answered. I pointed northwest, alongside the ravine. "That way."\par
\par
     With a sigh, Dlane ducked under a branch and began walking through the dappled sunlight. My truedark adventure had skewed our walking pattern; we'd have to go more slowly as the day ripened, or the anadi would droop like a flower without water. I followed her, unsheathing my spear with protesting muscles and pushing aside the wet, ropy vines that clung to the trees overhanging the stream. Water was scarce; it was no wonder they grew so darkly fat nearby that they nearly strangled the branches that supported them.\par
\par
     "Why are there no animals?" Dlane asked.\par
\par
     "No animals?" One of my brows lifted. "They're everywhere, ke anadi. Just hiding from our large, clumsy footsteps. You can hear them running from us if you listen."\par
\par
     Her lovely ears swiveled forward and back and she stepped more cautiously. Poor delicate female-she began to approximate the feral grace of an eperu that way, but she'd been anadi too long to take so easily to the wild. I cast my eyes to the branches, searching for one of the somnolent wingflutters-ah!\par
\par
     "Look up," I said, and gently prodded a tree limb with the point of my spear.\par
\par
     A flat, wedge-shaped head fell off the branch, eyes like cabochon opals sparkling as it nosed at my spear. A thread-thin tongue twinkled as it sipped our scent. Dlane gasped as it began raveling itself around the haft of the spear, spreading brilliant wings as it did so: stiff feathers of searing-hot blue and gold-green to match its skin, tipped with a pearlescent carmine red.\par
\par
     "What is it?" she said, backing away with a hand at her throat.\par
\par
     "A wingflutter\'85 this kind is harmless. Some of them are poisonous, but they're hard to provoke during the day. If you hold them they can't seem to tell that you're not just another tree." I caressed its head as it slid down the haft and over my hand.\par
\par
     "Are those real feathers?"\par
\par
     "Yes\'85 but hard. They can't fly as much as glide."\par
\par
     Dlane took a step closer. "It's so bright. Like a head-piece I've seen on one of the emodo."\par
\par
     "It might have been," I said. "They're easy to kill while somnolent." I unwrapped the flutter from my wrist before it could crawl any further up me and dropped it back on the branch.\par
\par
And then I froze.\par
\par
     The musty perfume of the wingflutter - it must have clogged my nose. The smell of the \'f1edsu struck me so hard I staggered.\par
\par
     "Ke anadi, run!" I cried, but even as I said it they boiled from the bushes on both sides of the stream. I cursed myself seven times a fool for letting down my guard.\par
\par
     Dlane let out a strangled cry and moved behind me, pressing her breast to my back and locking her arms around my waist. I cupped her rump with one hand, the other holding the spear and waiting for the master of the wailing beasts.\par
\par
     He stepped out of the brush a few minutes later, a different Jokkad from the one I'd deceived. My mouth dried on seeing him, and Dlane's throat moved against my shoulder as she swallowed hard.\par
\par
     He was simply beautiful. His skin looked like black glass, deep but with a sheen that gave its upper layers the illusion of transparency. The spirals across his skin were numerous and perfectly symmetrical, white and edged like frosted cuts in glass. They matched his mane and tail, a thunderous storm gray in all the shades of a darkening sky.\par
\par
     His eyes were calm and the complex gray-blue of the head of his spear: as hard as well. No other Jokka accompanied him, but he hardly needed the support. He was without doubt the most feral-looking emodo I'd ever seen. His feet-claws had been sharpened, and like the eperu on the hunt he wore only an abbreviated long-cloth.\par
\par
     He said nothing, only held out one hand to Dlane, who began quivering at my back like a wounded animal.\par
\par
     "No."\par
\par
     The word was mine. I had the presence of mind to wonder at the clarity of my voice.\par
\par
     "You are a nothing, eperu. A dead thing walking. It is not for you to decide her fate."\par
\par
     "It is as much for me to decide her fate as it is for you," I said. My grip on my spear tightened. "You will have to kill me before you take her."\par
\par
     "I won't have to," he said. "She'll come to me. Won't you, ba anadi?"\par
\par
     Still Dlane did not speak. I spoke for her. "She is not a child."\par
\par
     "She is acting like one," the emodo said.\par
\par
     My ears flattened. "Who are you? Have you no respect? Your name, at least, rude male!"\par
\par
     He must have been amused by my insolence, given my untenable situation. I could have bested him as long as I kept out of his stronger arms, but not with twelve \'f1edsu ringing me. "I am Roika Edze-Emodo. You are Dlane Ashoi-Anadi and the dead-walking Thenet Mated-Eperu. Now that we are acquainted with one another, let us end this thing."\par
\par
     "Stop saying that!" I said testily. "I am not dead, if you hadn't observed."\par
\par
     "You will be if you interfere," Roika said. He canted his head. "I am not interested in you, det eperu. You are dead as far as our world is concerned - a Houseless being, useful to no one. It is the ka\'f1a I've come to retrieve."\par
\par
     "Why do you care about her?"\par
\par
     "Because Ashoi has said whoever can bring her back will win her. And I want her for my House. I want my babes in her womb."\par
\par
     Dlane's trembling intensified. I could sense her revulsion in every shudder.\par
\par
     "But most of all, I want to take the clothing off of her and return her to the world she belongs to. The one you have forfeited, det eperu."\par
\par
     It was growing more difficult to ignore the snide title. I forced myself to focus on his eyes and said, "You might as well set the beasts on us now, ke emodo. We will not come with you."\par
\par
     Roika strode to us. I stared at the prints he left behind in the soil: I could fit two of Dlane's strides in his, one and a half of my own. Within moments he was standing before us and I no longer needed to estimate his size. Taller than I was by a head and so thickly sinewed across his upper body he could have broken either of us with his corded wrists, he loomed, allowing only a bare finger's breadth between my ribcage and his stomach. My only thought was that Dlane would shatter trying to bear this behemoth's vibrant children.\par
\par
     He had a curious look. "How can you possibly think to defy me?" he asked, honestly puzzled from the tilt of his ears. His eyes still held the hardness of metal. "You are two weak beings against me and my twelve hunters. You cannot possibly believe you can win against me. Would you honestly prefer death to your return to a proper life?"\par
\par
     "May I remind you that my 'proper life' is actually death at the foot-claws of the emodo of Mated?" I asked, letting my lip curl back from my fangs.\par
\par
     "I had not forgotten, eperu." At least he'd dropped the insulting prefix. "But I know about you. You were born to be guardian to this," he reached out and touched Dlane's chin over my shoulder. She shivered violently and I snarled as he continued, "How can you take her from her element? How can you allow her to risk her life this way? Have you gone mad?"\par
\par
     "She made the choice, not I," I answered. "Now it is for me to keep her as safe as I can. Not to command her. It is never for the eperu to command!"\par
\par
     "Except to keep the anadi and emodo safe. Do you forget yourself?"\par
\par
     His preoccupation with me both confounded and inspired. If I could keep him talking long enough, I might be able to catch him off-guard. Steeling myself, I said, "No, ke emodo. But I am an eperu without allegiance now. I can no longer go home. If this anadi wants to run away, to guard her is all that's left to me."\par
\par
     "You should have taken her home."\par
\par
     "And then submitted myself to death," I said. I could sense Dlane's confusion warring with her fear: her quivers were intermittent now, interrupted by stillness and the tension of her taut muscles against my back.\par
\par
     "Yes," said the emodo.\par
\par
     Unaccountably I found myself looking away from him. "I had thought to," I admitted, ignoring Dlane's hiss. "But I do not want to die, ke emodo. And neither does she."\par
\par
     He surprised me by taking my chin in his hand and pulling it back. "And if I offered you a place again?"\par
\par
     "Why?" I challenged. "Why would you bother? I am a dead thing walking, as you said. You could easily wrest her from me and leave me to the \'f1edsu."\par
\par
     Roika grinned. "I like you," he said, voice so deep it vibrated through his fingers to my jawbones. "You are afraid of me, but you are standing here all the same."\par
\par
     "And if I took your offer?" I asked. I was certain I wasn't interested - reasonably certain, anyway - but if I could convince him otherwise\'85.\par
\par
     "Then I will take you both home. Your beautiful anadi will become the ka\'f1a-befidzu and care for my House while she bears my children\'85 whom I expect will be as lovely and intelligent as she seems to be. And you\'85 may be her guardian."\par
\par
     I understood, then, that I was talking to the Head of House Edze\'85 and I was completely unprepared for the swell of longing that set my heart to thready racing. To belong again! To be part of a Household! To know my place again, to not have to worry about Dlane's strange ideas\'85.\par
\par
     His fingers gently spread over my cheeks. My indecision was as obvious as the sunlight. I thought about bathing tubs in cool stone rooms. Of flavored ices I could spoon into Dlane's mouth and silken sheets I could lay out for her star-silvered bed.\par
\par
     "Come with me," he said. His thumbs traced my cheekbones to my eyes, blocking the afternoon sun.\par
\par
     I thought of my anadi, trapped in the harness, of blind eyes and a slack mouth, washed clean of language by the drool of madness.\par
\par
     I shoved Dlane into the ravine. "Run! Swim!" Then I rocked back and kicked Roika in the stomach with an open foot, claws extended. It was like striking a rock. One of his hands grabbed my ankle, the other seized my throat. I dropped the spear and our packs to grab his hands. Behind me the uneven splashes of Dlane's feet receded as she stumbled away through the water.\par
\par
     He whistled to the \'f1edsu, sending them wailing in pursuit, then slowly returned his gaze to me. "Why did you bother?" he asked, and there was more curiosity than anger in his rumble-deep bass. "You know she cannot outrun them. You wanted to come home with me. You wanted respectability. I could see the yearning in your eyes."\par
\par
     A strange mix of shame and desire warred in me. I swallowed against his broad palm and said, "But it was not what she wanted, ke emodo. And I want what she wants."\par
\par
     Roika laughed. I wanted to hurt him, to smite him\'85 anything to make him stop. I wanted to bite him. So I did. My knee crumpled forward. My fangs punched deep into the tender scaled flesh at his shoulder. I tasted the white syrup of his blood. I sucked on it until the flesh came clean and then ripped back.\par
\par
     Startled, the emodo loosened his grasp. I twisted away and jumped into the ravine. My feet scrabbled for purchase and then I was off, sprinting after Dlane and the hunting beasts. I had only a handful of minutes before Roika followed: that we bleed copiously from open wounds is a definite danger, but those wounds clot quickly if held together.\par
\par
     Without spear, without packs, I raced after my anadi. Scooping up a rock as I splashed through the water, I pursued the wail-song of the hunting beasts until I could see the backs of the stragglers. The pebble whizzed from my hand and connected with the base of a bobbing skull. I snatched several more and stopped for a heartbeat. Everything had slowed: dust-motes in the sunlight glittered and fell with an aching languor. The Moment had retrieved me and I could walk through it like honey. Two more of the \'f1edsu fell and I was running again, only it felt as if I barely moved.\par
\par
     The thick black tree hunched over the ravine shot to me and I fell in the water trying to stop fast enough to avoid it. Dlane hung in its topmost branches, surrounded on all sides by the hunters. They took no note of me\'85 it was the anadi they had been set upon. Their single-minded dedication allowed me ample time to knock a few more down before the first realized the pack was under attack.\par
\par
     Then they turned on me.\par
\par
     "Thenet!" Dlane shrieked.\par
\par
     The \'f1edsu and I\'85 six of them and one of me. But I was in the Moment, and Roika had taken away all my fear. I let claws slide from the pockets in my finger tips, dug my feet claws deeper into the soil and snarled at them. Their gibbering moans rose in pitch to slavering wails and they rushed me.\par
\par
     I jumped, straight up, onto the tree. The leaves hissed as my claws sank into the flesh of the branch. From there my collection of pebbles flew from my hand to their tiny eyes, or down the maw of open throats. It was almost laughable, how quickly the last tottered and slumped.\par
\par
     Dlane whimpered. I shook myself from my fugue with difficulty and reached to her, unnerved by the swiftness of my hand. "Come on," I said, but my voice was hoarse. "He won't be long."\par
\par
     "I thought you would leave me-"\par
\par
     "You thought wrong," I said roughly. I dropped from the tree branch back into the ravine and held up my hands to catch her. For a moment she studied me, her eyes thinned to glittering golden slits. Then she dropped off the tree and into my arms. Our backs and bodies relieved of any burden, any aid, nevertheless we jogged down the stream without empty hands: holding the other's. The sunlight filtered through the leaves to dance merrily upon the sprays of water we splashed.\par
\par
     "Will he follow us?"\par
\par
     "I don't know," I said. "We must assume it."\par
\par
     Her fingers tightened in mine. "He\'85."\par
\par
     "Frightened you-"\par
\par
     "-Tempted you."\par
\par
     I glanced at her, then laughed. Quietly, too aware of the fragility of our safety. "It is not a sin to long for things, ke anadi."\par
\par
     "No," she said after a moment, "And you made the right choice."\par
\par
     Before I could reply to that, she finished, "You would make a bad wingflitter."\par
\par
     I snorted. "We might be caught yet, ke anadi. Let us run!"\par
\par
     And we did.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     By early nightfall Dlane was tripping on her own feet. I looped an arm around her waist and said, "Stop."\par
\par
     She needed no other incentive and sagged against me. Lifting my head, I tasted the air, sampled the hum of the insects for interruptions: only the plash of tiny beetles in the water and the scent of sap and water and acid soil. In the layered shadows of the darkness I could discern no abnormal movements, only the passing of small creatures, the weaving of branches in the earth-spiced wind.\par
\par
     "I think we're safe," I said quietly.\par
\par
     "Good!"\par
\par
     "For now," I finished.\par
\par
     We stood silently, enveloped in the cool hand of the forest. Dlane gulped at the air, sounding alarmingly like she trying not to sob.\par
\par
     "Ke anadi?"\par
\par
     "Will it ever end?" she asked, the intonation that might have made it a question failing. "You've killed fourteen beasts and saved me from two searchers. How much longer can we be so lucky?"\par
\par
     "This isn't luck, ke anadi," I said, pulling her into my arms and resuming our course down the stream. She did not resist.\par
\par
     "And now without food or clothing or even your spear! How will we survive?"\par
\par
     "As Jokka always have. Through skill and intelligence, and perhaps the will of the Trinity."\par
\par
     "The Trinity," she said bitterly. "What Trinity? What gods?"\par
\par
     "Hush, ke anadi, you're talking nonsense," I said. She could take many things from me, my anadi, but my faith in the Brightness, World and Void\'85 that she would not have.\par
\par
     Perhaps she sensed my rejection\'85 whatever the case she rested against my chest without speaking. Her body was damp, and the soft hairs cloaking her feet had curled from the run. I found a rhythm and strode down the ravine, trying not to disturb the water.\par
\par
     "Intelligence," Dlane murmured. "Always intelligence. If it is intelligence through which we survive, why do we destroy it in a third of our population?"\par
\par
     I opened my mouth, then frowned. "We do not destroy intelligence, ke anadi."\par
\par
     "Yes we do," Dlane said. "Breeding the anadi. It kills their minds."\par
\par
     Exasperated, I asked, "And the alternative? Shall we get children on animals?"\par
\par
     "In the end, that is exactly what we do," Dlane said. "And I would end up as intelligent and malleable as a \'f1edsu. Point me in a direction and give me a simple command, and wailing and moaning I follow."\par
\par
     The image so revolted me I stumbled.\par
\par
     "You see?"\par
\par
     "What can I say?" I said. "What would you have us do? If we did not breed, we would grow old. There would be no laughter of the young to sustain us. The last of us would finally die and there would be no Jokka. The wind would chase clean the fields we tended, the towns we built, scour them from the earth, and it would be as if we never lived. What would the world be then?"\par
\par
     "I don't know," Dlane said. "Would it matter? We would not be there to see it."\par
\par
     "A world without Jokka!" I didn't stop because the motions had become mechanical, beyond my control. I walked because stopping, I would fall. "I cannot fathom it! Dlane\'85 we are our race."\par
\par
     "Why does it matter so much to you?" she asked, golden eyes rising to mine. I did not spare a glance for her; to do so would be to drown in the gold, to acknowledge her utter earnestness. To acknowledge that she could hold the death of our race in her mind with enough detachment to measure whether it was good or bad. "You will die, Thenet\'85 and after that, you will know no more, or at least have no more concern with the Jokka."\par
\par
     "How do you know?" I asked. "My death will not make me any less a Jokkad."\par
\par
     "You'll be dead, Thenet. The only Jokku thing about you then will be your corpse, and even that will rot away and return to the earth," Dlane said.\par
\par
     I almost dropped her.\par
\par
     "When I die," I said, staring down at her, "I will be embraced by the World. It will help me to my feet and anoint me with the paint and long-cloth of the Earth and Wind. It will give me eyes of the Sky, so that I may hold the Brightness without blinking, and scales of stars that I may be held by the Void without shivering. With a spear I will hunt the unmaking spirits with all my comrades at my side, and make safe the World for the Jokka still living."\par
\par
     Dlane said, "For all eternity."\par
\par
     "For all eternity," I agreed.\par
\par
     "How wearisome," Dlane said.\par
\par
     My arms trembled. I stopped to draw a long breath of the cool air, then walked to the nearest tree. I pushed her toward it.\par
\par
     She climbed onto the branch, eyes wide. "Thenet?"\par
\par
     "Stay here," I said roughly. Turning my back on her, I began splashing down the way we'd come.\par
\par
     "Thenet!"\par
\par
     I didn't answer.\par
\par
     Behind us, where we'd encountered Roika, I'd dropped my spear. Retrieving it would arm me again, and perhaps take my mind off the anger and fear twisting my stomach into knots. The sky had grown far darker but I barely noticed it, so intent on my own thoughts that I had no attention to spare for the world around me\'85 a sad state for one of the eperu.\par
\par
     Without Dlane to slow me, I easily traversed the distance: even in the stream I was silent, walking in the shallows where the clammy mud muffled my foot-falls. My body ached but I did not tire\'85 and it was only when I'd drawn nigh to the place I'd dropped the spear that truedark surprised me utterly by falling.\par
\par
     Slowly I bent down and stretched my hand over the ground, feeling its bumps and nubs, the torn flesh of grasses, the tickle of insects. Sliding from the stream bed onto my hands and knees, I let my fingers see for my eyes. The skin on my back tingled at every brush of the intermittent wind and I shivered. To be so horribly exposed in the utter darkness without vision - I hadn't expected to travel again during truedark, and I'd quickly blocked from my mind how terrifying it had been.\par
\par
     My fingers scraped across the haft of my spear. I grabbed it, grateful for the feel of something familiar.\par
\par
     A firebright skated over my back, tracing a thread of light through the air so intense my eyes watered. I shaded them as several more of the dancing insects flew from the trees toward a point not sixty paces distant. A knot of the firebrights collected over a rock there, casting a warm yellow glow on the face of a Jokkad\'85.\par
\par
     Of Roika.\par
\par
     I drew back, the spear rattling against the ground.\par
\par
     "Who's there?"\par
\par
     He could not see me. The emodo could not see me! But I could see him. I drew myself to my feet and said, "No one worth your time, ke emodo."\par
\par
     "Thenet!" he hissed, sitting up. "You are out there, in the dark?"\par
\par
     "I am here, ke emodo," I said, and added cruelly, "And I am watching you."\par
\par
     The flickering of the firebrights painted his face in broad swatches as he leaned back against the tree and folded his hands behind his head. "Watch then. I do not fear you."\par
\par
     "You should," I said. "I am armed, and you would not see my approach. Nor hear it, unless I wanted you to."\par
\par
     "But you will not hurt me." He sounded supremely unworried. It annoyed me. "Although I admit to curiosity. It is truedark. Jokka do not travel during truedark."\par
\par
     "Ah, but I am a dead thing upright, remember, ke emodo?" I answered with asperity. The muscles of his midriff rippled as he breathed and caught the dance of the firebrights, reflecting them on glass-dark skin.\par
\par
     He chuckled. "Ah, my words to my face, is that it. Perhaps I underestimated the power she has over you, ke eperu, if already you are unyoked enough to brave the darkness of the deepest hour."\par
\par
     "What do you mean, unyoked?"\par
\par
     "Don't you understand?" He smiled. "She is the new way through the forest, and I am the path. Which way are you walking, ke eperu? And how long will it be before the other calls you back? How long before you cannot decide which is best?"\par
\par
     "You will not unnerve me with riddles," I said, my fingers drumming softly on the spear's handle. "I have my anadi and you do not, and that is the way it will remain."\par
\par
     "Don't worry\'85 you will come back to me. I don't think you're strong enough for the trail Dlane is making for you, ke eperu. No offense intended."\par
\par
     "No offense taken," I said, though my ears had slicked to my head. "Who are you?"\par
\par
     "Roika Ed-"\par
\par
     "No," I interrupted, angered. "Who are you? You answer sometimes in riddles but literally when it suits you. You know too much. You are more than a hopeful suitor."\par
\par
     He laughed again, a sound like rocks rolling against one another. "Ah! But perhaps your mind is indeed as swift as your feet, ke eperu. Very well. I am the Head of Household because I have no anadi."\par
\par
     "You are a junior House," I said.\par
\par
     Roika shook his maned head. "You are not listening, ke eperu; quite a failing in a hunter. I said I have no anadi."\par
\par
     "No anadi? None? Not even your mother? I\'85 I don't understand. You're not saying you are Broken?"\par
\par
     "And if I am?"\par
\par
     My vision thinned until I could see only him, and I would have staggered had it not been for my spear. I leaned against it and said, "There has not been a split of House within town in more ages than a mountain can count. I would have heard\'85."\par
\par
     "If I'd been from your town, but I am not. I came here to start anew. Ashoi needs the money I can offer and the promise of a trade alliance. I need the legitimacy Ashoi's ka\'f1a will bestow on my outrageous head."\par
\par
     It did not surprise me at all that he needed legitimacy if indeed he was Broken. Houses split with predictable regularity, based on allegiances and finances. But a House made by Break and not by Founding was unusual in the extreme. Only scandal or violence could Break a House, leaving me to wonder which had sent Roika a-field from his home turf. Afield from his home turf! Jokka did not often travel to other towns. I couldn\rquote t even think of the names of any.\par
\par
     "Is that all?" I asked warily.\par
\par
     "I don't know," Roika answered. He grinned. "Is it?"\par
\par
     "Somehow I don't think so."\par
\par
     Roika showed his teeth. "You'd be right. But you're not ready to hear the rest of it yet, ke eperu. And because of that I will let you go."\par
\par
     My heart tripped over itself. "Pardon, ke emodo?"\par
\par
     "You heard me. Leave the forest. No one will stop you\'85 I will see to that."\par
\par
     Suspicion crashed over me, tightening my grip on the spear. "Why?"\par
\par
     "Because neither you nor your anadi is ready. When you are, you will come to me. You will recognize in me the best of your choices."\par
\par
     "Never," I said.\par
\par
     He shook his head. "We shall see, shall we?"\par
\par
     "Look well on me, ke emodo," I said haughtily, straightening even though I knew his eyes could not part the smothering folds of truedark. "For this is the last time you will see me."\par
\par
     Said he only, "Time will decide."\par
\par
     Angry at him, at Dlane, at the world for dropping the deep darks on me, for catching me unawares, I turned my back on him and strode away with a confidence that had more in common with foolishness than prudence. Luck kept me from falling when my foot slid on the muddy edge of the ravine, and my spear bit into the opposite bank in time to give me leverage to remain standing. Poised there with my feet teetering on the slippery bank and the greater part of my weight resting against the haft of the spear, I realized the precariousness of my senses\'85 for though I could hear the noises of the woods, could feel the movement of the wind against my skin, I'd never really been trained to navigate by them alone. Without sight I was as able as Dlane - less so, because of my dangerous assumption of my own competence.\par
\par
     I glanced over my shoulder, but the light from Roika's honey was obscured by the trees. I was alone again.\par
\par
     Pushing myself back up, I very gently took the first steps into the ravine. Our dropped packs could not be far from here, but I hadn't the slightest idea where Dlane had dropped them. I could wait until truedark lifted to search for them\'85 but if I could see, so could Roika. He'd said he would not take us, but I could not believe him. I had only his word against the certainty of his mysterious and perhaps disreputable past.\par
\par
     The wind had a soft whisper but no rhythm to its irregular slough. The insects sometimes peeped and sometimes fell silent. I could hear no pattern. I felt disconnected.\par
\par
     I pressed on. There was little I could do otherwise.\par
\par
     Seeing Dlane later that morning re-acquainted me with the ire I'd thought had passed. Not even the signs of a night spent in terror could gentle my soul. She wanted my soul, did she? It already belonged to the legion of the World. My afterlife was too great a price to pay even for the strange extension she'd bought on my living.\par
\par
     So I did not say anything to her, only helped her off the tree. Her body was wracked with shivers grossly magnified by the sensitivity truedark-traveling had elicited in my skin, my fingers. There was a dark smear of tears along the edges of her mouth and down her throat. She smelled like a terrified animal, all acrid oil. Her body was softness: slender curves, padded by a luxurious and sheltered life. I could barely feel the knob of her hip bone through her skin.\par
\par
     I let her down onto her feet and began walking again. She followed, kicking up the water.\par
\par
     We continued well into the afternoon. I paused to drink and to pull a few of the more palatable weeds off the edge of the ravine. My stomach rumbled but I ignored it.\par
\par
     I was aware of Dlane stumbling doggedly beside me but I did not make allowances for her weaknesses. Did she wish to break the mold of the anadi? Did she wish to travel free as eperu do? Then let her do so.\par
\par
     Thus I led her well into the evening. I made a concession to her need to sleep by stopping then even though doing so would force her into a sleeping mode alien to the anadi, who rested during the heat of the day. By dawn I was up again, pulling her roughly behind me. She did not protest.\par
\par
     How she managed I cannot say. I wore anger as a shield and a blanket, and my lucid dreams when I dozed were violent, full of screaming \'f1edsu and emodo hunters with foaming mouths and poisoned spears.\par
\par
     Slogging through the broadening stream in water that reached up to caress my calves, I squinted against the rays of bronze sunlight that shimmered through the translucent leaves. The trees had grown sparser and past their trunks I could catch glimpses of the open plains. An occasional fish rubbed as it swam past, a sure sign of the encroaching edge.\par
\par
     A few steps later I stood in the sunlight, my shoulders thrown back. A perfect stillness descended at this final break in the canopy. Before me the vista of endless freedom stretched, framed only in a few delicate fronds. Brilliant were the bronze rays falling on the sparkling water.\par
\par
     The silence shattered with a splash. Startled, I twisted around to find Dlane in the stream, one hand alone keeping her chest above the water. Having barely looked at her in the past days I abruptly saw the lines drawn beneath her weary eyes, the gracelessness of her sagging head. The water sloshed around me as I dropped to my knees next to her, pulling her up.\par
\par
     "Dlane!" I paused, realized she was due far more respect than I'd been giving her lately and said, softer, "Ke anadi, my sorrow, I was cruel-"\par
\par
     She clung to me weakly.\par
\par
     "But you angered me."\par
\par
     Her soaked fingers caught in my mane braid, pulled at the glass beads woven in it. "Thenet\'85 I did not mean\'85 there is just\'85 if there are gods for me, then they have betrayed me by giving me this mind and then handing me an anadi's fate. Do you understand? There is nothing left for me. Nothing."\par
\par
     Even her voice had lost its smooth and lovely timbre. I pulled her body out of the water and against my own. Her skin was radiating an alarming amount of heat, slick fire against my waist, my thighs. What had I done to her? Had I killed her? Void forfend! Brightness forgive! "Ke anadi, please, please be well. I should not have pushed you so far."\par
\par
     "You were angry. You are not used to them. I have lived with them all my life, these terrible thoughts. They have driven me like whips, like a harness, they have stripped everything from me."\par
\par
     "Hush!" I said, touching my finger to her mouth. "Hush. Save your strength. Don't tax your body with vile thoughts. Just rest here. I won't let any harm come to you\'85."\par
\par
     A twitch of a sob almost freed a few tears from her teeth, but she was too tired, too dry inside. I held her curled to me like a shell and doused her hot forehead with the waters of the cool stream, gently brushed the sodden tendrils of hair from her beautiful cheeks.\par
\par
     "Don't die," I whispered to her. "Not after what you have done. It's only dawn, and I don't know what to do with the day."\par
\par
     Her ribcage rose and fell, too swiftly for my taste, too hot to my fingertips. I scooped her from the water and carried her as tenderly as any infant to the banks. There I divested her of her cold wet clothes and wiped her dry with my blouse. I held her and rocked her and sang her lullabies until the sun set.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     Through that insufferable night I remained there at the bank of the stream. Beneath the shade of a softly rustling white-leaf tree I sat, cradling Dlane in my arms. I braced her body between my legs, my hands curling her naked body to my dirty blouse.\par
\par
     I stroked the dye, the beads from her hair as the thin, shallow breath of her body slipped from her mouth. My fingers untangled the braid from her tail and combed it until it felt soft to the touch, its bright gold muted by the blue and purple of the eve. The last glass ornament dropped from her mane with a soft thunk, catching a crescent of light in its amber depths as it rolled behind a clump of grayed grass.\par
\par
     "Be as you are, ke anadi," I said softly to her. "For you are anadi\'85 at least today."\par
\par
     She did not hear me, for if she had she would have replied. But to have her nude at last, unfettered by the masks of pretense and wishes, relieved me even as it fueled my fears. No reasonable anadi would have been able to keep the pace I'd set.\par
\par
     Her body was still too warm. I held it close and set my chin atop her head, staring into the dusky horizon. The wind pulled a few strands of flat black hair from my mane and blew them over my eyes, caressed the soft scales of my cheeks with a cool dry touch that coaxed a shiver from the base of my spine. My ears cupped the trickle-speech of the stream, but I could not understand.\par
\par
     We were so close to freedom. Roika had guaranteed it. The plains were only a few steps away\'85 but I did not move. My reason for motion rested in my embrace and while she slept I had no purpose.\par
\par
     As the night layered its veil more and more heavily upon the world around me, I wondered at the strangeness of this cusp. What worth was a life that had no power? No direction?\par
\par
     The minutes dragged. My own breathing became an enemy; I heard it, compared it to hers, tried to breathe more rapidly and prayed for her to slow. We would meet in the middle, I told her, but her eyelids didn't even flicker. I traced the fall of a golden lash down her cheeks by the thin glint it gathered as it tumbled.\par
\par
     I closed my eyes. Our hair mingled, troubled my eyes. I ignored the itch inside my mouth. There was no time for tears. Or was there?\par
\par
     In the dark, safe when safety least mattered, I was no one: a dead thing, waiting for the verdict, waiting for the Void to claim me, or the Brightness. So I closed my eyes and let my ears see for me, and the sour tang of thick sadness welled from my teeth and tainted my spit when I swallowed.\par
\par
     Truedark came. I could sense it without even looking, for it obliterated the subtle play of the dark grays seen through my eyelids. With it came the silence of the world\'85 only the few insects still waking, only the bravest hunters who knew the earth with claws and noses walking. I listened to the wind through the trees and the occasional fish bob to the water's surface. I listened to Dlane's breathing and licked my fangs over and over again, catching my tears before they could stain her naked flesh. My first hissing sob surprised me, but I did not have the strength to stop it.\par
\par
     I laid my cheek against her head and wept the knot of pain and confusion tighter in my chest until it burned hotter than Dlane's sharp, thin ribs against my waist. Until the pressure was so great I thought I would die. Truedark is the Void's time. I waited for Him - I wanted Him.\par
\par
     Take me, I cried. I cannot withstand this.\par
\par
     The wind's chill hands on my fevered neck and shoulder sent shudders through my body. I welcomed this proxy, did not object to the cold fingers running toward my hidden chest. Be open, it seemed to say. But I could not with Dlane in my arms\'85 and I could not give her up.\par
\par
     The leaves ceased their murmurs. In the cool and utter dark I was spared\'85 or condemned.\par
\par
     When I opened my eyes, it was to the faintest smudges of gold on the horizon\'85 and the brilliant pool of gold in her eyes. They were open, and I could see my haggard face clearly on their surfaces.\par
\par
     "Dlane," I said, or tried. The hoarse rattle that my throat produced was only faintly like her name. I tried again. "Dlane\'85."\par
\par
     "You did not abandon me," she said softly.\par
\par
     "No," I said, trying to pull her closer, but we were crushed together. I tried to find words my throat would allow, but they did not appear. "No."\par
\par
     Her mouth pressed against my throat, and parted lips freed her tongue to lap against my skin. My head hung, and I closed my eyes, accepting the kiss. One last shudder rolled up my aching spine and in its wake came a serene emptiness, flooding my abused spirit like a tide on a dry and cracked shore. Filling the cracks. Easing them. She licked the knot apart and the day came.\par
\par
     "Now what?" I whispered.\par
\par
     "Now anything," she answered. "That is the wonder of it."\par
\par
