\par
Part 2\par
\par
 \par
\par
     I crouched, enveloped in the Moment. The heat of the afternoon sun prickled gems of cool sweat from my skin. Coiled tightly, my body sang: the muscles had already moved, they were only waiting for the world to catch up with them.\par
\par
     My shadow covered the broad stream as I leaned forward, my extended claws gripping the rock near its center. My arm held the spear out-thrust from my body as I waited, my beaded mane hanging in front of my shoulder.\par
\par
     As always, the first fish tentatively swam from the heat of the stream into the cool shadow I provided. Each tail swipe that propelled them had a languor so pronounced one wondered how they moved at all. I watched until a particularly fat one wandered into the optimal area. The twitch I sent through my body seemed slow to me, and my hips tilted backward, my arm swinging forward with an inevitability fashioned from my entire weight. The bright tooth pricked the surface of the water and came up flushed with pale blood.\par
\par
     The Moment shattered when I grabbed its wriggling, slippery body and jerked it off the spearhead. A quick twist and it was dead.\par
\par
     "Thenet?" her sleepy voice summoned me from my perch. I slid from over the stream, wiping my brow with the side of my arm.\par
\par
     "You're awake early," I said, crouching beside her. "I've barely finished catching our dinner."\par
\par
     She sighed and settled down on the bank beside me. "You are so good to me, Thenet. I don't know what I would have done without you."\par
\par
     "Eaten very badly, if at all," I teased gently. The bottom of my long-cloth hung sodden by the stream and I wrung it out. I touched her chin with wet fingers. "You look so unhappy. It is a beautiful day, ke anadi. Why the sad mask?"\par
\par
     Dlane shook her head. "It is nothing," she said. "I am restless, maybe. We've been here so long\'85."\par
\par
     I strung the fish up with a tough vine and looped it over the haft of my spear. It had only been two weeks, but for my purposeful anadi, I could see how it might chafe. "Come," I said. "We can talk about it while I cook."\par
\par
     She nodded, rubbing the edge of the blouse with her dry fingers. I'd given her my pants and shirt when we'd made our camp by the praying tree, both to return to her that strange sense of control over her destiny and to protect her more fragile skin from direct sunlight. I retained the patterned long-cloth, and occasionally the vest when I took cold\'85 more out of comforting habit than need with the summer beginning.\par
\par
     Together we walked to the praying tree, the only tree within several days walk along the banks. With its broad, flexible limbs and long, thin leaves, the hardy praying tree was barely troubled by winter or even the occasional wind-storm. Its smooth, yellow-gray trunk had an ageless elasticity that served it well in adversity, thus its name: for prayer makes of the soul a thing enduring, and faith lends a flexibility to the soul that lets it weather any inclemency. It seemed entirely appropriate to me that we had made our meager camp there, and that its blade-slender leaves provided our shifting shade in the warm breeze.\par
\par
     I dropped the product of my afternoon's labors onto the ground beside the small fire pit and started laying the fire ground: drying the twigs and leaves of the praying tree offered a more palatable cooking fire than other alternatives, and the fire burned well if fed with some of the thicker weeds clustered around the banks of the stream. I built a fragrant, smoky fire to roast the fish on and set to cleaning them - something easier said than accomplished. My spear head was attached to the haft, rendering the metal blade useless for this sort of work; even more annoying, the blade edge had grown dull and I had no proper tools to hone it without mangling its shape. Even if I'd decided to break one of my glass ornaments for its edge it wouldn't be large enough for me to safely hold.\par
\par
     In the end I contented myself with bone slivers scavenged off the corpses of the few herbivores that found enough sustenance on the scraggly back of the World to survive. The slivers were not large enough to make true bone knives of, but they sufficed once I wrapped them in fabric and fresh weeds. Good fortune had stocked the river with more of the skinned fish than the scaled ones, which meant I made a little less of a mess.\par
\par
     As usual, Dlane watched me at my labors. I'd never offered to teach her these simple chores; she needed more sleep with so little shelter to keep her cool, and conscious she tended toward more somnolence than I'd seen in most anadi. I alternated between concern and actual fear for her safety, and took care to keep her covered. My long-cloth served as an adequate additional cover when strung over the branches of the praying tree on especially sunny days.\par
\par
     "There is more I have to do than just stay here," she said, plucking at the tangled golden hair on her ankle. It had been quite a while since either of us had seen a comb. "Though you have made this pleasant, Thenet, it is time to leave."\par
\par
     "Leave for what?" I asked, and held up a hand as she opened her mouth. "And don't tell me 'to journey to the Birthwell.' We're not even close to equipped for such a journey."\par
\par
     "Can't we make what we need from the land and the animals?" Dlane asked.\par
\par
     I stared at her incredulously. "We are Jokka, Dlane. We do not live without other Jokka. There's too much I don't know\'85 I wouldn't even know where to begin to make a mobile town of two self-sufficient without even a sand-strip to keep a useful point on my spear."\par
\par
     "We are not going back to town!" Dlane said, baring her teeth.\par
\par
     I shook my head. "No. But there are more settlements than ours. We could travel to one of those, re-supply. Then we might have what we need to make a long journey."\par
\par
     "Then others would see us," Dlane said. "They would wonder what a lone anadi was doing, traveling with a single eperu for escort. They would ask us what House we hailed from, and what town."\par
\par
     "Probably," I said.\par
\par
     She glanced at me. "They would not ask a single eperu those questions, would they?"\par
\par
     I frowned, leaning back from my efforts to study her. Her skin was still cracking from the heat. She needed lotion - some of the scented, moisturizing kind made by the emodo from crushed leaves, flower petals and fruit flesh. Would that I had some to slather on her dry, tired face! "They would ask. A Jokkad traveling alone is still strange."\par
\par
     "But fewer questions," Dlane said, pushing.\par
\par
     I stuck the fish with the twig with a little more enthusiasm than was strictly necessary. "Ke anadi, you are asking me to leave you alone."\par
\par
     "Yes."\par
\par
     "For enough time to shop in town\'85 no! For enough time for me to beg or work for enough shell to pay for supplies in town? I could not do such a thing, ke anadi. I will not leave you alone for so long. You can barely keep yourself cool and you're asking me to leave you to the wind and the animals and the hunters? Are you mad?"\par
\par
     Dlane did not reply, letting her midriff down between her crossed legs and stretching her arms in front of her. I balanced the fish on their sticks over the fragrant fire and the first hints of their tantalizing aroma wafted toward my flared nostrils. I buried the entrails some distance from the camp; when I returned she hadn't moved.\par
\par
     "Thenet," she murmured.\par
\par
     I crouched before her, elbows resting on my pale knees.\par
\par
     "It has been a long time since I've felt clean."\par
\par
     I smiled wryly. Sand made a poor substitute for soap and did very little for mane and tail. Combs, shell and clothing were only the beginning of the things we'd lost in the packs. "You get used to it," I said.\par
\par
     "Do you?" Dlane wondered. She rested her cheek against her arm and looked up at me. "I didn't think I would miss civilization."\par
\par
     "It's no crime to want a little comfort in life," I said.\par
\par
     "Isn't it?" She sighed. "Comfort breeds stagnation. It helps you forget things. It helps you lose perspective. When you no longer have hardship, you can't make comparisons. How can you be happy, when you've never had the chance to be sad?"\par
\par
     I hunkered down beside her with a grunt and rubbed my shoulder. "That's nonsense."\par
\par
     She moved behind me and her small, warm hands smoothed over my back. "Why?"\par
\par
     I shivered as she pressed her thumbs against the knots beneath my shoulder-blades. "I don't know," I said. "Because\'85 well, because you're saying that it's useless to try and eradicate suffering, because there would be no happiness. That doesn't make sense."\par
\par
     "But happiness is stagnation," Dlane said.\par
\par
     I shook my head. "I can't see it."\par
\par
     "Well\'85 growth is a kind of change, isn't it?"\par
\par
     "Yes," I said, sensing the trap.\par
\par
     "But growth is painful, isn't it?"\par
\par
     "Sometimes," I said. "Only sometimes. Sometimes growth is joyous."\par
\par
     Her hands gently fanned over my double-edged spine. "I don't know," she said, resting her face against my shoulder. The moist warmth of her breath tickled the hairs at the base of my neck. "I don't think you can really grow without pain, or at least effort. Joy is a different thing. Fecund anadi ripe with child are happy, are content. They are not joyful."\par
\par
     "Aren't they?" I asked. "Children are a wondrous thing, ke anadi. Haven't you ever stopped to think about the process? From two people spring a third, not just half of each but a whole and different person, a unique person. Doesn't that strike you as somehow magical?"\par
\par
     "A parasite, leeching the intelligence of its mother for itself? I find that sordid, Thenet."\par
\par
     "Ke anadi!" I exclaimed. "Babies do not suck the minds from their mothers through their breasts or their wombs. That's\'85"\par
\par
     "Horrifying?" she suggested.\par
\par
     "A distortion," I said.\par
\par
     Dlane did not reply. She worked her way down my back, kneading the base of my spine above my tail. My eyes rolled back beneath their lids.\par
\par
     "Well, you will not have to worry about that, at least," I mumbled finally. "No one is interested in taking to House an anadi without a name or a family to negotiate with. So long as we don't return to our home, you will never be forced to bear any children."\par
\par
     "Good," she said, punctuating that with an especially strong push against my tail base. When I twitched she said, "Did I hurt you?"\par
\par
     I shook my head. "No\'85 it just felt\'85 strange."\par
\par
     "Oh." She returned her attention to my neck and shoulders. Only the scent of the fish managed to distract me from her exquisite attention.\par
\par
     "Let's eat," I said.\par
\par
     She smiled and nodded.\par
\par
     Later that night I sat with my back to the praying tree, counting the stars through the blade-thin leaves and listening to the wind's rustle and the stream playing over pebbles. The fire had died to rich red embers, buried in black soot, that gave off a rich perfume like the incense the keepers burn in the temples of the Trinity.\par
\par
     Dlane padded back to me and sat beside me, so that I could sense her warmth. After a while, she said, "Thenet?"\par
\par
     I glanced at her.\par
\par
     "May I sleep against you?"\par
\par
     Truth be known I'd missed sleeping with other Jokka. We tend to sleep in groups; the neuters in the biggest group beds and the fragile anadi and emodo in smaller ones. I paused, then nodded.\par
\par
     She slid an arm over my chest, tracing the edge of my collar bone, then wiggled closer. The swell of her belly pressed against my hipbone and she rested her torso against my side. She was softer than the eperu I'd cuddled with. It felt strange to be sleep-curled with someone who wasn't eperu. Her breasts were heavy against my ribs.\par
\par
     The cold metal of her navel ring slowly warmed against my hip until I ceased to notice it. I slipped an arm around her shoulders and held her close. It was not all too different from the times we'd sheltered in Neked Pamari.\par
\par
     Her finger roused me from my reverie, tickle-tracing the spirals across my lower belly. I jumped. "Ke anadi!"\par
\par
     She laughed. "I'm sorry! I didn't mean to startle you. You just have such unusual patterns across your stomach. Here is a flower-opening\'85 this is a leaf-from-stalk\'85 this is another leaf-from-stalk but it's much larger." She walked the line of the spirals beneath my slit navel. "And finally an unopened seed! Like an ornate belt slung around your hips."\par
\par
     "Oh, ke anadi!" I chuckled. "You make it sound so beautiful when the truth is that the markings are asymmetrical, of a horrible coloring, and far too crowded."\par
\par
     She did not answer for so long I feared I had offended her. "Ke anadi?"\par
\par
     "Oh, Thenet," she said. Her hand skated across my chest and settled, fingers dipping into the trough between collarbones. "Don't you have eyes of your own? Must you see everything through everyone else's?"\par
\par
     "I don't understand," I said.\par
\par
     "No\'85 you don't, do you." Dlane's chest rose and fell in another, longer sigh\'85 more resignation than sadness, I judged. Her skin in the twilight almost glowed with its richness. How could she think me beautiful, when all I did was reflect the wan light of the stars? And badly at that!\par
\par
     She slept against me then, a dead weight against my side. I was accustomed to the restless motions of dozing eperu; we rearranged ourselves constantly, tangling our limbs and tails and manes more and more tightly as our few hours of dreaming progressed. To have someone simply slumped against me, unconscious, was frightening.\par
\par
     I distracted myself by studying the world. At night you could see the wind: it bent the grasses, changing their colors from fulsome gray to a silvery hue touched with lilac. You could smell it as well, bringing stories of the places it had sojourned in the scent of the forest's dying leaves, or the spicy herbs of the broad, lonely plains. It was more comfortable to doze here. Very little could sneak up on us from such short scrub.\par
\par
     Hesitant, I stroked Dlane's golden hair from her brow, watched drops of starlight glow on her lashes. And I dreamed my lucid dreams, gentler ones of incense and clean skin and of combing my anadi's hair until it spilled between my fingers like the perfumed water of the inner sanctums of the Houses we'd forsaken.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     When the motley collection of covered wagons appeared in the haze of the green dawn, I thought I was still dozing. A few hasty blinks assured me I was most certainly not. Heedless of Dlane's comfort, I slid from beneath her and rolled onto my feet to scramble for my dull spear.\par
\par
     In the warm embrace of the summer dawn, I stood before my sleeping anadi, legs spread, arms open and spear held off the ground. The long-cloth dipped and touched my ankles occasionally in the breeze as I waited. Common sense suggested that no one would send a train of six wagons to recover two Jokka, but any stranger was a threat right now. We were too few and too weak.\par
\par
     The squeak of wooden wheels and the whicker of the teams of slender rikka grew in volume as the peculiar procession approached. They were the strangest wagons I'd ever seen, with floors as tall as my waist and long, thin poles springing from the four corners. A rectangular frame hung on these poles, supporting pots, pans and bags off the outside edges and an amazing set of triangular sails of pale cloth. These sails could be set at any number of angles; currently each wagon had its sails pointed in some fashion toward the east, though each had its own particular way of arranging them to allow for the best shade.\par
\par
     Accompanying this incredible train were several handful of visible Jokka, all eperu from what I could see. A pang of hunger twisted my heart, for they all looked so at ease, so confident, so healthy: hips dressed in embroidered long-cloths of misleading austerity, with spears and hair embellished both with decorations of glass and metal, tiny chiming sounds to match the bells woven into the harnesses of the rikka.\par
\par
     The wagon train made an impressive racket: the bashing of the pots and pans hung from the wagon frames mingled with the sound of laughter and chatter, all in the familiar ranges of high tenor to high alto\'85 ah, my comrades! I must look a poor and ragged thing to them.\par
\par
     I thought they would travel straight past me, half-hoping that they wouldn't. The stragglers at the end of the train rolled to a halt beside the water; of course, the stream was the first source of water in the open after the forest.\par
\par
     The driver on the first wagon pulled its reins to halt the team of rikka. "Hail the walker!"\par
\par
     I cleared my throat and called back, "World hold you, traveler."\par
\par
     The eperu climbed off the tall floor of the wagon and dropped lightly to the ground, its silvery-pale tail swinging. It was surprisingly lovely, a darker dun color across its skin with startling, thin spirals in black to match its lashes and its dark eyes. Strands of silvery-pale hair fell before its eyes and its mane, braided in purple and clear glass beads, and draped down a solid, well-formed chest. Its flared hips were sheathed in a tasteful long-cloth, tan and gray and beige with accents in black, purple, lilac.\par
\par
     The eperu reached forward, rested fingers on my shoulders. "Cousin!" it said, frowning. "You look terrible! What have you been undergoing to be so in dismay?"\par
\par
     I opened my mouth without knowing really what I would say\'85 which is when the dun eperu looked over my shoulder. Its eyes widened appreciably. "Trinity\'85 that's not an eperu, is it?"\par
\par
     What could I say? "No, ke eperu."\par
\par
     "Wearing clothing\'85 do not say it\'85 she is one of the unfortunates who Turned."\par
\par
     My heart skipped once. Would that be a plausible explanation for these strange wayfarers? I took the chance. "Indeed. It was an ally in another House and when the Turning took it, its House was so impressed with its beauty that she was made the ka\'f1a."\par
\par
     "What a horror story!" the eperu exclaimed. "To go from honored hunter and guardian to most valuable babe-maker? No wonder she fled."\par
\par
     I nodded. "I did not want her to go alone\'85 she does not take well to the new restrictions of the more fragile anadi constitution. She does not even like to be reminded of the entire situation."\par
\par
     "I imagine," the eperu said. It pulled back and smiled at me. "I am Ilushet, master of this trading caravan. We are traveling north, if you would journey with us a while."\par
\par
     "I am Thenet," I said, "And she is Dlane. I\'85 I am honored, ke Ilushet!"\par
\par
     Ilushet shook its head. "Enough of that. I am a cousin, and I understand your plight and what you gave up to flee." It lightly touched the heavy golden hoop hanging from my left ear, the one that signified that I had been recognized as a major source of House Mated's livelihood. "If you would travel with us, we will have you, and we will call you both cousins."\par
\par
     I bowed my head. "Truth be known, cousin, I'd join you just for the chance to use a comb again."\par
\par
     Ilushet laughed and squeezed my shoulder. "Good and good! You'll come with us, and you'll have the use of as many combs as you desire! Go wake up your companion and I will introduce you to my people."\par
\par
     While the caravan-master turned to tend its beasts, I darted to Dlane's side and touched her cheek, her throat. She roused at my finger-whisper, dreams filming her golden eyes.\par
\par
     "Ke anadi," I whispered.\par
\par
     "Mmm," she replied, sleepily wrapping her arms around my neck. I let her pull me down so that my forehead rested against hers. The sweetish scent of her slumber was strong on her breath. "Thenet\'85."\par
\par
     "I have found us something to do," I said with the faintest amusement in my voice.\par
\par
     That pricked a few of the dreams apart and she struggled to sit up. "What?"\par
\par
     "Look," I said, and she did, eyes widening.\par
\par
     "What\'85?"\par
\par
     "A caravan, heading north. They are traders, if I take their mien right. They've invited us to travel with them."\par
\par
     "Do they\'85 I'm dressed as eperu\'85."\par
\par
     My voice dropped even further. "They think you changed. You still look young enough for the second Turning. I told them you were a comrade from another House, and they assume you fled because you could not handle being female."\par
\par
     A tiny giggle flew from her mouth. "They are not far wrong, at least."\par
\par
     "Shall we?" I asked. I had agreed to Ilushet's offer already, but without her approval it wouldn't matter.\par
\par
     "Oh yes, Thenet!" Dlane said, hugging me. "Anything but sitting here, losing the days like a beach to the tide."\par
\par
     I wondered if she had somehow seen a beach, then dismissed the thought and said, "Come and meet them, then!" I pulled her up and toward the wagon in the center where the caravan-master was drawing aside a curtain of dry reeds. "Ilushet\'85."\par
\par
     The eperu turned, its hand being used to steady an anadi.\par
\par
     She was not beautiful, the anadi; her spirals were as asymmetric as mine, if more evenly distributed. Her sooty-dark skin had not the many-layered depths of Dlane's, nor was she especially tall or graceful. But her rust-brown hair fell in luxuriously large curls over her rounded shoulders; her dark red eyes held the mysteries of wine and the cunning of poets; and her voluptuous body begged for children, for fruitful and dangerous pleasures. She exuded an earthiness, a fecundity, that simply stole my breath.\par
\par
     Said Ilushet, "This is Magun Shethu-emodo. We have been tasked to escort her to Het Oihap, where she shall enter into House Farah and hopefully a fruitful trade alliance; one that will grant me and mine a joyful and profitable livelihood transporting the textiles and goods Shethu and Farah will exchange."\par
\par
     I looked for and found the gold ring pierced through the long slit of her navel, threaded through with a chain of clear glass beads and smaller brass droplets, cleverly draped to accentuate her broad, inviting hips. Had this hungry female ever truly been male? I couldn't imagine her before her Change!\par
\par
     "I did not know that marriages were made between Houses of different settlements," Dlane said. I could sense her trembling beside me and was not surprised; no one could stand within a few feet of this anadi and not be drawn.\par
\par
     "You\'85.," said Magun huskily, reaching out and touching Dlane's chin. "You could have the choice of any settlement on the World's Back."\par
\par
     Dlane tilted her head. "And if none of them were good enough?"\par
\par
     Magun laughed, a rising tone of utter delight. Even so her voice never touched Dlane's in range. Her voice had not lifted much from the young emodo's she must have had.\par
\par
     Clearing its throat, Ilushet said, "I am glad to see the two of you get along, for no one travels with us who does not have a duty. Dlane, cousin, I would much appreciate your tending to Magun."\par
\par
     "I would be honored," Dlane said as Magun ran fingers over the lapel of her blouse.\par
\par
     "You wear clothing," Magun said.\par
\par
     "I enjoy wearing clothing," Dlane replied.\par
\par
     "Does it not make you over-hot?"\par
\par
     "A little discomfort is good. It reminds you to enjoy a little pleasure when it comes along."\par
\par
     Again the older female laughed. "Come with me, strange anadi. We will walk while it is still cool."\par
\par
     The two drifted toward the bank of the stream, leaving me to stand beside a bemused Ilushet.\par
\par
     "That went well," said the caravan-master.\par
\par
     I chuckled. "Indeed."\par
\par
     "So, now, cousin\'85 what shall you do? Would you prefer guard, drive or scout duty?"\par
\par
     My soul longed to range ahead, free to exercise my body to its fullest, to crouch and leap through the grasses, staring ahead with my keen vision. But to be so far afield from Dlane - "I will take guard duty. I don't know how to drive the rikka."\par
\par
     "We'll change that," Ilushet said. "But for now, guard duty it is. The two eperu you and your companion will free I will assign to scouting. They have been yearning for it anyway."\par
\par
     "Are trains like this frequent?" I asked finally, tearing my gaze from the two anadi with difficulty. "I had heard the occasional story that there were Jokka that lived free of towns, wandering the plains, but I'd thought them\'85 well, mythical."\par
\par
     Ilushet thumped its hard stomach with a fist. "I feel quite real, I assure you, cousin!" It chuckled. "But no, we are not 'free-living'. We are traders. We allow settlements to exchange goods they wouldn\rquote t otherwise have access to. I am sure even your town saw a train or two a season. Or was your town so large it didn't need them?"\par
\par
     I looked away.\par
\par
     "I'm sorry. I understand you might not want to talk of the place." Ilushet gripped my shoulder with a hand. "You will bide with us for a while, and perhaps even enjoy it! Now come. I will give you that comb you were so desperate for, and something to replace that tattered excuse for a long-cloth you're wearing while we repair it."\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     Thus we began our wandering. Ilushet was far more clever than I'd initially believed, for Dlane's task kept her in the wagon with Magun where the sun would not tax her and she could sleep as much as she needed: an elegant way to fulfill my request that no one discuss her 'original' sex, while also accommodating her frailties.\par
\par
     I sat on the edge of the third wagon, its fragrant curtain of dried reeds clacking together as the platform swayed. It neared evening; about the time Ilushet usually stopped the train for the day. Though only a week had passed since we\rquote d joined them, I\rquote d discovered a routine and a pleasure to it: to be again with other people, particularly other eperu, was soothing.\par
\par
     "It's so exciting," Dlane said, slipping through the curtains to join me on the edge. Above us the clay pots and leather pouches clicked together, their shadows a stark purple in the approaching sundown. "To be moving all the time."\par
\par
     "Moving\'85 yes," I chuckled softly and looked at her. The combs were kind to her golden hair, and the new, gauzy white blouse she'd been given was far nicer than anything I'd ever had. The bleached suede trews were shorter than the fashion I was accustomed to, laced with dove gray to match the decorative long-cloth strung over her belly to hide her own navel ring. "How is Magun?"\par
\par
     "Well enough. She'll wake in half an hour or so, when the night is more fully advanced." Dlane grinned. "I don't know how she can sleep! It's so wondrous."\par
\par
     "Today's plains are no different than yesterday's, and yesterdays look not much different from last week's, when we started this," I said, amused at her enthusiasm.\par
\par
     "Oh, Thenet!" Dlane shook her head. "You need to learn to see."\par
\par
     "I see just fine," I said. I resettled my spear in the grasp of my feet, its haft pressed between my knees, and returned to the carving I was attempting to decorate the head: a piece of bone from the thigh of the nibbler I'd caught for the caravan's soup bowl the first day. "I'm just happy that every day carries us farther away from discovery."\par
\par
     Dlane shivered. "That too," she said softly. "Do you think he could find us?"\par
\par
     "I don't know," I said, because I didn't, and "I don't think so," because she needed to hear it. "Is this the life you want, ke anadi?"\par
\par
     "Traveling? Trading?" She looked puzzled. "I don't know the first thing about finances."\par
\par
     A new voice interrupted us. "Certainly you do. The first lesson in finance is that you are worth more than a House makes in the life of a Jokkad." Magun parted the curtain with a long-handled brush and smiled at us both. "You know that already. You just don't think about it much."\par
\par
     Dlane shivered. "I am not a token," she murmured.\par
\par
     "No? You should be proud," Magun said. "Your friend beside you is not worth so much, though it bears the golden ring in its ear. We anadi are highly prized. It is far better than being emodo."\par
\par
     "But bearing children\'85."\par
\par
     "Oh, a necessary evil. There are compensations." Magun smiled, lids dropping to shade sunset-red eyes. "The pleasure is worth the pain."\par
\par
     "Pleasure is a moment," Dlane said softly. "Pleasure is nothing. I want fulfillment."\par
\par
     "What better way to gain it than to be anadi? If you are cunning enough, you can wallow on cool blankets with a whiteblush ice while all around you the people you command carry out your bidding and your House prospers. Never for you the breeding harnesses after your first child\'85 if you watch your step."\par
\par
     \ldblquote You are speaking of running a House,\rdblquote  Dlane said, ears swiveling sideways.\par
\par
     \ldblquote A ka\'f1a-befidzu might as well do so,\rdblquote  Magun said. \ldblquote Very certainly she controls the anadi of the House. Who controls the children, controls the House.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I glanced at Magun, sprawled across the green and taupe pillows. With a lush hip cocked in the air she presented an air of utter, sensual indifference. But her exposed foot, each toe still articulated like an emodo's and bearing the exquisitely mobile thumb-claw of a male, was slowly curling into a fist and opening again. As if it could feel the throats of prostrate Jokka. I clenched my teeth and shaved another splintery piece off the bone.\par
\par
     Dlane twisted to look at Magun. There was a smile in her voice as she answered, "I want a simpler life."\par
\par
     "The simplest life is in the harness, little anadi. A few turns in it and I guarantee you won't ever worry again."\par
\par
     Dlane's ears flattened to her skull and the wagon rocked through eight more turns of the wheels: each one had a seam that produced a slight bump. I'd long since learned to let my hips move with the wagon and my carving was completely unaffected by the motion. Affected by my lack of skill, definitely, but not by the wagon's motion.\par
\par
     On that eighth bump, though, Dlane spoke. "Simple and stupid are not the same thing."\par
\par
      Magun yawned. "Yes, they are. You turn away from power. Why? You would wield it well. You seem smart enough."\par
\par
     "Why do intelligent people have to want power?" Dlane asked. "What good is power over other people? Power over yourself is more interesting."\par
\par
     I glanced at Dlane, confused; nor was I alone in that, for Magun rolled onto her belly and leveled a full stare on my anadi, her brows eloquent in conveying her skepticism.\par
\par
     "It's true," Dlane said only.\par
\par
     The descending twilight hid my face from her. Soon the eperu would light the lamps. I listened carefully, though, ears tilted toward them.\par
\par
     "Power over oneself\'85 what a useless desire! You are born with it," Magun said.\par
\par
     "Hardly!" Dlane said, her voice stiffening with something between disgust and\'85 anger? Contempt? "To master yourself you must know yourself. Too many fail to turn their eyes on themselves and honestly assess what they see. If they can see at all."\par
\par
     I blushed, unnoticed.\par
\par
     "Does it matter why you want something? When you want it? Getting it is the thing." Magun sounded less threatened and more intrigued. What a peculiar one she was!\par
\par
     Dlane sighed. "Oh. It is so much more than just to want a thing."\par
\par
     "There are things that everyone wants, things it is natural to want." That was my voice. I was speaking. What was I thinking? This was a conversation for anadi, for cold pools sprinkled with the essence of waterblooms, for the languor of the heated evenings, not for the eperu so rooted in concrete realities. So used to ground beneath their feet and spears in their hands\'85. "To be useful. To be healthy. To be safe. To be loved."\par
\par
     Magun snorted. "Your eperu is na\'efve, anadi. It believes in love."\par
\par
     "My eperu is a person in its own right, Magun," Dlane replied softly. "You will address it instead of treating it like an inanimate object."\par
\par
     "Love is a fantasy," Magun said, to both of us but looking at me. "And even if it were real, it would be a nuisance."\par
\par
     "I believe there is love," Dlane said.\par
\par
     Magun laughed. "You probably believe in love between sexes, don\rquote t you. Please, don't be ridiculous."\par
\par
     "I wouldn't go so far," Dlane said. "But love between anadi and anadi? Emodo and emodo? Eperu and eperu? I believe in that."\par
\par
     "Eperu do not love other eperu," I said.\par
\par
     There was a silence broken only by the thump of the wheels turning and the click-clack of the clay pots, the reed curtain. Both females were watching me so intently that my fingers trembled. The knife slipped and I nicked my thumb: a tiny hole, but I popped it in my mouth and pressed my tongue to it. The blood was sweet.\par
\par
     When the taste faded, they were still regarding me. My ears twitched against my head. "Well, they don't," I said defensively. "We may be companions to one another. Comrades. Friends accustomed to depending on one another, or cousins or teachers, students or elders. We may feel affection. Concern. But love? What is that? That is a breeder-thing."\par
\par
     "It has nothing to do with breeding," Dlane said. "In fact, it is everything that opposes breeding. Opposes stupidity and violence and casual acquaintance, opposes the use of others, the parasitic and sexual use of others, the trading and the buying and the selling of people into Houses and harnesses and parenthood. Love is a choice. All the rest of it," she waved a hand, "all the rest of it is chains."\par
\par
     There was little that could be said to that.\par
\par
     The cessation of the wagon's sway was startling. I put the bone carving on the platform's edge and hopped down, turning to help Dlane after me. I could feel her heart hammering through her clothes. After I let her go I could feel it fluttering on my hands still, like the memory of a wound.\par
\par
     Into the purple twilight I padded in search of Ilushet. The caravan-master was lighting the lantern on its wagon, hanging it off one of the protruding frame poles.\par
\par
     "Good even, cousin!" it said.\par
\par
     I smiled, though the melancholy and faint discomfort of the conversation hadn't left me yet. "Yes, Ilushet."\par
\par
     "Do you smell it on the wind?"\par
\par
     I lifted my head and snuffed, but the scent was too uncluttered. The forest, the hills - those places I knew. "I don't," I said. "What's coming?"\par
\par
     "Rain," Ilushet said. "Another day or so, perhaps, and we will have a storm. You can smell the water a week away. Comes stealthy and thin at first, but when it's about to pour on you it'll be as subtle as a blow."\par
\par
     "Rain!" I said. "I haven't felt rain on my body in\'85 well many lights. Since I was a child."\par
\par
     "Then you're past due for the experience again," Ilushet said, laughing. "Rain is infrequent. It should be enjoyed."\par
\par
     I took the lantern it handed me, the cool glass sucking the heat from my palms and with it the ghostly echo of Dlane\rquote s racing heart.\par
\par
     "Go, light the camp," Ilushet said gently. "The work will take that glazed look from your eyes."\par
\par
     "I\'85."\par
\par
     "Oh, I saw you talking with the anadi, cousin. They are not like us. Hear their talk with one ear and you'll do better."\par
\par
     "If you don't listen, how can you understand?" I asked.\par
\par
     Ilushet shrugged, a fluid motion that rippled across its lovely body in the deep amber glow of the lantern at its shoulder. "There are some things that cannot be understood, cousin. One should wonder why one even tries. We are as we are, and they are as they are, and we are all as the gods made us. What more is there to know?"\par
\par
     I did not reply. The lantern I left above the developing campsite, shedding light on the eperu who nodded to me as it dug the smoke pit. I sought Dlane and found her seated beside the wheel of the wagon, resting her thin shoulder against it. I crouched beside her. "Ke anadi?"\par
\par
     "I am fine," she said, brushing my knee with warm fingers. "Just a little tired. I may sleep until supper."\par
\par
     "Do that," I said, beginning to unfold.\par
\par
     "Thenet\'85."\par
\par
     I paused.\par
\par
     "Do not listen to any of them. Don't even listen to me, except for this: your mind is your own. Your eyes, too."\par
\par
     "I know," I said, puzzled.\par
\par
     "Do you?"\par
\par
     My frustration spilled over. My spear scraped across the top of the platform as I grabbed it and my little piece of bone. Eschewing the company and the light of the train, I stalked into the falling violet night. I sat in the middle of the sharp-scented grasses, took my borrowed knife and resumed carving with a little more violence than was strictly useful.\par
\par
     It was too much, all of a sudden: to have Dlane's crazed theories contrasted against Magun's so deviously anadi way of thinking, a way of thinking that was not discouraged in our society if not quite condoned\'85 on top of this strange way of living, itinerant and footfree, with only the layered sails between your body and the sky\'85 there were obviously many more ways to live than I'd been taught, and when confronted with all these glorious, bewildering choices I only wanted to go home.\par
\par
     My teeth itched, but I refused to cry. With knotted brow I stabbed at my bone ornament and straight through it, divesting it of half its length and almost divesting myself of a finger. I threw knife and bone away and pressed my hands to my mouth. I would not weep. I refused to weep even out of sight of the others; even if they didn't smell it on me, the sky would know, and I would know.\par
\par
     How utterly alienated I had become from normal Jokka\'85 even Jokka from other towns and customs. How apart I was, exiled from House and alive, guardian to an anadi with the brain of a goddess. Perhaps I was the walking dead, as Roika had said. Perhaps that was why as soothing as the company of the other eperu were, I couldn't find a way to fit myself into their delicate community. Or perhaps it was a strong community. Perhaps it was only I who made them delicate. Dlane's strangeness had made me a spear with a hard, sharp tooth. She wielded me, even when she was not beside me.\par
\par
     But it was the life I'd been given, and better than none at all. Wasn't it?\par
\par
     I hunted through the dark for my discarded knife and carving, spreading my fingers through the grass and searching with vision still clear in starlight. Before long I was seated again in the darkness, the summer\rquote s hot wind smoothing my flat-black hair from my forehead as I turned the bone over and over in my hands. I tried to conceive a new form for it, one that made use of its fragmentary rim. After studying it, I decided that its ragged edge could in fact be its essential element. Plying the knife with more sensitivity showed me that bone could respond, if treated carefully. If caressed. So I caressed it with the knife, and it responded. I shaped it there in the darkness and the quiet.\par
\par
     How much time I lost I cannot even guess, but eventually I realized I could no longer proceed without finer tools. Reluctantly I straightened, the bones in my back threatening to snap in their displeasure. I rubbed the base of my spine to placate them, gazing at the spangled skies with their stars all a-glitter on impenetrable black. When had it become so late? And my carving wasn't even half-done yet. I'd mangled so much of it with my earlier attempts.\par
\par
     With a sigh, I rolled onto my feet and sought the camp again.\par
\par
     "Ilushet," I called, seeing it near the outermost wagon.\par
\par
     It stopped and looked my way as I jogged to it.\par
\par
     "Do you have tools for working bone? Smaller ones than a knife."\par
\par
     "Getting complicated, I see," Ilushet said with a chuckle. "Come then. I have a few things in the chest that might work."\par
\par
     I tagged on its heels back to the head of the line. Together we pulled back the high floor of its wagon: really just a cover for the hollow hold beneath. The other wagons carried cargo in theirs, fabrics and exotic spices or rare curios to sell. Ilushet's carried items held in common trust for the people of the train and supplies, food, water.\par
\par
     Ilushet hopped into the bay and opened one of the chests, rooting through it. "Should be something suitable here. Some of us do carve on the longer journeys."\par
\par
     "This one isn't long?"\par
\par
     "Oh, no. There's a nice cluster of towns between here and our wintering spot. It's the spring-time journey to the southern towns that takes a long time. There's a stretch of barren nothingness that's enough to drive a person mad."\par
\par
     I watched it unpack, settling each bundle carefully aside before continuing its search. On impulse, I asked, "Ilushet, do we love?"\par
\par
     The eperu turned to me, the box in its hands forgotten. "Now I know you have been speaking too long to anadi, cousin. Love! A truedark story for fanciful emodo and anadi."\par
\par
     I frowned. "But it's there."\par
\par
     "Is it?" Ilushet shrugged. "I personally find the possibility unlikely. Have you ever seen love?"\par
\par
     I opened my mouth to answer, then frowned. "Ilushet, I don't even know what it is."\par
\par
     The caravan master handed me the box. "Here." When I stared at it blankly, it said with gentle amusement, "The tools you requested."\par
\par
     "Oh! Thank you. But cousin - "\par
\par
     "No more, dear Thenet. Too many stories and the darkness will strike you blind."\par
\par
     "Jokka do not travel during truedark," I murmured.\par
\par
     "Just so," Ilushet said, replacing all the bundles it'd excavated from the trunk and closing the floor of the wagon. "And it is bordering on that time now. Go back to your place and doze, cousin. Tomorrow is another day."\par
\par
     What else could tomorrow be, I wondered?\par
\par
     I returned to my post and glanced onto the platform: Magun and Dlane were sleeping in a nest of pillows, ribcages rising and falling in that obscenely deep slumber. The eddies of Dlane's golden hair had ensnared the gleaming of the lantern, and each tress seemed to sparkle as if lit from within. I reached to touch her but did not\'85 the golden glow surrounding her barred me. They were anadi, sleeping. Eperu did not sleep with anadi.\par
\par
     I jumped lightly onto the wagon's floor and climbed on top of the driver's block at the fore. Curled there with my knees to my chest and the spear propped at my side, I let my eyes droop half-closed and welcomed the whisper-webs of dreams.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     In the following days the skies darkened so that even a novice to the plains could see the coming storm. On the sixth day, I woke to find the burgeoning clouds so thick that the warm fog they sent before them almost merged with the constructs of my dozing. Several minutes passed before I convinced myself I'd really woken and even more slipped through my fingers before I was certain the slate gray murk was actually hiding a morning well advanced past dawn.\par
\par
     Picking up my spear, I hopped off the wagon block and joined the shadowed figures near the smoke pit.\par
\par
     "It will be thicker than tears soon," Benih was saying.\par
\par
     "Another hour at least," another opined.\par
\par
     "Will we actually travel in this?" I asked as I joined them.\par
\par
     "That's up to the caravan-master," said Benih.\par
\par
     "And the caravan-master is here," Ilushet said, stepping out of the fog. The graceful fan of its forelock had dissolved into a wet straggle of hair on its brow which it occasionally scraped away. "It will not rain for a few hours at least. We will travel for several hours and then stop when visibility grows poor."\par
\par
     "Visibility?" I asked, startled. "We don't have problems seeing. Even in this. Unless it'll grow as bad as truedark\'85?"\par
\par
     "No, no," Ilushet said with a quiet laugh. "But the rikka hate it. They will not walk when they cannot see well."\par
\par
     "Oh," I said, glancing at the tethered beasts with their slender limbs.\par
\par
     "So? What are you standing here for?" Ilushet said. It waved. "We go!"\par
\par
     "We go!" the other eperu echoed, with a few laughs scattered off the heavy fog. They dispersed to their driving blocks and guard and scout positions. I did likewise, checking on the two anadi: sleeping soundly, not even seeming to notice the water they drew in through their noses. The humidity lent a strange sheen to Dlane's cheek and a limpness to her hair that disturbed me, though I couldn't decide why.\par
\par
     I returned to my place alongside the wagon and some distance from it, spear loose in my hand. Not long after the wagons shuddered into motion, and curls of wet fog broke as the rikka forged through them. The visibility was no worse than at false dawn, or dusk; more interesting to me by far was the sensation of the wetness on my skin, my scales, dragging at my mane and tail. I'd not been out in this kind of rain since childhood, though as an adult I'd often hunted through the frequent mistings that spring delivers as a paltry offering to the forest. Storms that brought such thick runners were rare in the extreme, an occasion to hide inside. I suppressed a laugh when I observed that the fog dulled the other eperu's nicer hides to the same flat ugliness as my own. And so we are all equal beneath the thunder.\par
\par
     And what thunder! Not the indistinct sound I was accustomed to, muffled by a het\rquote s heavy roofs. It had its own timbre, and changed moods: the first few were like soft growls extended from the long throat of the cloud bank, barely audible. My ears flicked nervously to catch these warnings, as it would for an animal I'd hunted to a stand-off.\par
\par
     But the animal's belly was as broad as the sky.\par
\par
     The wheels of the caravan provided a thin, creaking counterpoint to the rumbling storm. I strode alongside. At some point the slap of my braided tail against my calves became more of a squelch, and a few drops of warm water rolled down my chest and back. This at least was familiar: our mistings in the spring had the same effect. The water didn't fall out of the air as much as bead onto everything beneath it.\par
\par
     It was a quiet morning. Usually I managed to catch a glimpse of at least a few of the plains-dwellers: tiny succulent nibblers fleeing the groaning behemoths of our wagons, or the less frequent drekdoks bounding through the grasses on fragile legs. Birds I saw more often: feathers in muted colors for the deepening summer spread against the brilliant sky. The few that flew overhead today looked eye-wateringly bright against the muddy clouds.\par
\par
     Several hours passed as the caravan tunneled through the fog. At approximately noon we stopped; approximately, for none of us could pierce the dark clouds to see the sun. Rivulets of water raced down my spine and my hair clung stickily to my neck and back and legs. I felt as if I was breathing water. Pushing my way to the front of the line I called, "Is it ever going to fall?"\par
\par
     "Soon enough," Ilushet said. "Help me with the rikka."\par
\par
     We unbuckled the harnesses off the skittish beasts and I led them to the makeshift shelter the other eperu were erecting. When I'd handed the last of them to the eperu beneath the canopy of the hastily built tent, I said, "What of us?"\par
\par
     "A little rain won't hurt us," the caravan-master said.\par
\par
     Wouldn't hurt us, certainly! Nevertheless I padded back through the mist to my wagon, squinting past sodden eyelashes. Climbing onto the platform, I glanced at the two females, still sleeping. I wondered if they were dreaming of hot pools.\par
\par
     The sails that formed the roof of the wagon had grown far heavier in the intervening hours. I grunted as I dragged them down and tried to arrange them so that they overlapped. They had not been designed to lie flat but I managed a facsimile and climbed onto the driver's block to drape a blanket over the exposed areas.\par
\par
     No sooner had I finished than the rain dropped. It did not simply fall: a solid wall of it came washing our way, so thick I could barely see past it. In amazement I stood frozen on the edge of the wagon's platform, and so took the brunt of the first fall on my chest and face.\par
\par
     I spluttered and dashed back into the rude shelter, pulling the paltry shield of the reed curtains in place.\par
\par
     There in that moist space I weathered my first thunderstorm outside the safety of town. The thunder's voice had a chilling intimacy as it snarled and bit, and the blinding flashes that accompanied each word from the sky stole my breath.\par
\par
     Even beneath the blankets and sails and hemmed by the reed curtains, there was water. Thin drops fell from over-saturated sails to plink almost inaudibly over the low, hard roar of the downpour. The humidity beaded on my skin and rolled off it onto the floor. I shivered and glanced at the anadi, curled up together in their nest of pillows and damp blankets.\par
\par
     Dlane roused herself with a yawn, tongue curling. Her brows furrowed as she pushed her chest from the clump of bedding. "Thenet?" she said.\par
\par
     "Rain, ke anadi."\par
\par
     Dlane crawled out of the blankets and leaned against me. "This isn't rain," she said. "This is a waterfall."\par
\par
     I chuckled. "Isn't it\'85."\par
\par
     "Magnificent?" she finished when I couldn't find the words.\par
\par
     "Yes," I said.\par
\par
     We spoke no more and watched instead. The rain filled in the silence for us. When some fifteen minutes had passed, Dlane said in wonder, "Will it ever stop?"\par
\par
     "It's not thundering as much," I said. "Soon, perhaps."\par
\par
     Ten minutes later, she shifted against me.\par
\par
     "Well, maybe in a little while."\par
\par
     She giggled when I sighed in exasperation a full half hour after the rain had started. It had lost some of its passion but now had a steady, dreary quality. "I'm tired of being wet!"\par
\par
     "Wet isn't so bad," Dlane said, licking my shoulder. The contrast of her hot tongue and the warm dampness made me shiver.\par
\par
     "Wet is unnatural," I said. "For eperu, at least."\par
\par
     "You hunt in the mistings."\par
\par
     "But this is no misting!"\par
\par
     Dlane lifted her head to gaze solemnly out into the undulating sheet of silver water. "No," she said. "This is as to the misting as the sea is to my mouth."\par
\par
     I frowned at her. That ancient word - the sea - as if she\rquote d seen one. But no Jokka had seen the sea since our ancestors had left the Birthwell. The word, the metaphors remained with us, without the reality to ground them.\par
\par
     In the hazy gray of the falling rain I could see the silhouettes of the other eperu. Most of them were huddled on the ground, unmoving. Others were lost against the rikka tent. One or two were still on their feet, crossing slowly from wagon to wagon.\par
\par
     "How can they bear it?" I wondered aloud.\par
\par
     "Bear what?" Dlane asked.\par
\par
     "Walking through that!"\par
\par
     Dlane laughed. "Maybe it's fun!" She pushed open the reed curtain and, before I could object or even grab her, tumbled out. I lunged after her and almost struck her head with my chin as she laughed in my face. "Oh, Thenet, it is fun! Come!" She grabbed me by shoulder and mane and pulled me after her.\par
\par
     Being partially wet was bad. Being drenched was to being damp as the sea was to her mouth. Brightness save! The mud sucked my knees and feet in as the rain beat me into the ground. It was nothing like mistings; each drop was a spear with a point as discrete as a knife's.\par
\par
     "Oh, it's lovely!" Dlane exclaimed, rolling onto her back. I wondered if there was anything she didn't find novel and intriguing. I crawled through the mud and positioned myself above her, belly hanging over her face.\par
\par
     "Thenet?" she asked, twisting to glance at my chin from between my arms.\par
\par
     "Keeping you dry," I said with a sigh. "This can't be healthy."\par
\par
     Great glistening lashes fluttered twice, and then she started laughing. "Oh, Thenet! It's just rain! All the animals get wet."\par
\par
     "You," I said pointedly, "are not an animal."\par
\par
     "Aren't I?" she asked. "Aren't we all?"\par
\par
     I ignored her and remained there, wondering when she'd get tired of the rain and crawl back into the wagon where she could stay dry. When her finger lit soft as a breath on my navel slit, I jumped.\par
\par
     "Ke anadi!"\par
\par
     "It's strange to see one without a ring," she murmured, barely audible over the drumming of the rain. "So clean. The fold is\'85 uninterrupted."\par
\par
     She must have taken ill\'85 she was babbling. And she was tickling me. "Get back in the wagon, ke anadi."\par
\par
     "But it's nice out," she said.\par
\par
     "In!"\par
\par
     She laughed softly and hugged my waist. "Very well, my protector. I go!" Scrambling from beneath me, she climbed back onto the platform, and with a soft growl of effort pulled herself inside. The last of her tail slithered between the reed curtains, still muddy. I'd have to brush it clean later. Blessed combs.\par
\par
     Which left me in the rain, soaked to the scalp. Squinting, I looked up at the clouds and realized that the world had a different, cleaner smell and a strange sound\'85 and even a taste, of airborne drops flavored with pollen and heat.\par
\par
     So maybe it wasn't so unpleasant. I took a few experimental steps, with the soil sinking beneath my feet. The sleeting water seemed to connect me to the earth, and we were all one thing: sky to back to foot to ground. Curious, I kept walking\'85 and then jogged\'85 and found myself loping toward the plain. Every part of my body tingled, reminded of itself by the drops splattering against it. To run for the sake of running, and not because of pursuit - what a glorious thing! I skidded to a halt and my momentum pulled me to my knees, and I was laughing.\par
\par
     As suddenly as it came, the fall stopped. My head lifted to the sky to find the sun I could just sense behind the clouds, and encountered instead a vast nothingness.\par
\par
     The emptiness of the plains and the immensity of the sky struck me so violently I flinched. I had hunted the forests and the mean hills, and had occasionally climbed out of the shallow bowl of our town's skirts to see the broad back of the World\'85 but never pierced it so, days into its oppressive flatness. I had never really understood the size of the place, never held it in my mind and heart.\par
\par
     Here, with the curtain of silver water abruptly lifted, the sight was as a new one to me. It shocked me to vomiting in the mud. Trembling and choking, I blinked water from my eyes to grope hungrily for the view of the wagons - there! To ground me, take away the dizziness, the sudden sense of my own utter inadequacy. My very smallness.\par
\par
     I stumbled back to the wagons. Quivering ears managed to pick up enough conversation for me to know that we wouldn't be moving further until the mud stiffened, probably tomorrow morning. My limbs shook like an old one's as I clambered back into the damp confines of the wagon and dumped myself onto the platform. I curled into a ball and tried to stop shivering, to stop seeing the endless plains on the backs of my eyelids.\par
\par
     "Thenet?" Dlane's soft whisper in my ear sent a shudder down my spine.\par
\par
     I tried to find my voice but couldn't. The words I fumbled for fled my mind completely when the first press of her breast and stomach registered along my spine. A cold circle burned against the base of my tail before her navel ring warmed to my flesh.\par
\par
     She tucked an arm over my ribs and chest and pressed her thighs against mine.\par
\par
     "K-ke an-n-nadi," I said. My teeth were chattering. I tried to make them stop. "An-n-nadi d-don't sleep with ep-peru."\par
\par
     "This one does," she said simply, and that was the end of that.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     Neither of us slept: of that I was certain. I came close, but my doze-dreams were horrifying recreations of my adventure in the rain and kept catapulting me from rest. Why she didn't sleep I don't know, but her breathing never deepened, a fact of which I was kept intimately aware by the swell and press of her breast against my back. To curl around her and shelter her while we were fleeing civilization\'85 that seemed as appropriate as running in the first place. But to do this while we were in the company of others, while we were surrounded by other Jokka - it reminded me of what was proper. This was not proper.\par
\par
     I stared between the reed curtains, one ear swiveled toward Magun who was still deep in the grip of slumber. Watching the other eperu walk to and fro between wagons, I found some sense of equilibrium.\par
\par
     Dlane's hand gently rubbed against my chest. "Better?" she said into my ear.\par
\par
     I closed my eyes. "I think," I said hoarsely.\par
\par
     "Should I\'85 can I ask?"\par
\par
     I didn't reply for a while, and I could sense in her not patience but some other thing I could not name: as if she was content to merely lie with me.\par
\par
     So after heartbeats too numerous to count, I said, "Ke anadi\'85 what if we are\'85 insignificant?"\par
\par
     Her breathing didn't change, nor even her voice. There was no challenge in her words, no triumph, nothing but the same gentle connection. As if she were giving tongue to a conversation we'd already had in our minds. "Strange words from someone cradled in the Brightness, World and Void's hands."\par
\par
     "What if it's true, though?"\par
\par
     "Insignificant to whom, Thenet?"\par
\par
     My eyes opened so I could frown. "Whom?"\par
\par
     "Significance\'85 it implies a comparison. What are you comparing your life to, that it doesn't measure?"\par
\par
     "I\'85 I don't know," I said, ears splaying. "I just\'85 have you seen the size of the World? And have you seen how pale our flight, our peril seems in comparison to the work-a-day lives of these eperu? I feel insubstantial. Like nothing matters because I've forgotten how things matter."\par
\par
     Dlane's soft chuckle somehow did not offend. "You are beginning to sound like me." Her hand slid down to press against my stomach, and I noticed that my fetal curl had loosened.\par
\par
     "What is your answer?" I said, twisting my head to look back at her.\par
\par
     Her smile was rueful. "I don't know, my Thenet. But I will tell you when I find out."\par
\par
     "You're supposed to have all the answers!" I said, torn between laughing and disappointment.\par
\par
     "I will. Just not today," she replied, and ran her hands up and down my stomach. I broke into giggles and writhed away from her hands, which is how Magun found us when she woke: tickling one another into heaving fits.\par
\par
     "Oh stop," Magun said, throwing a pillow at me. "You'll tax her to fainting, and then where will you be without your precious anadi?"\par
\par
     I threw the pillow back. "You're just jealous because she's more precious than you are."\par
\par
     Magun guffawed. "Oh, I don't ever have to worry about that. She's only precious if she rejoins the society I'm entering. Which I think she won't deign to do."\par
\par
     I was surprised she'd taken the comment seriously. I certainly hadn't meant it that way. At least, not completely.\par
\par
     "And you," Magun said to Dlane, "Act as if you were still indestructible. When your body begins to fail you, don't be surprised."\par
\par
     Dlane folded her arms over her chest and looked down on Magun. She was magnificent: slick and wild and haughty and I wanted to hug her for her passion. "Better to have used it up than to linger in it, pale."\par
\par
     Magun snorted, pushing her frizzy rust-brown curls over her shoulders and reaching for a comb. "Spoken like a breeder."\par
\par
     "Don't talk to her that way!"\par
\par
     The strident words rang in the stillness, accentuated by the occasional drip from the sails hanging overhead. I barely recognized them as my own, but they were.\par
\par
     Standing slowly, Magun canted her head and said, "Do you deny the order of things, eperu?"\par
\par
     The hair on the back of my neck and spine rose at the blatant lack of respect the unvarnished title wielded. "Do you, anadi?" I said, returning her favor. My hands at my sides had curled into claws. "You speak so smoothly of fighting your way to the top of a House where you won't be forced to mate and bear young. How natural is that?"\par
\par
     "Natural enough if I want it," Magun said, tossing her mane back. The humidity did not favor her the way it did Dlane. She looked hot and ill-groomed. "How else are we to cull the unwitting? The stupid? In our world only the smartest live well."\par
\par
     My nose wrinkled, lip sliding back to expose some of my sharp teeth. "No wonder there's no love among anadi," I said. "As you would have it, your lives are so brutal and angry that there's no time for friendship - only opportunity to size up enemies and be rid of them. How distastful to be anadi!"\par
\par
     "Not at all!" Magun exclaimed. "Are you really so stupid? Anadi rule the world! The eperu live to serve us. The emodo toil to buy us. Who would not want to be one of us?"\par
\par
     Dlane stepped between us, startling me. I'd forgotten she was even there. Lifting her chin she said clearly, quietly, "I wouldn't."\par
\par
     Taking my hand, she led me out.\par
\par
     "Was that wise?" I asked her in the mud and the gray. She shrugged, a listless motion of hip and shoulder. "I don't know. Was it?"\par
\par
     "You never answer me when you can reply with a question," I said, torn between exasperation and affection. I took her shoulders in my hands and gave her a little shake. "Ke anadi. What is it?"\par
\par
     "I hate being female," she whispered, looking at the ground.\par
\par
     Again the ambivalence pinned me between concern at her unhappiness and unease at her sentiments. She wore an eperu-style patterned long-cloth, hanging to her ankles, but no blouse; probably left to dry. Yet she crossed her arms to hide the swell of her breasts. I found myself drawing the damp shirt from my body and gently hanging it over her, lacing it over her throat. Her arms unfolded to slip through the sleeves, and then she hugged me, tucking her head beneath my chin.\par
\par
     "Oh, Thenet, Thenet. What if it is all meaningless?"\par
\par
     I cleared my throat and pet her back. "In relation to what, ke anadi?"\par
\par
     She laughed.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     "That's it," Ilushet said. "Het Bakka."\par
\par
     A tidy blot of black and brown on the olive back of the plains at sunset, Het Bakka could not have been more than two dozen buildings. But the surrounding fields grew in wet, dark squares and the deep amber lights hung on the poles outside were familiar and well-tended.\par
\par
     "How long will we stay?" I asked after I'd taken my fill. Somehow the contrasts were as charming as they were disturbing and I wasn't sure what answer would satisfy: that we would stay long, and assuage my curiosity, or but briefly pass through, and avoid my discomfort.\par
\par
     "Two days," Ilushet said. "Het Bakka is small\'85 one day to sell and one day to buy is usually enough." It glanced at me. "It is usual to allow a few hours of leisure for the caravan's members, to spend the shell they've earned with their labors."\par
\par
     Startled, I said, "Cousin, I had no idea I was earning shell\'85."\par
\par
     "Not much," Ilushet said with a chuckle, "But your labors are worth something to the caravan. We do not earn simply to spend again on new goods. If there is no profit, how do we prosper?"\par
\par
     "I suppose I'd never thought about it," I said.\par
\par
     Ilushet laughed and tapped my shoulder. "Go see Atha tonight. It is the purser. And don't forget to withdraw for your companion's labors."\par
\par
     "Thank you, cousin," I said bemused.\par
\par
     It shook its head, chuckling, and walked back to the lead wagon.\par
\par
     In the week that followed the soaking the plains had yet to shake off the rain; the ground was still somewhat soft, and the eye-bright greens of new plants had pricked through the surface of the soil in thousands of places. The air still retained the faintest softness, a weight that whispered of water. Concentrating on these things helped me reduce the inevitable sense of vertigo that attacked when I let my eyes drift to the horizon. I did not mention my new disability to my comrades, and even Dlane did not see my occasional trembling fits.\par
\par
     The smudge that had grown into Het Bakka had proved a welcome distraction\'85 and it was not solely of interest to me.\par
\par
     I handed off my rikka to another eperu and pulled myself onto the platform. Before I could even straighten, Dlane was on me.\par
\par
     "Is it true, is it a town, are we finally here?"\par
\par
     I laughed. "Yes, yes and yes!"\par
\par
     "Oh, Thenet! We must go. To see another place at last\'85! How exciting! How other Jokka live-"\par
\par
     "Not much differently than we did," I said, amused. "We are all Jokka."\par
\par
     "That does not make us all the same," she said, shaking her mane back and grinning. "When can we go?"\par
\par
     "When Ilushet gives us leave," I said.\par
\par
     "And pray tell me, anadi, are you going dressed?"\par
\par
     My eyes narrowed as I stared over Dlane's shoulder at Magun, rising languorously from her bed of blankets. Her ample assets slid over her lush body, unfettered, and I rolled my lower lip between my teeth. Dlane was no Magun, but her shape was unmistakable, even through clothing. Seen from afar she might be taken for eperu, but close scrutiny would reveal her. How far might stories of fugitive anadi travel? Across rain-soaked plains to distant towns? Black specters, vast as the Void, rose from the shadows cast by the lanterns.\par
\par
     "Maybe the females wear clothing in Het Bakka," Dlane said, turning to smile at Magun.\par
\par
     "They don't," Ilushet said from the ground.\par
\par
     Startled, I glanced down at it. "Cousin?"\par
\par
     "I came to tell you that you have leave to go tonight, if you want," it said.\par
\par
     I did not have to look at Dlane to know her answer. "We do."\par
\par
     Ilushet nodded. "Good. Take Magun if she wants to go." It glanced at Dlane. "And consider your choices wisely."\par
\par
     I turned to Magun as Ilushet walked away. The female grinned and said, "I'd be delighted to go, particularly with such a protector."\par
\par
     Of course. Said I, "Go to the smoke pit, then. We'll join you shortly."\par
\par
     She glanced at us both, then hopped down from the platform and vanished into the purple dusk.\par
\par
     If the stories had traveled, then letting Dlane walk naked through town would advertise her presence to all who would capture and return her. But if the stories had traveled, perhaps hiding would be the best way to draw attention. I touched my anadi's cheek and said, "Sometimes it is better not to be a mystery."\par
\par
     A darkness stole over her golden eyes, and her laugh was husky. But it was a laugh nevertheless. "Beautiful, wonderful Thenet. And you say you are not subtle."\par
\par
     "I am subtle as a rock," I answered. "And lovely as one too. You will not be nervous?"\par
\par
     "I have been anadi all my life. To be anadi a little longer in exchange for the sight of a whole different town\'85 that is worth it."\par
\par
     But it was with reluctance that she unyoked her embroidered long-cloth of bronze thread and purple and rolled down her dark olive-brown pants. The white blouse was the last to come off, leaving her naked.\par
\par
     Naked. Not nude. Somehow the gleaming ring through the slit in her belly skewed her skin out of balance. The weight of her breasts and the curve of her hips, the delicate creases between her legs were all magnified.\par
\par
     Her eyes rose to mine. She touched my cheek. "Ah, Thenet. How quickly the soil of your soul has drunk up the rain."\par
\par
     "What?" I said, shaking myself from my bizarre and uncomfortable reverie.\par
\par
     She smiled softly. "Come. Het Bakka is waiting!"\par
\par
     It was a simple thing to retrieve the soft leather bag from Atha. I glanced inside and was surprised to see ten shells; far more than I'd expected, though naturally much less than I made as protector and hunter for House Mated. But the caravan was not House Mated, and I had occasion, more than once, to be thankful for that. Gathering my two wards, I led the way across the spice and rain-scented plain to the edge of the town.\par
\par
     Dlane touched the side of the outermost building. "It is not wood," she said softly.\par
\par
     I paused and ducked into the shadows cast by the overhanging reed roof. The buildings looked like any other to me, if a little thicker somehow, as if they'd bowed in the middle from the weight of their walls. Now that Dlane had lured me closer, I ran my hands over the side and felt the rough granulated surface. "Clay, I think. Baked," I said, from some faint memory of a makeshift oven created for a summer festival.\par
\par
     "I'm sure the owner of the house would be much pleased to have you fingering it," Magun said, the words lolling with boredom.\par
\par
     Dlane wrinkled her nose in the dark and I almost laughed. Instead, I turned and said, "Let's go into the light, then, and see what Het Bakka has to show us."\par
\par
     "Looks like mud and dirt so far," Magun said.\par
\par
     I shrugged. "Mud and dirt are a part of life." We walked onto the thoroughfare between buildings on the outskirts: more a plain path beaten clean of plants by passing feet, spacious given the small dwellings it separated. The spirit of the plains blew here: the Jokka knew how much land they had, and had not thought to cramp their town. It was strange after the tight alleys and crowded streets of home.\par
\par
     There were a few others walking down the thoroughfare who called quiet greetings in the darkness. The yellow lamps set on the tall brick poles shed too frail a light to touch one another, and so we passed in and out of the purple twilight as we walked. The evening was unseasonably cool for summer. Dlane chafed her arms against the wind\rquote s occasional caress, though she was too busy staring greedily around her to notice. Magun kept her silence.\par
\par
     After a succession of small dwellings, we at last came upon the center of town: little more than a broad square edged in more buildings. It had little to recommend it architecturally, but it was clustered with carts lit by petite lanterns, each merchant with its own. Aromas both herbal and succulent rode the free breezes to my nose, and the faint murmur of several score voices wove a tapestry above it.\par
\par
The center of the square had been commandeered, if the relative coziness of the merchants was any indication, by a small raised platform I imagined quite precious, made as it was of wood. Around it several Jokka milled while others lit braziers of elegant brass. They looked familiar: not unlike the coal bowls from one of the temples at home.\par
\par
"What's this?" Magun wondered.\par
\par
     I shrugged and leaned toward a fruit-seller. "What's the commotion, ke emodo?"\par
\par
     The male glanced at me. "Trader, are you? Picked a good night to come. The Trifolders are putting on a dance tonight. Should be starting soon."\par
\par
     "Thank you," I said, and he nodded absently. I wasn't buying, so he wasn't interested.\par
\par
     "The Trifold\'85 isn't that your belief?" Dlane whispered to me.\par
\par
     "No. I follow the Trinity. The Trifold is\'85 different." Different! That was a gentle way of saying it.\par
\par
     "How?"\par
\par
     "Would you like something to eat?" I asked Magun, who was beginning to drift toward one of the carts with spun candies. I handed her a shell and watched her sway toward the merchant, there to make inviting noises no doubt. Turning back to Dlane, I said, "The Trifold and the Trinity both believe in the sanctity of the three sexes: anadi, emodo, eperu. But the Trifold believes that we are each godlike by virtue of being one of these sexes. The Trinity holds that the three sexes are each associated with a heavenly body, and it is by kinship with them that we arrive at our fullness of self."\par
\par
     "So you put your faith in gods and they put their faith in themselves," Dlane said.\par
\par
     I frowned. "It is not that simple!"\par
\par
     "It's not?" she asked. She had at least the grace to look sad and somewhat accommodating.\par
\par
     I sighed, looked at her so utterly anadi in her nakedness, though she kept one arm over her breasts. Then I went to rescue the hapless emodo merchant from Magun's charms. Taking her by the elbow, I guided her and her new bag of sweets away.\par
\par
     "We are staying for the dance, aren't we?" Magun asked, nibbling on a spun sugar square with her coarse fangs.\par
\par
     "I don't think we should be out that long," I said.\par
\par
     "I'd like to see it," Dlane said suddenly.\par
\par
     I tried very hard not to glower. The last thing I wanted to do was to sit through a dance by a religion that glorified its own members with two anadi, both of them mad. While I decided what to say, Dlane took my hand and said, "Let's walk around first."\par
\par
     "Very well," I said, tail flicking in agitation.\par
\par
     With Magun in tow, suckling on her candies in a lascivious display, I guided Dlane around Het Bakka's small market. She stared at the beaded baubles like the chain hung 'round Magun's waist, tried hard not to finger pouches and clothing meant for emodo and eperu, and lingered over the jewelry in the warm darkness. Lingered too long, really. When I looked over her shoulder, I found her staring with fixed, shadowed eyes at the rings. They were laid in neat rows on a cushion: the smallest for the ears of the eperu, the medium-sized for the navel slits of the anadi, and the broadest for the bases of the spines of the emodo. All of them priced as they should be, so that only the most affluent could afford to be awarded them.\par
\par
     "Ke anadi," I murmured in her ear, "Perhaps you shouldn't stare."\par
\par
     "Do you remember when they punched the hole in your ear, Thenet?" Dlane asked.\par
\par
     I paused, then said slowly, "Yes." Glancing toward the merchant, I was relieved to find him occupied with his evening meal.\par
\par
     "Did it hurt?"\par
\par
     My brow furrowed. "I suppose. I don't remember it very well, in all honesty. We really shouldn't be hovering here if we're not planning to buy anything, ke anadi."\par
\par
     Obediently she stepped away, her hands smoothing over her body to rest finally in a frame around her navel. "I had no idea they cost quite so much," she murmured.\par
\par
     "Gold is expensive," I said.\par
\par
     "And so am I?" she said softly. "What does this thing say about me? I am a thing to be purchased at great cost?"\par
\par
     I glanced at Magun, sucking sensually at her candies while watching the passersby. "Magun would say that to be the object of someone's extended labors is enviable."\par
\par
     Dlane looked away, the yellow lantern light skidding down her nose as it turned profile to me. Her shoulders fell. "Magun can survive our world, Thenet."\par
\par
     A shiver traversed my spine. I called, "Magun! Come." And when she joined us, I led the way back through the market. Counting out my own shell sparingly, I put my mind from weighty matters and concentrated on getting the best price for a few luxuries the caravan didn't provide: hair softeners, scented lotions for dry skin, a net for filtering the sandy water we picked up at streams and small pools.\par
\par
     "Hoping that a few magic potions will make you beautiful?" Magun asked archly as I bought a file for my claws and Dlane's. "You're wasting your money."\par
\par
     "It is beautiful," Dlane said, her voice deepening. "You simply do not see it."\par
\par
     "Oh, I see it just fine," Magun said, batting her rust-dark lashes. "You're just sun-struck, dear sister."\par
\par
I ignored her and finished my purchases. I'd much missed the items we'd lost to Roika's chase, and dragged both females with me as I completed my errands. It occurred to me to me when I was done that I still had half a bag of shell left.\par
\par
     "Ke anadi? Would you like to buy something? You've earned shell yourself."\par
\par
     She hesitated, then shook her head. "Come," she said. "Let's see the dance."\par
\par
     I'd hoped she'd forgotten about the dance. Fighting to keep my ears from lying flat, I allowed Dlane to guide me back to the center of the dais. By this time the revelers had set up rough benches, and Dlane slid onto the one in the front. I sat beside her, with Magun on the other side of me.\par
\par
     As we waited for the performance to begin, a trio of Jokka walked among the gathering crowd, balancing baskets against their hips and bellies. The eperu came our way, stopping before us. Fire from the nearby bowls licked its face, and as it dipped forward a pendant swung into the light. My eyes were tangled in the whorls of its design, but I couldn't see it very well.\par
\par
     "Ke Jokka," it murmured in a sweet, low voice. "Would you buy refreshments? Cool juice or clean water, a sugar roll or slice of cheese?"\par
\par
     "No, thank you," Dlane said for me, because I had no voice. I shook myself from my reverie to find it moving on to the next group.\par
\par
     Discomfited, I sat braced between the two anadi as the benches filled behind us. Soon the crowd-working trios had vanished into the deep shadows clustered around the dais, and the lights on the fire bowls dimmed. One of Dlane's hands slipped onto my knee and squeezed, and in the darkness I covered it with one of mine\'85 aware, for just one brief moment, that hers was smaller and smoother beneath my calluses.\par
\par
     A vast drum boomed out of the dark. The chatter of the marketplace died with its first beat and the resulting silence shuddered beneath the implacable onslaught. Slow and rhythmic, like the heart of the Void. Thun. Thun. Thun. My heart slowed to meet it and my breathing as well. Thun. Thun.\par
\par
     Into this primeval darkness came the first dancer, emodo, dark as the Void. The side of his body glowed with the faintest of lights cast by the rightmost bowl. He stepped in time with the drum. I did not realize the pace was swiftening, slowly, oh so slowly, until my chest was leaping and the dancer had reached the dais's edge. Then the lights from both foremost fire bowls burst into life, shattering across the emodo's skin. Two more emodo joined him, and the dance began in earnest.\par
\par
     Oh, how they danced, the emodo! In the safety of the unusually gentle summer night, embraced by the life-giving breeze, they leaped with the power and delicacy that was their birthright. Their strong, sinuous fingers and toes outstretched, curled in, their broad chests emblazoned, their tails flags over proudly bunched buttocks and hard thighs. They went naked, staffs hard as the rippled muscle of their stomachs. They were not simply emodo, but the prime of their kind.\par
\par
     When at last they fell back I found myself breathless and caught myself, swallowed past a dry mouth. Beside me Magun panted, fragrant with the spice of her desire. In stark contrast Dlane sat upright, transfixed and seemingly untouched.\par
\par
     The sonorous drum had slowed and a new, smaller drum joined it, syncopated so that my hard-won composure fell apart. I struggled for breath as my heart tried to decide which beat to follow. Eventually the two settled into a two-toned rhythm, heralding the arrival of three eperu.\par
\par
     Oh, my kind, my kind! And they were so beautiful. As the emodo drifted into the darkness at the back of the stage, the three eperu, splendid in their nudity, in the smoothness of their supple bodies, commenced their dance. It was a thing of predatory power and enchained fates: their hands running over one another, lifting one another into the air on powerful arms. The fire touched their flat bellies and chests, their starkly featureless crotches, ran like liquid up and down the lines of their muscles. I longed to join them. Too soon they faded back into the dark, leaving only the twin drums to fill my ears.\par
\par
     I waited to see the last of the triad, wondering how they would be heralded.\par
\par
     The two drums sang on, but over them glided the sudden shivering of zills. Tiny cymbals clashing softly together, the sound of intricate, small bells\'85 it was to this high-pitched, shimmering sound that the anadi came.\par
\par
     Magun moaned beside me, and even Dlane shuddered. They danced as one, the three anadi, a sensual tangle of limbs and waving manes, wet eyes and mouths and slick thighs. Their hands trailed over one another's bodies in deliberate display, lifting one another's tails and cupping the round slopes of breast and buttock. Rubbing cheeks and chins and bodies, they seemed less three Jokka and more like one great beast with innumerable limbs, stroking itself in open invitation.\par
\par
     At the crescendo of the drumbeat, eperu and emodo joined the anadi in the light and they separated into triads, one of each sex in each. They danced together, so smoothly it seemed impossible: where two formed a circle of arms and body, the third had a graceful arm to insert into the space. They were interwoven, dancing with one mind. Emodo and anadi pressed to one another, touching, evoking the mating that would bear fruit in her womb, and above and always around them, eperu, eperu, protecting, observing, ever apart.\par
\par
     Into this chaos came a final triad from the back of the stage, born on a palette by shadowed dancers. The platform seemed to drift to the dais of its own accord to display its prize. Upon a couch reclined an anadi, her belly so heavy with child she probably couldn't move without aid. Her weighted breasts slid over her ribs, and one hand rested on her navel where the gold of a ka\'f1a's ring glinted, draping a chain of gold and amethyst over her generous hips. At her feet crouched an emodo, hair tousled by the breeze, staff held so hard the bronze chain threaded through the ring in his spine was looped around its thickness, and the garnets on it glinted against his skin.\par
\par
     Straddling the couch was the eperu, wearing the hips of an anadi and the shoulders of an emodo, composed of both and yet its own self, stripped of the hormonal trappings of either. In its hand it held a spear, and its heavy earring swung from one cocked ear. Its waist too was decorated with a chain of silver and creamy moonstone.\par
\par
     The dancers swept around this tableau in a frenzy as the beat thundered in my ears. My hand clenched over Dlane's on my knee. When at last I thought my heart would burst in the race to meet the drums, they stopped and all the dancers fell. The lights guttered, except one: backlighting the three in the front, the eperu with the spear between its legs, the silhouette of the virile emodo over the heavily pregnant anadi, their jewelry and rings catching the last of the light as it faded.\par
\par
     In the darkness and the silence, I looped an arm around Dlane's body and felt her touch her cheek to my shoulder. She was so stiff she barely bent against me. The scent of arousal on the air was not hers\'85 if anything, her sweat stank of fear, and the very tiny shiver I felt her still at her tail was revulsion.\par
\par
     First one lantern, then another lit and the marketplace slowly let the veil of silence fall. I glanced surreptitiously at Magun as the merchants pushed the dark back and found her gaze both hungry and contemptuous.\par
\par
     "I," she said as the lights revealed the stage echoingly empty, "will never be trapped that way on a couch."\par
\par
     "It is the natural order of things," I said because I wanted to see her scowl. But Magun only smiled.\par
\par
     "For some," she said. She stood and stretched languidly, as if proud of her fertile body. I stared\'85 how could she so brazenly display herself after that? I could smell the stickiness of her hunger on her body, catch glimpses of it as she spread her legs\'85 almost as if trying to entice someone. But there was no one watching her but me.\par
\par
     I looked away, gently brushed Dlane's hair. "Ready to leave?"\par
\par
     She roused herself and nodded. "Yes," she said after a silence so long I began to worry. As we stood, she placed a light hand on my wrist. "I think I have something to buy."\par
\par
     I followed her gaze, saw her watching a few of the dancers. They had at last emerged, the emodo and eperu modestly clothed and the anadi more demurely nude.\par
\par
     "Ke anadi\'85."\par
\par
     "Ssh," she said, touching a finger to my chin. She took the bag from me and walked toward one of the eperu.\par
\par
     "What do you suppose she's up to?" Magun said, back to snacking on her sweet candies.\par
\par
     I managed a chuckle though my throat was dry as a hard summer. "Whatever she pleases, as usual."\par
\par
     "Ah. In that at least, she acts like an anadi."\par
\par
     I thought but did not say that Dlane was all anadi, no matter her strange desires.\par
\par
     After earnest conversation, I saw the flash of shell and metal changing hands and Dlane returned, tail sifted by the breeze.\par
\par
     "Bow your head," she said.\par
\par
     I didn't even think as I did so; it was only after she'd put the pouch down and was leaning over me that I started to ask, "\'85 What?"\par
\par
     "Hush," she said, looping a leather cord around my neck. A smooth piece of metal struck my chest as she pulled me straight, then arranged it against my skin. "There."\par
\par
     I looked down. It was a round, cut medallion like the one the eperu offering drinks had worn; in the light I could see it better. Three spokes, embellished with endless, eye-mazing designs, emanated from a central point\'85 but the central point was skewed to one side, as if favoring one more than the others.\par
\par
     "Eperu," she said, covering the medallion with her hand, pressing it to my skin. In her mouth the word was not an insult, not naked\'85 merely elemental. The essence of the Jokku neuter, stripped of title and social meaning. It was a definition.\par
\par
     "You name me," I replied hoarsely.\par
\par
     What Magun thought of this, I had no idea.\par
\par
     We returned to the caravan afterwards, leaving the lights of Het Bakka behind for the lanterns and the soft flickering of the fire in the smoke-pit. Magun climbed into her pile of blankets and rested there, staring out at the other eperu until her lids drifted closed and she slept, body still wreathed with the scent of her hunger.\par
\par
     I helped Dlane onto the wagon's platform and arranged the pillows around her. Tucking a sheet thin as a mist around her, I leaned down and pressed my cheek to her head. The medallion swung beneath my neck, grazing her ribs.\par
\par
     "Thenet," Dlane said, and I finally heard the fear in it that I'd expected. Her fingers lit on my jaw.\par
\par
     "Ssh," I said, catching her hand in mine. "No matter how strange it is to me, if you don't want it they'll have to kill me to force it on you."\par
\par
     She looked up at me, her gratitude written in the release of her brows. I could smell the tears on her breath when she kissed my fingers.\par
\par
     "Sleep now," I said, and she did.\par
\par
     I spent the night on the driver's platform, watching the stars, my spear beside me, my purple and red and black long-cloth tangled between my legs and the medallion warm against my breast.\par
\par
