\par
 \par
\par
     The back of the rikka rolled like the grasses beneath the wind. It had taken me days to acclimate to it, and still I was only a passable rider. Atha had kindly given me the most docile of the beasts when Ilushet had invited me to ride with it.\par
\par
     "They will pull the wagons readily enough," it said when I glanced dubiously at the saddle. "But they prefer something more intimate, to bond rider to ridden. Occasionally we will indulge them."\par
\par
     So in the weeks following our stay at Het Bakka, I learned what it was to ride: to feel the bone of an animal between your legs, the steady pressure of their barrels lifting and falling beneath your calves, and more than anything, the gait: a long-shouldered walk. For days my vertigo at the sight of the sky was mistaken for motion sickness as I fumbled with the reins and the saddle, and perhaps the eperu watching me were not far from the truth in that.\par
\par
     I learned the trick, at last, of letting my hips sway with the rikka while my spine remained upright. That helped. After that, my occasional morning rides with Ilushet became a pleasure.\par
\par
     "Three nibblers already," it murmured as it glanced at the fresh kills hanging from the saddle. It was a particularly clear day, and the memories of the dance were hazy and far away.\par
\par
     "My luck has been good," I replied, rocking with the animal beneath me. The hot wind tousled my mane, tugged ineffectively at the heavy glass beads anchored in its flat black plaits.\par
\par
     "Your luck is always good," Ilushet said. Unlike me, it rode as if it had been born a-back\'85 and perhaps it had been. "There is not a day you go out to hunt that you don't return with something. The others wonder if there is magic in your spear."\par
\par
     I laughed. "Only the magic in my muscle\'85 and in the Moment, perhaps."\par
\par
     It cocked a dun-colored ear at me. "The Moment?"\par
\par
     I nodded. "Yes. When in a battle you enter a place and time falls away. Then you can choose your strike exactly as you want. One of the eperu at home taught it to me."\par
\par
     "Ah. I had heard of such teachings, but they're rare. We do not battle much, we Jokka."\par
\par
     "Don't we?" I asked, but it was an honest inquiry. I didn't know.\par
\par
     "Not that I have heard," Ilushet said. "Perhaps tales, but they are only stories. What truth there is in stories is something I leave to you to figure."\par
\par
     "What would we fight over if we could?"\par
\par
     Ilushet glanced at me, dark brows lifting. "I don't know," it said. "I suppose if there were cause, we'd already be at it, wouldn't we? But there is more than enough land, and what resources there are we all share."\par
\par
     "How many Jokka are there?" I asked suddenly.\par
\par
     Ilushet squinted. "I have made this circuit many years," it said, hand smoothing down the arched neck of the rikka. "Have seen many settlements. Have seen ruins of settlements, even. I would say the all the towns I trade in combined have held, at one time or another, some hundred thousand of us."\par
\par
     We rode in silence for a while, the plains seared gold by the sun and the wind. The perfume of soil and warm grass bruised my nostrils. After a while, I asked, "Is that a lot?"\par
\par
     "Ten thousand bushels of grain is a great deal," Ilushet replied after a while. "Ten thousand beats of a heart is not so much at all. And ten thousand drops of rain is enough to the earth when it's parched. What is a number without context?"\par
\par
     "Not much," I admitted.\par
\par
     It grinned at me, white teeth against dusky lips. I returned the grin. We rode quietly, the creak and rumble of the caravan behind us. It was easier to look at the world from between the large, oveate ears of the rikka: the frame mitigated the vastness of the world. Dlane slept, and Magun as well: no trouble for either with the sails to shade them and the blankets in case the breeze cut too swiftly. I relaxed at last, content in my niche with these other eperu.\par
\par
     Which is when my hand strayed to the medallion and I frowned. "Cousin\'85 how will the caravan go on when you are gone?"\par
\par
     "I don't understand, Thenet."\par
\par
     I looked at the eperu, bracing myself against the saddle as a faint sickness threatened my stomach. "You have no breeding pairs. What will happen when you need new people?"\par
\par
     Ilushet chuckled. "I trust the gods to bring them to me when we need them. Do you honestly believe anadi and emodo would long survive the pace we set, cousin? And we have been slowed by the need to keep Magun safe."\par
\par
     "Magun," I muttered.\par
\par
     Canting its head, Ilushet invited elaboration. I fidgeted, then said, "She troubles me. She is not demure as anadi are. She\'85 displays herself."\par
\par
     Ilushet chuckled. "It is not abnormal to be pleased with one's body."\par
\par
     "I didn't say that well. She displays herself as if inviting copulation. Who would invite such a thing?"\par
\par
     "Who indeed," Ilushet said. It smiled, hands flexing on the reins. "Perhaps it is not so comely a trait, to want to breed. But if no one did, how would we live?"\par
\par
     I could find no answer to that.\par
\par
     Later that night I stretched myself on the ground to work the kinks from my body. In the smoldering fire only bones testified to the meal my nibblers had provided for the caravan. The eperu had dispersed to their tasks\'85 and indeed, I'd brought mine with me; the carving tools and assorted bones rested within arm's reach on the cool earth. I had started several pieces before finishing the original bone piece\'85 I didn't feel ready yet to try and work it to its final shape. But the others I used to practice, to grow in confidence, and several already decorated the haft of my spear on a pull string I could use to silence them when I hunted. I had a few new pieces I was working now, larger than the last ones: those I'd brought with me and now ignored, for the World's breath had stilled for once, and a full belly and the flickering of the fire distracted me from the slow easing of my spinal bones.\par
\par
     The weight that settled on my rump surprised me and I opened my mouth to protest, but all sound died in my throat at the fingers pressing against my shoulder blades.\par
\par
     "You are tight," Dlane said. Her thumbs were slick with something soft and scented like crushed flower petals and mint. "Riding, driving, hunting, walking\'85 I don't know how your body stays together."\par
\par
     I gurgled as her hands grasped the muscles over my shoulders and plucked, leaving a trail of heat and scent behind. After swallowing, I managed to rasp, "It is what eperu do, ke anadi. We work."\par
\par
     "The emodo work as well," Dlane said. Not a reprimand; she sounded absent as her hands drew designs with my skin as canvas and hot oil for paint. The air seemed cooler on the squiggles she left behind, accentuating the lack of the breeze.\par
\par
     "Not the same," I said. "They sit in shops and hammer at jewels or count grains or sell goods. They spin and weave and keep accounts and run Houses. That is work of the mind and hands, not of the body."\par
\par
     Gently she rubbed her fingers through the hair along the ridge of my upper spine, dragging the fragrant oil through it. "So upon the backs of eperu do we make our society, is that it?"\par
\par
     I frowned. "I don't think\'85."\par
\par
     "That sounds wrong to you? Who harvests the grain we eat?"\par
\par
     "We do," I said.\par
\par
     "And who builds the houses?"\par
\par
     "The emodo plan them. And they help to build them," I said, trailing off.\par
\par
     "But in short shifts. It taxes their bodies, does it not?"\par
\par
     Reluctantly, I nodded.\par
\par
     "How long would it take to build a house if only emodo built it, I wonder? Who guards us from animals? Plants the harvest? Hunts food? Pulls trade wagons from town to town? Mines the hills and mountains for stone and metal?"\par
\par
     "I guess we do," I said.\par
\par
     "The anadi and the emodo would not live long without the eperu," Dlane said.\par
\par
     "The Jokka would not live long if it were but eperu," I said after drawing in a long breath.\par
\par
     Dlane chuckled softly, pressing the flats of her hands against my lower back. "No\'85 we were not meant to be without one another."\par
\par
     A twinge of well-being crackled beneath her hands, as if the coals in the smoke pit lived somewhere in my body. The hair on my back stood on end. It was a peculiar sensation, and relaxation and tension came in its wake and nestled together somehow in the pit of my body.\par
\par
     I felt her shift forward by the slide of her hands, the pressure of her weight transferring farther up my back. Her voice sounded close to my ear. "Thenet? What is it?"\par
\par
     As I opened my mouth, the feeling bled away. I sighed. "Nothing," I said. "Just\'85 nothing."\par
\par
     Her hands paused, then resumed their work. "If you say so."\par
\par
     I let her massage my back - how could I stop her? I was bone in the knives of her hands. She caressed and I conformed to the shape she desired. I realized belatedly that I was drooling and licked my lips furtively, thinking.\par
\par
     "Ilushet said something strange to me today." That was my voice. I hadn't expected to say anything.\par
\par
     "Oh?"\par
\par
     "It said it had seen the ruins of settlements."\par
\par
     "Ruins!" Dlane exclaimed, her surprise punctuated by an especially deep stab between my shoulder-blades. My body rocked beneath her hips, but her center of balance never changed. She already knew how to sway. "But\'85 that would mean there were once far more Jokka than there are now, wouldn't it?"\par
\par
     "Or that we moved settlements from a less favorable spot to a more favorable one."\par
\par
     "What a strange thought," Dlane said, her voice now taut with excitement. "What do you think happened?"\par
\par
     I stretched one foot-hand slowly, closing my eyes. The fire's light was orange on the inside of my eyelids, and the night warm on my skin. "I don't know," I said. "You'd think there would be stories. Histories of them. Have you seen any histories?"\par
\par
     "Only a few," Dlane said. "They are much longer than most stories, so the clay-keepers rarely display them. Stories mostly of the Houses that have helped run the town\'85 who first began them, who ruled them, what decisions they made. Nothing about the Jokka as a whole."\par
\par
     "Don't stop!" I exclaimed as her fingers drifted to a halt.\par
\par
     She laughed. "Just a moment." Her body shifted across my hips, and I heard the scratch of stone against stone: she was unscrewing the top from a bottle. A few minutes later she rubbed her palms together with a thin wet sound. Sheathed in hot mint and flowers, her fingers resumed their dance across my spine. I let out a long low breath.\par
\par
     "Why do you suppose we know so little about it?" she asked.\par
\par
     "Do we know so little about history? Or is it just you and I?" I said after thinking.\par
\par
     That she laughed surprised me, and I rotated one ear back toward her quizzically. She tickled its tuft with a fingertip before saying, "You're beginning to ask the right questions, at least. Maybe next time we come to a town we can see the local clay-keeper."\par
\par
     A frisson of dread ran down my back. She must of felt it because she leaned back, though she didn't speak until my shiver subsided.\par
\par
     "Didn't like the Trifold, did you?"\par
\par
     "They glorify our living. It's wrong. We live and the living kills us. We're no great mystery\'85 just people. If we are all there is to greatness, what use is greatness?"\par
\par
     "If we can't ever become great, what good is it to have greatness at all?" Dlane asked.\par
\par
     "So we have something to strive toward," I said.\par
\par
     "Why bother if you can never achieve it?"\par
\par
     Frustration drove the distraction from me. I twisted onto my side so I could look at her as she straddled my body. "We can't be great, Dlane. Greatness is for gods and heroes and stories."\par
\par
     "Why not?"\par
\par
     "Because we are base!"\par
\par
     We stared at one another, and I think she was as surprised at my bitterness as I was. I had never had these thoughts before. In what dark soil in my heart had these roots sent down their streamers?\par
\par
     Dlane touched my chin with slick fingers. "Oh, Thenet," she said. "Who taught you to hate yourself so?"\par
\par
     "I don't think I do," I said, but she'd managed to confuse me yet again and I couldn't meet her golden eyes. "You seem to be the one filled with hate, ke anadi."\par
\par
     "Anger, Thenet. Anger. Not hate. And not at myself\'85 at the world I was given to live in. The one that couldn't hold me."\par
\par
     I glanced at the stars, the deep sky, the broad plains and the wagons fencing us in. "It seems to be doing well enough."\par
\par
     She laughed low, reached over and kissed my forehead. "My silly eperu. We'll teach you to love yourself yet."\par
\par
     "Love is for anadi," I said, wrapping an arm around her and squeezing. "But I'll let you try and fill my head with fancies."\par
\par
     Dlane pulled back and grinned, showing off petite, fire-polished teeth. "Oh, Thenet! I already do."\par
\par
