 \par
\par
     The plains were coy and barely displayed the passing seasons. It was easy to let time loose and forget about it, to exist not day, to day, but in a sort of permanent now. The sun rose and fell and rose again, but it was all one time, and the weeks scarcely mattered. So it was with our time with the caravan.\par
\par
     Driving one day I chanced to look at my hands and saw that the sun was falling full on the flat white skin and through the tufts of hair embroidered around my wrists. It occurred to me as strange indeed that my hands were not warm despite the sun's touch. Somehow, autumn had come and I had not caught it.\par
\par
     "Preoccupied?" Ilushet asked, drawing alongside.\par
\par
     I looked down at it, walking beside my team of rikka. "I\'85 had not realized how long it had been."\par
\par
     "Since you joined us?" Ilushet smiled. "Your arms have helped unload the barrels into four towns, cousin. You have ridden rikka for almost a month and a half, and it has been almost three since you and your companion first joined us. Does that help you regain your balance?"\par
\par
     Three months! My hands tightened on the reins, but practice by now kept my wrists lax. The team trundled on undisturbed, bruising the brittle grass with their clawed toes.\par
\par
     "It is longer than you'd anticipated," Ilushet said, lifting a black brow.\par
\par
     I nodded dumbly.\par
\par
     The caravan-master smiled, its staff biting into the earth at a regular rhythm, thumping the pace for each stride. "You had much healing to do."\par
\par
     "Cousin?" I fumbled for my bearings. "I was not wounded."\par
\par
     "Weren't you? I saw the signs on your body when you washed, and on your mind at other times. Your companion's soul is still perturbed, but that is not altogether surprising. She is anadi. No, cousin," Ilushet shook its head, pale plait glistening with glass beads as it shifted, "Your escape harrowed you both. But you're far now from the cage that bound you. Perhaps you might think of where you might fly now that flight is no longer a fearful thing."\par
\par
     My mind leaped from image to image: from Roika to the emodo of House Mated hunched over me, to the forest and the hunting beasts. I longed to divulge the secret to Ilushet, to beg its advice. "Is flight not fearful? I think that we may run forever, the anadi and I, and never outrun the danger."\par
\par
     "There is peril in every course," Ilushet said. It glanced around, then tossed its spear up to me. I grabbed it absently as it climbed onto the driving block beside me. Its body wore a halo of warmth that made pointed comment of the cold wind. I leaned toward it.\par
\par
     Setting its hands on its knees, Ilushet said, "There is peril in life, Thenet. Safety is illusory."\par
\par
     "That's not what I wanted to hear," I said bitterly. "I live to keep her safe. If there is no safety, why should I waste my energy?"\par
\par
     Ilushet sighed. "It may not be what you want to hear but it is the truth. You must at some point decide what is an acceptable risk\'85 and at what point your life is no longer worth living because of the activities you refuse out of fear."\par
\par
     "I am not afraid," I muttered.\par
\par
     I knew it didn't believe me. I didn't believe myself. I was afraid for Dlane: afraid that she would be discovered and destroyed, afraid that even if she wasn't discovered she would maim her own spirit with her strange thinking. And I was afraid for myself, though I couldn't articulate why. I knew only that every day some part of me reported its foot had reached the edge of a cliff and that the drop down it was long and cold and lethal. On the yawing edge of that abyss nothing seemed normal, and so I lost myself in the Now: the Moment that the caravan's languorous journey had become.\par
\par
     Ilushet covered one of my knees with its warm, smooth hand. "It is okay to say the truth sometimes."\par
\par
     My ears flipped back. "One truth leads to another, cousin\'85 and when your soul is bare, even the wind can wound it."\par
\par
     "Then the secret is to make wounds inconsequential, isn't it?"\par
\par
     I frowned and looked at it, lips pulling back from my teeth. "Riddles, cousin!"\par
\par
     "More truths," Ilushet said. It sighed and looked away. "I would help you, Thenet. I would help you more than I am allotted. But I cannot speed you down the path to a greater understanding. Simply consider this: to be afraid of pain presumes that pain gives others an advantage over you. If it doesn't, then you can walk naked as a newborn into the darkness and let it claw you, and even if you die it will have had no victory. No victory that you did not allow it."\par
\par
     "I don't understand," I said through a parched throat.\par
\par
     The caravan-master took back its staff and planted it on the ground. It hopped off the driving block as the wagon creaked on and looked up at me. "You can," it said, and then walked on.\par
\par
     I stared after it. The absence of its body reminded me that I was cold, and my flesh contracted. Had it not been for the living scent of the rikka and the sight of the other wagons placidly pacing mine, I might have fled, or wept onto the back of my hand.\par
\par
     And yet I could not fathom why.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     When Dlane woke I was sitting cross-legged, carving a thigh bone into an abstract shape by the light of a lantern on the wagon's floor. My shadow flickered, dusky yellow, within the puddle of gold light that cast away the gloaming. Her drawn breath was so soft I wouldn't have heard it had I not had an ear twisted that way. Then the gossamer sheet hissed as it slid off her body and she drew herself against me, pressing her head against my thigh. She yawned, complete with a tiny squeak at the apex.\par
\par
     "My Thenet," she said, voice warm and thick with her dense anadi dreams. She wrapped an arm around my back.\par
\par
     I smiled down at her, put my carving tools aside. "Good evening, ke anadi."\par
\par
     "Where is Magun?" she asked.\par
\par
     "She went to sit by the fire," I said. "How did you know she was gone? You didn't turn to look."\par
\par
     Dlane chuckled. "Your body is stiff when she's about."\par
\par
     "It is?" Bemused, I looked down at her golden eyes, her subtle face.\par
\par
     "Oh yes."\par
\par
     I chuckled. "There is little I can say to that, I suppose." I glanced down at her; she was staring out through the reed curtain, long lashes painted in shadowy reverse on her smooth gray cheek. "Ke anadi, where are we going?"\par
\par
     "I don't know," Dlane said. "I thought you were the one who spoke with Ilushet about such things."\par
\par
     "No, no. Not where the caravan is going. Where are we going?" When she twisted to look up at me, I took a breath and let it out. "We can't stay with the caravan forever."\par
\par
     "Why not?" she asked, her gaze intent. Not a question, but a probe.\par
\par
     "I\'85 I don't know. It seems abnormal. Do you want to live this life?" She was confusing me again. As usual!\par
\par
     "It is not a bad one," Dlane said. She shifted so that her head rested on my thigh, golden hair piling in soft, thick waves in my lap. She played with the beads in my tail. "Quiet, the people are kind. The work is interesting."\par
\par
     "Carekeeping a crazed anadi? That's your idea of interesting?" I asked, ears flipping back.\par
\par
     Her eyes rolled up to mine, innocently framed by lifted golden brows. "You don't like Magun?"\par
\par
     "Who could?" I said, folding my arms and looking away. "She's obnoxious. And proud of it!"\par
\par
     "Pride is not evil," Dlane murmured.\par
\par
     Exasperated, I said, "Why do you always have to talk in circles? I asked where we should go. You were the one who dragged me away from the hands of the Void. You were the one who fled your House and bound me to you. Now we're fled, and my charter is only to keep you safe, not to decide your fate for you. So tell me, ke anadi\'85 where are we going?"\par
\par
     Said she simply, "I don't know."\par
\par
     "That's it?" I said, my hands balling into fists.\par
\par
     "I'll know where we need to go when we need to go there," the anadi said.\par
\par
     My thigh slid out from under her head. I found myself on my feet, parting the curtain and dropping onto the cold ground. I strode out across the plains. We'd stopped for the night beside a pool of water, and a few stunted trees had broken the soil nearby. I chose one to sit beneath and slid to the ground, the tangy odor of the tree's flaking bark in my nostrils. The lights of the caravan were barely five wagon-lengths away, but the distance seemed uncrossable.\par
\par
     I took a deep pull of the chill air and stared up at the stars, daring the vertigo to come for me. Now that I understood - now that I knew I'd been living my life in a stasis, hoping never to reach the dizzying horizon - I wanted to move on. The wagons stifled; the reins had grown heavy, the creak of the wheels cloying, the sight of the endless, empty land depressing.\par
\par
     "Bored of us already?"\par
\par
     My lips drew back from my teeth but I didn't look at her. "Go away, Magun."\par
\par
     She swayed closer, hips rocking back and forth. The wind passed through her hair and brought me the scent of her desire.\par
\par
     "I didn't want you to be lonely."\par
\par
     "Did it occur to you that there might be a reason I'm here, Magun? Alone?"\par
\par
     "A few," Magun admitted. "I thought perhaps you'd tired of your female."\par
\par
     I snarled. "Speak of her with respect. She is twice your equal."\par
\par
     "Oh?" Magun tilted her head. "Was she born anadi, then?"\par
\par
     I realized my slip too late and looked away, ears burning. I didn't care if Magun knew that Dlane was born anadi. I worried more about what Ilushet would think of me if it knew I'd lied to it.\par
\par
     "I see," Magun said. "That explains a lot\'85 and leaves even more to be uncovered. What does she really want, eperu? And how," crouching before me and scooping the medallion off my breast, "Did she buy your silence?"\par
\par
     Her audacity was beyond belief. I glared at her but did not reply.\par
\par
     "You are so tiresome, Thenet," Magun said at last with a sigh. "What about her has you so bewitched? I am a far more tractable female than she ever will be; surely you realize that. Why is she running? Is she barren? Why are you following her?"\par
\par
     "If you could understand," I said, "You already would have."\par
\par
     "Do you honestly believe such opaque answers will dismay me?" Magun asked, lifting one rust-dark brow. She shifted, knees parting. I turned away from the sight and smell of her: not unpleasant, just like her personality, overpowering. "You're using them as a staff, Thenet. Don't think that because anadi do not fight that we don't understand defense and attack."\par
\par
     "What do you want from me?" I asked, at last, frustrated. Anything to get her away from me so I could breathe in the smells of the world, shut out the cares of the day.\par
\par
     "I want to understand what drives you both," Magun said, stroking the medallion flat to my chest. I ignored her fingers. "I want to know why you're fleeing."\par
\par
     "But why?"\par
\par
     Magun grinned. She had an emodo's coarse teeth. "Because to understand people is to rule them. Until today I have understood the motivations, the fears and the hopes of every person I've encountered. We Jokka are a simple folk, Thenet. But you and Dlane\'85 you refuse the mold. You may not speak of it, but your actions, your expressions, your bodies scream your furtive ploys. I want to know you." She leaned toward me. "I want to have you."\par
\par
     Her breath had the honied sweetness of candy, and I could smell the blood pulsing at her skin. Her hunger was appalling. I was eperu! No part of the cycle she evoked with her desire, with her smoky red eyes, with the slick drops of saliva dripping too slowly from her fangs. Her tongue dragged over my cheek, one of her hands pressed on my chest and her knees spread over mine.\par
\par
     I panted, felt her closeness. "No!"\par
\par
     Magun tumbled away before I even realized I'd propelled her. I stood, trembling. "No," I said again, calmer. I swallowed. "I am eperu. I will be no part of you. You cannot have me\'85 you cannot even touch me. And if I find you near my anadi again, I will tie you down on a mirror for the sun to burn. Do you understand?"\par
\par
     She backed away, stumbled once. Her delicate feet flexed against the ground, and then she spun and ran back to the wagons.\par
\par
     I watched her go, my body still shaking. My arms I held apart from my body, for all my limbs felt unnatural. Even my breath only seemed to call attention to the strange, tight dryness of my windpipe. It hissed in and out through a mouth and nostrils that didn't seem mine.\par
\par
     Slowly, my knees unlocked enough for my body to slide back down to the ground. Pressing my legs together, I slumped against the bark and took great gulps of the air. Anything to wash the smell of her away.\par
\par
     The darkness, the towering sky made me feel small. For once I was grateful, grateful to be pushed into a tiny space by forces outside myself. Anything to keep my blurry soul confined in my uncertain body.\par
\par
     The night advanced in fits and starts, measured by periods of stillness and shaking. At some point the former began to outlast the latter until finally, at some hour I could not name, I had come to an equilibrium. The wind tousled my mane and traced patterns on my bare chest, and the medallion was cold against my skin. Strips and pockets of warmth lined the insides of my thighs and calves, huddled in the cracks of my elbows and folded knees. I was exquisitely aware of the temperature differences, of the melange of scents the wind brought, of the high sounds of the tree's thin branches rubbing together, like the crackle-dry skin of an elder's limbs.\par
\par
     My eyes closed, because sight had become superfluous.\par
\par
     "Thenet?"\par
\par
     Her clear, soft soprano shattered my peace - so sublime, oh! I felt the knife drive in, saw the pieces fly away. When I opened my eyes I expected my hands to be bloody.\par
\par
     They weren't. I lifted a head made heavy but could barely see her. I blinked to clear my vision, but still she remained a hazy shape.\par
\par
     "Ke anadi?" I said huskily. The cold had dried my voice in my throat.\par
\par
     "Thenet\'85 it's almost truedark. I was worried\'85."\par
\par
     More than worried from the skitter of her voice. I cleared mine and said, "I am here."\par
\par
     "You should come back-"\par
\par
     "No."\par
\par
     She came closer: I could smell her, rich, floral. Could hear the cloth crinkling: a partial vest, from the way the sounds changed across her chest, a long-cloth and a belt of clinking glass crystals, perhaps a few stones. "I don't understand," she said. "Did I offend you earlier? I didn't mean to drive you away."\par
\par
     "I know," I said.\par
\par
     Dlane paused, then sat next to me. Her body was far warmer than mine. We remained thus in silence as the night thickened toward the hour.\par
\par
     "It's getting colder," Dlane whispered.\par
\par
     "Sit here," I said, patting the ground. She fumbled toward me in the near darkness, clumsy without sight to augment her grace. Cautiously she leaned back until her spine rested against my chest and then she sighed and her body grew lax. I drew up my knees, one on either side of her, and rested my wrists atop them.\par
\par
     "It's so dark," she whispered.\par
\par
     "There's nothing to fear," I said softly. "I am here, ke anadi."\par
\par
     She turned against me and shivered, tucking her head against my collarbones. "Thenet," she said. "The world is so dark. It's as if everyone wanders blind to the hopes and fears of one another. I'm\'85."\par
\par
     Her pause was horrible and endless. The bitter black had taken our sight from both of us, and the lack magnified the tiny quivers of her side, her shoulders.\par
\par
     "I'm afraid."\par
\par
     I closed my eyes and listened to her breathe.\par
\par
     "There's no safe place. Everywhere I go I must always ask who will come for me next. How long it will be before my measure is up. My luck can't last forever."\par
\par
     "I'm here," I said again, wrapping my arms around her.\par
\par
     "I know," she said. I could feel her shifting to look up at me even though there was no seeing in truedark. "But not even you can turn aside fate, setasha."\par
\par
     Her tongue left a cold spot on my chin and lips, but I was too far gone to stop her, to protest.\par
\par
     Setasha, she named me. Love. I am not part of that circle. But she has named me before, and names me again. I am her creature. I cannot be setasha - I am not - but I cannot deny her - my confusion was beyond words, beyond explication. I opened my mouth and felt her lapping at my teeth, too close for a true kiss for comfort. I turned my face from her.\par
\par
     "It's so cold," Dlane whispered, stealing the breath as it came out of my body. I was wracked with a shudder so intense it had to be a negation, but she rode me and my hand slid down her back to the base of her tail. My fingers threaded through the soft hair and then curled into a fist, pressing against her spine. She shivered.\par
\par
     I did not recognize the motions I made, nor what moved me\'85 only that her fear was as unbearable as the thought that she loved me, and that to touch her so, on her breast and hip, drove away the acrid scent of tears I could smell on her lips when they brushed across my cheek and throat. There was a tension in her body that grew as I undressed her and my fingers came away slick from her tacit reply.\par
\par
     It was the Trifold's dance, but skewed. I was no emodo to move an anadi to this. I had nothing to give her in response to her open heart - for I did not need to see her face to see that I owned her. That my hands and my body and the heat of my skin had undone all her careful disguises. Writhing in my arms, she was anadi full and complete.\par
\par
     And beautiful in it. I found myself longing to see her face.\par
\par
     "There," she whispered in my ear. "There-oh, setasha." Her voice calmed even as it thinned to silence, and the liquid length of her body curled inward, stiffened. I could feel the taut grimace that exposed her teeth as I held her against my body.\par
\par
     With a whimper, her muscles went lax. She pressed her head against my neck and it was only when a rivulet of cold streaked down my heated skin that I realized she was weeping against my throat, bared teeth grazing my flesh and the deadly poison mitigated by the length of her tears.\par
\par
     "Ke anadi?" I whispered, and then huskily, "Dlane?"\par
\par
     "I feel sick," she said, then gulped back a sob. "It's so wrong, so wrong to feel so happy and then so ill\'85."\par
\par
     I couldn't begin to imagine the release, much less the giddiness of an overworked body. In all my years, I'd never once felt faint as so many of my kind do, even other eperu. I could not share with her the experience of being dizzy, not with pain or with pleasure.\par
\par
So I rocked her and held her tightly, and wondered what I had done.\par
\par
     Truedark was lifting its veil when I felt her egg break apart against my thighs. I was no emodo to give her the seed to make it take in her womb; and even if I had been, I'm not sure I would have knowing what it would do to her. But the feel of it hot against me made very clear indeed what I'd done.\par
\par
     Dlane shuddered but did not weep. She'd long since run out of tears.\par
\par
     "Thenet," she whispered, her soprano cracked and tiny.\par
\par
     I pressed a finger to her lips. "No." I felt the stirring of her body and added, "No. Not what you're thinking\'85 not a denial. Do you think me mad?"\par
\par
     She deflated against me and I pulled her tightly to my chest.\par
\par
     I had given an anadi a very private pleasure, a thing between mating couples, a thing to facilitate the birth of children. A barren union, my fingers and her flesh. It was meaningless\'85 wasn't it? And yet, I couldn't imagine any eperu doing what I'd done. Pleasure was for mating, a messy and dangerous duty left to males and females. Neuters were no part of that circle.\par
\par
     Dlane was still against me, her clothing scattered on the ground beneath the eaves of the tree. I held her in my lap and curled my limbs around her to shield her from the cold. The heat that sealed our bodies together was damp with sweat and smelled strongly of my exertions, her desire. The wind carried it away, but always an eddy brought it back to cloud my mind.\par
\par
     The night lifted and I felt her calm attention on the rising colors. She hadn't slept; I could feel her chest pressing against mine and I knew its rhythm. When the first sliver of the sun crested the horizon, I said, "We can't go on."\par
\par
     Her head dragged against my shoulder and I finally looked at her. There were smudges beneath her filmy eyes. Matted golden hair suggested a metal veil across her forehead and one cheek, and her mouth shone gold with the residue of her weeping. And yet there was a softness in her expression that I had never seen, a calm and a stillness, and as in a pool of water I could see further in that stillness than I ever had.\par
\par
     She was radiantly beautiful.\par
\par
     Gently I pushed the tangled hair from her face.\par
\par
     "Go on?" Dlane said wearily. "Or go back?"\par
\par
     I couldn't decide on the answer to that. Despite the revelation of the sight of her, I had no emotional energy left to rise to the level of her speech, so demanding. "Go on with the caravan," I said. "Go back to that life. I\'85 couldn't face them."\par
\par
     Couldn't face the other eperu, knowing that for one night I had had an emodo's hands. I was no longer wholly neuter.\par
\par
     "Them," Dlane said. "Magun."\par
\par
     "Ilushet. Atha. All of them," I said, though the thought of being found thus by Magun was terrifying. I drew in a long breath to calm myself and looked down at her, cupping her cheeks with my hands. "Is it time to move on?"\par
\par
     A fragile smile fluttered over her mouth. "Setasha, you are learning."\par
\par
     I licked her cheek, and her words gave my spirit wings even as they brought me crashing down in pieces. I didn't want to understand. I didn't want this world where emodo and eperu and anadi are not unlike. I didn't want a Trifold world without gods, where mere Jokka could aspire to greatness. If Jokka could be great, who was I?\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     "You're leaving," Ilushet said before I could open my mouth.\par
\par
     I stood, jaw agape, and it reached over and touched my shoulder with a chuckle.\par
\par
     "Yes, it is that obvious. Do you think I've never had transients before? You are not the first, cousin."\par
\par
     "But how do you know?" I asked at last. I hadn't come prepared to ask; I had my spear with me, decorated with its bone and glass ornaments, and wore a simple set of gray short pants and a knee-length long-cloth patterned in gray, gold, black and auburn.\par
\par
     "In the way you stand," Ilushet said. "The look in your eyes. They reveal you, that you are intently focused on something. Before your gaze drifted to and fro." It smiled. "So am I right, then? You and your companion are leaving."\par
\par
     I nodded. "Yes." I paused. "We'd like to go to a town and stay a while. Do you have any recommendations?"\par
\par
     Ilushet studied me with calm, dark eyes for several minutes; it was sitting on the platform of its wagon, oiling the leather of one of the rikka's harnesses. The other eperu were preparing for the day's journey, but here there was a lacuna. "Het Narel," it said presently. "They will ask you no questions. Do not forget to collect your shell from Atha before you leave."\par
\par
     I nodded and turned to go. Stopped before I could think better of it and said, "Ilushet, there are so many things\'85."\par
\par
     "That you wanted to tell me?" Ilushet shook its head, tawny mane drifting in the cool autumn winds. "I told you it was okay to say the truth, cousin. It's also okay to keep secrets." It smiled. "Go. Remember to walk unafraid in the dark. And take the tools."\par
\par
     I bowed my head and backed away.\par
\par
     Dlane was in our wagon packing as silently as possible. When I looked up onto the platform I glanced toward the blankets: Magun was sleeping.\par
\par
     "Here," Dlane said, her voice hushed as she handed me a pack.\par
\par
     I looked into it. "Ilushet gave me the box, the bone-working knives and awls."\par
\par
     Dlane's golden brows rose. She picked up the box and handed it to me, and I carefully placed it in the pack and closed the flap. I helped her down.\par
\par
     "Ready?" I asked.\par
\par
     She smiled brilliantly at me.\par
\par
     I offered my hand and she took it. While the other eperu hitched the rikka for the day and Magun slept, the two of us slipped into the gathering day heading east, toward Het Narel.\par
\par
     "Do you have a plan?" I asked.\par
\par
     "Yes," Dlane said, grinning. "This is it!"\par
\par
     I laughed.\par
\par
 \par
\par
PART 3\par
\par
 \par
\par
     "It has no trees," Dlane whispered to me as we walked out of the great and lonely plains into the nub of civilization and comfort that was Het Narel. She pulled on the chain of her long-cloth, then pressed against me. I tugged on her hand as a mind-boggling amount of Jokka passed us, some waving, some eyeing us with mild curiosity. After the tiny towns on Ilushet's route and days of a handful of caravan members, to have more people in eyeshot than I could count seemed incredible.\par
\par
     "Oh, there are trees. Just not big ones," I said.\par
\par
     "Or many," she muttered.\par
\par
     I laughed. "Will you speak badly of the gift?" I asked her. "Look at all these people. They aren't staring at us like we're insane, anadi and eperu walking alone hand-in-hand out of the desert! This place is a blessing."\par
\par
     "Yes, but now what?" Dlane asked.\par
\par
     "I thought you were the one with the plan?"\par
\par
     She grinned. "We've exhausted the entirety of it by arriving."\par
\par
     "I should have know," I said, torn between amusement and weariness. \ldblquote Let\rquote s rest a moment.\rdblquote\par
\par
     We sat on the ground on the edge of the thoroughfare together, her hip leaning against mine, her shoulder tucked under my arm.\par
\par
     Het Narel wasn't much like our home. The streets were broader and weren't paved or marked with stones; instead bare, hard soil testified that the passage of thousands of feet had driven the memory of pungent grass and bloom from the earth. The buildings were baked brick accented with colors: ecru, beige, salt white and funnel-cloud gray-yellow. Some of the houses were even adorned with expensive wood, no doubt brought by caravans like Ilushet's.\par
\par
Closer to the center of town, the buildings rose to a startling three or four stories high, probably housing the richer families. It was a broad sprawl, but I could see little of it at a time; the land was so unrelentingly flat that there was no place from which I could take a long view.\par
\par
     And Jokka! Through this subtle backdrop of gray and pale brown and beige and clay they were as bright as banners. Clumps of eperu in festive white and purple and teal blue, emodo wandering from shop to shop, hair in shades of black and blue and red, white skin and glass-yellow, spirals in every shade. They were flowers in the autumn pallor.\par
\par
     "We could get lost in here," Dlane said.\par
\par
     "That's what I'm hoping." I pushed my braid back over my shoulder and watched the crowd stream past. Apparently mid-day was a busy time. "We don't want anyone to think us abnormal. The more people live here, the better."\par
\par
     "What will we do, though?" Dlane said. She pressed closer as a wind ran through the streets, and I blessed the coming cold. Having the anadi overheat was the last thing I needed.\par
\par
     "Now," I said, standing and helping her up, "We go find Transactions."\par
\par
     "Are you sure that's wise?" she said, wide-eyed.\par
\par
     I tucked her blouse more neatly around her body. At the perimeter of the city we'd stopped to bind her breasts and give her shoulders a little more bulk with a wrapped scarf. While her face was still delicate, with an artificial chest she could pass for just-Turned. "We need to live somewhere," I said. "Better that we live legitimately than in fear of the authorities. Come, ke anadi! This is only the beginning of our adventure. Don't tell me you're ready to give it up already?"\par
\par
     "Oh no!" Dlane said, and a smile grew on her face again. "Never that." She took my hand, squeezed it. "I trust you, Thenet. Take me where you will."\par
\par
     I hesitated, then squeezed her hand in return, still awkward with this language she spoke with her eyes and body. Anadi fancies, I thought, but her hand in mine was warm. I shook my head and pulled her with me through the dusty gold and gray and eggshell of the town. Into the crowd, so fiercely scented of people: of perfumes strong and subtle, of sweat, of pheromones tickling the top of my mouth.\par
\par
     It took us the better part of twenty minutes to navigate Het Narel, which was much larger than I'd originally believed. Nor was it laid out as I'd expected. At home, shops and markets had been in the center of town with the habitations of the major Houses fanning around them and the riches of the House slowly decreasing the farther from the center of town one walked. Because Het Narel had also appeared to have small Houses on the fringes of town, I'd made the mistake of assuming a similar layout\'85 but the middle layer of Het Narel was the shop and market layer, with the great Houses clustered in the center.\par
\par
     It was around this middle layer that we found Transactions, fully known as the Place of People Transactions. This was the most powerful branch of Town Services, for it was here that the trade in anadi and the occasional emodo and eperu took place. Whether you were a neuter seeking employment for a different House or an anadi being dowered into a new family, Transactions kept the records.\par
\par
     Here, as at home, the building was marked by a sign with a painted spiral shell. I walked into the narrow, two-story building, Dlane at my heels. The smell of baked clay clouded my nostrils, acrid and thick.\par
\par
     Behind a desk in the front room sat an elder emodo, his slate-blue skin sliced through with spirals of rich, deep brown to match his mane. His polite eyes when he looked up at us were a clouded amber that was neither the unalloyed beauty of Dlane's nor the beast-ugliness of mine. Some intermediate shade. I found it pleasant.\par
\par
     "May I help you?"\par
\par
     As before the hunting emodo so long ago, some strength I was unaware of used my voice, spoke for me. "Please and thank you, ke emodo. My companion and I would like to put down a stone."\par
\par
     One of his brows lifted. "Is it just the two of you, ke eperu?"\par
\par
     "Yes," I said, trying not to twitch. I could feel Dlane's amazement, but was gratified that she hadn't moved.\par
\par
     The emodo cleared his throat. "You are aware that you cannot build a House with two eperu, ke eperu?" He glanced at Dlane, then back to me.\par
\par
     "Respect, elder, but my companion is Turning emodo. It will be the head of House. I have several crafts, and so does it, and we believe we can trade our wares for enough anadi to begin a proper Household."\par
\par
     He squinted at Dlane, beckoned her nearer. She walked to him with her anadi's grace and he took her hand, turned it. He gazed into her face. "It does already have an emodo's bearing, ke eperu. Does it still have an eperu's mind?"\par
\par
     "I do," Dlane said for me. I could see the strain in her face; it displeased her to be spoken about as if she not present.\par
\par
     The emodo leaned back. "While it is unusual for anyone to put down a stone without a larger group, the law says that if you would put down a stone, you require only a breeding Jokkad and shell to pay for the certificate. So long as you have these things, ke eperu, we can accommodate you."\par
\par
     I let out a breath. "Thank you, ke emodo." I pulled my pouch from my belt. "What is the fee here?"\par
\par
     "Thirty shell," he answered.\par
\par
     I flinched. That was almost half of our combined salary. I understood the purpose of such a stringent price -- those who would build a House must be prepared for far greater expenses than the legal fee - but still. I counted each shell out, one by one, beneath the watchful eyes of the emodo. When I'd measured them out to his satisfaction, he opened his drawer and removed a stone and one of the precious sheets of bleached bark. I'd never actually seen paper, and longed to touch it, but did not. Even at home paper was rarely used.\par
\par
     He began to write the details that would make us an official House in Het Narel. It occurred to me then what a risk I was taking. To give our names to this Jokkad would be to expose us to anyone who cared to come looking; House stones were a matter of public record. But when I looked at Dlane, she had a resolute set to her jaw, and her gaze on me approved.\par
\par
     "What are your person-names?" the emodo asked.\par
\par
     "I am Thenet, and this is Dlane."\par
\par
     "And your birth-genders?"\par
\par
     "Eperu and anadi," I said.\par
\par
     He glanced up at her and said, "From female to neuter to emodo? You have had a tumultuous youth, pu eperu."\par
\par
     "I am glad to have settled on something at last," Dlane said, and the old man laughed.\par
\par
     "And what will be the name of your House?"\par
\par
     My mouth froze open. Of course the House needed a name, but I had been Mated so long I couldn't conceive of being aught else\'85.\par
\par
     "Re\'f1a," Dlane said without hesitation.\par
\par
     The emodo nodded as he continued penning the writ, the sharp aroma of ground nuts rising from his wet ink. I glanced at Dlane, who smiled at me.\par
\par
     "Thenet Re\'f1a-eperu, Dlane Re\'f1a-anadi, I will need your signatures here, and here."\par
\par
     He handed me the reed pen first, and I squinted at the document. I could not understand half the words he'd written, but 'House', 'new', and our names I did. On the space he'd tapped, I carefully wrote out my name and then signed my personal symbol beneath it: a circle with five claw marks marring one side. The circle for the completed circle that the eperu is, requiring neither emodo nor anadi to be complete, and the claw marks for what I had been: guardian to anadi, ever vigilant. I handed the reed to Dlane and stared at the mark, realizing suddenly that I had become it again: eperu to House Re\'f1a, guardian of my most precious ke anadi.\par
\par
     Dlane wrote her name with more confidence and grace that I had mine, and her symbol beneath it perplexed me: a set of three curving lines sprouting from a single point, and above it a flattened oval with a line through it.\par
\par
     The emodo had withdrawn from one of his drawers a corked pot almost as large as his fist while we\rquote d signed our names. This he opened, and from it came a hard, sharp odor, strong as the smell of burning oil. Dipping a brush in this, he painted our stone with a mark, like a combination of our signatures. My circle with the five marks, encased in the three curving lines like grasses. The center line of the grasses rose straight through the circle. This he also painted in the same ink on our parchment.\par
\par
     Handing us the stone, the emodo said, "On this day House Re\'f1a put down a stone in the place of People Transactions in Het Narel. It was duly recorded here by Marzed Ketti-eperu. In the future when you build your House through marriages, contracts or employment, please remember to come here to have the transactions witnessed and recorded. Congratulations, ke eperu, pu eperu, and much luck with your House-building."\par
\par
     We thanked him and backed out of the building; only then did I look at the stone, warm and real in my grasp compared to the cool breeze that ruffled my wrist fluff, tickled my fingers. Dlane pressed against me and stared at it.\par
\par
     "Did we just do that?" she asked.\par
\par
     "Declare our own House in a town months from our birthplace?" I closed my fingers over the rock, measured its weight and flicked my ears forward. Grinning, I said, "I am reasonably sure we didn't imagine it."\par
\par
     "But\'85 a House!" Dlane wrinkled her nose. "I am not displeased," she said slowly, "Though I still believe I should take the journey."\par
\par
     "We will go to the Birthwell," I said, for the first time feeling it in my heart. "Just not now. We need to wait for the proper time\'85 isn't that what you told me?"\par
\par
     "Told you and taught you, apparently!" Dlane exclaimed. She grinned and hugged me tightly, rose-spice sweat in my nostrils. I looped an arm around her back and laughed.\par
\par
     "We should get out of the middle of the road, I think."\par
\par
     "And now what?" Dlane said. "There are so many things to do! I've never run a House before. Even a House of two people!"\par
\par
     "Mmph. There are two things we should do." I untied the pouch of shell and counted out five of our remaining thirty-five and handed them to her. "You need to see a clay-keeper and ask him for the House-builder's guide."\par
\par
     "You want me to wander alone?" Her tone was more curious than frightened.\par
\par
     I looked around at the Jokka passing without even glancing our way. I got no sense of menace from them at all; simply polite disinterest. "Right now you look like eperu, and I imagine none will harm you. But if we are to make enough shell to run a House, we will have to be used to being apart." The idea elicited a twitch from my lower spine, but some part of me was also relieved. She wanted so much to be not-anadi, my Dlane, but she would never take that first step unless she began to be self-sufficient. To help her become what she wanted to be, I had to push her out on her own.\par
\par
     Knowing this didn't help with the guilt. Nevertheless, I said firmly, "So you go to the clay-keeper."\par
\par
     "And you?" she asked.\par
\par
     "I will go look for a building for us to live in! Let's meet where we first came into town by sunset. Agreed?"\par
\par
     "Agreed!" She hugged me once more and then trotted into the crowd. I watched her with lowered brows and ears\'85 she had the mien of a child at a festival, but I noted with approval that she also seemed alert, glancing to and fro every other step. Satisfied that there was nothing more I could do, I went in search of a dwelling.\par
\par
     I'd never rented a building before, but a few dim memories from home guided my feet toward the edge of town. I didn't even bother investigating the center of Het Narel: the rent in that district would be more than Dlane and I could afford without a stable of emodo to do craftswork for us. So I jogged back to the fringes of town and began investigating, looking for empty houses. Exploring the first few revealed a plate with a name and signature -- the name of the emodo who owned it, no doubt - and a set of two numbers. I guessed those were the amount needed immediately, and the amount per month in rent.\par
\par
     We were not, I could see, going to be able to afford even a small house.\par
\par
     I sat on a bin beside one of these empty buildings and let loose a sigh. Even the meanest of the available buildings required at least fifty shell up front and another twenty-five a month. I toyed with the thought of building us a shelter, but doing so would require more work and more learning than I had. We needed to spend our first few months settling in, building a client base with whatever skills we decided to sell.\par
\par
     "Why so dark a face, ke eperu?"\par
\par
     I glanced up to find an emodo leaning against the fence. He was older than I, but not so much so: his skin the color of baked red clay, hair black and lustrous. Brown eyes studied me, curious and lively, shades lighter than the near-black spirals across his body. He wore a black long-cloth patterned in red, beige and silver over brown trews, and his brightly polished thumb claw shone in the aging sunlight.\par
\par
     "I am afraid I am not heartened by the cost of housing here," I said, lifting the stone so he could see it.\par
\par
     "Oh! I see. A new steader! Where is the rest of your House, ke eperu?"\par
\par
     "It is getting the House guides from the clay-keeper," I said, a rueful smile tugging at my face.\par
\par
     "It? You make it sound as if there are only two of you," he said with a grin.\par
\par
     "That would be because there are only two of us," I said.\par
\par
     The Jokkad canted his head, one ear tilted outward half in jest. "You are serious, ke eperu? It's no wonder you're disheartened over the cost of housing."\par
\par
     I chuckled. "I am as serious as I look dark, I'm afraid. It's just the two of us and this," I held up my little bag and shook it so that it clacked softly, "bag of money."\par
\par
     He snorted. "Doesn't look like much, ke eperu. But maybe you just need new eyes."\par
\par
     "New eyes?" I asked, wondering if everyone I met was going to talk like Dlane.\par
\par
     "Come on," he said, and having little else to do, I pushed myself off the bin and joined him.\par
\par
     In the wake of the jocular emodo I walked around the perimeter of the city until we reached the northwestern edge. There were few people here, and I was startled to pass several large rocks. I let a hand drift over one as he walked on.\par
\par
     "Surprised?" he said with a grin.\par
\par
     I nodded.\par
\par
     "We're not so far from the mountains as we might seem," the emodo said. "Perhaps a month\rquote s journey to their base."\par
\par
     "So close?" I glanced at the horizon. I'd been traveling so long, staring at them, that I'd lost perspective on how much ground we'd covered. Maybe they did seem closer.\par
\par
     "Truly," he said. "I wouldn't lie to you, ke eperu."\par
\par
     "And why is that?" I asked archly.\par
\par
     He ducked around another of the stones, this one nearly waist-high. "Because I like you. You look honest."\par
\par
     "Do I," I murmured.\par
\par
     "Besides, you're wearing the medallion of the Trifold."\par
\par
     My hand stole to the carved metal. I'd almost forgotten the necklace, since I'd never taken it off. I opened my mouth to say I did not follow the Trifold, but what use was it? Trinity or Trifold, I had stepped outside the circle of any named religion the night I'd worn an emodo's hands.\par
\par
     "Here we are," he said, stopping.\par
\par
     I came to a halt beside him and blinked a few times to clear my eyes. He'd brought me to a little building, two stories high and made of the ubiquitous baked clay stained a mellow yellow-gray. It had a few windows on the inside, and a balcony that could have supported maybe two people, little more than an overgrown ledge.\par
\par
     He stepped onto the raised ledge while I rubbed my hands over the nearby stones; three on one side of the front of the house and two on the other, like teeth guarding a throat.\par
\par
     I hastened to follow him inside and found what I'd expected: it was one large room on the bottom with a firepit against the eastern side, and a circular ramp led to the second floor, another large room.\par
\par
     "It even has a water closet," the emodo said. "First floor only, but the waste falls in a pit so you can collect it for compost. And here," he stopped beside the firepit and opened a small door above it, "Is an oven for slow-cooking anything you want to preserve. This lever will control how much of the updraft from the flue it receives."\par
\par
     I wandered after him as in a dream, stopping only at the back door. It opened on a view of the mountains to the north that Dlane would adore.\par
\par
     "This is your property, isn't it," I said.\par
\par
     "It is indeed. And you can have it for twenty-five shell now and an additional seven a month."\par
\par
     Shocked, I said, "Why so cheap?"\par
\par
     The emodo started laughing. "Careful, ke eperu! Such unguarded honesty would tempt a lesser Jokkad to raise the price. As to why so cheap\'85." He walked to the front door and opened it. The town was a smudged blur. "That," he said, "Is a good ten minute walk. And I haven't met a House or a business yet that wants an abode quite so far from the middle of things. But you..," he grinned, "You, I think, will like it here. Am I right?"\par
\par
     I stared at Het Narel. Ten minutes wasn't so far, really. And it would keep people from seeing us too often. "Ke emodo, if good salespersons are made by being able to read a customer, then you must be one of the best."\par
\par
     "I like to think so," he said. "So, are we agreed?"\par
\par
     "Absolutely, ke emodo."\par
\par
     "I'd be pleased to have you use my given name, ke eperu. I am Barit Dzeri-eperu."\par
\par
     "Thenet Re\'f1a-eperu," I said, clasping his hand and shoulder as eperu do. "Let's go make this legal, landlord!"\par
\par
     And so we did.\par
\par
     That evening I escorted Dlane to our new home.\par
\par
     "We are walking far from the town," she said to me.\par
\par
     "Yes," I said, relieved to smell and feel her, familiar as my own skin, more familiar even.\par
\par
     And then, "Oh\'85!"\par
\par
     I grinned.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     "The clay-keeper offered to take me as an apprentice," Dlane said to me the next morning. She'd dropped to sleep immediately on getting home and I too had dozed more heavily than was my wont\'85 but only after examining the precious sheet of waxed bark that were our House guides, instructions I could barely read on how to conduct transactions legally in Het Narel.\par
\par
     Sitting on the threshold of the back door, gazing out to the mountains, the anadi said, "He\rquote ll pay ten shell a month, and he'll give me lunch each work-day. Was that a good price? Should I have said no?"\par
\par
     "It will pay for rent," I said, unfolding our clothing. We had no furniture for the items Ilushet had so kindly sent away with us, but spreading them out made the house look a little more lived-in. "And a few shell more. Truth told, ke anadi, I think he may be overpaying you. But we shouldn't complain!"\par
\par
     She grinned. "I'm so glad you approve! I was frightened you wouldn't\'85."\par
\par
     "Why would I do a thing like that?"\par
\par
     "Because anadi don't work."\par
\par
     I stopped. Forcing my hands to resume the mechanical motions of folding proved more difficult than I anticipated, but I managed. Tuck. Tuck. Straighten. Had her way of thinking proved so insidious, that it had finally crept fully into my mind?\par
\par
     "What I want to know," I found myself saying, "Is why he hired you, since indeed anadi don't work."\par
\par
     "I convinced him that I was still mostly eperu. The clothes and chest-binding worked\'85 the rest of it was out of pity, I think." Dlane rolled her lip between her teeth and worried at her long-cloth. "As long as I keep looking mostly eperu, I don't think he'll mind paying me. And I'll get to learn!"\par
\par
     With a sigh, I turned from the clothing. "Fine. But when summer comes again we'll have to rethink this plan. I don't like the idea of you wandering in the heat where you can faint."\par
\par
     "Are we staying that long?" she asked.\par
\par
     "How am I supposed to know?" I said, exasperated - fondly so, but still. Picking up a brush I sat behind her and gently combed out her messy golden braid with my fingers before applying the brush to it. "I'm still trying to figure out how I'm going to make us any money."\par
\par
     "You could carve," Dlane said.\par
\par
     My brow wrinkled. "I don't think I'm that good yet."\par
\par
     "Maybe you'd be surprised."\par
\par
     I refused to answer that. "When do you start your work?"\par
\par
     Dlane grinned. "In an hour!"\par
\par
     Startled, I helped her brush out her hair and rebraid both mane and tail. After wrapping her all-too-anadi breasts, she donned a fresh set of clothing and was on her way back to town, leaving me to lean on the post of the front door, dizzied. I hadn't let her out of earshot for months and within one sunset the world had gone topsy-turvy - again!\par
\par
     Which left me with our much reduced bag of shell, a lack of food, and no purpose. What did a guardian eperu do without an anadi to guard? I stared at the brittle eggshell sky of the autumn dawn, tempted to lapse into melancholy.\par
\par
     House Re\'f1a was poor. The cupboards of House Re\'f1a were empty. It was time for this lone eperu to go shopping.\par
\par
     I returned some time later with a bag full of simple foodstuffs and some seeds. We lived on the outskirts of town; there was no reason we couldn't grow at least part of our own food and save the expense. Unless that reason was that neither of Re\'f1a's members knew how to grow so much as a flower.\par
\par
     I walked out of the back door and onto our vast lawn, stopping to stare at the mountains. It was quiet\'85 too quiet. I missed the sound of her breathing as she slept, the sound of her laughter when she woke. Shaking my head, I sighed and jogged out further onto the plain. Choosing a place beside a thin ribbon of a stream, I settled down to dig, my bag of seeds crumpled beside me. The cold nagged at me, but I ignored the faint warning it insisted on whispering. I had other problems.\par
\par
I had no idea how far apart to space the seeds. Or how large they would grow. Or how much water they needed. Frustrated, I leaned back on my heels and wiped sweat off my brow, reflecting that it was strange how one never realized how much of life depended on the collected knowledge of other people\'85 until forced to rebuild all that knowledge alone. Perhaps I should be the one going to the clay-keeper.\par
\par
     Leaning forward, I clawed a pebble from the tumbled soil. That couldn't be good for the seeds. I resumed my work.\par
\par
     I planted in tidy rows, and by the end found myself almost on top of the stream, my hands wet with mud. It clung to my arms and stuck in my wrist-fluff, drying in hard nodules. Frowning, I sat beside the stream and stuck my hand into one of the holes I'd made, clawing at a hard lump on the bottom\'85 another rock to foul my poor seeds.\par
\par
     I dug it up and held it in my hands. There was no sign of the rock beneath the layers of dirt, so I scraped at it.\par
\par
     And scraped. And scraped.\par
\par
     There was no rock. It was just a big, hard hunk of clay. Was it all the way under my plot? How could the tender new plants dig through this? I kneaded it while my mind worked furiously, trying to decide whether to claw up all the seeds and ruin the afternoon's work or to take my chances.\par
\par
     It was only when I'd stolen a moment to breathe between my ruminations that I realized that kneading the clay was fun. I turned it in my hands: a reddish-brownish-grayish blob, with a consistency altogether pleasurable to my fingers. I twisted around and stared at the stream\'85 more of the same colors were buried in the exposed soil.\par
\par
     Clay.\par
\par
     The bag of seed I left behind, but the lump I took with me back into town. The last of our shell I spent on a potter's wheel.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     My first pot exploded in the house's tiny oven, and that after days of trying to figure out how to make the thing stay on the wheel.\par
\par
     So did the second pot.\par
\par
     The third pot only cracked.\par
\par
     Dlane viewed my experiments with obvious bemusement, lolling on the second floor and staring down into the first through the ramp cut-out. She avoided the heat of the firepit, as well she should, and kept her own council.\par
\par
     She was wise, my anadi. I was beyond speaking. The clay had seized my tongue and my mind, and even when I slept my fingers twisted and curled, shaping the doze-dreams that overlaid reality like glaze on the pot I wondered if I'd ever make.\par
\par
     My failures multiplied, each one slightly different from the next. The clay cracked in the fire - it exploded. Holes with spiderweb weaknesses appeared in some. Others never even made it to the oven\'85 they came out disfigured, lips gaping as if broken-jawed, misshapen bellies, twisted necks. I despaired of ever creating anything.\par
\par
     I'm not sure if it was need for shell that drove me; it certainly never entered my mind, though pottery was a lucrative business in any settlement. Perhaps it was the need to make something: perhaps the same eperu who had taken on an emodo's hands now wanted to borrow an anadi's womb.\par
\par
     Maybe I was abnormal.\par
\par
     The third week of our stay in Het Narel, I was again home alone, my anadi away to learn the paths of clay and letters. The single-minded intensity I'd been devoting to my efforts remained, but with it finally came a certain patience. I kneaded the blue-brown clay, gently working each of the pebbles and twigs from its body. When I tossed it to the wheel it stuck in the center. I closed my hands and felt it as I spun the wheel, and its body seemed to breathe.\par
\par
     I caressed it, felt the walls shiver as they grew. Shaped and coaxed and trimmed. It was not so different, perhaps, than it had been with Dlane.\par
\par
     While the pot dried, I went to survey my garden. It occurred to me then as the breeze ruffled my tangled mane that perhaps planting just before the onset of winter had not been the most intelligent of my actions.\par
\par
     The next day, in silence, I trimmed the bottom of the pot and put it in the oven. I went for a long walk afterwards, meandering through the fields. I followed the stream for several hours with the cool sunlight on my back. There was little but fields and hills for miles, but the scent of the brown grass and the water cleared my nose of fire and clay. I breathed deep, and remembered where I was and who I was.\par
\par
     Thenet. Thenet Re\'f1a-eperu. Guardian to anadi, bringer of pleasure, carver of bones and hunter of animals, confused and elated and simply alive.\par
\par
     I washed in the stream before making my way back home, chilled but laughing.\par
\par
     That night when I pulled the fragile pot from the oven it had no crack or flaw. Its slender neck rose straight from an elegantly bowed belly, and its lip pouted like the mouth of a lovely anadi.\par
\par
     Looking over my shoulder, Dlane said, "It's beautiful."\par
\par
     I turned and saw her for the first time in three weeks. She was radiant, her golden eyes dancing, resplendent in the patterned clothing of a neuter. I touched my hand to her hair, to her cheek, then crushed her close. The spiced sweetness of her scent tangled in my nostrils with the smell of hot coals, the dried herbs I used to perfume the fire.\par
\par
     That night I borrowed an emodo's hands to give Dlane a gentle pleasure before she slept. It did not seem so odd to me, who had so lately worn an anadi's womb to birth a pot from the reluctant clay of the earth.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     That first pot I put on a new shelf I built for our house with a plank of wood bought by the sale of the next. Another shelf joined it; then a table and chairs. The doors and windows were soon decorated with curtains of deep wine-red, and even later extravagant wooden doors. We bought rugs in wine-red and gold and purple, draped them on the floors and walls. Dlane's salary paid our rent and some of our food, but it was mine that made a home of our house, and a House of our home.\par
\par
     Re\'f1a, said the townsfolk, makes good pottery.\par
\par
     I built another, roomier kiln outside for my larger pieces, and the stream bank soon showed the signs of my labors. The year turned colder, and Dlane slept snugly in a blanket. I watched her carefully for signs of discomfort from the heat but like the eperu she pretended to be she found no fever in the sheets.\par
\par
Through the winter I worked. In a way it was almost unfair: it was the emodo who ran the businesses of the Jokka, the emodo who made the pots and cloth and tracked our histories. But being eperu, I did not need to sleep as the emodo did, and my pottery wheel kept me occupied well into the coldest hours. The fire in the kiln outside rarely slept; like me, it only dozed, its coals faint and crimson-cool. No emodo could have made the amount of shell I made in the same time, given that handicap\'85 but Re\'f1a needed the money, and so we were lucky.\par
\par
     For a while, life was far more normal than I'd had the chance to experience since being thrown from Mated. As winter cast its bitter winds and lifted the blanket of warmth from the plains, I found a serene center from which to live my life in that little house.\par
\par
     Dlane skittered inside and said, "Quick, quick, look out!"\par
\par
     I glanced over my shoulder. "Ke anadi?"\par
\par
     "Oh, quick, you'll miss her!"\par
\par
     Puzzled, I joined her at the window and glanced past the wine-red curtain. A flicker of silver? Like ice. "What was that?"\par
\par
     "Who, you mean. She's been following me home for the past three days now," Dlane said with a giggle. "She honestly believes I don't hear her behind me, I think. I've never been able to catch more than a glimpse of her, though\'85 I'm afraid if I confront her she'll run away."\par
\par
     "An anadi? Following you? Why?" I squinted out into the gathering dusk. It was unusual for anadi to be about, particularly alone, but winter was the best time for them to wander if they were going to do so.\par
\par
     "I don't know," Dlane said. "I'm hoping she'll be brave enough to approach me before my curiosity demands scratching!"\par
\par
     I looked up at the sky, then back after the glint of silver. "Maybe I'll follow her."\par
\par
     Dlane straightened. "Oh, Thenet\'85 can you? It's getting dark already. And it's cold!"\par
\par
     A grin pulled my lips apart. "I have been in the house all day with the heat. It'll be good to feel the breath of the World on my back for a little. Besides, I can see in the dark."\par
\par
     She threw her arms around my bare middle and squeezed, pressing her cheek against my chest. "Be careful. We live far from the lights of town."\par
\par
     I let my chin rest on her head for a few moments, inhaling the essence of her, the honeyed wash I gave her hair every tenth day. She was everything that was anadi, and all that was Dlane as well. I hugged back, then slid from her embrace and onto the trail that led back to town.\par
\par
     My fingers touched the tracks first. The ground had hardened in the cold, but there were signs for a hunter to see and taste: scattered pebbles, and when I lifted my head the taste-smell of warmth left by a body, and the faintest hint of a perfume, lilacs. Potter's fingers, emodo's hands, eperu senses. I came alive in the darkness and stole after the trace of the anadi. She'd taken a curving path, approaching Het Narel from its edge. I'd been traveling only ten minutes when I spotted the hint of silver yet again.\par
\par
     I froze and watched - indeed, there she was, stumbling along as if she could barely see the trail she walked. I drew closer and, once I judged I'd come close enough, kicked over a rock.\par
\par
     She flung herself around, waves of long hair spinning from her thin shoulders. Anadi, oh yes. And even in the dark I could see her skin was what mine could have been: a lustrous white holding a thousand layers, as if her skin was only the topmost glaze on a slim pot of exquisite workmanship. The flash of silver I'd seen had been the reflection of her hair on her skin, masses of soft silvery tresses, straight for most of their length before ending in large curls.\par
\par
     Her mouth formed the rounded shape of a gasp. I straightened.\par
\par
     "I have a few questions to ask of you, ba anadi," I said, for she seemed young.\par
\par
     "I'm not a child!" she said, her voice a soft soprano only a few notes darker than Dlane's.\par
\par
     "Only a child would be out and about so," I said, "Or following Jokka when they should be at home by their reflecting pools."\par
\par
     "So why does she get to dress like an eperu and go to work?"\par
\par
     My mouth worked, and what finally came out was, "She was very lately eperu-"\par
\par
     "Oh, and I was very lately a rock. That Jokkad was born anadi and is still anadi, and she can tell that story to the clay-keeper until the sky bleeds white but she doesn't fool me!"\par
\par
     I did the only thing I could to such a blatant threat to our cover\'85 I grabbed her by the waist, slung her over my shoulder and started back home. She scrabbled against me, but her struggles seemed only inspired to acquire a more comfortable position. Then she lay still, as if this had been her plan and not mine. Maybe it was\'85 I wouldn't be surprised to discover that every anadi who hadn't yet lost her mind had a scheming one.\par
\par
     So it was that I opened the door to our dwelling with our unlikely spy on my shoulder. "Ke anadi\'85."\par
\par
     Dlane's head popped out from the ramp cut-out and she blinked a few times. She laughed. "Oh, you didn't have to bring her back, Thenet!"\par
\par
     "I most certainly did," I said, pulling her off my shoulder and settling her firmly in a chair. "She knows a little too much for my comfort."\par
\par
     In the firepit's light the girl's skin soaked up the reds and yellows and set them glittering in the creases of her body. She had large, pale violet eyes to go with her silver and white coloring, and these eyes she trained on us as if eating through them, and she the victim of a famine as long as her life.\par
\par
     Dlane padded down the ramp and plucked the teapot off the hook in the firepit. With a set of cups I'd made she set out places for us, and sat. And she met the girl's eyes.\par
\par
     Two of a kind, I thought with mild exasperation and sipped the spiced tea.\par
\par
     "And you are?" Dlane asked.\par
\par
     "Linuthet," she said. "Linuthet Kathara-anadi."\par
\par
     Dlane glanced at her navel, then back at her face. "You haven't had your second Turning yet, have you."\par
\par
     "How did you know?"\par
\par
     "Because if you were fully anadi, you would have had a ring by now. You are too pretty." Dlane smiled sadly. "I would know."\par
\par
     "But you're here. And you're working. You wear clothing. You\'85 hide your breasts." Linuthet's ears flattened. "You won't be bought for some other House's harness, will you?"\par
\par
     "Dlane," I said, trying to curtail this episode before it got out of hand. Bad enough that the girl knew of Dlane's subterfuge, but for her to know of Dlane's unorthodox views as well might be tempting the Void too much.\par
\par
     "No," Dlane said. "I will never be sold. Or bred. This is my House, and in my House no anadi is sold who does not want to go."\par
\par
     "I can't imagine\'85," she trailed off.\par
\par
     "Dlane," I said.\par
\par
     "You don't have to imagine," Dlane said. "It's real."\par
\par
     "Dlane!" That at last caught her attention. "Perhaps we should talk a moment over there," I jerked a thumb at the back door.\par
\par
     She stood and joined me at the back while Linuthet played with her cup nervously.\par
\par
     "We can't go saving every anadi in the street," I whispered to her, ears flat to my head. "We are not a temple."\par
\par
     "No," Dlane said. "We're a House. And Houses need anadi."\par
\par
     "You're not thinking seriously of engaging her!" I exclaimed, perhaps a little too loudly. I hissed and lowered my voice. "Do you want to go buying every anadi you can save from the harnesses? You want to build a House of unmated females with one eperu for guard?"\par
\par
     One of Dlane's golden brows lifted, and her eyes were steady on mine. My soul sank.\par
\par
     "You are considering it, aren't you," I said.\par
\par
     "This is what I wanted when I let you lead me to the place of Transactions," Dlane said, passion stirring in her voice. "To build a place where no female need fear for the loss of her mind. That is why we're saving money. That is why we have a House."\par
\par
     "Dlane! If people notice what we're doing we'll be run out of Het Narel! And if we get enough anadi and keep them idle, trust me, people will notice!"\par
\par
     "We'll worry about that when it happens, then," Dlane said and walked back to the table. I stared at the two of them: shining anadi, lovely and ripe and\'85 unrealized. Women without children. What was I thinking, all this time? These months in the company of an unnatural female had made me unnatural as well. To find normal the sight of them so earnestly talking was not normal at all.\par
\par
     I opened the tall, thin cabinet beside the door and extracted a few items of clothing. Pinning a sash across my breast and a winter long-cloth to my hips, I said curtly, "I'm going out."\par
\par
     "Okay," Dlane said, and returned to her conversation with Linuthet. That was it - just 'okay'. My anadi had no eyes for me. She had a soul to save.\par
\par
     Well, so did I.\par
\par
     The door bounced behind me as I strode from the house and down the trail leading to town, my muscles so tight I barely felt the touch of the wind. I couldn\rquote t identify the origins of my anger - perhaps it had come twinned with a premonition of the kind of danger Dlane invited with her course. To send herself into peril alone by flouting the ways of our kind, that I could only barely countenance: she was anadi, to be protected and calmed. But to take with her a House full of fledgling philosophers? All their ideals would shatter into bone shards and screams when the mob came from Het Narel to put order to the chaos of a House that would deem itself above the cycle of childbirth and marriage. A Houseful of anadi with no emodo in sight! And their lone eperu would die first, inadequate to the task of guarding them all.\par
\par
     My hand stole to the medallion at my breast and with a growl I tugged it off. My body twisted with a violence that was born of my heart and I flung it - and the night seized its breath to watch. In the Moment, I felt the pendant leave my fingertips, the cool disc brushing my skin. The scratchy leather cord followed, stinging. It flashed a dull, milk-pale spark as it twisted.\par
\par
     Gods damn the Trifold! I could worship nothing that gave us these powers of choice, to choose what could damn us for the hubris of preserving our minds despite the cost.\par
\par
     But she had given me that medallion.\par
\par
     It was the Moment, and as the pendant glittered dully just beyond my arm and the leather burst free of my tingling fingers, my other hand leaped from my side and caught the cords.\par
\par
     The World breathed again, and the Trifold pendant was in my hand. With a sigh I tucked it into a pouch on the side of my long-cloths and continued walking.\par
\par
It was no surprise to me that I ended up in the Temple quarter. Here all the religions that wanted an edifice could put up Houses to their gods or their ideals, whichever mattered more to them. The solemnity of the quarter at night suited my dark mood. I walked in and out of the yellow light of lanterns, savoring the layered differences between the amber cast of the illumination against the cooler, bluer light of the stars and moon.\par
\par
Barely a soul breathed among the clay columns of the temples, and it was not the stillness of the wild, where no Jokka stirred, but the stillness of the silenced. Of the willfully mute. Of the obedient and temperate and dutiful soul. I approved.\par
\par
     The temple of the Trinity was marked by the sun, star and world of the gods. Long strips of black and purple shadow cast by the facade's columns dappled the stairs leading to the doors. Straightening my clothes, I ascended and passed into the sanctuary.\par
\par
     The temple enveloped me. It was dark, but the cool, blue dark of stars, not the suffocating dark of a womb. It was silent, not the open silence of the unbounded wilderness but the silence of an enclosed space undisturbed, a receptive silence sharp with the cold, hard stone of the floor. And the incense! Drifting in barely visible arabesques, the incense suffused each breath with a subtle palette of spices and sweet herbs, fire and resins so delicately mixed I stopped where I stood to simply bathe in the taste-smells.\par
\par
     My coming interrupted the air currents, and I could feel them stirring the delicate hairs on the insides of my ears: talking of me. Each footfall on the hard stone sent a spike of cold up my leg, and the scrape of my claws and hiss of my foot-hair changed the quality of the silence irrevocably. I had intruded into the sanctity of the place, and it had accepted me\'85 but barely.\par
\par
     Amid columns of quarried stone shone to mirror brightness I lost myself, accompanied only by my black reflection on the floor. The starlight was transformed by the paneled glass of the windows, to blues and whites and grays and a lilac so subtle it wavered between gray and colored. In the patterns of light cast on the floor I could see the incense smoke as shadow, twirling in a sinuous, endless dance.\par
\par
     I sat then, on the circle of light. The smoke fell over my shoulders, tickling my neck and cheek: asymmetrical spirals of spice to complement the asymmetrical spirals of skin that twined over my collar bones. My breathing slowed, opened. With my hands resting on my knees, I pulled in sweet air and released my troubles in long plumes through my nose.\par
\par
     My life was in the hands of the gods. It always had been. I hadn't had much chance lately to consider my afterlife in the ranks of the World's hunters, and when I tried to imagine it I failed. Too many things had yoked me to this life for me to reach for the next. But the presence of the Brightness, the Void, the World in my life\'85 that I needed, reached for and found in the cool, clear, perfumed dark.\par
\par
     "Sibling," a quiet voice said: not soprano nor bass, and not measurable in between. It could have belonged to any of the genders. "Welcome to the temple of the Trinity."\par
\par
     I looked up. A hooded figure had come to the crisp edge of the window's cast light. It had brought the darkness forward, robbed it of some of its haunting depth. The long folds of its robe rose from the black ink of the stone floor, but the nearest edges of the cloth were not so lush a darkness. Nor could I really see the color of my visitor's eyes, only that they were wet, touched with a highlight the color of stars.\par
\par
     "Thank you," I said, and my voice was hoarse and small. It did not echo across the sharp floor with the stranger's clothing to muffle the breadth of the silence's receptivity.\par
\par
     "Do you need anything, Sibling?"\par
\par
     I opened my mouth. My hands clasped tighter on my knees as my thoughts tangled on the shape of an anadi, the shape of my heart. "What I need is here," I said at last. "In the Trinity."\par
\par
     "It is the Void's time, Sibling," the voice said from beneath the hood. "Be careful what you ask when He is ascendant. He is cold as the light of stars, as the marble in winter."\par
\par
     My chest was full of the spiced incense. "The World is always here."\par
\par
     "But what is the World at night? Every god has his time."\par
\par
     The sensual shadow of the incense crawled across my lap, regal as Dlane curving beneath my hands. And the emodo Void was all around me. I had brought the World into the temple, riding my soul. But how much of me was the World when I was a maker of things and a bringer of anadi pleasure?\par
\par
     I shivered. "Leave me," I said, my voice a low, trembling thread.\par
\par
     "As you wish, Sibling."\par
\par
     And the silence spread open again as the stranger left. I bowed my head and let the incense caress my cheek and neck. Something inside me grew cold.\par
\par
     When I uncurled from the floor my joints creaked with the chill of the stone. I dragged my hand across its polished surface as I stood. Such expense for the temple\'85 quarried and dressed marble to remind the innocent of the power of the gods. I thought of Dlane and myself, and the Void without His vessel and shivered, hastening outside.\par
\par
     And into total darkness. Time had slipped from me and ushered in the truedark while I sought my center and the natural order of things, the same natural order of things that said Jokka do not travel when they cannot see. I was paralyzed, standing on the steps of the temple, blinded and seized by a frigid wind. The world had vanished. I held up a hand and could not see my fingers, and the acrid taste of panic flooded my tongue and teeth.\par
\par
     A slip of lank hair had escaped my mane's braid. Its touch had the tenderness of a caress, and I twitched. Skin tracked its soft trail across my shoulder and onto my collar bone. Sensations returned, not with the force of a punch but gentle, subtle, one by one. The panels of my winter long-cloth brushing over my trembling legs. The wind's cold fingers on my chest and belly. The swing of my braids\'85 the liquid wet of my eyes as I blinked. The world smelled open, full of the subtle herbal tang of grass and the sharper bite of ice. And my ears could twitch to follow the path of the wind through the unfettered streets.\par
\par
     It was the sound I followed from the temple quarter, through the blinding darkness and out of town.\par
\par
     I did not question that I should travel through truedark. What good was there in rhetorical questions? I had done it before. I was cold and tired, and small, small, small. I wanted to go home, so I did. As before, every sense I had came alive and guided me, though I couldn't quite close my eyes.\par
\par
     When I pushed open the door, the fire blew away the veil from the world. Dlane stood on the ramp, a lantern in her hand.\par
\par
     "Thenet," she said, a smile growing on her face. She held out a hand.\par
\par
     I came into her arms and wrapped mine around her slim waist. "What are you doing awake?"\par
\par
     "I couldn't sleep, knowing you were out there," she said. I could smell the heaviness of her fatigue on her breath, see it in the slow droop of her eyelids and the weighted grace of her movements. I took the lantern from her.\par
\par
     "You should sleep," I said. I glanced around. "Where's the female?"\par
\par
     "I sent her home. Tomorrow we will go to Transactions and buy her from Kathara."\par
\par
     I sighed. "Can we afford an anadi so soon?"\par
\par
     Dlane's tail flicked. "We must, dear Thenet. We are too small." I wondered if she thought us too small because of our solvency or if she was thinking of all the anadi she had yet to save. "Besides, Linuthet is not past her Second Turning. Kathara will sell her cheaply since she's not a known quantity."\par
\par
     "How do you know they'll sell her at all?" I asked.\par
\par
     "Oh, they will," Dlane said, "If we sign the clause that says we won't fine them if she Turns some other sex."\par
\par
     "What if she becomes eperu?"\par
\par
     Dlane chuckled. "That's fine. Another eperu can always be of use to you."\par
\par
     "And if she becomes emodo?" I asked archly.\par
\par
     She frowned, but shrugged after a pause. "Emodo too have their place."\par
\par
     "Do they," I said. I shook myself and guided her back up the ramp. "Dlane\'85."\par
\par
     "You're frightened," she said, and somehow she didn't seem as tired as she had. "I know, setasha."\par
\par
     A shiver rolled down my spine. "Don't call me that," I said softly, the chill of the temple still in my marrow.\par
\par
     "You love me, don't you?" she said, cupping my cheeks in her delicate hands. Her eyes on mine were more painfully bright than the heart of the lantern.\par
\par
     "I don't know what love is, save breeders' fancies," I said, my voice lowering to a husk. "I don't know what I am."\par
\par
     "You are eperu, my Thenet," Dlane said. She looked not at all disturbed by my protestations. "You will come to peace with that soon." One of her fingers drifted down to the meeting point of my collar bones, there to linger on the hollow. Devoid, I remembered suddenly, of the medallion she'd given me. My ears flushed white and I looked down.\par
\par
     "Oh, setasha," Dlane said, a sad smile on her face. "You went looking for your gods, didn't you?"\par
\par
     "And if I did?" I lifted my face to look at her, the rasp in my voice growing more marked as the tone dropped out from beneath the words. They were my gods, not hers. My soul to save, not hers to change.\par
\par
     "Did you find what you were seeking?" Gentler than I'd expected, given her opinion of religion. Perhaps it was this unexpected gentleness that prompted my candor.\par
\par
     "I don't know."\par
\par
     She sighed, and her hand splayed against my cheek, warm fingers. "My poor love. Silence where you needed music. I'm sorry."\par
\par
     "For what?" I asked, unnerved by her nearness, by the heady scent of her, the warmth of her body.\par
\par
     "For everything I've done to you," she said, and then her lips touched mine.\par
\par
     The kiss of a Jokkad is a thing of danger and invitation. Fangs that weep poison are too sharp and perilous for soft lips. I'd never witnessed an actual kiss across mouths, for they were to be shared between breeders - this heat, this languorous lapping, this tasting of self is not for the eperu. But when she licked my teeth with the tip of her tongue, I could only let my jaw hang open so she could continue, and then she drank the water of my mouth from the cup of my tongue.\par
\par
     My limbs receded from my body. My world narrowed to the taste of her in my mouth and tingle of fangs itching to answer her.\par
\par
     I don't know how, but I returned it. Braved the poison I'd seen her use and explored her mouth, learned the roof of it, the length of it, the spars of her teeth, the sharp points of her fangs. Sampled the water of her mouth from the depression in her tongue, flavored with the bright bite of the honey her fangs had already proffered\'85 not yet the sweetness of the poison, only the glaze.\par
\par
     When she drew back, a thin liquid thread joined us at the mouth before it grew too heavy and broke between us. The sound and sight of it shattered the heavy, wet heat between us and I gulped in a breath. My body broke into violent shudders.\par
\par
     "Sssh," she said, touching my mouth with her fingers. My lips felt bruised. "Sssh. There's nothing to fear."\par
\par
     "Ke anadi, this isn't right," I whispered.\par
\par
     "What's right? What's wrong? Does it matter? Did it please you?"\par
\par
     "Yes\'85 no\'85 I don't know!"\par
\par
     She hugged me then. I held my arms out. I couldn't figure out what to do with them. To hug her\'85 maybe the scent and feel of her alone would transform the rest of me into an emodo. Could she transform me by the bare power of her touch alone? And yet I knew full well she wanted me as eperu - that if I had been emodo, she would have shunned me. So wrong! How could an anadi desire an eperu with whom she cannot mate?\par
\par
     My teeth itched now for a totally different reason, and all my good intentions about not weeping in front of her dissolved. Amber tears leaked from my mouth and I stifled them as best I could, but she heard. Her ears flicked sideways.\par
\par
     "Thenet, setasha. Lie down with me," she said, pulling me to the nest of blankets and pillows.\par
\par
     I had no resistance to her. I lay down beside her and let her pull me into her arms. She arranged my head against her breasts and rested a leg over my hips - broad as her own, though I'd never thought about it. An emodo's chest, an anadi's hips. Eperu, some unnatural amalgamation of them both. Maybe the eperu were some nightmarish creation, born to be tormented by their place outside the circle of life and birth and mindlessness and death.\par
\par
     I closed my eyes and listened to her breathing, the beat of her heart through her skin, but it did not calm me. She smelled moist and sweet and warm.\par
\par
     "What are you making of me, ke anadi?" I asked.\par
\par
     "I don't know," she answered. "But whatever it is, it is where you want to go. I am only\'85 only showing you."\par
\par
     "And you? You never change." I touched her ribs.\par
\par
     She laughed, a flutter of her chest beneath my cheek. "Oh no, my love. I am changing every day. I am becoming something too."\par
\par
     "I did not ask for this journey," I whispered.\par
\par
     "No one does," she answered, her fingers gently combing my hair back from my neck. "No one asks for a mind, or for the pain of change. But to deny it is to be the dead thing walking that emodo claimed you were."\par
\par
     "Roika," I said with another shudder.\par
\par
     She shivered, but did not stop stroking my mane. The heat of the kiss was dissipating from me, leaving only a drowsy warmth. "Yes. Roika."\par
\par
     "I wonder where he is."\par
\par
     "Not here," she said firmly.\par
\par
     And yet I felt an ache at the reply. I did not want Roika here, but this life Dlane wanted to build, a life of eperu and anadi without emodo, lacked a balance, a center.\par
\par
     "Tomorrow," she said, "We will begin augmenting our House."\par
\par
     "Yes," I said.\par
\par
     She rumbled contentedly and held me close. The rush of her breathing slowed and I listened to her heart as it evened for her dreaming. My tears dried and as the shadows of the room grew and danced to my own on-and-off dozing, I could believe our kiss had been a similar fantasy, a twisted thing just one step to the side of what was real and normal.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     I was trying to etch a design on the lip of one of my pots when Linuthet arrived the next day. The door that closed behind her let in a swathe of the cold and a rectangle of golden morning sunlight. I didn't greet her, too concerned with the motions of the knife: it was easy to pierce the lip in the leather-hard clay and render it flawed.\par
\par
     She did not speak, for which I was thankful. The heavily built fire soon restored the room to its comfortable temperature and the crackle of the flames and the soft scrape of the blade were the only sounds in the room for a while. When I'd completed the design, a set of interlinked spirals, I stood and turned.\par
\par
     I'd forgotten all about the anadi, she'd been so quiet. She was sitting on a tiny stool by the fire, hands on her knees and a bag at her feet.\par
\par
     I sighed. "I'm not going to eat you."\par
\par
     Her head was bowed, but her eyes rose to fix on my face. "No, but you don't like me much, do you."\par
\par
     I flinched. How to explain my ambivalence? "I don't dislike you, Linuthet. I'm just not certain Re\'f1a can afford you yet."\par
\par
     "I'll work for the House!"\par
\par
     "Doing what?" I asked, one brow lifted.\par
\par
     She deflated. "I don't know. I've never been given the chance to work before."\par
\par
     She was a pretty thing, wearing that layered white skin and with all those silver curls. It was painful to see someone so young so dejected. I ran a hand through my hair and said, "Well, we'll find something later. Why don't you go upstairs and put away your things, and then maybe cook something?"\par
\par
     "Okay."\par
\par
     I turned from the firepit after she'd vanished up the ramp and shook my head. Linuthet might be willing to work, but what could she do? The kinds of work available to a fragile anadi were few. That was part of being anadi, gods damn it - they weren't supposed to work. But Re\'f1a had no pool of cool waters for its anadi to bathe in, no flavored ices for them to sip. If we were going to grow large enough for our anadi to live in comfort, if ever they decided they wanted to, then work of some kind we'd have to put Linuthet to.\par
\par
     I walked outside to the front of the house and leaned on one of the teeth framing our porch. The wind ruffled my mane, reminded me that I needed to do a better job of braiding it if I was going to keep the slip out of it.\par
\par
     A familiar red figure was toiling up the path to our House. I waved a hand. "Hail, Barit Dzeri-eperu!"\par
\par
     "Hail the House Re\'f1a!" the emodo called back as he joined me. Our landlord's grin never changed; perhaps because Re\'f1a was his last stop on his monthly route and never failed to pay in full. "It's a brisk winter, don't you think?"\par
\par
     I clapped him on his cloak-covered shoulder. "Brisk enough for a warm drink by the House's fire, I would say."\par
\par
     "As always, your hospitality is quite welcome," Barit answered.\par
\par
     "Come, then."\par
\par
     Inside, I relieved him of his cloak and hung it by the door before retrieving a few of my nicer cups. Linuthet had put tea to brew over the firepit, so I brought the pot with me and a tray of the nut pastries I sometimes brought home from the confectioner's. I liked Barit\'85 he was emodo, but he had been eperu once, and it was good to talk with someone a little more down-to-earth once a month.\par
\par
     "I see you're hard at work, still," Barit said as I prepared the food. "The people speak well of your wares."\par
\par
     "It's shell in the bag," I said. "And I enjoy it."\par
\par
     "I haven't seen you in town to sell in a while."\par
\par
     I grinned and sat across from him. "It's been a long time since I've had to. Most of my work is commissioned now."\par
\par
     "That is the place to be!" he said with a laugh, warming his hands on the walls of the mug as I poured the tea into it. "Though I suppose it renders my proposition unnecessary."\par
\par
     "Proposition?"\par
\par
     Barit stirred the tea, releasing a few spirals of wet white heat. "I have a new property opening next month. A small storefront."\par
\par
     "Oh?"\par
\par
     "In the Green."\par
\par
     "Oh!" I leaned back, trying to keep my eyes from widening. In the center of town, where the gardens of the Green were tended stubbornly against the seasons and the harsh climate, the most affluent of houses and businesses made their homes. "Barit, that's\'85 quite a locale."\par
\par
     "They say location is everything," he said with such innocence that I laughed.\par
\par
     "Do they, now. But what would I sell there?"\par
\par
     "You could take a month off to do some pots to put on the shelves," the emodo said. "Don't tell me that you can't do it, ke eperu. I know you can work most of the night and often do just for the love of it."\par
\par
     My ears dangled asymmetrically. "I don't love it\'85."\par
\par
     He gave me such a look that I spread my hands and laughed again. "Okay. Maybe I do. But even if I did, I couldn't man the shop and make pottery. I would lose too much time."\par
\par
     "Rumor would have it that Re\'f1a has a new member," Barit said coyly.\par
\par
     "Yes. Anadi, and without skills. Do you want me to send her to tend a shop in the Green alone? What would Het Narel think of Re\'f1a then?" I said sharply. A little too sharply, for Dlane's unnatural attitudes had been wearing on me.\par
\par
     Barit looked away, sipped his tea. His ears rotated forward so slowly I thought he was recovering from my outburst, but he was staring at the tea. "What is this? It's fantastic."\par
\par
     I sniffed the tea, then took a sip. It rolled over my tongue, peppery sweet - such a flavor that I swallowed more out of surprise than design. "I\'85 have no idea. Linuthet?"\par
\par
     The anadi appeared on the ramp. "Ke eperu?"\par
\par
     "What is this?" I said, pointing to the tea pot.\par
\par
     "Something I made," she said, blushing white at her ears and cheeks. "Was it bad?"\par
\par
     "Bad!" Barit exclaimed, "I've never had anything like it. It's sublime! What's the aftertaste? Something hot and spicy, almost, but sweet, too."\par
\par
     "It's a combination of things\'85."\par
\par
     I stared at her.\par
\par
     "A combination of things, she says," the emodo said, chortling. "As if anyone could mix them up. Whatever it is, could I have some?"\par
\par
     "I could make you up a packet to take home," Linuthet said, looking at the emodo in confusion.\par
\par
     Barit grinned slyly at me. "Don't let me forget to compensate Re\'f1a for the services of its herbalist."\par
\par
     Herbalist! "Linuthet, do you do this a great deal?"\par
\par
     She shrugged, a motion rolling across her shoulders, down her plumed tail. "I like the taste-smell of things. I always used to play with the herb cabinet at home, make my own hair washes, my drinks, seasoning for food."\par
\par
     I cleared my throat. "If you could get a packet of this tea for Barit to take home, that would be wonderful."\par
\par
     "Okay!" She jogged back upstairs.\par
\par
     Barit was grinning at me over his mug when I turned back to him. "Still not interested in a store in the Green, ke eperu?"\par
\par
     I sighed. "Barit\'85."\par
\par
     "Concoctions make a lot of money. Particularly in the Green, where they like everything to smell unusual and they use perfume daily."\par
\par
     "But I can't send her to sell it!" I said. "I am still the mainstay of this House's income, and I cannot go to town every day to sell perfume to the rich. I have orders to fill."\par
\par
     "You will let her work, though."\par
\par
     I paused.\par
\par
     "As you let the other anadi work."\par
\par
     I'd never tried to hide Dlane's comings and goings from our landlord; he knew the money had to proceed from somewhere. But on the issue of her sex, I'd never said clearly whether she was anadi, or emodo, or emodo-turning anadi or the other way around. Feeling my way forward by the sobriety in his gaze, I finally nodded.\par
\par
     The emodo played with his cup, ears flicking backward. I couldn't read the expression on his face: his mouth and eyes were set, but his brow had lowered, casting brown shadows between his nose. "Last week, as I have been in the past, I was called to service by my House. We have an ongoing contract with Rabeil and I was chosen for this cycle's duty." He rubbed his thumb over the handle of the mug: one of mine, with a silvery purple glaze. "She was not unlovely, and did not resist me. There was nothing in her to resist me. It is an open contract, so we were allowed to exchange names at the end\'85 she could not hold mine in her mouth. She forgot the first sound while speaking the last."\par
\par
     I almost reached out to touch him, but his sorrow was too private. He looked up at me. "We all have duties, ke eperu. That doesn't mean we have to enjoy their results. I am idle most of the month, and need only a handful of days to collect payment from the properties I manage for Dzeri. If you want to rent the storefront in the Green, I would be honored to take an employee contract with Re\'f1a."\par
\par
     Stunned by the admission and the offer, I stared at him blankly.\par
\par
     A white hand clasped my shoulder as Linuthet joined us and laid a fat packet stitched with a bow to hold it closed on the table. "For you, ke emodo," she said. She hesitated. "I don't know your name\'85."\par
\par
     "Linuthet, this is Barit Dzeri-eperu," I said, "Our new employee." I grinned with teeth at him as he sat up. "Let's talk of prices, shall we?"\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     A week later I entered the Green through the delicate bronze gate, my arms full of pots and Linuthet at my heels. She opened it for me: a formality, really, a tiny thing barely waist high and without a lock. You are entering another realm, it said by its blatant lack of utility, watch your step and your manners.\par
\par
     Well, I was watching. Re\'f1a was new enough that we could jeopardize our standing with the older, more affluent Houses by simply being too arrogant. We had a reputation for taste, thanks to the elegance of my work, but reputations are fragile things, subject to the whims of a fickle society.\par
\par
     We walked across the octagon that formed the center of the Green. The gardens had taken on Winter's seeming: thin, spiky trees pruned so their branches formed graceful patterns, their bark scraped so that the color of the skin and the fluctuating hues of the bark, some aged, some fresh, proffered interest to the wandering eye. The expense of maintaining the Green's gardens was shouldered by the Houses and businesses that took advantage of them, and reflected their shell flow with sobering accuracy.\par
\par
"Have you been here before?" I asked Linuthet.\par
\par
     "No," she answered, balancing the jar I'd given her against her hip. It was one of my carrying models: two handles nearly flush to the skin through which one could thread a broad leather strap. She had filled it with her herbal teas, each in a special, soft leather sack to keep the smells from wandering into one another. "These Houses have far more money than Kathara ever did, and probably ever will."\par
\par
     "Luck," I said. "Their anadi bred true and often, and produced fortunate amounts of females and males."\par
\par
     "You call it luck," Linuthet said. "I call it cruelty."\par
\par
     I glanced at her, and she wore obstinance badly over her fear. Unlike Dlane, she had not yet become accustomed to voicing such unspeakable thoughts aloud. She continued. "They breed the anadi like animals until they become them, and then it doesn't matter anymore how often they're pregnant."\par
\par
     Resettling the bowl against my body, I sighed and said, "I know, ba anadi. Say no more about it here, please? The last thing we need is for all Het Narel to hear such craziness. Particularly in the Green."\par
\par
     "It's not crazy, though, is it, ke eperu?" Linuthet asked.\par
\par
     I shrugged. "The wheel must turn. Come on."\par
\par
     She hastened after me, and my long strides put to rest any questions she might have plied me with following my ambivalent answer. I didn't want to discuss it again. Dlane's answer might give dignity to the anadi, but it would eventually destroy our race, and that particular solution to the plight of the anadi was not an acceptable one. Not to me and certainly not to normal Jokka!\par
\par
     My haggling with Barit had taken quite a while, and I saw why when I stopped in front of our new storefront. Not only was it in the Green, it faced the central gardens and sat on the main walkabout of the octagon. The great Houses of Het Narel were on the roads leading from each of the sides of the octagon, but Re\'f1a's new storefront\'85 you could sit outside, were there chairs, and watch the cool winds through the branches. Prime property indeed.\par
\par
     Barit was waiting outside, a black cloak embroidered with burgundy spirals whirling around his solid legs. He held it closed over his chest and complemented it with a somber look. He'd taken his employee contract very seriously indeed\'85 enough to make me think that the incident with the mindless anadi had troubled him even more than he'd allowed me to know with his confession.\par
\par
     "You brought items!" he said by way of greeting. "A lot more than I thought you would."\par
\par
     "You have the wheelbarrow?"\par
\par
     He grinned. "Parked just inside the shop, as promised."\par
\par
     I nodded. "Good. I'll drop these things off and leave you with Linuthet to discuss how to arrange them while I go back for the rest of the wares."\par
\par
     "Ke Linuthet," Barit said, inclining his head.\par
\par
     Linuthet giggled. "I'm not that old yet," she said, then walked past him into the store with her jar bumping her hip. I'd dressed her in pants and an old blouse, uncertain as to how to keep anadi warm without making them too hot. I couldn't just let her walk around in the kind of cold that Het Narel was beginning to serve even during the days. It was far colder here than it had ever been back home in the south.\par
\par
     Barit grinned and shook his head. "Feisty thing, isn't she."\par
\par
     "Yes, well, just another product of topsy-turvy Re\'f1a," I said dryly.\par
\par
     "Herbs, pots and anadi sharper than glass. I suppose it could be worse," Barit said.\par
\par
     "Oh?"\par
\par
     "Yes. You could be staging a revolution," he said, tail swaying in amusement. He ducked into the store and returned with the wheelbarrow. I was still standing where he'd left me, frowning after him.\par
\par
     He laughed. "Pulled your ears well and good, didn't I."\par
\par
     "Oh, be quiet," I said, torn between affection and irritation. "I'll return shortly."\par
\par
     Back and forth I went that day, hauling the pottery and herbal packages Linuthet and I had put together. I'd barely even dozed the previous week, so busy had I been on finishing my orders as well as creating new work. I transported the wares, not without pride, barely noticing the hours passing; though my eyes took in the changing hues of the shadows, deepening and growing richer in hue as the sun crested and fell toward the horizon. The air chilled as well.\par
\par
     The shadows were long and an enticing shade of plum purple against the darkened gray-yellow of the soil when I left the house with the final load. This one contained the lettered signs created by Dlane for the inside and outside of the storefront. Like most stores wholly owned and operated by a single House, ours was named by its affiliation, not by what it sold: Re\'f1a-kuresthe\'f1a, House Re\'f1a's storefront. Dlane had painted the letters on the board Barit had brought back after we'd finished haggling, and as I drew up to the store I climbed on the stool Barit had left outside and hung it carefully from the carved willow-wood branch, too aware of how expensive both board and hanger were.\par
\par
     When I climbed down and examined my work, I chuckled. The setting sun set the words aglitter, for Dlane had painted over the black letters with some of my purplish glaze and outlined the results in a bronze metallic ink I occasionally used, one so very sublime it neared silver in hue.\par
\par
     I walked into the store to find Linuthet lecturing Barit on the contents of one tiny clay vial after another. I chuckled. "What, not done yet?"\par
\par
     "I'm beginning to think I'll never be done," Barit said, ears splayed.\par
\par
     Linuthet giggled. "He'll understand. Just not immediately. Maybe I can write it down in the meantime, so you can consult the list if you forget."\par
\par
     "That would be wonderful," Barit said.\par
\par
     I chuckled. "Fine. That's for tomorrow. Barit, will you be ready for opening two days from now if I bring you the labels tomorrow?"\par
\par
     "I could open tomorrow," the emodo said. "I might not know one perfume from the next, but I can tell which rack is for perfume and which is for washing manes. That's enough to sell to a normal customer."\par
\par
     I was sorely tempted; the storefront had cost quite a bit, and I liked the idea of being able to recoup some of that expense immediately. But I shook my head. "No\'85 we want a style, a way of doing things, to be associated with Re\'f1a, and part of that style is for you to be knowledgeable about what you're selling. If it has essence of thornrose from the northern mountains in it, I want you to be able to say so."\par
\par
     He grinned. "As you will, ke eperu. I will do my studies when you bring the labels."\par
\par
     "Very good," I said, then smiled. "You did well for the House in renting us this storefront, ke Barit. Thank you."\par
\par
     "Ah. You are a pleasure to work with, my friend. Much more so than others."\par
\par
     "Mm." I gestured to Linuthet. "Come on, ba anadi. It's time to get you home before you grow too tired."\par
\par
     "Be careful, ke eperu. I'll see you in the morning."\par
\par
     "Good evening," I said, ushering Linuthet out. I checked her and pulled a cloak off the wheelbarrow, tucking it around her. "Are you cold?"\par
\par
     "A little. Not uncomfortably so," Linuthet said. Her lilac eyes were shining. "Oh, ke eperu! It's so exciting. I am making shell for our House!"\par
\par
     "In two days, most probably yes," I said, amused. "Come  now."\par
\par
     Out through the gate of the Green we went, into a darkness that had welled up from the eastern sky, enfolding the buildings in a deep, dark blue. I wore only a short vest and a pair of pants, and the chill was far sharper than I remembered.\par
\par
     "I wonder if it\rquote ll snow like in the mountains," Linuthet whispered to me, leaning into my side. She was taller than Dlane, and our hips bumped together uncomfortably as we walked.\par
\par
     "Snow?" I asked. "We\rquote d better hope not."\par
\par
     Linuthet grinned at me with chattering teeth. In the fast-falling night her irises were almost transparent with the faintest sheen of purple. Her swollen pupils seemed to hang in her eyes like ornaments. "I think it would be fun! I\rquote ve never seen snow."\par
\par
     "How would you know it was fun, then?" I asked. "It\rquote s cold enough without wishing mountain-weather on ourselves."\par
\par
     As we neared the edge of town we encountered more Jokka\'85 the rich folk of the Green might go to sleep early, or retire to talk by the light of expensive lanterns, but outside the town's privileged center, people were only just closing shop and heading home, having squeezed every last cockle shell out of the day. The creak and rattle of fold-up carts crowded people on foot into the sides of the streets at the busiest intersections.\par
\par
     One of the carts lumbering past brushed against my side and I glanced at it. The breeze whacked my nose with the strong scent of spun sugar as the brightly painted cart blocked my peripheral vision. I whirled around - a candy cart, like the one I'd bought Magun's trifles at. I shivered and turned back.\par
\par
     "Linuthet? Linu-Trinity take it!"\par
\par
     Linuthet had been accosted by two emodo whose flat ears and lashing tails told me more than I wanted to know about the just how obvious the shape of the anadi's body was through her clothing. I approached the group with clenched hands.\par
\par
     "It's cold!" Linuthet said, torn between anger and fear. Her voice wobbled as she backed away. "Would you rather I freeze?"\par
\par
     "What are you doing out in the cold in the first place?" the first emodo asked.\par
\par
     "Pardon me," I said, stepping up behind them. Their heads turned toward me, and starlight flashed in their eyes, blanking their gazes. "Is there a problem, ke emodo?"\par
\par
     "This female is wandering alone in the dark in clothing."\par
\par
     "She's with me, actually," I said. "And I'm just escorting her home from visiting with friends. Really, ke emodo\'85 do you need to scare her so?"\par
\par
     "Visiting with friends," the male hissed. "In clothing? That makes no sense."\par
\par
     "It's a little cold to be wandering without them, don't you agree?" I said, walking between them and taking Linuthet's upper arm in my hand. I could see her opening her mouth, but a quick, sharp squeeze stopped whatever it was she was going to say. "It seemed better to clothe her than to risk her safety. The head of House says she might be our next ka\'f1a."\par
\par
     "Mmm," the second emodo said. "She is a pretty one. A little young, though."\par
\par
     "Indeed," I said. "Unless you need anything else, friends? I need to get this anadi home and put her to sleep. She's had an exciting day."\par
\par
     Linuthet squeaked her outrage but I pressed my fingers harder against her arm.\par
\par
     The two emodo shrugged, tails rippling in the breeze. "No\'85 we're just glad she wasn't alone. Good evening, ke eperu."\par
\par
     "Good evening," I said, pulling Linuthet after me.\par
\par
     We walked. Stiffly. Linuthet stumbled alongside as if the arm I held was diseased. Glancing over my shoulder to make sure they were gone, I sighed and let go. "All right, ba anadi."\par
\par
     "The\'85 the\'85 animals!"\par
\par
     "We are all animals, Linuthet. They didn't mean you harm."\par
\par
     "Harm! It depends on your definition!" Linuthet rubbed her arm, ears sagging. Her voice was still bobbing from anger to misery. "Oh, gods\'85 they were so awful! So hungry and angry."\par
\par
     I frowned. "Ba anadi, they were just emodo. Average emodo at that."\par
\par
     "I hate emodo!" she said, hiding her head in her hands.\par
\par
     "Sssh!" She was going to get us in trouble with such outbursts. "At least wait until we're home before you start. Besides, you like Barit, don't you?"\par
\par
     "Barit was eperu once! He understands!"\par
\par
     I sighed. "Hush. We're almost home."\par
\par
     By the time we'd reached the house Linuthet was less obviously distraught. I ushered her in and sat her in front of the fire, then gave her a cup of warm tea. "Drink," I said. "Relax." Then I went upstairs in search of the Head of the House.\par
\par
     Upstairs, soft wet candles bathed the room in a flickering bronze light. The nest of pillows and blankets that Dlane and Linuthet slept in gained a secretive air, crannies and nooks riddled in shadows the color of rust at dusk. Even the plain walls seemed more exotic, painted in long streaks of gold fading slowly to burnished bronze and finally to umber shadows touched ever-so-slightly with the deepening purple that crept in through the balcony windows.\par
\par
     Reclining in the nest was my Dlane, my crazy anadi\'85 luxuriously naked save for a long-cloth that spilled over her broad hips, glittering gold, crimson and plum-violet in intricate floral patterns. The cords that connected front panel to back panel fell naturally into the depression between the crest of her hip and her leg, leading my eyes toward the secretive darkness pooled near her tail. She was resting on an elbow, staring at a polished surface; when I drew near I could see it was the lacquered round of bark. Every month Holdings would wipe its surface clean and write out our account's withdrawals and deposits with a wax pencil. To this Dlane applied herself, since she was better with writing than I was.\par
\par
     I tasked myself to remembering why I had sought her. Clearing my throat, I stepped into her haven of flickering light. "Ke anadi."\par
\par
     She glanced up, and a smile spread across her mouth. "Thenet! You're back! How did it go today?"\par
\par
     My tail twitched in irritation. "Well enough. We'll open in two days. Ke anadi\'85 what are you teaching that girl?"\par
\par
     Dlane's ears flicked outward. "Pardon?"\par
\par
     "Linuthet. She is convinced that emodo are evil."\par
\par
     At that, Dlane drew herself slowly from her elbow and sat straight, the round on her lap. Her shoulders rose. "Emodo\'85 evil?"\par
\par
     "A couple stopped her to ask her why she was wearing clothing. She over-reacted. Ke anadi, you aren't teaching her that emodo are evil, are you?"\par
\par
     "Not precisely. Only that what they do to us is wrong," Dlane said.\par
\par
     I growled and crouched in front of her. "It is not what you make it sound like - as if it were their intention to make you dumb. You know the emodo don't want that."\par
\par
     "They don\rquote t?" Dlane asked, one golden brow lifted.\par
\par
     "No!" I exclaimed. Trying to lower my voice, I hissed, "Barit doesn't!"\par
\par
     "Barit is one Jokkad, Thenet. Only one."\par
\par
     "So, you would teach Linuthet and all the anadi we buy that one third of our race is their enemy? What kind of teaching is that, Dlane?"\par
\par
     "The only teaching I know how to give them to keep them safe!" she said, frustration narrowing her voice. She touched my shoulder. "Dear Thenet. Can't you see? I need to keep them away from the emodo so they can find their own way."\par
\par
     "What I see," I replied, "Is that you'll never free the anadi forever by making war against the emodo. If you want this to work, you need the help of all the Jokka, not just the anadi and one battered eperu."\par
\par
     She traced one of the thin creases down my chest, remnant of the attentions of the emodo of House Mated. "You are wiser than you know, setasha."\par
\par
     "So will you stop it?" I asked. "Teach them something else!"\par
\par
     "I will think about it," she said, looking at me with those eyes of hers. She touched my cheek, let her soft fingers drift over my lower lip.\par
\par
I caught her hand in mine and said, "Promise me."\par
\par
     She nodded. But when I went downstairs and walked around Linuthet on my way out to catch some of the breeze, the haunted veils over her eyes blinded her to my passing.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     Barit did finally learn the ins and outs of Linuthet's complicated wares. I stood in a few days listening to him charm the passersby, and no eperu or emodo who entered the shop could simply walk out without letting him entice them. A quick whiff of a specially blended essential oil of stonewhite and pale-blossom might turn inexorably into a lecture over its possible uses, and before the unwitting Jokkad knew it the shell had changed hands and it or he had gone home.\par
\par
     They returned, though.\par
\par
     I learned to read the ledgers while working through Barit's lacquered rounds on repeat customers. Within weeks we had to close shop for a day to give Linuthet time to work up another shelf full of merchandise. This only served to heighten our desirability. Scarcity made for good business, particularly when your House was the only one with as discriminating a nose as Linuthet's.\par
\par
     A month later I was sitting on the edge of a stone fountain near the center of the Green's winter-stark gardens, nibbling on a piece of dried soli fruit. My cloak piled in heavy folds on the ledge before dropping to the cold, hard ground. It was a rich thing of god's breath, a complicated fabric that approximated the hue of a beautiful Jokkad better than most. Mine was dark crimson with a nap of truedark black, so that at times it seemed the color of autumn wine, at others pale as flame, still others as shadowed as a purple dusk. The black embroidered hems tickled my toes as I watched a gray cloud drift across an ambivalent sky.\par
\par
     "Ke eperu?"\par
\par
     I twisted and squinted at the figure approaching: limber beneath the weight of a woolen blue-gray cloak, shoulders a respectable breadth and hips flared like one of my more graceful pots. The voice was a neutral tenor, neither male nor female.\par
\par
     "May I help you?" I asked.\par
\par
     The eperu drew near. Its skin was the color of soil in winter: that cool and subtle gray-brown. Richer brown hair framed its face, and eyes a slate blue. It had the palette of early winter, pleasing to the eye. I found myself wishing it had some splash of color somewhere though: a scarlet scarf, or a bright gold earring\'85.\par
\par
     Earring. The elegant sweep of its right ear was marred by a break.\par
\par
     This close I could see the pallor of its cheeks - it was nervous, perhaps? Embarrassed?\par
\par
     "Ke eperu\'85 you are Thenet of House Re\'f1a, am I right?"\par
\par
     "I am it," I agreed with a cant of my head. I waited.\par
\par
     It drew its cloak closer around its body and said, "I am Seper."\par
\par
     The name had an unusual cadence: accented on the last syllable, with the trill of the 'r' softening it further. The lack of a given House name made one of my ears swivel forward in curiosity. "Ke Seper. How may I help you?"\par
\par
     It glanced at the gardens, then back at me. "May I sit?"\par
\par
     "Of course."\par
\par
     It touched the stone before seating itself, moving with a fluid, feral grace not at all marred by a curious fatigue. A long silence separated us, but I felt at ease\'85 it had been long since I'd been able to sit beside another eperu. Not since the caravan summer, and even that had been in an uncomfortable place. Being eperu in a town\'85 that I understood.\par
\par
     "It is a beautiful day," it said.\par
\par
     I smiled. "If you like winter. It's a bit cold for me."\par
\par
     Seper drew in a long breath. "Ke eperu, I do not want to waste your time\'85 but I wondered\'85."\par
\par
     "If I have space for you in House Re\'f1a."\par
\par
     It paused, eyes widening. It had lovely lashes, thick and richly brown. "Yes. You knew?"\par
\par
     I touched my own ear, where the golden hoop still hung after all this time. It looked down, ears flattening.\par
\par
     "Is it so obvious?"\par
\par
     "To one who was looking, perhaps," I said. "What happened?"\par
\par
     The sinuous movement of its throat contracting caught my eye, and then it turned to me and slowly drew its cloak from its body and held out its hand. Perplexed, I studied it and made as if to touch it; when Seper's expression didn't change, I turned its hand over and hissed.\par
\par
     A long scar, barely healed, drew a deadly line over the top of its hand. When I gently pressed its wrist, the long fingers barely twitched. "Hunting accident," I said, looking up at it.\par
\par
     It nodded.\par
\par
     "And when you didn't heal correctly, your House ignored your excellent service and took all your rank, reasoning you could no longer serve it as an injured Jokkad."\par
\par
     Again, it nodded.\par
\par
     Full contracts weren't cheap. Dlane and I had spoken of buying other eperu to fill our numbers, but unlike anadi and emodo, eperu could negotiate their own contracts. As the true workers of a House, they could charge large sums for their House affiliation contracts and expect to win them\'85 particularly if, like Seper, they could claim an earring. Nevertheless, this example of waste angered me past any prudence. To cast out a Jokkad simply because it could not do all that it once did - because it made a mistake, some part of my mind whispered - struck me as a moral crime.\par
\par
     "Come," I said. "We will go to Transactions at once."\par
\par
     Seper rested a hand on my wrist. "Ke eperu, you hardly know me, and I would rather not get you my House record."\par
\par
     Of course; it would have to go back to the House that had dishonored it so to retrieve the record that listed its accomplishments. I shrugged, my tail jerking.\par
\par
     "I will see no one thrown out like garbage. You were cast to the bottom of the rung to serve in the most menial tasks because you were hurt while serving your House. I need eperu to help me. Will you help me, ke Seper?"\par
\par
     Its ears swiveled forward under the hood. "They say Re\'f1a is a House to watch, ke eperu. And softly spoken, they also say that Re\'f1a treats its people well. I would be part of such a House."\par
\par
     "That is enough for me," I said, and stood.\par
\par
     Seper stood. It was taller than I was by several fingers. "Thank you. You will not be sorry\'85."\par
\par
     "Thanks!" I laughed. "As if this were an act of charity. Ke Seper, I plan to work you hard."\par
\par
     The eperu grinned. "Good," it said. "I have been missing work."\par
\par
     "We'll see if you feel the same way in a month," I said. Its grin was infectious, and my spirits lightened. "Come. And please call me 'Thenet'. Ke Thenet makes me feel withered."\par
\par
     "Then I am just Seper."\par
\par
     "Well, then, Seper. Let's go buy your contract."\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     "A new contract so soon?" Dlane leaned forward against the table and watched me pour brown tea. Linuthet was already asleep and Seper, newly transferred, was out hunting, an activity I'd enjoyed but found time-consuming when combined with my pottery-making duties. Not only that, but I found myself clumsy in the cold. "Are you sure that's wise?"\par
\par
     "Have you seen our returns from the storefront lately, ke anadi?" I asked. I sat across from her, wrapped my hands around the cup. "We can more than afford it." I frowned. "Besides, I couldn't leave it that way. It had been cast out for no other reason than it had lost some of the strength in one hand."\par
\par
     "So not only a new contract, but one for a cripple," Dlane said.\par
\par
     Startled, I looked up at her only to find her grinning. The knot in my midriff unraveled. "You approve," I said with a heavy sigh. "Why didn't you just say so?"\par
\par
     "Why do you think you need my approval?"\par
\par
     "You're the Head of Household\'85."\par
\par
     "Oh, please." Her hand stole to mine, curled on top of it. "Thenet, we went into this venture together. If there is a Head of Household for Re\'f1a, it is us together. You are the one who made possible my education at the clay-keeper. I could not learn were it not for you."\par
\par
     "Have you learned anything interesting?" I said into the uncomfortable silence after that. Dlane rarely told me about her daily efforts, though she came home content.\par
\par
     She laughed. "Mostly accounting! But also stories\'85."\par
\par
     "True ones?" I asked hopefully. "Histories?"\par
\par
     The anadi shook her head. "Only that from what is known, we did indeed come from the north, for there is more evidence of our ancestors in the northern lands than there were where we lived."\par
\par
     "The ruins," I said.\par
\par
     She nodded. "And other artifacts. I have even seen one\'85 a large piece of an ancient stone tablet. The writing is not so different."\par
\par
     I leaned back, amazed. "What did it say?" Some epic poem, some great wisdom, some knowledge from the depths of time\'85 my curiosity itched like tears.\par
\par
     "It was an incense recipe," Dlane said.\par
\par
     "A\'85 what?"\par
\par
     She laughed at my expression, and I joined her after a moment.\par
\par
     "No profundities to be found, eh?" I said.\par
\par
     Dlane chuckled. "Not on that tablet, no."\par
\par
     "I wonder\'85 if we could make that incense?" I liked the idea - a link to our past. To think that the incense burned in our home had been burnt by Jokka so old I could not trace their lineage\'85 it comforted me with a sense of our continuity. One day I would die, but there would be Jokka after me, just as there were Jokka before.\par
\par
     "It's not so different from what they burn in the temples, I think," Dlane said. "I could bring home the ingredients to see what Linuthet could make of it\'85."\par
\par
     The door creaked open and I twisted around to find Seper, cloak hissing around its feet. It closed the door behind it and said, "There is meat outside in the cache."\par
\par
     "The cache?" I said, bemused. "I didn't know we had one."\par
\par
     "We do now," Seper said with a laugh. It spied Dlane and walked to her, kneeling before her. "Ke anadi. I am honored to be the newest member of your House."\par
\par
     Dlane smiled and touched the eperu's cheek. How lovely, how suited to this role she seemed. "Welcome, Seper."\par
\par
     "I have a gift for our House\'85." Seper drew from beneath its cloak a broad pelt and set it on Dlane's lap. It sparkled with the beads of a thousand drops of water, melted frost. Brown guard hairs could not disguise the softness of the sand-pale fur beneath, and as it dimpled over the anadi's knees it took on different shades: a near white at the top of a fold, a rich, tipped gray-brown in its folds.\par
\par
     "What is that thing?" I asked, stunned. In all my sojourns around the area, I'd never seen anything like it. It would have been able to bite my stomach had it stood on its back legs -- and that pelt!\par
\par
     "One of the genarabi," Seper said. "A predator, and in its winter guise it is hard to spot. They frequent the watering places at night."\par
\par
     "It's beautiful!" Dlane said, petting it. She turned it over and rubbed the edge.\par
\par
     "It wants a little more care," Seper said, "But afterwards, it should be salable."\par
\par
     "Seper," I said sternly, "You did not tell me you were a trapper."\par
\par
     Seper grinned. "That's because I am not a trapper."\par
\par
     "How do you explain that, then?" I pointed at the pelt, which from my vantage seemed flawless.\par
\par
     "I am a very careful hunter," the other eperu said.\par
\par
     I laughed. "Oh! I bless the House that threw you away. Their loss is Re\'f1a's very gain."\par
\par
     Dlane's eyes sparkled as she glanced at me. "Maybe we can afford another few contracts after all."\par
\par
     Seper had the grace not to look smug.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     That winter Re\'f1a made its name in Het Narel. Spiced incense thick as temple smoke, delicate floral washes, elegant pottery and thick, glossy furs were by no means necessities, but how the folk of the Green loved them! Which is not to say we didn't sell to Jokka of lesser means, but they learned to come to our House directly during the noon-hour and buy from myself or from Seper.\par
\par
     Ah, Seper. What would I have done without it! It seamlessly took over duties I had been working: cooked and cleaned, hunted and ran errands, all accomplished with an excellence that belied the weak hand. Its attentions allowed me the freedom to walk Dlane to and from the clay-keeper's place, something that relieved my mind. Our House became far more pleasant to live in, and if such menial tasks were beneath Seper's skills, it didn't stop the eperu from earning the shiny brass earring that I myself affixed to its right ear to replace the one so ludicrously revoked. Nor did it complain when we used one of its own furs, a silvery-gray kippel, to edge the new cloak we bought it at winter's end.\par
\par
     Nevertheless, it was with relief that I greeted our first spring in Het Narel. The warming weather brought a riotous selection of flowers to run like water down the slope of the road. Linuthet's tiny gasps and thrilled exclamations had followed her like a veil the first day she walked outside and found a cluster of berrybrown blossoms nestled against the edge of our porch. Her spring fragrances had all the verve of new life, verdant floral bouquets evoked with a single whiff. Her facile touch with the mortar and pestle made its way into our house, in lotions and hair-washes that gave even my hair a little softness, a little color.\par
\par
     It was indeed a very satisfying arrangement.\par
\par
     "There's a caravan on the horizon," Seper announced as it walked in with a bundle of fish hanging from its spear.\par
\par
I glanced up from my work detailing the belly of a bowl for House Weffa, ears flipping forward. "A caravan?" I asked. "From what direction?"\par
\par
     "South," it said. The eperu opened the canvas bag and withdrew the flesh, dropping it in the pot simmering over the firepit. "It should be here in an hour."\par
\par
     I put down my brush and cleaned off my fingers with the cloth on the desk. "We should go greet them."\par
\par
     "Oh?"\par
\par
     "I traveled once with a caravan," I said. "I'm curious. Besides, if we come to them, we'll get first bids on anything they're carrying."\par
\par
     Seper laughed. "Thus speaks the shell-counter!"\par
\par
     "Frugality is good for the purse," I said.\par
\par
     "I'm glad you don't tell that to the custom," Seper teased, and I chuckled as I stood. I pulled on a short, sleeveless vest of crimson and black embroidery and buttoned it, the cloth-covered buttons coarse to my still-sensitive fingers.\par
\par
     "Let's go," I said, and Seper didn't argue.\par
\par
     We closed the house and checked on the kiln and the cache on our way out\'85 the slender thread of the road became more tenuous around Re\'f1a, but we followed what there was of it to the twin stones that marked the edge of Het Narel's zone. Beyond it the road ran, a twisted and broken path of beat grass and pebbles. Past these rocks the people of Het Narel no longer maintained the trail, and staring down it I found myself wondering who had built it in the beginning. Ilushet's caravan had not followed a set road because there hadn't been one. Or had there? Had it simple fallen into disrepair, engulfed at last by the ardent grasses?\par
\par
     The old road, deeper into the north where our ancestors had lived. Perhaps they'd built better in their youth.\par
\par
     "Why do we not maintain the roads?" I mused.\par
\par
     Seper had sit on the opposite rock, its spear planted firmly in the ground. The broad plait of its mane hung over one shoulder, twinkling with tiny beads red as sunset's eye. "I don't know," it said. "Who would do it?"\par
\par
     "I don't know," I said.\par
\par
     And so in silence we awaited the approaching caravan, a snake of nine wagons. As it drew nigh, I called, "Hail the travelers!"\par
\par
     "Good greetings!" the caravan-master called back, pulling its wagon to a halt. As I\rquote d anticipated it was not Ilushet but another eperu, sooty brown as the ash of a cool fire with blowing white hair. "Very good greetings, as we've never been greeted thus before. You're of Het Narel?"\par
\par
     "A new House," I said, "House Re\'f1a. We are close enough to the edge of town to have seen your coming and thought we'd stop by for the news."\par
\par
     "News?" The eperu looked bemused.\par
\par
     "Of other towns to the north," I said and added with a grin, "Also to see what you have to sell."\par
\par
     The caravan-master laughed. "That I understand. But news\'85 do you have friends in distant towns?"\par
\par
     I shook my head. "No. Just curiosity. What is it like? How is the weather there? Is there enough food, contentment? Have any of the towns grown or shrunk?"\par
\par
     "Strange questions," the caravan-master said, and even Seper was staring at me. "But I can try to answer. Will you share a cup with me?"\par
\par
     "Better yet, come to our House," I said. "It's on the edge of town and you can spend the lunch hour with us."\par
\par
     The eperu laughed. "Done!" It slapped the reins on the backs of the rikka and the caravan lurched again into motion, the wheels spraying pebbles on the first turn. I grinned at Seper and led the way back to the House.\par
\par
     Linuthet appeared at the door when the entire caravan train came to rest on the flowered plain across from our front yard, the pale brown road a vague marker between us. Her mouth dropped open as I jogged to her, Seper at my back. My tail waved as I stopped beside her. "Linuthet, ba anadi, put on your prettiest vest. We are entertaining."\par
\par
     "How many people are in that caravan?" she asked, voice wobbling between curiosity and a very amusing horror.\par
\par
     "Enough that Seper and I are going hunting now," I said with a grin. "Go make one of your special teas! And make it a big batch!"\par
\par
     "I will-eep!" Linuthet said as the caravan-master and several of the eperu walked over the road toward us. She vanished inside, tail flaring, and I turned to the caravan-master.\par
\par
     "My name is Thenet Re\'f1a-eperu and this is Seper, also of my House."\par
\par
     "I am Sisheh. Your hospitality is\'85 unwonted, ke eperu, but heart-warming all the same. May we hunt with you to feed our people?"\par
\par
     "That would be welcome indeed," I said, my grin growing broader.\par
\par
     How good it was to hunt with other eperu again! How wondrous to work as a group, to knit the tapestry of camaraderie tightly enough to find sustenance for us all. The spring's balm in the breeze sifted my clothing, my mane. The blossoms sprung beneath my foot pads, cushioning them and silencing my step. I would have traded nothing for that warm morning under the sponge blue sky, sliding through the hills in search of the tiny grass-eaters that run the plains, or the fish that had regained their feisty vigor after their winter somnolence.\par
\par
     We returned with food for all the caravan, dug pits and nursed hot fires with wind-blocks formed by careful arrangement of the wagons. At the end of the lunch, I sat inside with Sisheh over a mug of Linuthet's berrybrown tea and listened to the caravan-master with attentive ears.\par
\par
     "So three stops we made before turning back: Het Noidla, which is the furthest north, Het Nekelmi, and Het Kabbanil. Het Noidla was under snow when we left. They'd had a good harvest, but a few of the families lost more than the usual amount of people to the weather and birthing problems, so they were not wont to purchase as much as usual. What little we did sell were condolence items\'85 trinkets people buy themselves when they sorrow."\par
\par
     Sisheh sipped the tea, paused and looked into the mug. It drew a long pull of the moist heat rising from it and looked at me. "What is this?"\par
\par
     "Our youngest House member mixes herbs. She makes enticing teas and incense and perfumes."\par
\par
     "If all of them are like this, I shall want to speak with you about buying some for trade. This is amazing!" Sisheh dipped its spoon back into the mug and stirred before glancing at me with arched white brows. "She?"\par
\par
     I nodded. "She is not ready for mating, and is not yet past her second puberty. It seemed harmless to allow her to grind herbs, so long as she doesn't feel sickened by the work."\par
\par
     That explanation seemed to suffice for Sisheh. It continued. "Het Nekelmi was in better straits. We found it very much as we did last year. They're somewhat more self-sufficient than the other two het, but still hospitable enough.\par
\par
     "It's Het Kabbanil that's my favorite. The largest I've ever seen, and I have seen many! Its buildings are peculiar\'85 built of stone. Some Jokka whisper that it's built on the original foundations\'85 that unlike the new towns, this one never went to ruins and had to be rebuilt elsewhere. That the glory of the ancients are still in the stone roots of Kabbanil."\par
\par
     How Dlane would gasp if she knew of this strange town and of\'85 "Ruins? There really are ruins?"\par
\par
     "Really are ruins, it says!" Sisheh twitched a shoulder. "Ke eperu, there are ruins not half a day from your House. Have you never seen them?"\par
\par
     A chagrined smile touched my mouth. "Look around you, ke Sisheh. We are a new House of four members. We have work enough to do without loping around half a day from town, searching for oddities." I hesitated. "Of course, if we knew exactly where to look\'85."\par
\par
     Sisheh laughed. "Then it would be your duty to check such ruins to ensure they do not present a danger to your outlying House."\par
\par
     I nodded sagely. "Just so."\par
\par
     So it drew me a map, and then I escorted it back to the caravan where we had a haggling session as bracing as the sweet breeze. I traded pots, Linuthet's wares and a few furs for perishables: grains, fruits and vegetables. I bought glassware: beads for our manes and tails, bowls and glasses. I bought clothing from distant lands and intricately woven blankets and pillows.\par
\par
     "You," Sisheh said, following me back to the House with an armload of my purchases, "Are a hard customer, ke eperu."\par
\par
     I grinned. "I would have it no other way\'85." Squinting. A silhouette against the road, tinted in bronze and purple - when had it become dusk? I trotted out to meet Dlane.\par
\par
     "We have guests!" she said, startled. Her round eyes danced like flame. "Is it\'85."\par
\par
     "No," I said, "But I think you'll like Sisheh just as much."\par
\par
     So I introduced my anadi to the caravan-master.\par
\par
     "This is the Head of Household, Dlane Re\'f1a-anadi."\par
\par
     "Lovely ke anadi!" Sisheh said. "I am honored indeed."\par
\par
     Dlane laughed. "You are too kind, ke eperu. And so is my Thenet. It is also a Head of Household."\par
\par
     "You have\'85 two\'85 Heads of Household?" Sisheh glanced at me uncertainly.\par
\par
     "We do things a little differently in Re\'f1a," Dlane said, her serenity so compelling it seemed to surround her like a second cloak. Which made Sisheh's expression when Dlane doffed it to reveal her elegantly appointed outfit remarkably comical by contrast.\par
\par
     "It is still a little cool out," I murmured.\par
\par
     "Even anadi grow cold, I suppose," Sisheh said, but the caravan-master sounded bemused. "And it is a lovely piece of work, your long-cloth."\par
\par
     "Thank you," Dlane said.\par
\par
     "I bought several more," I added. "The caravan-master is carrying a wagon of interesting clothing."\par
\par
     "I hope you bought some for yourself!"\par
\par
     "Well, they're interchangeable," I said, caught off my guard. Clothing had never really mattered much to me, as long as it kept me warm and didn't get in my way. I suppose I gave some eye to making sure it didn't look ugly when combined in all its parts, and that it was clean and neat, but\'85.\par
\par
     Dlane turned her golden gaze on Sisheh. "I don't suppose you have something lovely we could clothe our Thenet in, do you?"\par
\par
     "Ke anadi!" I exclaimed. "What would I do with it?"\par
\par
     "Wear it," Seper proffered from the corner.\par
\par
     "As a matter of fact," Sisheh said, tapping its chin. "I will be back in a moment."\par
\par
     I stared helplessly after the eperu as it left, then turned back to Dlane who was lighting a candle, her face opaque with innocence. "What am I supposed to do with a new set of clothing? I don't need one. I have plenty!"\par
\par
     "You don't give yourself enough, Thenet," Dlane said, sitting at the table and folding her hands together. "We all have little allowances to buy ourselves things that give us pleasure, whether it's candy or jewelry or brushes\'85. But your shell has been climbing and you don't use it."\par
\par
     "There's a reason for that," I said dryly. "Perhaps I'm not interested in any of those things."\par
\par
     "Frugality," Seper said from the corner, "Is good for the purse. But not for the heart."\par
\par
     I opened my mouth, then closed it. Even Linuthet, sitting on the ramp with the box of glass vials we'd bought for her perfumes on her lap, looked up at me and canted her head.\par
\par
     "Oh, very well," I said, irritated. "I'll look."\par
\par
     Sisheh entered the House with a long copper mirror as the two eperu behind it manhandled a trunk into the common room behind it. It thanked them and waved them back out, then issued a sly smile that, matched with twinkling eyes, I distrusted immediately. "The most beautiful clothing," it said, warm tenor wetter and smoother than one of Linuthet's honeyed drinks, "comes from Kabbanil."\par
\par
     It unlocked the trunk, flipping the expensive brass tabs up, and pulled up the heavy wooden lid. I was so busy staring at the trunk, trying to estimate its worth, that I missed the flourish of Sisheh's presentation of its contents. Muffled gasps from the rest of the room made me lift my eyes.\par
\par
     The first words from my mouth were never more truly spoken, and to my heart they touched the core of why I never thought much of clothing for myself. "I am not beautiful enough to wear that!"\par
\par
     And I wasn't\'85 but oh, how my heart pounded at the sight! In all my days I'd never seen an eperu gown, though I'd heard of them. They are the formal wear for the eperu, as the tights and blouses are for the emodo. The under-gown was cream of the egg, a fabric I'd only heard mentioned but never seen: soft and incredibly heavy, but thin, its hue the richness of an egg, creamy with the faintest tint of silver-gray. The hems of the under-gown were embroidered in black with spiral flourishes bordered in black lines and black knots. The full, heavy sleeves were also cream of the egg, and like the skirt were given body by a sheath of crinoline.\par
\par
     But as marvelous, as subtle as the under-gown was, it was the over-gown that stole the eye. I had never seen such a velvet, a rich crimson that bled toward vermilion and hazy orange in the light, then thickened to a complicated tuscan red, plum-purple and violet in the shadows. The bodice was embroidered in floral black-bordered gold, and the same patterns repeated on the inside borders of the split along the front of the skirt, stylized into massive knotwork blossoms at the floor and trailing in golden leaves and stems all the way around the hem.\par
\par
     The cuffs matched the bodice, and scarlet sashes tied the heavy sleeves at the elbows.\par
\par
     As I stared, Sisheh smiled and its words had the feeling of a final punch to a downed foe. "It comes with a fan." It presented the translucent fan, a rich scarlet oval with flared sides, its panels of golden-yellow and orange outlined in black, with a black rod.\par
\par
     "Try it on," Dlane said. Her voice was implacable.\par
\par
     I glanced over my shoulder at her, unaware of when I'd stood. "What?"\par
\par
     "Try it on," Dlane said. Linuthet and Seper were nodding.\par
\par
     "I couldn't possibly-"\par
\par
     "Thenet." Dlane stood. "Come with me. May I, ke eperu?"\par
\par
     "Certainly," Sisheh said, resting the gown over Dlane's extended arm. When Dlane walked up the ramp I followed, as if drawn on a cord.\par
\par
     In the candlelit upstairs, Dlane unlaced my long-cloth. "Help me with this."\par
\par
     My confusion brought back older days, when every word and movement from the peculiar anadi who'd claimed me could set my mind to reeling. Completely off-balance, I was helpless before her suggestions, and I drew off my blouse as she tugged my breeches off. And then she slid the dress onto me.\par
\par
     The sensations were overwhelming. I could barely feel her brushing behind me and tightening the laces in the back. My arms were too distracting - they were heavy, tickled by the lining of silk between my skin and the crinoline in the sleeves. The same silk caressed my thighs, my hips. The smell of the fabric intoxicated me: musty, complicated, heightened by the pungent fragrance of the wood in which it had been resting.\par
\par
     Dlane growled in effort, then patted my back near my tail. "There. Laced." She walked in front of me and held my arms out from my body. "Oh, setasha\'85."\par
\par
     The bodice cut almost beneath my pectorals, two embroidered flaps in gold folded down from the vee in the center of the chest line. It was off-the-shoulder, and my shoulders felt naked contrasted to the weight on the rest of my body. "I look ridiculous, don't I."\par
\par
     "Oh, no. Come on, the mirror's downstairs."\par
\par
     "What!" I let go of her hands. "I'm not going down there in this!"\par
\par
     "Why?" Dlane asked, eyes wide. "You look so beautiful, love!"\par
\par
     "Painting the rock doesn't make the rock lovely, ke anadi," I said, perhaps a touch bitterly.\par
\par
     Dlane took my chin in her fingers and shook her head slowly, eyes never leaving mine. "I don't know why you insist that you're ugly, Thenet."\par
\par
     "Because I am!" I said, exasperated and trying to keep my voice low enough to escape the hearing of those downramp. Why did she have to keep reminding me? "Look at my skin! My hair! Flat as paint!"\par
\par
     "That's one of the reasons you are so beautiful, setasha," Dlane said.\par
\par
     "What?" Dressed in this incredibly ornate and beautiful confection I couldn't begin to grasp the brain spasm such a statement would have required on her part. "Look at me!"\par
\par
     "I am," Dlane said. "And your skin and your hair don't distract me from the lines of your face and your body. Your nose, your shoulders, the set of your brows, the size of your eyes\'85 they are all so perfectly proportioned, so classically beautiful that a more translucent skin, more complicated hair would have distracted me from seeing them. Without that, they're\'85 severe and uncompromising."\par
\par
     I stared at her, and she stroked my chin, the line of my jaw, her eyes following her fingers and her brows furrowed\'85 as if she were seriously considering the flare of my bones. As if she were serious. Could she be serious? Can a Jokkad with ugly skin and hair still be pretty? She was crazy!\par
\par
     She had always been crazy. But that hadn't always made her wrong.\par
\par
     "Come with me," she said again, and this time I let her lead me, reluctant but not unwilling, down the ramp.\par
\par
     "Here it is," Dlane said simply, preceding me. She cleared the ramp and slowly I drifted down it, frightened that I would wrinkle or hurt the gown, frightened that I disgraced it with my body. It rustled all around me when I moved.\par
\par
     Sisheh was the first to speak. It chuckled low in its throat and says, "I think I'll be leaving that here."\par
\par
     "Why can\rquote t the anadi wear pretty things like that?" Linuthet said, ears flipping forward. Her eyes were round. "Oh, ke Thenet. It's so beautiful!"\par
\par
     Sisheh gestured toward the mirror it had brought and, throat dry as the plains, I stepped toward it.\par
\par
     And in the polished copper, bruised by its travels as it was, I saw myself in the gown of a truedark tale and it was as Dlane said: I was not at all unlovely. I was transformed.\par
\par
     Numbed as I was by these revelations, I stared at the point of my collar bones where they joined my neck. The hollow there, the dimpled bronze shadow, seemed far too vulnerable for any eperu. I swallowed, watched my throat move, and turned around.\par
\par
     "But where would I wear it?"\par
\par
     "An opportunity will present itself, I'm sure," Dlane said. "Don't your gods do everything for a reason?"\par
\par
     How could I argue with that?\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     A week later, Seper and I girded ourselves for a day's excursion. I put aside my pottery and it put aside its chores and together we left Linuthet to tend the house while we set out along the path laid for us by Sisheh.\par
\par
     It was no hardship to walk the back of the World near Het Narel in high spring. The further from town we traveled, the more dimples in the land, and we walked on sage-green ridges cresting with the foam of saffron yellow and pale pink blooms, all of them releasing the heady, wild and thin scent of wildflowers. Vast and cloudless, the sky felt flat. I stared at it as we hiked through the fields together, untroubled by its endlessness. It hardly mattered how large the world was\'85 Dlane and I had found a place to stay. We had a House, a successful one. We had House members to care for, friends in the city, a thriving business. I had roots. What did it matter that the World did not?\par
\par
     We hunted our breakfast on the way to the site, crunching the bones of our kill over a quick fire before continuing on. By lunch we'd found them - the ruins.\par
\par
     If I hadn't known to look I wouldn't have seen them, obscured as they were by vast banks of flowers and rambunctious climbing vines. But the teeth rising from the ground were not the smooth stones of the World's bones, but shaped rocks on crumbled pillars. Tumbled bricks lay in disarray, submerged in green grass and waving weeds.\par
\par
     Seper and I moved into the site, our footsteps soft and hesitant. The whisper of the wind rendered the ruins unreal. This place of death and memory should have been silent and cold and gray, not sprinkled with white and red flowers and rife with the perfume of grass and soil.\par
\par
     "This way," Seper called. "There are more places intact."\par
\par
     I turned from my examination of the tumbled columns and jogged after the eperu. As I wended my way west, larger and larger blocks of stones thrust their heavy bodies above the soft blankets of grass until I joined Seper beneath a makeshift arch formed by two columns. Mostly intact, the two behemoths had fallen against one another and formed a join so old and stable flowering vines had crawled up to their intersection and put roots there.\par
\par
     I let my fingers trail down the ridged bodies of the ancient pillars. Their skins scraped my fingertips, rough and granulated.\par
\par
     "There's a door down this way," Seper said. "I found it almost by accident\'85." It ducked beneath the arch that led nowhere and walked around several other stones to a pile of rubble almost head-high. There Seper knelt and pointed at a hole in the stones. I squinted after its finger and saw what it had found: a smooth angled surface, obviously a door-frame. Who would build a door at such an angle, though?\par
\par
     "Come on," I said. "Let's move the rocks."\par
\par
     An hour later I discovered why the frame was angled: it led into the earth. Its door hung open into the depths.\par
\par
     "How\'85 peculiar," Seper said, blinking. Like me, its skin had developed whorls of grime and sweat and rock dust.\par
\par
     "How amazing!" I said. Grabbing my spear I started down the ancient ramp. It proceeded far enough that my head was below the level of the earth before it turned into a corridor. The walls were smooth, faced with stone panels cool to the touch. I stopped to examine the sconces set into the wall on every third panel: delicate items of expensive metal, worked into floral arabesques, and hanging lamps of glass and iron. One of those fixtures alone would have fed a Jokkad for a year, and the workmanship\'85.\par
\par
     "Exquisite," Seper said over my shoulder. I glanced back at it, then moved on down the corridor.\par
\par
     The room at the end of the hall was only the first of wonders. Round with a ceiling like the dome of an egg seen from within, complete with a near translucence at its apex. I squinted at it for several minutes, standing in the shaft of filtered sunlight, until my eyes adjusted and I could see the thick panes of glass. They must have been dug into the soil near the ground.\par
\par
     The room was paneled in stone, with a strip as broad as my arm decorated in\'85 words. Or I assumed them to be words, though I couldn't understand them. I glanced at Seper - it shook its head as well.\par
\par
     "Did they speak a different tongue?" I wondered, my voice low. The hard, cool soil beneath my feet soaked up my whisper, and I flexed my claws against it.\par
\par
     "Maybe," Seper said. "Or maybe they just wrote differently."\par
\par
     Together we explored the hall of the ancients. There were other rooms, but all of them were smaller, budding off the corridors that led to the main hall with the dome. Some of these rooms had friezes, decorated with stylized Jokka dancing or sitting together. Others had the wall trim of letters we couldn't read.\par
\par
     "Thenet!"\par
\par
     I jogged down the cool, silent halls and found Seper crouching in one of the smallest rooms, fingers scraping the hard-packed earth. A flash of wet light ran down the disc of glass in its hand, which it offered to me. It reminded me of the glass lozenges hanging in the temple; like those, it was lined with metal. Its faces were incised and shaped and painted with gold. The design was strange\'85 like a bank of clouds rearing, but with too much weight. I rubbed my fingers along the skillful lines, the glass smooth and without ripple.\par
\par
     "A gift from our ancestors," Seper suggested.\par
\par
     "I'm not sure we would take it\'85."\par
\par
     Seper folded my fingers over it and stared up at me with its earnest, slate-gray-blue eyes. I sighed and said, "Oh, very well." It grinned at me, and I returned the smile after a moment.\par
\par
     We left not long afterwards, and arrived to find the anadi had already eaten and gone to their nest. And for some reason I was compelled not to speak of our explorations.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     Spring ripened into summer; the shell flowed into House Re\'f1a, and with it the anadi. Like flowers to decorate the crown of our House, they came: subtle Witha with her breeze-whisper voice; husky Degerra with her skin of amazing yellow-gold and iridescent crimson; Osa the clever-fingered, once emodo and now full-breasted; Ne\'f1a and Koise, costly and fire-bright twins, unusual and much desired among Jokka (and therefor to Dlane's mind much more in need of protection). A string of adolescents, freshly Turned, followed in the late months of the season: Adzani, Sekootet, Madad, Iihaf, each one bringing laughter and joy and her own brand of mischief.\par
\par
Nor did we neglect the eperu, for Seper and I brought home an additional two to join us, Fooshet and Larin. The four of us erected two additional rooms on the first floor to accommodate the anadi while Barit watched in bemusement. He alone of the emodo of Het Narel had any significant interaction with the gaggle of females and neuters Re\'f1a had collected, and for the most part they treated him as mindful anadi will treat an older brother: harmless, amusing, fun to tease.\par
\par
     Never once did we consider selling contracts for our anadi. There were no harnesses in the rooms of House Re\'f1a.\par
\par
     Perhaps Het Narel talked of the strangeness of the House on the edge of town. Whatever the case, the townsfolk never stopped patronizing the store in the Green as our anadi added their sharpening skills to the luxuries available for sale. Thus I was not entirely surprised, when summer had waned to the pale blue of autumn, to receive the Leaf.\par
\par
     "What is it? What is it?" The anadi clustered around me as I closed the door on the back of the messenger.\par
\par
     Dlane waded through the females, eyes wide with curiosity and a smile pressing at her lips. "Thenet?"\par
\par
     "It is\'85 the Leaf," I said, and showed them all. A chorus of appreciative gasps and delighted giggles fell on my ears like summer's last sunlight. I grinned.\par
\par
     "The Leaf!" Dlane took it from my hands and turned it to and fro. It was lovely this year, shaped like its namesake and painted in ductile metals of coppery red and greenish bronze over white and clear glass. The year was incised in its center, and a hook allowed the recipient to hang it in his storefront window\'85 as certainly we would, for the prestige of having been invited to the preeminent gathering of the year would draw even more customers to Re\'f1a. "Oh, Thenet! I didn't think we'd be asked so quickly."\par
\par
     "We have made great strides, and our products are high quality luxury items peddled with great charm and skill by a handsome emodo on the Green's parkway," I said with a chuckle. "What did you expect?"\par
\par
     "Will you go?" Madad asked, ears straining toward me.\par
\par
     "Oh, yes, ke eperu, will you go?" They all took up the words until I couldn't help but laugh. Who could resist so much enthusiasm? I looked toward Dlane, saw her serene smile, and turned back to them.\par
\par
     "Dear Jokka, when the most influential Houses in town ask you to their invitation-only autumn ball, you do not send them back the Leaf."\par
\par
     They cheered.\par
\par
     "Let's get together a special supper to celebrate," Dlane said. "Ba anadi, be about it! We'll eat in an hour!"\par
\par
     How Dlane loved them - her daughters, as she called them. They cheered and dispersed to their tasks, familiar with the preparations, for we'd had such special dinners each time we brought home a new member of the House. As the fire rose in the pit and the scent of herbs bit the air, Dlane held out her hand to me.\par
\par
     The dusky room upramp was ours alone now; the other anadi and eperu slept in the new rooms annexed to the common room. She led me into it and rested her hands on my bare chest.\par
\par
     "Are you happy, ke eperu?" she asked.\par
\par
     "Happy? Oh, yes," I said. I didn't have to think about it. As abnormal as our emodo-less House was, it was still ours, and it was successful and full of love and laughter. We had friends - family - a business - and now the approval of high society, which was as good as an invitation to become an influential House ourselves. The warmth and comfort that suffused me at the thoughts sent a flush to my skin, and I looked down at Dlane.\par
\par
     Her face was cool, her eyes thoughtful and clouded. I frowned. "Ke anadi?"\par
\par
     She shook her head. "Nothing, my Thenet." A flash of a grin now. "You are comfortable going alone to this gathering?"\par
\par
     "Alone!" I started. I hadn't really thought about the particulars of attending. I'd made the easy assumption that Dlane would be with me, that as joint Heads of Household we would arrive together\'85.\par
\par
     "Alone," Dlane said firmly. "We may share the responsibilities of running the House, but that's hardly normal. An anadi should not go to a ball."\par
\par
     It was a baffling time for her to have embraced the illusion of orthodoxy. I blinked a few times to clear my head, found it didn't help. My ears swung back against my skull, earring jouncing against my temple. "Ke anadi, I can hardly go without you! You are the soul of Re\'f1a."\par
\par
     "And you are the mind and body," Dlane said, touching my cheek. "We have done so much for this House, setasha. We shouldn't ruin what we've built. The Houses of the Green are even more inflexible than the Jokka of our old home. Do you truly want to risk everything we've accomplished?"\par
\par
     Her argument flabbergasted me. It was as if some other person wearing Dlane's body and face was using her mouth to speak in her voice. The Dlane I knew would risk everything for a principle.\par
\par
     "I\'85 ke anadi, I don't understand," I said finally, because it was all I could say.\par
\par
     Dlane's ready smile returned; my confusion must have been as plain as the spirals across my shoulders. She traced them and said, "It's not just about me anymore, my love. We have a house full of anadi to protect, and your eperu as well. There are good risks and bad ones, and there is wisdom in being able to choose between them. Going with you to the Leaf gathering is a bad risk. Sending you alone would strengthen our position in Het Narel and allow us to do the more important but eccentric things, like continuing to buy anadi for the House without putting out breeding contracts. Do you understand?"\par
\par
     It did make sense\'85 but I couldn't shake the belief that she was holding back. I touched her chin, studied her eyes; she didn't blink or look away. I took a breath and said, "I understand. And I don't mind going alone."\par
\par
     "Good."\par
\par
     The aroma of baking sweets reminded me of dinner brewing downramp. "I'll want quiet after I eat, though," I said. "I'd like to go to the temple."\par
\par
     Surprise chased the highlights from Dlane's eyes briefly, and then she nodded. "Of course." Another warm smile. "Let's go celebrate with the others."\par
\par
     After supper, I took myself from the house where the others were still sipping fragrant teas and laughing over pastries. The relative silence of the night was bracing, and I drank it in with attentive ears. Looping my scarf around my neck, I started down the road to Het Narel through the chirp of insects and the sigh of the breeze. I found I welcomed the quiet. It was rarely quiet in Re\'f1a anymore.\par
\par
     The temple was much the same as I'd found it before: forbiddingly cold, rich in its darkness and its colored light. I ascended the steps and passed beneath its columns into the subtle world it created. Again I was enveloped in the dark that was the Void's province; again the heady texture of the incense, tickling with its heavy veils, suggested the Brightness. And again I gave myself over as ransom for the World: eperu, neither male nor female but something wholly other.\par
\par
     I took one of the brass eggs of incense resting in the prayer racks, cupping the warm metal with my hands. The insulation protected me from the metal chamber in the center where the incense burned, but the plumes accompanied me as I padded deeper into the temple, my claws scratching against the marble tiles. What I sought I couldn't name; I hadn't found it on my last visit and it was likely that I wouldn't find it now. Perhaps\'85 perhaps there were no gods, as Dlane suggested, and the Trinity was just some fanciful conceit dreamed up by frightened Jokka to explain the irrevocability of death. Perhaps I was a fool, for believing in the gods.\par
\par
     No, I couldn't believe that.\par
\par
     Why had she seemed so reticent earlier? What had she been holding back? Wasn't she happy, saving the anadi from the harness in Het Narel? Wasn't it enough?\par
\par
     Was anything ever enough for a Jokkad like Dlane?\par
\par
     Strands of glittering charms hung from the ceiling near the back of the temple as I explored its recesses. My eyes adjusted quickly to the rich, cool blacks and pulled out the faint gray glimmer of the glass and metal, reminding me of the amulet I'd found in the ruins. One strand depicted fetishes of the Brightness: many-pointed stars of flat gold, their centers hung with faceted crystal drops that threw off subtle, grayed crimsons and violets.\par
\par
     The strand beside it had been dedicated to the World: lozenges of glass carved into the shape of fetal Jokka, clasping their knees to their chests, their faces without features suggesting the heads of every Jokkad. The glass ovals had been ringed in gold, and the chains that connected them were also of gold, its gleaming muted brown and hazy blue-black in the dark.\par
\par
     There was no string of charms for the Void. My body and heart ached at the realization; my mind wanted the completion of the Trinity. Without the Void, the Brightness and World did not balance. I toyed with the idea of what such a string of fetishes would look like, had it been created: black marble balls, smooth and hard and heavy, perhaps. Or a drape of black silk sewn with a net of glass droplets, painted white on their undersides so they flashed like stars.\par
\par
     How long I stood amid the chains, I did not know. The ribbons of incense uncurled in mind-bending shapes from the brass ball, tickling the front of my nose with the bite of a spice and the back of my throat with a deeper, milder resin. Beside the two streamers I was an intruder: the third place had been spoken for, and it was not for another eperu.\par
\par
     When my feet scraped forward again, it was to propel me  on a search for the emodo, for the Void, for the balance of male, female and neuter. There was a circle, a proper circle. Images from the dance so long ago in Het Bakka flashed in my mind, seen through the murk of my reticence. But beneath the arching ceiling of the temple, through its riddled darknesses in deep purples, cool blues, hard, high browns, I could find no sign of the Void except the night sky.\par
\par
     I returned to the prayer rack when the incense trail accompanying me finally petered to several thin threads. Setting it on its cradle, I murmured my thanks to the gods without knowing what to thank them for - and bewildered, because hardly half a day ago I had had everything to thank them for.\par
\par
     Walking out of the temple, I realized that I'd not only not found what I'd been looking for, but I'd lost something as well. The sky was the dull blue of the hour before truedark. Disturbed, I returned home.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     The day of the Leaf gathering came soon enough. I spent the morning relaxing outside, a blanket furled around my shoulders as I watched the thick, gray-blue clouds scud past. The anadi could not stop dashing hither and yon, excited beyond measure by the honor that had been conferred upon our House; my arrival at the ball would signify that Re\'f1a had accepted the honor and was ready to move into the inner circle of the Green's society.\par
\par
     I for one wanted to get out of the anadi\rquote s way. The closer the day had come, the quieter I'd become inside\'85 for I had been observing Dlane, and in the moments where she did not laugh with her chosen she was somber, too somber. Her eyes had grown distant, as if focused on some object on the horizon. My eyes, too, turned more toward the mountains; not out of any desire for travel, but out of anxiety, of grim appraisal.\par
\par
     That evening I shooed the anadi away and bathed myself before the firepit where I'd destroyed my first pot. I combed my own hair, leaving it loose - the style of a Jokkad not at work. I picked up the glass beads on the table, rolled them between my fingers, then discarded them without understanding why; I didn't want anything in my mane or tail. In the privacy I'd commanded, I carefully pulled on the gown I'd never thought I would use.\par
\par
     "Do you need help?" the soft soprano asked from the ramp.\par
\par
     My ears flicked back and I smiled at my hands. "I think so."\par
\par
     Dlane came down then, helped me lace the back of the crimson bodice. In her silent gaze I saw approval of my choice of hair style.\par
\par
     "Could you bring me the box upramp in the bottom drawer?" I asked her, sitting in front of the small bronze mirror we'd hung in the common room. She nodded, leaving me to my reflection. I opened the pot of kohl and examined my face, spreading my hands over my cheeks like the fanned wings of a lithrekid. Then with fingers steady as stone I painted my eyelids with a stripe of black, thickening the line of my lashes. I outlined both eyes so, lighter on the lower lid than on the upper, and blinked a few times to dry the paint. Cosmetics among the Jokka tended toward stripes following the major lines of the nose, the cheeks: accentuating facial features. I hadn't seen anyone rim their eyes so, but I thought it might be a pleasant effect.\par
\par
     Dlane arrived with the box and handed it to me. She paused. "Setasha\'85!"\par
\par
     I canted my head.\par
\par
     "That's\'85 beautiful. Your eyes look so large!"\par
\par
     A dim warmth suffused me and I looked again at my reflection. It worked, then. I opened the expensive wooden box and withdrew from the right side the Trifold pendant Dlane had bought for me so long ago. I held it out to her.\par
\par
     She stood behind me and drew it around my throat. I didn't have to tell her to tighten the strand until it rested between my collarbones\'85 like me, she seemed to understand that unseemly fragility of the hollow there needed concealment.\par
\par
     From the left side of the box I pulled the glass disc I'd found in the ruins, hung on a piece of delicate lace ribbon.\par
\par
     "Oh," Dlane murmured, catching it as it swung from between my fingers. She laughed. "You thought of everything, didn't you! A House token!"\par
\par
     I chuckled softly. "Help me?"\par
\par
     "Of course." She went to one knee in front of me. While I pulled back the flaps of the over-gown, she located the tiny loop at the waistline sewn onto the cream of the egg under-gown. She tied the lace through the loop. When I let the flaps drop back to the floor, the glistening amulet peeked from beneath the over-gown, suggesting that Re\'f1a thought well enough of itself that its representative could trade favors rather than shell or contracts.\par
\par
     Dlane straightened, then nodded. "Ours will be a proud House at the Leaf gathering."\par
\par
     I held her eyes, then smiled. "Let the others in. I'll never hear the last of it if I leave without letting them see me first."\par
\par
     But the anadi did not exclaim over my dress when Dlane opened the door to their annex. They crowded into the common room, followed by the eperu, and fell silent and still. I began to fidget after the weight of their gazes seemed to bear down on my naked shoulders. "What?"\par
\par
     Seper stretched one knee before it and bowed forward, tail arched. The others in the room rustled as they followed suit. The sign of deference disturbed me - such motions should be reserved for moments of great emotion, when pierced to the soul with beauty or sadness. I could not be the crux of such a feeling.\par
\par
     Could I?\par
\par
     Dlane was smiling from behind the arched backs.\par
\par
     "I\'85 will be back later," I said, and brushed past them. The door shut with a heavy thump behind me and I gasped in the cool air, the bones of the bodice straining against my ribs. Once I'd regained my composure it occurred to me that my exit had been anything but decorous. But bowing\'85! I would have to speak with Seper sternly when I returned!\par
\par
     Rucking my expensive skirts up to keep them from gathering dust, the fan clutched awkwardly in one hand, I started down the road toward the Green. By the time I'd reached the bronze gate leading into the gardens, I'd calmed enough to begin to look forward to the evening.\par
\par
     The site of the Leaf gathering proved easy to find. A vast hall perched on the northern edge of the Green, facing the mountains, its heavy roof and pediment bore down on the thick support columns as if driving them into the sandy soil. The columns had none of the fluted grace of their predecessors lying in the ruins, and as I ascended the steps I paused to drag my hand along them. Standing beside them on the portico just outside the circles of yellow lamplight, the hall's relationship to the Trinity's temple was almost painfully obvious.\par
\par
     The hall's main floor was the second; it had been built on lifted piles over a fold of rock so that half of the main floor hung out over it, an outstretched balcony. I passed through the night's purple-black shadows toward the sound of music and talk, toward the amber light streaming from the opened double doors.\par
\par
     And into a warmth that dispelled the autumn evening. My gown rustled as I came to an uncertain halt. Before me, a cavernous room stretched, complete with two wings tucked near its outer edges: one that led to a raised dais visible from the front of the room, the other to the darkened balconies. And crowding the hall were the Jokka of the Green: mostly emodo with a scattering of eperu. The intellectual knowledge of the amount of Houses in the Green and the power they wielded had not prepared me for the spectacle of them all in one place, their influence and riches displayed on their bodies in the form of ornate and splendid vestments.\par
\par
     None, I realized after staring for several heartbeats, were as rich or as ornate as my own.\par
\par
     That gave me the fortitude to press into the room. I recognized several emodo from visits to our store in the Green and greeted them. Their reactions to me proved baffling: a sort of hesitation, then a broad and appreciative smile. Bemused, I wandered through the crowd, ears pricking to the occasional strand of music: they were playing 'mingle-music', pieces that wandered and had no beginning and end, popular during the first hour of a dance since it demanded no attention.\par
\par
     I was here to be seen, but now that I'd arrived there was little to do. I found myself wandering without aim. A substantial clump of people had gathered in the eastern wing on the raised dais, so I glided that way, the unaccustomed weight of the gown dragging at my limbs.\par
\par
     At the outer edge of the circle of spectators I caught a whiff of musk that set my hair on end. The world contracted, and imprinted on my mind every detail of the alcove: the bisque and sand-gold tile of the smooth stone floor, still cool beneath my bare feet, the straight columns rising to the low ceiling, the light and shadows in every color gold: dusky sepia corners, brilliant yellow auras around the lamps. The backs of the emodo melted away as I pressed through them, and my ears tilted forward to catch the sing-song timbre of a voice - a voice that should have been an emodo's, but had the purr of an anadi's.\par
\par
     There on a nest of pillows lifted from the floor, Magun reclined in all her atavistic succulence. Her charcoal skin gathered all the butter-warm light over her polished, naked hip-bones, glided over the weight of her heavy breasts, invited the eye to delve into the crannies hidden from the lamplight between her oh-so-carefully arranged legs. Her eyes were heavily shadowed by the fringe of thick red lashes, and her crimson hair had grown since I'd last seen it: it fell over the pillows in lush, gleaming waves from her head and buttocks, decorated with tiny brass and clear glass beads. They matched the chain threaded through her navel ring.\par
\par
     I was as unprepared for my reaction as I was for her presence: for the disgust, the anger, and even worse, the fear.\par
\par
     "Why, Thenet! Is that you?" Magun slid up to her elbows and stared at me with feigned innocence, the weight of her hair sliding into the indentation in the pillow left from her shoulder. I hated her voice on my name. "I almost didn't recognize you!"\par
\par
     "Magun. You look\'85," I paused. 'Ripe' didn't seem particularly polite. I couldn't bear to say 'lovely', either.\par
\par
     "At a loss for words, I see," she said, rolling onto her stomach and pinning me with her eyes. Oh, she loved having me there, loved my uncertainty. The light poured down her dimpled back like liquid.\par
\par
     "You know ke Thenet Re\'f1a-eperu?" one of the emodo asked. "How splendid! Ke Thenet, Magun is one of several Jokka who have traveled to visit our het. Strange, isn't it?"\par
\par
     "Very strange," I murmured. It made no sense. Hadn't Magun been destined for some town? Oihap, wasn't it? For a lucrative contract with House Fatha. What was she doing here?\par
\par
     "Re\'f1a-eperu, is it?" Magun said. "A House of your own?" She reached over and caught my favor-token. "A wealthy one as well!"\par
\par
     I slapped her hand away before I could control myself. Clearing my throat I said, "It's breakable, anadi. I don't think you could afford to replace it."\par
\par
     This comment brought a few amused chuckles from the watching emodo, who were not so mesmerized by Magun that they were willing to forget their exalted status. Rallying, I looked down at her and waved my fan idly, saying, "So, Magun\'85 have you had children yet? You seem past the age."\par
\par
     Ah, I'd caught her now. The emodo waited for her answer, ears erect. Naturally they'd assumed she'd already thrown a few babies given her obvious sexual maturity. To say differently would invite difficult questions, questions that might force her to explain her feelings about using other anadi to breed for her so she wouldn't have to bother with pregnancy. She knew it too, for her eyes smoldered as she smiled at me. With teeth.\par
\par
     "You wound me, Thenet. I'm not that old," she said.\par
\par
     "So what's brought you here?" I riposted. "I'd heard you were bound for Het Oihap."\par
\par
     Magun rolled onto her back, hips still twisted to one side to tease the emodo with the shadows that kept her lower body hidden from their eyes. "Oh\'85 I was detained by a far more convincing offer. I didn't think Shethu would mind once they realized what kind of emodo was prepared to purchase my contract." She grinned, eyes heavily lidded. "You'll have to meet him. He's here, somewhere."\par
\par
     "An interesting man," one of the local emodo said, ears splayed sideways.\par
\par
     "Tell us more about yourself," another said, distracting Magun from the predatory staring match she was trying to win with me. Once she looked away, I retreated and found a chair near the front hall. The gown sighed around me as I sat on the amber pillow. I caressed the black pole of the fan, ears sagging. Magun in Het Narel. What could it mean? Was she staying? Would she try to make trouble for Dlane and myself?\par
\par
     Stupid eperu! Of course she would. She had always thought us strange, and then when I'd pushed her away that night\'85 there are some Jokka who will never forget a rejection. Magun was certainly one of them.\par
\par
     I sighed and listened to the conversation of passers-by. Most of it centered around the visitors, particularly Magun. I listened half-heartedly while I rested on the pillow.\par
\par
     My ear twitched to follow a thread. Magun, they said again. Magun Edze-emodo.\par
\par
     Edze-emodo.\par
\par
     The name woke a dim whispered memory in me, but I could not see through the darkness. I half-lifted from my chair, anxiety rising through my body to grip my heart. Edze-emodo. Wasn't that-\par
\par
     And suddenly I smelled him. I stood, my fingers gripping the fan's handle so tightly my hand trembled.\par
\par
     "Ah, Thenet."\par
\par
     I jumped, and the gown whirled around my feet as I turned. My voice was a bare whisper, stripped of shield or spear. "Roika."\par
\par
     He had changed, I felt in my bones. Oh, he looked the same: skin like black glass with cut-frost spirals, mane a tumbled stormcloud gray, eyes that complex stone-blue. But something had invested him with a power beyond the masculinity that had impressed me before.\par
\par
     He wore an open blouse in soft white tucked into high-waisted breeches that clung to his waist and hips, the fabric an exotic black that gathered a pale dew when struck by the light. A long-cloth of expensive tapestried gold, silver, wine-red and black hung between his legs, and the black, gold and red repeated itself in a sash that tied the sleeve down at his left arm.\par
\par
     He was magnificent. I was terrified. I would have backed away had his gaze released me.\par
\par
     "Ke eperu," he said after a moment that paused my heart. He held out a hand to me. "Even knowing what to expect, you surprise me."\par
\par
     I stared at his hand as if it would leap into my chest and pull out my heart. His fingers splayed after I made no move to take it, and his rumbled chuckle moved me to lay my fingertips delicately over his. He curled his fingers in to trap my hand against his broad palm. I swallowed.\par
\par
     "I fear you have the advantage of me, ke emodo."\par
\par
     "As you once did with me?" He smiled. "The truedark-traveler, breaker of custom."\par
\par
     "You\'85 you knew I was here," I said, finding courage in the reference. "What do you want from me? Why are you following us?"\par
\par
     "Following you?" Roika's gray brows lifted and he laughed. "You and your Dlane, is that it?"\par
\par
     "Magun told you where we were, didn't she? She found out somehow and told you. And now you're here to take us away! You couldn't have her then and you can't have her now!"\par
\par
     Roika was watching me with surprise and laughter in his eyes. I hated him, but couldn't release his hand. My body bowed from our joined fingers and I quivered with the ferocity of my rage.\par
\par
     "Ke Thenet, ke Thenet\'85 really, the world does not revolve around you and your pretty anadi."\par
\par
     "W-what?"\par
\par
     "I didn't come here to steal you away," he said. "Though finding you here is an opportunity I fully expect to take advantage of."\par
\par
     "Not\'85 here because of us?" The breath in my lungs escaped in a long, shuddering sigh. I felt dizzy and sat again. "I\'85 I don't understand. If not following us, why are you here? Jokka do not travel."\par
\par
     He pulled up a chair, never releasing my hand. Few Jokka came near us, and I desperately wished they would. Anything not to be alone with this male.\par
\par
     "Jokka do not travel, is that it?" he said. He chuckled. "You should know better, ke eperu. You traveled with a caravan for a season. You wear an obviously imported gown." He paused, and added, "One that suits you very well."\par
\par
     My ears dipped and I looked away.\par
\par
     "From what I hear, Re\'f1a holds the reins to all the caravans coming into Het Narel, so do not tell me that Jokka don't travel."\par
\par
     \ldblquote An exaggeration,\rdblquote  I said, turning the fan handle in my fingers.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Is it?\rdblquote  he asked.\par
\par
     "You are not here to trade," I said, turning my head back to his.\par
\par
     "No," he said. "I am here to rule."\par
\par
     I almost dropped my fan.\par
\par
     He laughed again, delighted. "Oh, ke eperu! Such a look, so priceless. But even as you sit in shock, you understand, don't you?" He reached with his free hand, cupped my cheek and guided my face so that he might look on it better. "Our people are in disarray. Towns barely speak to one another. What passes for government is disjointed and spare. But it was not always that way."\par
\par
     "The ruins," I whispered.\par
\par
     Roika cupped the token on my lap, tilting his palm to and fro so that the glass and metal caught the lamplight and glittered. "You have been to one of them, I see. They loved the ocean, our ancestors. When they came out of the Birthwell, over the mountains, they brought that love with them. They built roads, cities that would have made Het Narel look like a grain of sand on a world of beaches. They had trade and couriers, and some sort of system to keep all the cities and towns informed very quickly of events. And then something happened."\par
\par
     He looked up from the token. "What, I don't know. But we fell from our glory, ke Thenet. And I want it back for us."\par
\par
     My throat wouldn't stay wet. "And you plan to do this how?"\par
\par
     "First, by surveying what we have. Then, by leaving arms of my House in every town: my children, some of my anadi, and at least one worthy eperu. And then by setting up a network by which we can communicate quickly. After that, I will take control." He smiled. "Someone can. Someone must. The roads are rotting. The towns are dwindling. Our ancestors fell to dust. I would not have us follow them."\par
\par
     My hand fluttered over my lap, plucking at the fan's handle. The other remained trapped beneath Roika's on the stone chair. "And you do this out of some altruistic desire to help our race."\par
\par
     "Gods no!" He laughed. "What do you think me made of, Thenet? Stone? Oh, no. I want a better Ke Bakil for myself and my children. I don't want them to be born to the twilight of our race. I don't want to die in the dusk. And I want to be the one remembered for bringing back the sun."\par
\par
     The question that left my mouth hadn't been planned, nor the acid that laced it. "And you bought Magun for this?"\par
\par
     "Oh, she's vicious. But she's genuinely intelligent beneath all that flesh." Roika smiled thinly. "I need smart children. She'll do."\par
\par
     The idea that the same male who had wanted Dlane could live with Magun as a substitute revolted me. I glanced away.\par
\par
     His low laugh irritated me. "You don't like that, do you? My treatment of the anadi as the breeders our natures intended them to be. But you are looking at them as people, ke Thenet. I do not need my anadi to be people. In my plan, they serve the purpose they were born to: bearing children. Children who will take back Ke Bakil from the dark. Don't you want that for our race?"\par
\par
     I pulled my hand out from under his and hissed. "Of course I do! But not at the cost of the anadi!"\par
\par
     "Not at the cost of the anadi?" He straightened. "It can't be done without their sacrifice. Have you considered how selfish it would be for them to deny their role? Not just in my plans, but in our life?"\par
\par
     "Of course I have!" I exclaimed, ears flattening. "But it doesn't seem right\'85."\par
\par
     "There will always be injustices in life, forced on us by our bodies, by nature, by death. Dignity is meeting them," Roika said.\par
\par
     I blushed at the ears, and he brushed a lock of hair from my cheek. The silence as his hand drifted over my skin set my body to trembling. Then: "What have you done to yourself?" he murmured. "You are quite lovely. Happiness has made you beautiful, ke eperu."\par
\par
     "I am not beautiful," I said.\par
\par
     "You are beautiful. Beautiful and thoughtful. I need thoughtful eperu."\par
\par
     I lifted my chin and said haughtily, "Edze could not afford a contract out of Re\'f1a."\par
\par
     Roika laughed. "Don't be so quick to judge." He stood, one ear tilting sideways. The musicians had stopped playing the mingle-music. "Dance with me, ke eperu."\par
\par
     "I don't-"\par
\par
     "Dance with me. You hunt, you run, you laugh and live. You can also dance."\par
\par
     The musicians began a stately slow-dance, one for breeders: the pace of the song allowed anadi a chance to join even if somewhat fragile. A heady, dangerous feeling rolled through my body, weakening my knees. I stood. "Very well."\par
\par
     Roika tilted his head. "This is a song for anadi and emodo."\par
\par
     "And?" I said, smiling with narrowed eyes.\par
\par
     Roika grinned. "Just reminding you, ke eperu."\par
\par
     He led me onto the cleared floor and steadied my waist with one hand. The other slid over my bare shoulder down to my shoulder-blades, fingers weaving through my mane. My skin was cold - my flesh hot. I shivered in his embrace.\par
\par
     Anadi dances were easy on the feet and the mind; their rigid patterns, once learned, danced themselves. It did not take long for me to find the rhythm of the dance, leaving me alone in the arms of a dangerous emodo, whose skin gave off an enticing, warm scent, whose able arms guided me, whose very body exuded an intoxicating, frightening amount of power. His thighs brushed against the front of my dress, his hips against mine.\par
\par
     How easy it would be to give in. To let him make my fate for me. To follow him as I did in the dance.\par
\par
     My head dipped toward his chest. I couldn't resist the impulse to rest my cheek against him. To listen to his heart count out the minutes of his life, thumping slow and hard against his skin. To let the spice and heat of him invest my nostrils with the balance that had been absent for so long from my House and life. Emodo, oh emodo. Last of the sacred Trifold.\par
\par
     His chin rested against my hair. Perhaps he felt it too. For all our struggles, all our anger and pain, for all the sense we tried to make of the bodies nature had given us and the lot those bodies gave us in society, we all felt that primal need to be completed: anadi-eperu-emodo.\par
\par
     The music segued, clean and bright, into an energetic dance for eperu and emodo with more stamina. I didn't have time to ask Roika if he would like to stay on the floor before his hands slid to my waist, clasped it hard, and I was in the air.\par
\par
     I laughed. So, he was up to the challenge? Half-flight dances had been my guilty pleasure as an adolescent, for the closer dancing came to athletics the better my limbs responded. Not even the encumbrance of the gown could keep my feet on the ground.\par
\par
     How we danced! There was no time for reflection, for the guilty realization that I moved in yoke with a male who would serve Dlane her worst nightmares. There was only the relentless beat, spurring us on, the scrape and leap, the swift turns and the ceiling rising to meet me.\par
\par
     And after that, there was another dance\'85 and another. My world narrowed to his black hands and broad chest, the smell of him, his laughter and mine.\par
\par
     We did not come off the floor completely for several sets. By the time we did, Roika had to hold me up and I no longer noticed that I leaned against him. I was too busy being amazed that he was in better shape than I.\par
\par
     "Done?" A sharp voice asked. My ears swept back.\par
\par
     "Magun, leave it be," Roika said in the tone of one who'd uttered similar words many times before.\par
\par
     "Why do you bother with the eperu? You don't need it," Magun said. She strode to me and stared me in the eye.\par
\par
I squinted. "Then you go out and dance three sets with your emodo," I said.\par
\par
     Magun bristled. "I do not need to exert myself to prove myself to the emodo."\par
\par
     "Enough!" Roika said. Irritation lent his low rumble the frayed edge of a growl. "I choose to dance with whomever I please, Magun. You will not forget who you address."\par
\par
     "I am ka\'f1a-befidzu!"\par
\par
     "You can be replaced," Roika said.\par
\par
     Magun's hands balled into fists and her voice jumped up an octave. "With what? Its false female?" She pointed at me. "The one who wants to live forever? The one who never wants to have children? Who wants to be some sort of not-Jokka? Please!"\par
\par
     "Her opinions don\rquote t make her less anadi. She has a womb like any other female.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Magun sniffed. \ldblquote Wombs are cheap, and anadi dumb.\rdblquote\par
\par
     "But the ka\'f1a-befidzu should be more," Roika said, and looked at me.\par
\par
     It was as if the wall of water from my first summer storm on the plains hit me again. Cold water. The Moment came on its heels, so powerfully arresting that my breath hissed to a stop in its grasp. I pulled away as if falling, looked back at Roika and saw him suddenly as the Void: powerful. Inescapable. Smotheringly cold, engulfing, destroying\'85 the darkness to the light of the Brightness, the sky that owned the World during half the day. I trembled, and my fear set time in motion again.\par
\par
     Magun's snarl eased and her voice took on a contemplative air. "Of course, if you do take her, you would get children on her. Many children. I would like that."\par
\par
     The utter venom of the statement, the obvious hunger and enjoyment she found in letting it loose to color the air, sent me back a step.\par
\par
     "Thenet?" Roika said, one hand cupping my elbow to steady me.\par
\par
     I could not defy him, but I had to. He did not want to destroy Dlane. But in his quest to restore our race, she was both expendable and desirable. A token in a game where the stakes reduced the importance of a single person to pebbles on the road.\par
\par
     "I\'85 feel a little ill," I said.\par
\par
     "Too much dancing," Magun said sweetly.\par
\par
     "Perhaps you should rest," Roika said.\par
\par
     I nodded. "I\'85 think I will go home now."\par
\par
     He smiled. "Do so. I'd like you to be fresh for when I see you next. House Re\'f1a may be rich, but I still think her contract prices are not out of the range of Edze."\par
\par
     How I managed to laugh, I don't know. But it had a brittle sound to me. "We shall see. 'Til then, ke emodo."\par
\par
     I did not bother to bid farewell to Magun; nor did I stop to issue my goodbyes to any of the other Jokka of the Green beneath the hall's eaves. I managed to exit the gathering without due haste until I stood outside in the cold dark.\par
\par
     Then I picked up my expensive skirts and ran.\par
\par
     In and out, my chest pressed against the boned bodice. Urgency lent my feet a lightness, and fear chased me down the streets. I would not weep: I had no time. No time for anything - my simple life was over, again. The buildings blurred as I sprinted past them.\par
\par
     I burst into the house, startling Seper from its seat by the door.\par
\par
     "Pack! Pack now! Only what we need. Wake the anadi. We're leaving tonight!"\par
\par
     "What?" Seper rose unsteadily, blinking away its doze-dreams. "Thenet! What is it?"\par
\par
     I grasped Seper's shoulder. "There is no time to explain. We are in great danger and we must leave tonight. Pack everything you'd require for the House to live outside town."\par
\par
     "But where are we going?"\par
\par
     Where indeed. My mind grasped at strings. "The ruins," I said. "If you block the tunnel mouth no one will suspect you're there. We have to hide-go!"\par
\par
     Seper nodded and vanished into the annex.\par
\par
     Upramp, I stopped beside Dlane's sleeping shape. For a long heart-beat I crouched there, fabric pooled around me. She had a loveliness in sleep, with her brow unlined and her golden lashes glittering on her domed cheeks. Even the long-cloth and blouse she wore were a part of her, without which she would simply not have been Dlane. Not my Dlane. My\'85 love.\par
\par
     "Setasha," I whispered, ignoring the ache in the roof of my mouth that presaged the tears I had no time for. "Setasha, wake up."\par
\par
     "Thenet?" She lifted a head heavy with anadi dreams. "You're back\'85 so soon\'85?"\par
\par
     "Dlane, he's here. Roika is here."\par
\par
     Her body stiffened. "No\'85."\par
\par
     "We have to go," I said. "Seper is packing and waking the anadi. We are moving to the ruins. He is coming in the morning and I don't think anything will stop him from taking you. Taking us both."\par
\par
     "No," she whispered again.\par
\par
     "I won't let him," I said. "Come with me. I know a safe place for Re\'f1a while we're gone."\par
\par
     "Gone?"\par
\par
     I touched her chin while I swallowed my tears so she wouldn't see them. "Your heart has been on the road to the Birthwell for many weeks now, setasha. Do you think me blind?"\par
\par
     She threw her arms around me and I held her close, listening to the flutter of her heart in her neck. It was easier to be strong when she needed me to be.\par
\par
     "Come on. Let's take care of our House."\par
\par
     That night a long line of anadi and eperu, their spines distorted by bags and packs, snaked from House Re\'f1a and onto the dark road toward the ancient ruins. I supervised the evacuation, my gown rustling in the insistent breeze, holding the unlit lantern. When the last anadi walked off the back step, I checked the house.\par
\par
     All my dreams. My happiness. To live the life of normal Jokka\'85 to have shell to spare and family to share it with\'85 here in this house there was nothing but false promises. Ears flat against my head, I closed the back door and strode down the road after the others, and the wind and the dark whispered through my soul as if the Void had already claimed it.\par
\par
 \par
\par
Part 4\par
\par
 \par
\par
     I pushed them as hard as I could through the chill darkness, pausing only for truedark; moving so many anadi through the truedark would have been problematic, and I dare not summon the firebrights. We started marching again as soon as the faintest scrap of gray rose into my view. Together we managed to make the ruins just before dawn.\par
\par
     Teeth clattering together and bodies shaking with exhaustion and cold, the anadi padded into the silence of the ruins, tripping over the numerous bricks hidden by the low white mist. Seper and I guided them to the door, prying it open and watching as one by one they tramped down the ancient stairs. I let the eperu go before me and followed, closing the door behind us. It would look recently used if scrutinized, but there was little to be done about that. My gown hissed against the stone as I hurried after Re\'f1a.\par
\par
     Seper had led them into the center room with the skylight, anointed now with a few thin strips of dark-greenish light from the threatening sun. There they had sit, exhausted, all save for Dlane. I stepped in and took a breath, allowing myself to relax just a little. For now we were hidden.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Now what\rquote s this about?\rdblquote  Linuthet asked, ears flattened. She hugged herself, still wearing her long sleeping shirt.\par
\par
     I glanced at Dlane, who nodded, her golden eyes sober.\par
\par
     Closing my own, I sighed. When I opened them I found all the anadi and eperu staring at me, calm and attentive despite their exhaustion. \ldblquote Many of you were attracted to our House because of our unorthodox ways\'85 knowing that you had freedom and a chance to earn a living. Such ways do not only attract Jokka with similar ideas, though. When Dlane and I left our home, we were hunted by those who thought our ideas bad. One of our hunters was a male\'85 his name was Roika Edze-emodo. And he managed to track us here. He has designs on Dlane and now all of Re\'f1a, for he is looking for females and neuters of high caliber to expand his House. It is my assumption,\rdblquote  I glanced at them all, \ldblquote that your ambition is not to become the decorations of House Edze.\rdblquote\par
\par
     An angry mutter replied me and I nodded. \ldblquote As I thought. Seper and I guided you here because it was the first place I could think of that was both large enough for us all and secretive enough to hide you if you take care not to be seen. It is here that I charge you to stay under Seper\rquote s direction while Dlane and I are gone.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Gone?\rdblquote  Koise asked, her voice tremulous. Her question was in all their eyes.\par
\par
     Dlane stepped up beside me. \ldblquote There is something we must discover, Thenet and I. About our race, a place we must go.\rdblquote  A minute frown touched her lovely brow. \ldblquote My daughters\'85 my sisters\'85 House Re\'f1a needs more than just a building on the edge of a town. It needs a philosophical cornerstone, one I haven\rquote t finished uncovering yet. Without it, we cannot be strong enough to face off the Edzes that will come for us. And I want us to be strong. I want\'85 I want a Ke Bakil of Jokka who will not build their race on the backs of their females\rquote  minds. But we can\rquote t simply stop having children.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I glanced at Dlane, startled. She\rquote d never admitted to that before.\par
\par
     \ldblquote So Thenet and I are taking a journey to the Birthwell. As soon as we return, we\rquote ll have our answers.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Seper leaned on the wall, exhaustion dragging at its shoulders. Said it, \ldblquote You wish us to stay here, ke eperu, ke anadi, until you can come back?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I nodded.\par
\par
The eperu smiled wearily, the ruby beads in its hair glinting dull in the reluctantly rising light. \ldblquote Then we shall do so.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote But how will we eat?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote There is game,\rdblquote  Seper said. \ldblquote And there is a stream nearby. We\rquote ll manage easily, if we\rquote re careful.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote When are you leaving?\rdblquote  Linuthet asked.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Tomorrow morning,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote The sooner we\rquote re away, the farther we\rquote ll be from you in case we\rquote re caught.\rdblquote\par
\par
     That brought forth a chorus of moans.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Peace, peace!\rdblquote  Dlane said, holding out her slender hands. \ldblquote Everything will turn out as it should, you\rquote ll see. You need only bide here and be safe and furtive. We\rquote ll come back for you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Rest now,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote It\rquote s been a long march.\rdblquote\par
\par
     They were loath to sleep but they\rquote d been walking all night. Within minutes all of Re\'f1a had slumped to the ground, curled up together for warmth, the eperu for once almost as deeply mired in dreams as the thick-slumbering anadi.\par
\par
     All of Re\'f1a\'85 save her founders.\par
\par
     Dlane and I walked into one of the nearby corridors, stopping in a room with a sliver of a skylight. There she looped her arms around my waist and tucked herself against my body. The heat of her seared my skin as though my gown were made of tissue, and I closed my own arms around her, one hand cupping her head.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Oh, Thenet. Will they be safe?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I thought of Roika, of his knowledge of ruins. He may have found this one already, or not. It might be the first place he checked\'85 or he might assume it too obvious. I could not guess his next action; that was one of the reasons I feared him so. Now I was the one trapped in truedark, he the hunter. \ldblquote I don\rquote t know,\rdblquote  I murmured into her golden hair. \ldblquote But where else can we go? The town will scatter them, or give them to him. Another town is too far to go with so little, and we would be easily discovered on the road. We can\rquote t take them with us, and they can\rquote t live in the open. It\rquote s the best I can do.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote What about us?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Two are more easily hidden than an entire House, ke anadi,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote We will be fine if we are alert.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Just like those first days,\rdblquote  Dlane said softly, cheek pressed against my chest.\par
\par
     I chuckled, the sound rasping in the small, empty room. \ldblquote Just like,\rdblquote  I agreed. \ldblquote You should rest. We must start in a few hours, and you have been walking just as all the rest.\rdblquote\par
\par
     She nodded and did not object when I slid my hand under her knees and pulled her into my arms. I sat on the floor in the corner, my back against the wall, and held her in the spilled fabric of my lap. She lapped a kiss against my throat and slept, almost instantly, as if she\rquote d only been awaiting my permission.\par
\par
     I? I could not even doze. I stroked her golden tresses, soft as silk and scented like mist and honey. So deeply had she succumbed to slumber that she\rquote d descended past the point where dreams could trouble her, for her face was slack, lashes still against her subtle gray cheeks. I envied her that oblivion; left to my own devices I could only turn again and again back to my dances with Roika. He too, I could smell, the musk and power of him lingering in my nostrils to mingle with the dust of the room and the perfume of my anadi\rquote s sweat. They did not make a bad combination.\par
\par
     My shoulders dipped forward and I bowed my head. Unworthy eperu, I, to have such thoughts. To be able to hold in my head a combination that would destroy us all\'85 for to give Dlane to Roika was to see her mind, her bright flame of a mind extinguished, and after that, how could I be happy?\par
\par
     And yet, even Dlane had admitted that the anadi had to bear young.\par
\par
     How could it be done? How could Roika\rquote s vision of empire and Dlane\rquote s vision of freedom ever be reconciled? Was it even possible?\par
\par
     I let out a small sigh and let my head rest against the cool stone.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     That evening I stood and peeked outside the room, Dlane sleeping still in the corner.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Seper?\rdblquote\par
\par
     My hissed whisper echoed in the empty corridor, and a few minutes later I was rewarded by the soft hush of the eperu\rquote s foot-falls. I walked out of the room and presented myself, chagrined, and I didn\rquote t even have to ask before Seper ducked beneath my outstretched arm and began to unlace the back of the bodice. The gown seemed no worse for the wear, though the bottom of the skirt would have to be carefully washed to excise the mud and dirt encrusted on the fabric by my flight. I realized belatedly that I was without the fan; I must have left it in the hall.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Are you really leaving tomorrow morning?\rdblquote  it asked.\par
\par
     I glanced over my shoulder, surprised. \ldblquote How\'85.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote It will be easier for them if you\rquote re gone before they wake. Leaving early seemed reasonable. There.\rdblquote  It stripped the cords from the back and the front of the bodice sagged forward. Together we pulled it off my body, pausing only to undo the glass token hanging from the waist loop. Seper took the lozenge and turned it in its hand.\par
\par
     \ldblquote I was told it is the ocean,\rdblquote  I said.\par
\par
     \ldblquote The ocean? The sea, you mean?\rdblquote  Seper asked. \ldblquote Like the Birthwell.\rdblquote  It opened the pouch at its belt and withdrew a leather cord, threading the loop on the top of the lozenge and then hanging it around my neck. It bumped against the middle of my ribcage. \ldblquote You should take it with you then, and carry the blessings of our ancestors.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I looked at my naked body, then bent down and folded the gown carefully. Such an expensive garment, now not only heavy with its own cost but with the memories of the Leaf gathering. I handed it to Seper. \ldblquote I should also take some clothing with me, don\rquote t you think? Something a little more practical.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Seper laughed softly. \ldblquote Come. I\rquote ve already sent the other two eperu out to hunt, but I think we\rquote ll do well enough alone.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Together we prepared for the journey: two packs, the lighter one of clothing and food for Dlane to carry and the heavier one with hunting and medical supplies, blankets, and tools for me. I found it a relief to once again wear more practical clothing: a pair of pants and a blouse with a vest made with one of Seper\rquote s kills, the tanned, supple brown skin of a qedonni lined with its soft gray fur. The glass lozenge I tucked beneath the blouse and vest, and the Trifold medallion nestled against my throat, just visible above the collar of the blouse.\par
\par
     \ldblquote It\rquote s good to be ready for this,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote Even though I don\rquote t know how far the Birthwell is\'85.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote A month, perhaps,\rdblquote  Seper said. \ldblquote You\rquote ll be crossing the mountains in winter.\rdblquote\par
\par
     My ears flattened to my head. \ldblquote Just what I need. A snow crossing in a mountain, and no knowledge of either!\rdblquote\par
\par
     Seper chuckled. \ldblquote Just stop by one of the villages before you go up. They\rquote ll be able to advise you, and surely by that time you\rquote ll be too far for it to matter that you\rquote ve spoken to other Jokka.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I hope,\rdblquote  I said quietly.\par
\par
     A soft soprano spoke from behind us. \ldblquote So is that it?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I turned to Dlane and gave her an outfit like mine. \ldblquote Just to dress you for the cold and we\rquote re ready.\rdblquote\par
\par
     As she donned her clothing, I faced Seper again and touched its shoulder. \ldblquote I know you were not expecting this, my friend. Are you certain you\rquote ll be fine?\rdblquote\par
\par
     Seper\rquote s somber eyes did not flinch from my gaze and with great deliberation it covered my hand with its - the one that its previous House had decided would never be useful again. A smile spread across my face and my ears lifted.\par
\par
     \ldblquote You have trusted me with your House, ke eperu,\rdblquote  it said. \ldblquote I will carekeep it for you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     My fangs itched and I turned away, trying not to wrinkle my nose with the pride of it. Dlane was waiting, dressed in gray skins edged in soft brown furs a few shades darker than her hair. In her golden eyes I saw the same sublime emotion. We had built this House. It was hard to leave it.\par
\par
     \ldblquote We\rquote ll be back,\rdblquote  I said, tugging the pack over a shoulder and taking my spear from the set propped against the wall.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Be careful,\rdblquote  said Seper.\par
\par
     I led Dlane out.\par
\par
     A cold wet fog clung to the ground as we exited the ruins, the stars obscured by moist streamers of dark gray clouds. But the sky visible through the patches shone a rich, deep blue and on the northern horizon the mountains crouched, serene and black. I stared at them, taking deep draughts of the chilly air, and laughed very low.\par
\par
     Dlane\rquote s hand twined in mine.\par
\par
     \ldblquote And now what?\rdblquote  I asked her, just to tease.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Now\'85 the answers to all our questions,\rdblquote  Dlane said.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Our questions? As I recall, it was yours that pushed us on this crazy journey.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Dlane grinned. \ldblquote Can you truly say you haven\rquote t found your own set after all this time?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I opened my mouth, then closed it and frowned, tail swishing once.\par
\par
     Dlane chuckled and pulled me after her, back toward the rotted road that led north. The stones that lined it showed dark gaps like the mouth of an old Jokkad, but the roadbed had at very least been pounded smoother than the surrounding terrain. In the fog visibility would be limited, so I judged it safe enough to use until the night cleared. It would be easier on my anadi as well.\par
\par
     We set off then, cleaving a path through the fog. My ears swiveled, taut to the sounds of the evening\'85 but I heard nothing unusual, only the piping cries of waterseeker birds, the shrill note of the insects waxing and waning, the occasional rustle of a creature moving through the low grasses. I could see their eyes gleaming as they looked our way.\par
\par
     \ldblquote It\rquote s strange,\rdblquote  I said a few hours later.\par
\par
     Dlane cocked her head.\par
\par
     \ldblquote To be traveling again. To be alone.\rdblquote\par
\par
     The anadi laughed, but quietly, aware still as I was that we were vulnerable in the open, with Roika no doubt seeking us. Surely by now he\rquote d called on House Re\'f1a and found it empty. Thinking of the barren rooms, I found myself wondering what Barit would think, and what would happen to our beautiful store on the Green.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Oh, Thenet. Surely you found the domestic life perhaps a little wearing?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I only shook my head. \ldblquote We built something.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote That something was more than the earthen walls of a building, and the wooden racks of scent in a store,\rdblquote  Dlane said, one ear lifting askance. \ldblquote We left it safe in the ruins. It is the sorority we made, the family we created, the bonds of love and trust and happiness.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Still. It is easier to nurture such things in a real house,\rdblquote  I murmured, digging my spear a little harder into the ground as I strode forward.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Is it?\rdblquote  she asked, her voice innocent. \ldblquote We seemed to do well enough with our own bond.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote That\rquote s different. We were a special case,\rdblquote  I said. I sighed. \ldblquote Oh, ke anadi. It\rquote s not wrong to want a quieter life than we\rquote ve lived. Though I could hardly call the success of our House in Het Narel a quiet affair!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Maybe not,\rdblquote  she said.\par
\par
     We walked in silence a ways longer, close enough to occasionally bump hips. The fog appeared to be draining into the ground and I watched it with narrowed eyes. Too much lower and we would have to leave the road, though it would be prudent anyway to begin searching for a place to camp a few hours.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Thenet\'85 what was he like?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I glanced at her sharply, then away, ears burning. My grip tightened on the haft of the spear, and my voice when I replied was a rasp. \ldblquote Magnificent.\rdblquote  The word hung in the air and I cleared my throat hastily. \ldblquote He looked well, ke anadi. Powerful. He found legitimacy for himself and his House long ago, from how he carried himself. Another thing\'85 Magun was with him.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Magun!\rdblquote\par
\par
     My lips curled back to bare my fangs. \ldblquote Oh, yes. He found us by accident, he said, but I don\rquote t doubt that she was thrilled to have us caught in his grasp. She has no love for us.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote No,\rdblquote  Dlane said, and I was surprised at the sadness that touched her reply. \ldblquote No, she does not.\rdblquote\par
\par
     We walked together, and I could not keep it from her any longer. I had to know what she thought of it. \ldblquote Ke anadi, Roika wasn\rquote t seeking us. Other plans brought him to Het Narel, and only accident re-introduced us. Other plans\'85 other ambitions.\rdblquote\par
\par
     She cocked her head and her eyes drew down to narrow, golden slits. \ldblquote Oh?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote He wants to reunite the Jokka, Dlane. To rebuild our people and create an empire as glorious as that of our ancestors. One with real roads, and real communication between towns, with increased trade\'85 the wonders we\rquote ve lost.\rdblquote\par
\par
     She did not speak for many minutes. I couldn\rquote t guess what she was thinking, but I was curious.\par
\par
     Then she laughed. \ldblquote Frivolous emodo dreams.\rdblquote\par
\par
     One of my brows lifted and I said, \ldblquote As frivolous as a certain anadi\rquote s.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Dlane grinned at me. \ldblquote And we both take them as seriously, I\rquote m sure.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Of all her reactions, I hadn\rquote t quite expected this cavalier dismissal. Uncertain of how to react, I turned my attention back to more mundane things and said, \ldblquote The fog is thinning. We should find a place to stay a few hours before continuing.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Lead on, my love.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Love. Another breeder frivolity. Perhaps I shouldn\rquote t be surprised that Dlane couldn\rquote t take seriously the insane visions of another breeder, with hers so often ridiculed. Finding a fat stone on top of a hollowed ridge, I dropped my pack there and dug out one of the blankets.\par
\par
     \ldblquote We\rquote ll stay here until after truedark,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote You should sleep if you can.\rdblquote\par
\par
     She curled up there, beneath the blanket. As I stood, she reached out and touched my thigh. \ldblquote Thenet\'85 you know it\rquote s silly, don\rquote t you? The Jokka who left the ruins - they are our past, not our future. We can\rquote t try to remake what they made. We must create our own vision of the future instead of seeking wisdom from bones and tumbled towns.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I squeezed her hand. \ldblquote I hear you,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote Rest now.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Dlane nodded and settled down.\par
\par
     I climbed up beside the rock and hid myself behind its bulk, occasionally squinting past it into the cold dark. There was only silence on the road, and I tried to imagine it as it had been when new: flat and shining silver-gray in the starlight, rimmed in pale, smooth stones, thick with traffic.\par
\par
     Oh, I heard my anadi. But whether I agreed with her I couldn\rquote t say.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     After truedark\rquote s passing we resumed our journey, this time off the road. We labored among the uneven terrain in the quiet of the approaching day and stopped when the sun\rquote s fulsome disc broke the horizon\rquote s sharp edge. Our pattern was thus established; we moved only when the day was weak, during overcast afternoons, or foggy mornings, and saved most of our travel for the night when the dark would hide us best.\par
\par
     The fourth day, while Dlane slept I went through my pack searching for the clutch of cloth-wrapped dried fruit I knew I\rquote d included somewhere. My searching hand bumped into a lumpy bag and I withdrew it, puzzled. I hadn\rquote t remembered packing it. Opening the flap I found a few of my bone-working tools and the misshapen abstract piece that had been the object of my first, most violent attentions back when my skill was uncertain and our lives were defined by the routine of Ilushet\rquote s caravan.\par
\par
     I rubbed my thumb over the hard, pale surface, traced the jagged edge where I\rquote d destroyed the thing\rquote s shape. Dlane must have put it in my pack\'85 but why?\par
\par
     On impulse I dropped the bag onto my lap and applied one of the smaller tools to the piece. There was something in it that wanted freedom, and I\rquote d left it in an impolite state. It deserved better than my first poor attempts.\par
\par
     The afternoon had passed before I realized I\rquote d spent it with the bone and the carving tools. I glanced up and down the road and found no one on it, then turned to find Dlane yawning and stretching.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Thank you,\rdblquote  I said, for I\rquote d spent the day engaged in something pleasurable instead of fretting over whether Roika\rquote s absence held some sinister meaning.\par
\par
     She reached out and petted my tail. Her smile was her reply.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     The terrain gradually sprouted a few more trees, enough to instill in me the beginnings of a sense of security. The flat plains were too open for me, and it was with relief that I saw that the land furrowed more frequently the further we traveled, providing trenches deep enough for us to walk unseen by any on the road.\par
\par
     On the sixth night, we cautiously regained the road in the thick fog that had descended. Dlane trotted alongside the deep ruts gouged in the earth from passing caravans - their worn edges suggested that it had been a while since any wagons had made them. Perhaps Sisheh\rquote s visit was responsible for their creation.\par
\par
     A thin wail rose from the mist. My hand darted out and grabbed Dlane\rquote s arm near the shoulder and we both froze, ears flicking to and fro. It had originated from behind us, definitely, but not as far as I would have liked.\par
\par
     \ldblquote\'d1edsu?\rdblquote  she whispered.\par
\par
     Another piercing ululation followed the first, differently pitched - a second.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Brightness hold us,\rdblquote  I said, ears flattening, \ldblquote More than one of them!\rdblquote  I took her hand and resumed striding up the road again, but my feet fell silently as eperu\rquote s can. Dlane stumbled after me and tried to keep time; she was far more adept on her feet than she had been when we first met, but she could not equal my skill.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Do you think\'85.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I squeezed her hand, more to quiet her than to reassure. My ears strained the fog behind us, listening for the sounds of a hunting pack. There was no reason to believe they\rquote d found us yet; the thick fog was impenetrable even to Jokku eyes and their cries had been the eerie songs of idle \'f1edsu, not the hungry moans of a pack on the trail. If we could just put enough distance between us without drawing attention to ourselves\'85.\par
\par
     A few snickers and snuffles answered my taut ears and I bared my teeth. They were closer. I could hear no Jokka either. I prayed for the fog to obscure us, sight and scent, and hurried along the path with Dlane. Our swift breathing filled the strained quiet, accompanied by the soft click of our thumbclaws on the ground.\par
\par
     A chorus of howls shattered the bubble around us, wails and moans that broke the illusion of safety.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Run!\rdblquote  I cried, and taking her by the hand I sprinted down the road. My mind raced faster than my legs: how could we outrun sleek \'f1edsu on flat ground? They were faster by far than any Jokkad. There was no way we could remain on the road, not if we expected to go free. Our only chance was off it, trusting to the trenches folded into the nap of the surrounding earth. I tugged Dlane after me, and we ran off one of the ridges abutting the road and jumped into the ditch alongside it.\par
\par
     \ldblquote There\rquote s nowhere for us to go!\rdblquote  Dlane cried out. \ldblquote Thenet, they\rquote ll catch us for sure!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Save your breath for flight,\rdblquote  I said, letting go of her hand and scrabbling down the shallow bowl of earth between two dark hills. The cries of the hunters did not wane, and my ears pressed to my hair as I led Dlane further from the road, running only as fast as she could. As she panted, I snatched a few pebbles from the ground to augment my spear. We would have to choose a place for a stand soon, and then it would be only me, the \'f1edsu, and the Moment. If I was lucky, I could kill them all without being hurt, and be away with Dlane before Roika arrived.\par
\par
     Dlane stumbled and I grabbed her by the elbow before she could fall. Her eyes, lifted to mine, quivered with terror. The giggle and wail of the pack had grown much closer; close enough that I knew they had our scent if not the sight of us. It was time to pick a place to make a stand.\par
\par
     We sprinted for the final stretch to a drooping ragedna tree with its spindly, naked limbs. Reaching its knobby roots, I grabbed Dlane by the waist and pushed her into the branches. She pulled herself onto them without protest, though her pants and gulps for air filled my ears like the wind of my own breath. The limb she clutched was hardly taller than I was, but it would be better than having her on the ground where the beasts could reach her.\par
\par
     Turning my back to the trunk, I held my spear out and crouched, waiting. As the wails and chuckles grew louder, claws slid from my feet to scythe into the cold, hard ground. I could smell the hot musk of the hunters.\par
\par
     They erupted from the fog, sinuous, corded bodies bursting from the nearest banks as if vomited. Thin, angry heads wiggled on the stalks of too-long necks as they snarled and circled me, wary of my spear. I recognized them - it was hard not to remember the crazed faces and patterns of Roika\rquote s pack. I hissed at them when they danced too near, and swatted the ones that dared to snap at my ankles.\par
\par
     Without a tree tall enough to ascend or a forest to hide in, I dare not kill them. The blood-scent of even one would send them into a frenzy. So I waited with bared teeth for the master of the beasts.\par
\par
     To see him again\'85 to have failed so quickly\'85 and yet, I almost wanted to see his face again. To throw myself against him and break my body fighting him. To listen to the rumble of a voice so deep it made my jaw ache to hear it. To see those considering eyes.\'85\par
\par
     A figure darkened in the haze, gathering shadow until it graduated to full-fledged silhouette. As I waited, mouth dry, the Jokkad stepped out of the mist.\par
\par
     I gaped. \ldblquote Who are you?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I was going to ask you the same thing, ke eperu,\rdblquote  the boy said - for so he was. I couldn\rquote t imagine him much past his first Turning, even. An unprepossessing brown with darker hair, he wore breeches and a loin-cloth and a cape to surmount it, and in his hand was a smooth wooden staff.\par
\par
     \ldblquote These beasts,\rdblquote  I said, pointing at one of the circling \'f1edsu with my spear. \ldblquote They\'85 they\'85.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Chased you up a tree,\rdblquote  he said. He paused to struggle with a smothered giggle before his face grew studiously innocent once more. \ldblquote I\rquote m sorry, ke eperu. I did not mean any trouble. I am running them for a very important emodo and they are still not good at obeying me.\rdblquote\par
\par
     He bent down and held out a hand to one of the beasts, which sidled up to him and hesitantly rubbed the bottom of its pointed jaw against his palm. I stared, flabbergasted.\par
\par
     \ldblquote A very important emodo?\rdblquote  I asked finally.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Oh yes! He paid me several shell just to exercise his pack!\rdblquote  The boy grinned. \ldblquote He\rquote s a visitor to Het Narel, and says he has such pressing business he can\rquote t stay. I think he\rquote s going to be someone very powerful one day, and then maybe he\rquote ll remember what a good job I did with his beasts and he\rquote ll want my contract!\rdblquote\par
\par
     So young to be thinking of such things\'85 Gods, he might not even remain emodo through his next Turning! I ran a shaking hand through my hair, feeling the hammer-beat of my heart slowing. I pulled the conversation along, aware of a need I could barely define. \ldblquote And the name of this powerful male?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Oh! He is called Roika Edze-emodo,\rdblquote  the boy said, straightening. Amazingly the beasts had congregated at his feet and were now lying in a pile there.\par
\par
     \ldblquote I know Roika,\rdblquote  I said, tapping my finger to my chin and staring into the air as if in deep thought. \ldblquote Perhaps you could take him a message for me?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote A message?\rdblquote  he asked, and a sly light twinkled in his eyes, one I recognized. Not only was he a future-thinker, then, but of a mercenary bent. I couldn\rquote t help but chuckle, and flipped open the pouch of my belt.\par
\par
     I tossed him a shell, which he snatched adroitly from the air. \ldblquote A message to Roika Edze-emodo, ba emodo.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote What shall I tell him?\rdblquote  The boy asked.\par
\par
     I grinned. \ldblquote Tell him this: \lquote Not while I am Thenet Re\'f1a-eperu.\rquote\rdblquote\par
\par
     The boy scratched his cheek-fluff with a puzzled frown. \ldblquote\rquote Not while I am Thenet Re\'f1a-eperu\rquote ?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote He will understand.\rdblquote\par
\par
     The boy shrugged with a twitch of his spine and grinned. \ldblquote Okay. And I\rquote m sorry again about scaring you.\rdblquote  He glanced up at Dlane\rquote s shadowed shape on the branch. \ldblquote And chasing your friend up a tree.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I laughed. \ldblquote We\rquote ll survive. Go on, then, and take those Void-cursed creatures with you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     The boy clapped his hands together and chivvied the animals back into the fog. They were reluctant to go, but they followed him and I let out a long breath when the last of them slunk into the wet mist. Turning to the tree, I held up my hands and Dlane\rquote s slid over mine. She clambered off the branch and fell untidily into my arms.\par
\par
     She started laughing. A few seconds later I joined her, and we laughed from the pit of our hips, laughed until at last we had to lean against the trunk to seek our wind again.\par
\par
     Dlane rested her head against my shoulder, her breath warm and moist against my neck as she gulped down a few final giggles.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Me too,\rdblquote  I said, stroking back her hair.\par
\par
     \ldblquote I thought for certain,\rdblquote  she said, then sighed. \ldblquote Oh, it is funny how the world works sometimes, isn\rquote t it, my love?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I kissed the top of her head and said, \ldblquote Sometimes, yes.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Her eyes rolled up to meet mine. \ldblquote Do you think it was wise?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote What?\rdblquote  I asked, though I knew very well what she referred to.\par
\par
     \ldblquote The message.\rdblquote\par
\par
     My tail flicked once, the beaded braid slapping against the backs of my calves. \ldblquote Maybe, maybe not. But it was\'85 courteous.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Courteous!\rdblquote\par
\par
     I smiled. \ldblquote I don\rquote t know if I can explain\'85 but he is a worthy adversary, ke anadi. If I\rquote m in the position to do him the honor of acknowledging my move in our game, then I should.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Is this a game?\rdblquote  Dlane asked.\par
\par
     I resettled my pack over my shoulders and started off down the hill. \ldblquote You\rquote re the one who tells me that life has no purpose, that there is no afterlife to give meaning to what we do. So what can life be, if not a trifle?\rdblquote\par
\par
     She caught up with me, breath puffing from her mouth in soft white clouds. A hint of exasperation colored her soprano as she said, \ldblquote My life is no trifle to me, Thenet. Just because there are no gods doesn\rquote t mean there is no meaning in life.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Just because you believe there are no gods,\rdblquote  I corrected.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Just because there\rquote s no proof of a god or several gods,\rdblquote  she said, stubborn. \ldblquote You must give life its own meaning. Decide what it is you want to accomplish with it and let that goal drive you on.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I glanced at her, drank her unlikely beauty, her incongruous intensity, followed the line of golden hair, creased bronze with shadows, as it fell over her cheek to brush her shoulders. And I sighed and returned my gaze to the full-forward, watching for uneven ground. \ldblquote And what of the people who have no driven goal the way you do, ke anadi? What meaning is there in life for them?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote None,\rdblquote  she said.\par
\par
     I hadn\rquote t expected that. \ldblquote None? None at all?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote You make the mistake of thinking that everyone needs their life to have meaning. Many Jokka are content to be just that: Jokka. To just live, and not to examine that living.\rdblquote  She smiled, but it was a private smile, and her eyes when I stole a surreptitious look were veiled with dreams and secrets. \ldblquote That is one of the reasons I love you so, ke Thenet, setasha. Because I know you have it in you: to want your life to be more than just living.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote You\rquote re riddling again, ke anadi,\rdblquote  I mumbled, uneasy. I didn\rquote t like the concept of my potential as a maverick. Learning to accept Dlane\rquote s craziness was difficult enough without guarding against the disconcerting possibility that I might surprise myself with such notions.\par
\par
     \ldblquote I think you understand me quite well,\rdblquote  she said, smiling at me. \ldblquote But you can say what you like. Your heart and your actions all speak louder than your mouth. Though there are ways that your mouth speaks loudest of all.\rdblquote\par
\par
     My ears flushed white, though I had no way of being certain she was referring to my kisses. It certainly wasn\rquote t usual for eperu to be kissing anadi the way I had in the past.\par
\par
     \ldblquote We should move quickly tonight,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote When the child gets back to him, he\rquote ll know for certain that we\rquote ve escaped, and I want to be far from the place the \'f1edsu tracked us to.\rdblquote\par
\par
     She nodded, but though her expression remained somber I couldn\rquote t help but think my discomfort was as plain to her vision as if it were full noon.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     Slowly, slowly, we traveled north, and the plains at last unraveled at our feet into shrublands and finally furtive forests. We were several weeks out from Het Narel when the World became foreign.\par
\par
     I felt it in my bones and joints - the ground was harder than it had been at home, or on the plains. When I bent close to it and clawed marks in the soil, the heady fragrance that tickled my nostrils recalled the richest of Linuthet\rquote s perfumes: moist and fertile, whispering promises of greens more fruitful than those I\rquote d ever known.\par
\par
     \ldblquote The land is changing,\rdblquote  I said, low-voiced, to Dlane. I spread my hand across the flank of the ground and gently drew my palm over its cool flesh.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Changing?\rdblquote  she asked, but the question was half-hearted; I knew she could sense it. A Jokkad would have to be blind and dumb not to sense it.\par
\par
     \ldblquote It\rquote s\'85 fecund,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote It releases nourishment less grudgingly than the plains or home did.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote It\rquote s the Birthwell,\rdblquote  Dlane said. \ldblquote We are heading for the womb of the world. This is the land that birthed our ancestors.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Why did we move?\rdblquote  I mused. \ldblquote We spread south, where the earth is angrier. Why?\rdblquote\par
\par
     She shook her head.\par
\par
     And so through the wilderness we walked, watching as the World changed its character. Even in autumn, when everything prepared for sleep, it could not disguise its fertile weight. The scent of wholesome decay carried on the wind spoke as clearly as if the World itself gave tongue. Game was plentiful despite the season, and the earth offered its own bounty: roots and wild grain and vegetables, mushrooms tucked into the shadows of hunch-limbed trees. We\rquote d brought jerky and dried fruits, but we found we ate infrequently from our packs.\par
\par
     The palette of our journey was muted by the nights that made our travels covert, but still I enjoyed the layered thickness of the colors: the blue-tinged silvers, the soft, subtle browns and oranges, the blacks that faded to reds so thin they bled near violet beneath the dark blue skies. And occasionally, when I deemed the cover thick enough, we traveled during the early morning, the later afternoon, and then the vista truly engaged the eyes: such graduations of hue! So many shades of brown, from the greenish dull ochers to the reddish sienna browns that neared orange; the scarlet streaks laid down by tired suns; the manifold dull greens of dying foliage adding a welcome contrast to the throbbing cerise of sunrise, sunset.\par
\par
     \ldblquote I can\rquote t understand it, but I feel more alive,\rdblquote  I said one late evening, resting on my stomach. We\rquote d diverged significantly from the road to take advantage of the cover provided by the thin forests. In a glade surrounded by a fringe of low trees, I\rquote d judged it safe to light a fire as we prepared to wait out the truedark.\par
\par
     Dlane fished a small pot from her pack and opened it, releasing a strong, surprising perfume mixed of the tang of herbal cleansers and the thick resins that Linuthet had used for her ointments. \ldblquote You seem it,\rdblquote  she said. \ldblquote I think this part of the world is good for you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Before I could roll over and ask after the pot, yet another thing I seemed to have missed while packing, Dlane straddled my hips and attacked my shoulders with her slender hands. Defeated even before the first pause in her onslaught, I sank back to the ground and almost into it as she worked her oiled fingers into the knots all across the back of my neck.\par
\par
     I licked the saliva from my teeth and said, \ldblquote You\rquote re\'85 good at that.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Dlane laughed. \ldblquote It is not a difficult skill,\rdblquote  she said, her weight shifting to press harder on the base of my hips as she worked her way down. \ldblquote Particularly with you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Why me?\rdblquote  I was impressed that I remained coherent.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Because, your body is always so tense it fairly screams where you hurt.\rdblquote  As if to punctuate, she pressed her thumbs beneath my shoulder-blades and leaned onto them. I squeaked as red stars burst from the back of my eyes.\par
\par
     When I could finally breathe again, she said, \ldblquote See?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I managed a halting laugh. \ldblquote Oh, yes!\rdblquote\par
\par
     Her fingers wiggled down my spine, caressing the bones that formed the dimpled patterns on either side of the center of my back. When her hands slid back up onto the curve of my buttocks, I opened my mouth to question her\'85 and then the base of her palm dug hard into the flesh on either side of my tail-base and I forgot what I was going to ask.\par
\par
     After the first, startling rush, I identified something different about the sensation. I couldn\rquote t name it, nor was it strong, but there was an element to the pleasure that I couldn\rquote t quantify. I would have enjoyed a few minutes\rquote  respite from the massage to consider it at length, but Dlane continued, fingers working into the bottom of my spine and sending shivers running back up to the base of my skull. The feelings nestled there, itching and burning. They almost sickened me with their strength, their alienness. I wanted to tell her to stop, but could not compel myself to speak.\par
\par
     Dlane grasped my tail and pulled it back, and the fire at my head rushed forward, spreading all over my body. The pressure of her hands pushing against the underside of my tail briefly registered and then vanished in the overwhelming tide of a sensation so intense I cried out. Every nerve jangled, and my hands and feet twitched. My limbs contracted, and I would have curled into a ball had Dlane not been sitting on my back - but I could hardly feel her. I could hardly feel anything for the pleasure of it.\par
\par
     No, the rapture of it.\par
\par
     It was an ecstasy.\par
\par
     When awareness of the World seeped back into me, I found I was sobbing softly against a hand, great sticky tears unadulterated by the saliva my dry mouth had long since stopped producing while I gasped. The warmth of the fire on my face and shoulders disconnected me from the rest of my body, which throbbed in time with a heart that raced fast enough to skip.\par
\par
     Dlane rolled off my back and with an arm around my ribs pulled me up into her arms.\par
\par
     The feel of her: softness, curves, heat and warmth. The fire dying in my hips gave a few soft flutters.\par
\par
     I pushed her away, wiping my mouth, my ears flushed.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Setasha?\rdblquote  she whispered.\par
\par
     \ldblquote What did you do to me?\rdblquote  I asked, and even my voice shook shamefully.\par
\par
     Her hesitation infuriated me. My own body - I didn\rquote t recognize it. It quivered like a breeder\rquote s. \ldblquote What did you do to me!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I\'85 wanted to give you what you have given me,\rdblquote  Dlane said, her soprano low.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Give me\'85 you\rquote re not\'85 what I\'85.\rdblquote  The words melted away before I could grasp them. \ldblquote You\rquote re not talking about\'85.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I read,\rdblquote  Dlane said. \ldblquote While I was with the librarian, in Het Narel. I told you he had some of the texts from the ancients. I read one\'85 it mentioned the passion of the eperu. I did a few experiments-\ldblquote\par
\par
     Experiments! Always with her experiments! Passion of the eperu? \ldblquote You don\rquote t mean that,\rdblquote  I said, though she\rquote d used the word that referred to the breeders\rquote  passion. \ldblquote We are eperu. I am eperu! Eperu are not breeders. Eperu do not experience that!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote They do, Thenet,\rdblquote  she said.\par
\par
     \ldblquote They can\rquote t!\rdblquote  I cried out.\par
\par
     \ldblquote You just did.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Time slowed, flowed into the Moment and ceased to exist. I was able to observe, separate entirely from my emotional state, the scintillating colors the fire threw onto Dlane\rquote s beautiful face - the warm, rich yellows, oranges, the golden halo around her lashes and hair. How startling the flame-bright profile was against the vibrant dark blues and blacks of the forest at night behind her.\par
\par
     And then the Moment shattered, destroyed by the depth of my horror.\par
\par
     I leaped to my feet, which almost dumped me back to the ground in their pleasured weakness. As Dlane stretched a hand out to me, I turned on her and fled.\par
\par
     Through the trees I crashed, flinching from their touch across my sensitized skin. I jumped over crevasses in the earth, barely seeing them flash past before they were behind me. The fecundity of the northern earth clogged my nostrils with its heavy scent, and the bite of the cold barely kept me sane. I could hear nothing but my own pants, feel nothing but the whip of twigs against my body and the dying coals of my own ecstasy. My wobbly legs propelled me unevenly, and I lurched through the dark.\par
\par
     An emodo\rquote s hands, an emodo\rquote s mouth, an anadi\rquote s hips, an anadi\rquote s womb - was my eperu strength, my eperu body only a convenient skin to hold together all these disparate parts? Was I just a failed breeder, constructed of the castaways of the emodo and the anadi?\par
\par
     Were the eperu truly part of the cycle I wanted so much to deny? If I could feel pleasure, could I too lose my mind?\par
\par
     My feet tangled, throwing me into the dirt. I skidded across the forest floor, striking a tree\rquote s roots and losing the skin off my chest to the force of my landing. And in the sudden quiet, I realized I had run, mad and unheeding, until the truedark\rquote s falling\'85 for I could see nothing, not even the hand in front of my face.\par
\par
     The scrape on my chest stung, distracting me from the last tendrils of pleasure that tugged at my body. I felt a stranger in my own skin, as if I didn\rquote t live in this flesh; gingerly, I turned onto my back and pushed myself up.\par
\par
     With the softest of touches, I brushed at the soil mixed up in the blood on my chest and ribs. My lips pulled back from my teeth and I hissed. I was glad I couldn\rquote t see the body that had betrayed me.\par
\par
     It had betrayed me, hadn\rquote t it?\par
\par
     Could it be\'85 no, it couldn\rquote t be normal for eperu to feel breeders\rquote  passion. Could it? If it was, what did it mean?\par
\par
     The feeling had been incredible. I could not deny the weight of the experience, the release, the rapture. But for no reason I could understand I hated it immediately. Hated the twitching, the flailing, the gasps I could not control, the tears, the utter lack of dignity, of self-possession. My desires had been completely ignored\'85 my body had had its own agenda, one I\rquote d never even recognized existed.\par
\par
     How many other surprises was it hiding?\par
\par
     I couldn\rquote t just sit in the dark, alone with these thoughts. They would unmake me. Fear and horror are poor companions when you cannot banish them with sight. I slid onto my hands and knees and felt the earth with my fingers. I had a vague memory of the direction I\rquote d taken in my flight, augmented by the furrow I\rquote d made in the soil when I\rquote d fallen. With the cool earth\rquote s ridges to guide me, I crawled slowly back the way I came.\par
\par
I concentrated on the images of the camp to center myself, but when I thought of Dlane, the welter of confusion rose with my gorge. I focused instead on the warmth of the fire, and my knife and the thigh bone of the animal we\rquote d killed for our last dinner. Something to do, something to occupy my mind. Fixing those things firmly in my mind\rquote s eye to replace the blank dark before the real ones, I continued my journey.\par
\par
     How different the face of the World in the black. How much cleaner and fuller the scent of the soil, the harder, more acrid scent of the roots of smooth-barked trees, the wet, pungent aroma of decomposing leaves. I could even catch the faint heaviness of water, though I could not hear a stream: a pool, perhaps, somewhere near.\par
\par
     No wind gave the forest breath, but my ears caught the rustle of passing animals, low for those in the bushes, high for those on the branch. The thin pipe of insects filled in the silence.\par
\par
     In this wonder, I could almost forget my misery.\par
\par
     Almost.\par
\par
     I clawed my way back into our glade just as truedark lifted; my eyes seized hungrily on the flickers of red of fire\rquote s dying embers. By that almost-light I could catch the slump of my pack, the slender rod of my spear\'85 and in one corner, covered in a blanket, my charge.\par
\par
     I crouched beside my bag and packed it, mostly by feel, then slung it over my shoulders. A few quick scuffs doused the fire\rquote s last glow and then I cleared my throat.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Wake up,\rdblquote  I said. I couldn\rquote t bear to add her name or even my usual \lquote ke anadi\rquote . \ldblquote We\rquote ve a while to travel yet.\rdblquote\par
\par
     She rolled over - she hadn\rquote t been sleeping then, given the depth of anadi sleep. A few minutes later she stood with her pack on her shoulder, silent. When I walked out of the glade, she followed without comment\'85 for which I was glad.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     I realized in the days and nights that followed that my feelings toward Dlane made the difference between a pleasurable journey and an intolerable one. We spoke little, and then only to exchange the barest amount of information required to continue our travels. The sound of the trees no longer intrigued me; the rich aroma of the earth turned my stomach. My skin reported the cold more frequently, and my joints ached from the constant changes in the terrain.\par
\par
     When we stopped, Dlane did not engage me in idle conversation, did not rub my back or watch me carve; she took her blanket and her food, ate and fell instantly to sleep\'85 leaving me to doze a few careful feet away, or to stare restlessly at the sky and wish for something to do to relieve the monotony. Even my bone and knife failed me, for my agitation communicated to my blade and made even the simplest of shavings impossible.\par
\par
     And all the while, as I washed my body with revulsion and shied from any touch with Dlane, myself, and even animals, I worried at the concept of eperu passion.\par
\par
     What could it mean? Was it possible that such things were normal? And if so, when should it be invited? Was this some mystery other eperu knew that I\rquote d somehow missed?\par
\par
     No, it couldn\rquote t be. Had it been known, it would have been part of everyone\rquote s life.\par
\par
     The spires of Het Kabbanil appeared in the distance several weeks later, and still I could not reach out to my anadi. I had no intention of entering any of the towns, for all my curiosity about this largest het that Sisheh had praised. I could see from Dlane\rquote s frequent glances to the east that she too wondered about Het Kabbanil. Nevertheless, I avoided it, leading the anadi into the hollows off the road and finding us our hiding places well before dawn. There was more traffic on the road around the town.\par
\par
     The day we drew abreast of it I sat on the hummock above our hollowed nook. The grass had long since browned, and in the mornings the dew grew almost cold enough to crackle. The autumn had advanced nearly to winter, and my body shivered despite my clothing. I ignored it, as I now usually did, and stared instead at the glory of the northern hets.\par
\par
     I had not seen a larger town. As Sisheh had said, many of its buildings were the cool gray of stone rather than the sandy brown of bricks. And the buildings! So tall and so many, flying banners in gold and black. Streamers in brilliant crimson flapped serenely against the pale blue sky. What must it be like to live in such a large town? Perhaps you could get lost in Het Kabbanil. Perhaps Re\'f1a would have been safer there, hidden in one of those old buildings with its heavy stone base and the words of the ancients to guard it.\par
\par
     The thought of Re\'f1a twisted my gut. I hadn\rquote t recalled them in weeks now, too firmly entrenched in the endless rut of shame and anger. I glanced over my shoulder at Dlane, snug in her blanket with only a few locks of golden hair loose to blow in the breeze. Smoothing a hand along my knee, I forced myself to study my own body.\par
\par
     Hesitantly, I leaned forward and pressed my hands against the back of my rump. I\rquote d sit on the thing all my life and never quite felt the sensations Dlane had evoked from my virgin flesh. What exactly had she done?\par
\par
     Trepidation stole the grace from my movements as I curled around, sliding my hands down my backside. When I finally found the courage to grasp my tail at its base, the movement was jerky enough to cause me pain.\par
\par
     How long I spent on that hummock in the cold, poking at myself in frustration and fear, I cannot clearly remember. But at some point my fingers found the right combination and the sensation so overwhelming I almost sprained my wrist snatching it away. And then the tremors came over me, and I hugged my knees to my chest and waited for them to fade.\par
\par
     So I could do it to myself, too.\par
\par
     When the last of the rapture had sloughed from me, I lay on the dead grass. The itch attacked my nose, and it wasn\rquote t long before I was weeping as quietly as possible, my ribcage aching with the wrench of the sobs I gulped down. I cried myself out and stared at Het Kabbanil\rquote s floating banners.\par
\par
     I still hated the feeling, but I couldn\rquote t say why. The languor of my satiated flesh repulsed me. The uncontrollable desire disgusted me. Nothing about it seemed right or normal. What did the pleasure matter? What was it good for? Eperu weren\rquote t breeders! They didn\rquote t need a breeder\rquote s rapture! What good was it to be able to experience the passion without the reproduction? It was senseless! I abhorred it!\par
\par
     That night, Dlane woke and stood to follow me as always. But unlike the nights before, she spoke.\par
\par
     \ldblquote You gave it to yourself, didn\rquote t you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I stared at her, my ears so hard to the curve of my skull they shook with the strain of keeping them there. A shudder ran up my spine and I started walking.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Why do you hate it so?\rdblquote  she asked, drawing up beside me. \ldblquote You can experience it without the faintness, the weakness the anadi get. It should only please you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote You are assuming that I\'85 found it,\rdblquote  I said, not wanting to even name it.\par
\par
     \ldblquote I am not assuming,\rdblquote  Dlane replied. \ldblquote I can smell it on you. If I kissed you, I could taste it in your mouth.\rdblquote\par
\par
     If she could get past the acrid tang of the tears. I resisted the urge to sniff myself. \ldblquote I just don\rquote t like it,\rdblquote  I said. To forestall any more questions, I asked, \ldblquote Do you?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I don\rquote t like what it means,\rdblquote  she said. \ldblquote It means I\rquote m anadi, that I was built to bear children that will steal my mind from me. I don\rquote t like feeling weak and sick when it\rquote s over. But do I like the feeling? How can I not?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote That\rquote s part of it,\rdblquote  I said, baring my teeth as a surge of anger rose. \ldblquote You can\rquote t help it.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Dlane glanced at me, one brow lifted. \ldblquote You are second-guessing your body? It knows how to make you happy. When you eat good foods and sate your hunger\'85 is that evil? Or when you run to stretch your muscles\'85 is that wrong?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Those are different!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Only in degree.\rdblquote\par
\par
     We walked in silence, and I so miserable I barely noticed the uneven ground, the World around me.\par
\par
     \ldblquote But I wouldn\rquote t want it at the hands or staff of an emodo. Only from someone I love, someone I trust not to hurt me. From you, Thenet.\rdblquote\par
\par
     My cheeks practically glowed white with the depth of my embarrassment. \ldblquote I take your will from you. I make you mine. I own you in those times, ke anadi. Doesn\rquote t that bother you?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote No. It\rquote s one of the ones I show you that I love you. Thenet\'85 after all we\rquote ve seen and done together, and all the ways you\rquote ve protected me and aided me, I think you deserve a few moments where I am yours completely.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I am no one\rquote s completely but my own!\rdblquote  I cried out, horrified. \ldblquote I didn\rquote t ask for your soul, Dlane. I don\rquote t want to be responsible for taking it from you, even for a little while. To reduce you to that\'85 I know you enjoy it, that you like it, but I can\rquote t bear to think of it. It\rquote s wrong! Wrong! You of all Jokka should think so. It is stealing your identity, your self from you. Your body shoves your mind aside, and you cannot think or feel except what it wants you to\'85 and all because of someone else!\rdblquote\par
\par
     I was shaking so hard I had to stop, touch my fingers to the corners of my eyes and try to regain my composure. When I gathered it, I found Dlane standing in front of me, her eyes bronze-dark and her cheeks and nose glowing in the light of the stars. She reached to my face and deliberately touched my chin. I shivered.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Can it be?\rdblquote  she murmured, with sadness and bemusement both, \ldblquote that you feel what I feel more deeply than I do? Oh, Thenet. There\rquote s a time for everything. If you cannot let go, you can never open your heart.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote You\rquote re talking about love again,\rdblquote  I whispered. \ldblquote This strange construct you\rquote ve created - love across genders, love that touches on parts of breeding, without ever the act. Aren\rquote t you?\rdblquote\par
\par
     She nodded, the solemnity of her expression lending the motion an aching grace. \ldblquote It\rquote s about trust,\rdblquote  she said.\par
\par
     \ldblquote And about choice. There are no choices when your body activates that way. It makes its own choices, and it\rquote s always in favor.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Oh, setasha. Don\rquote t you see? Lending yourself so to someone is a sign of value. When you allow yourself to be vulnerable in front of another, you tell them more strongly than you ever could with words that you trust them with your heart, your life, your soul. That\rquote s why I like for you to do that for me, even though it makes me ill. That\rquote s why I thought you would like it as well.\rdblquote\par
\par
     What could I say to that? It was preposterous, insane. It was not for me to be vulnerable in front of her. I trusted her, but there were parts of me that should be for me alone\'85 and this new facet of my body was too new for me to decide who it belonged to. Not while I was still trying my hardest to repudiate it. My ears splayed, and her hand reached further and cupped my jaw.\par
\par
     \ldblquote If it doesn\rquote t mean the same to you, I won\rquote t do it, Thenet.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I shuddered, and that was apparently answer enough for her. She let her fingers drop and started north again.\par
\par
     I followed, relieved to at last be speaking with her again; relieved that the conversation was over; relieved that she wasn\rquote t going to pursue it. But I couldn\rquote t help but think I\rquote d disappointed her, and the concept dogged my mind. Vulnerability, trust, strength, pleasure\'85 it just seemed so perverse.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     Our hike across the north toughened even Dlane. By the time we\rquote d passed Het Nekelmi, we were both hard and thin despite the frequent game my spear brought us. The mountains drew ever nearer, and with them came the winter. Though there was no snow, the wind took on a bitter nature.\par
\par
     \ldblquote We\rquote re going to have to stop for supplies,\rdblquote  I said finally, standing in the wind and clenching my teeth to keep them from chattering.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Are you sure we should?\rdblquote  Dlane said.\par
\par
     \ldblquote It\rquote s not a question of should we or shall we. We must. I haven\rquote t caught anything for days and our dry supplies are running out. We need more food\'85 and maybe a few days in the warmth to rest before we continue on.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Dlane squinted back to the south. \ldblquote I don\rquote t want to backtrack.\rdblquote\par
     \ldblquote We should be close enough to Het Noidla. Sisheh said that was the last het in the north.\rdblquote\par
\par
     She shivered. \ldblquote How close is \lquote close\rquote ?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Close enough if we keep going for as long as we can.\rdblquote\par
\par
     She nodded and gamely started again. I led her onto the road; we would need the extra strength to get to town. I had vastly underestimated how barren the north would become as the season advanced, and though I\rquote d kept my voice even in our discussion I was far more worried than I wanted her to know. We had neither packed nor dressed for this weather, and not even the anadi cold-loving constitution could take the kind of wind coming off the slope of the mountains.\par
\par
     I stared up at the peaks as we trekked down the road. They crouched just above us, blocking our view of the horizon and wearing clouds as dark mantles. They sent their breath to dissuade us from our course, but I would see Dlane over them and to the Birthwell if I had to break the way open with my hands.\par
\par
     As determined as she was to go on, halfway into the night Dlane staggered and fell. I crouched beside her and found her fighting back the tides of sleep.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Ssh,\rdblquote  I said, and she let out a long breath and dropped into unconsciousness. I sheathed my spear through the loops in my pack and scooped her into my arms.\par
\par
     Powerful memories accompanied me as I made my way through the gloaming. Had it really been nearly two years since I\rquote d first carried her away from our home so far from here? I had met her in the spring\'85 the summer with the caravan\'85 then two autumns spent in Het Narel, building Re\'f1a. And now, in the final months of the year I was cradling her to my breast again, carrying her through weather she could not fight despite her courage to shelter and safety.\par
\par
     She gave me her weakness. I gave her my strength. We both thought them appropriate love-gifts for this relationship we had wrought, somehow.\par
\par
     I closed my eyes for just a moment, to chase away the itch that threatened to draw the tears from my teeth. There was no time to weep. Het Noidla was still a distance away, and I was the only thing standing between our survival and starvation, exposure.\par
\par
     I walked on.\par
\par
     The cold slowed my body, dulled its senses. I lost track of my feet, knew only that they moved because I had some sense of the landscape passing on either side of the road. Dlane I held close with arms I\rquote d ceased to feel.\par
\par
     I promised her I wouldn\rquote t fail her. I kept on even as I stumbled. But the wind was crushing me down and I lost count of the times I almost fell.\par
\par
     When I finally did, I couldn\rquote t rise. With my body furled around my anadi\rquote s, I fought the rising tide of darkness and lost to it.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     Perhaps I had died, for the unmistakable scent of Linuthet\rquote s amber-rose tea drifted through my nostrils\'85 and it was warm! Fabric pressed against my legs and body, and from its softness wafted the most delicious warmth I\rquote d ever felt. I tried to dig into it, to soak it into my body. If this was the afterlife, then it was a wondrous thing.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Burrowing into it won\rquote t make you one with it,\rdblquote  said a gentle voice.\par
\par
     I was sure I was dead. But when I opened my eyes, there it was: Ilushet, sitting on a stool beside me, pouring a cup of tea from a round pot. The highlights thrown by the fire danced in the beads woven through its silvery-pale mane.\par
\par
     I sat up, shedding blankets and pillows in my haste. \ldblquote Dlane!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Peace, cousin. Your companion is sleeping on the other side of the room,\rdblquote  Ilushet said. \ldblquote She fared better than you did.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I reached out and touched its arm, but my fingers barely reported the sensation; they tingled and ached. \ldblquote Ilushet? Are you really here? I was\'85 I was on the road to Het Noidla, and I fell.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote And we found you there. Luckily, for you were close to dying,\rdblquote  Ilushet handed me a cup. \ldblquote Hold it carefully; your hands are not your own yet.\rdblquote\par
\par
     It was right; when I took the cup I could barely feel it between my palms. The heat of the liquid seeped through the walls of the cup and burned at my flesh. I sipped - it was indeed Linuthet\rquote s amber-rose tea.\par
\par
     Ilushet must have seen the question in my eyes, for it laughed. \ldblquote House Re\'f1a\rquote s wares have spread far and wide, cousin. You should not be surprised to find them in my cargo holds.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote What are you doing here?\rdblquote  I asked, still amazed. \ldblquote And where am I?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote You are in a wayhouse in Het Noidla. And I and mine were just settling in to wait out the worst of the winter before returning to the road. As I said, you were lucky that we came upon you\'85 a few hours earlier and we would have missed you; a few later and we would have been too late.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Thanks to the Brightness, World and Void,\rdblquote  I murmured, shaken. \ldblquote They must have sent you, cousin.\rdblquote  I glanced at it and a wall of shame overcame me, at my deception of it while we\rquote d traveled in its protection during the long summer. \ldblquote Ilushet\'85 there are things I must tell you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     One pale brow lifted. \ldblquote Cousin?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Please,\rdblquote  I said, glancing toward Dlane\rquote s bunk; only the swell of her hip and shoulder was visible beneath the heavy blankets, but from the rise of her shoulders she was deeply entrenched in her sleep. I put my cup aside. \ldblquote It is a long story, but you must hear it.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Ilushet rested an elbow on the table. \ldblquote I am listening.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I told it everything. How I\rquote d met Dlane, the anadi\rquote s outrageous beliefs, Roika\rquote s pursuit of us through Neked Pamari, the discovery of the caravan\'85 the incident with the dance, my feelings about the Trinity and the Trifold, Magun\rquote s brutal attentions. My voice faltered when I reached the description of my emodo\rquote s hands, first discovered beneath the tree on the plains. I could not bear to look at Ilushet for the expression on its face.\par
\par
     All our months building Re\'f1a I explained. The ruins. The strangeness of our House and Dlane\rquote s philosophies. The Leaf Gathering, Roika and Magun, our flight\'85 and haltingly, my discovery of eperu passion. Finally to our mission to the Birthwell.\par
\par
     When I finished, I turned from it, my cheeks and ears so brightly flushed with shame that I could feel the heat radiating from them. The pop and crackle of the fire in the hearth filled the silence.\par
\par
     \ldblquote I am sorry, ke Ilushet,\rdblquote  I whispered. \ldblquote I lied to you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Yes,\rdblquote  Ilushet said after a long pause. \ldblquote But I might have done the same.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I looked up, incredulous.\par
\par
     The eperu sighed and shook its head. It poured me another mug of tea, added a dollop of clear liquid from one of the bottles on the table. \ldblquote Drink.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I did, and the fire searing my nostrils and opening my throat tore a gasp from me.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Did you truly think I would blame you, when you have been so besieged? Cousin\'85 a lesser Jokkad would have come to pieces long ago. You are still here, can still feel, speak, reach out to another of your kind. It is miraculous, and the hands of the Trinity are in it.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Its absolution almost unmade me. I pressed the back of my numb wrist to my nose to quell the tears that threatened.\par
\par
     Ilushet busied itself with its own tea while I composed myself.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Thank you,\rdblquote  I said softly.\par
\par
     It nodded. \ldblquote Think no more of the matter. It is closed. But I should tell you now - there is no way over those mountains.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote What?\rdblquote  I straightened, ears jerking sideways so hard my earring bumped my head. \ldblquote Cousin, there must be. The Jokka came over the mountains\'85.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Then they were magicians. Look at yourself, Thenet. Your fingers, your toes\'85 we barely saved them from the cold. On those slopes it is snowing, great clouds of snow as high as a knee or a hip.\rdblquote\par
     \ldblquote But the Birthwell\'85.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote If it exists, then the secrets to reaching to it have died with the ancestors it birthed, cousin,\rdblquote  Ilushet said, its voice gentle.\par
\par
     That couldn\rquote t be it. Not the end of all our travails. To have our hopes die here, in the frigid north, with my fingers numb and the mountains triumphant\'85\par
\par
     \ldblquote You should rest,\rdblquote  Ilushet said, tucking the blankets around me again. \ldblquote You have been through much. You and your anadi are safe here with us.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I did not want to rest. But despair made it easy to sink back into the warmth of the bed.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     The following day I felt well enough to stand and dress myself. Ilushet and its people avoided us, which suited me - after the intimacy of my confession, a subtle discomfort plagued me when I thought of the serene caravan-master. It had forgiven me for my deception, but that did not redeem me. I should have trusted it.\par
\par
     Shouldn\rquote t I have?\par
     I sat beside Dlane\rquote s bunk, watching her ribs expand and contract with each long breath. She hadn\rquote t woken yet, and I could sense the hunger and power of her healing dreams. What was it like? What did her mind construct in that nothing-time? I sighed softly and pulled a lock of golden hair from over her eye, tucking it back with the rest of her mane. I\rquote d known her so long, and still she mystified me - my anadi, my setasha, my Dlane. Her drives, her motivations\'85 I could barely understand how her body worked, much less why she thought her crazy thoughts. How had I gone through life so long without realizing how little I knew about the rest of my race?\par
\par
     \ldblquote Ke anadi,\rdblquote  I whispered to her slumbering shape, \ldblquote How can I tell you that the Birthwell is barred?\rdblquote\par
\par
     She gave me no reply, her brow unmarred, lids fluttering ever-so-slightly. My hand passed over the medallion at the base of my throat, the same temperature as the blood running through the arteries beneath it. The gods moved our destinies. But the enigma of the flesh - that was not so simple as I\rquote d thought.\par
\par
     I occupied myself throughout the day with simple tasks. I prepared food and ate, tended the fire, mended our clothing. I was stropping my knife when her hip moved.\par
\par
     I was at her side before she\rquote d finished shifting. On the pillow her head lolled toward me, surrounded in a halo of golden hair. How smooth the line of her neck, joining her jaw to her fragile collar-bones! How much I\rquote d come to love the weight of her, could feel without touching it the breast sliding across her ribs as she moved, knew the stretch and tense of her muscles as she looked toward the scent of me and opened her rich, endless eyes!\par
\par
     \ldblquote My love,\rdblquote  she said.\par
\par
     How could I resist her? Deny her? She was in me. I leaned down and brushed her mouth with mine, and when her lips parted in lazy assent, I dipped my tongue to touch her fangs, to lick the water from the backs of her teeth. Her arms slid up to cup the back of my neck, resting lightly.\par
\par
     It was a soft, smoldering thing, that kiss. When I pulled back her hands arrested me, and she lapped at my lips and chin.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Welcome back, setasha,\rdblquote  I whispered.\par
\par
     She giggled against my jaw, let her hands slide down to rest on my knees. \ldblquote I wouldn\rquote t mind waking that way every day,\rdblquote  she said, fingers curling on the hem of my tunic and tugging.\par
\par
     I laughed.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Where are we?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Het Noidla\'85 through the providence of the gods alone,\rdblquote  I said, and told her about Ilushet.\par
\par
     She glanced at the wall and was silent, and even her fingers fell still. \ldblquote It is enough to make you believe in the Trinity,\rdblquote  she said. I marked her reluctance, but also her wonder, and smiled.\par
\par
     Turning her face back to mine, she said, \ldblquote What about the mountains? When will we leave?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I\rquote d known she would ask, but hadn\rquote t been able to decide how to reply. Now the question had come and I found myself answering. \ldblquote As soon as I scout a path for us to follow. I can do it faster than we could together, and with the winter thickening speed is our ally.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Are you sure?\rdblquote  she asked, touching my fingers. \ldblquote You have been so strained already\'85 I don\rquote t want you to overwork yourself.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I shook my head. \ldblquote No, I must.\rdblquote  I stroked her cheek-fringe away from her face with fingers still somewhat numb. \ldblquote I\rquote ll go later tonight.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote So soon?\rdblquote  Her ears splayed. \ldblquote Will you be able to see?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I laughed. \ldblquote Oh, my love! The darkness has no power over me. No veil it can drop over my eyes anymore. Not even the truedark can steal the World from me.\rdblquote  And as I said it, the confidence flowed through my body, warming me from the core to the tips of my toes. If there was a way over those mountains, I would find it for her.\par
\par
     Curling my hand over hers on the blanket, I said, \ldblquote I\rquote ll go now, to prepare. Do you need anything?\rdblquote\par
\par
     She shook her head and yawned. \ldblquote Just more rest. I feel a little weak.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I leaned down and kissed her forehead. \ldblquote I\rquote ll be back in the morning.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Be careful, my Thenet.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Her Thenet. I touched her hand one more time, then left, looking for Ilushet. I found it in the antechamber, talking with several of its people. When I entered, it dismissed them and stood. We clasped shoulders before the hearth.\par
\par
     \ldblquote You look much healthier.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I feel much healthier,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote Ilushet, cousin\'85 I need a favor. I need a true cold-weather cloak, and gloves and footwear. Do you have any?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote You\rquote re not thinking of going out there!\rdblquote  Ilushet stared at me, ears twitching back. \ldblquote Cousin\'85 it is cold, and you have only just regained your feet!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Nevertheless,\rdblquote  I said. I saw the expression setting on its face and pulled it to the table by the fire. Taking its hands in mine, I leaned forward and said, \ldblquote Ilushet, I must do this thing. I don\rquote t know why. The Trinity has been with me all my life, has saved me again and again from ridicule, imprisonment, abuse and death. I can\rquote t believe I am living still so I can make the journey of a lifetime and then stop before I complete it. This sojourn\'85 it\rquote s not just about an anadi\rquote s whim. Or even about an anadi\rquote s dream of self-determination. There\rquote s something on the other side of the mountains that will break the chains of Ke Bakil and let the Jokka free, and it\rquote s calling me.\rdblquote\par
\par
     The fire hissed over the logs as I waited, trembling in the intensity of my desire. Ilushet stared at our joined hands, its ears flat to its skull, strands of blond hair draping around its slim neck. At long last, it sighed, and the warmth of its breath brushed our knuckles.\par
\par
     \ldblquote My cousin, ke eperu, I fear for you. But I will not deny you your godsquest.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I squeezed its hands. \ldblquote Thank you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Ilushet shook its head and stood. \ldblquote Come,\rdblquote  it said. \ldblquote The night is failing, and you will need what light there is.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Ilushet outfitted me in a thickly-furred cloak in bronze-tipped black, leather gloves lined with fur, and then wrapped my legs in long strips before pulling the boots over them. I\rquote d never seen such complicated shoes; in all my life in the south I\rquote d only seen the occasional slipper or sandal. The pads of our feet grow callused after only a few months in the world, and our claws make daily footwear impractical.\par
\par
     In this weather, I could see their appeal.\par
\par
     Ilushet straightened, and I unbuckled the bag at my belt with some difficulty, handing it to the eperu. \ldblquote Keep this for me?\rdblquote\par
\par
     It pressed on the bag and then frowned. \ldblquote Cousin, this is all your shell.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I will not need it tonight. Besides, these things you\rquote ve lent me\'85 if something should happen, you will need the money to replace them.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote You will come back,\rdblquote  Ilushet said, alarmed.\par
\par
     I chuckled. \ldblquote Yes. But still.\rdblquote\par
\par
     It studied me a few moments and said, \ldblquote The anadi has shaped you, cousin.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I set a hand on its arm and said, \ldblquote Does the wheel shape the pot, Ilushet? Look elsewhere for the hand of the potter.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote And is that you, or the gods?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Is it the Trinity or the Trifold?\rdblquote  I asked in return and grinned as it shook its head. \ldblquote I will see you soon, my friend.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Be careful.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I went into the dark, to fight the mountains for their secrets. Het Noidla sat on the roots of the rock of one of the range\rquote s vast peaks, and as I strode away from the town I decided that investigating the vicinity immediately around the het would be logical. Perhaps the town had been established by the Jokka crossing the mountains.\par
\par
     \lquote Perhaps\rquote  was an uncertain word to balance a mission on. As I walked on the crumbled stone flanks of the mountains\rquote  very feet, I couldn\rquote t help but wonder how I could possibly defeat them. The night had gathered quickly and thrust itself into every nook and cranny of the land, and thick, cold clouds occluded the moon. The layered clothing disconnected me from the feel of the air, and insulated as I was I stood and stared up the slopes.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Gods,\rdblquote  I whispered. \ldblquote World and Brightness aid me.\rdblquote\par
\par
     No divine hand wiped aside the cloud cover that would have facilitated my search. Ears pressing to my head, I turned and resumed my trek, seeking the crumbled black rock that might tell tales of a pass.\par
\par
     I labored long in the fierce cold, and weariness at last doused the fire of enthusiasm that had leaped into my heart when I set out. Frustration lapped at the edges of my awareness, and I cast my gaze again to the sky.\par
\par
     \ldblquote It will not end this way,\rdblquote  I said. Then louder, \ldblquote It will not!\rdblquote\par
\par
     I punctuated the statement with a thump of my fist against the cliff-side and jumped as I was showered with pebbles. With a frown, I directed my gaze toward the flow and saw a purple-shadowed crack in the cliff. The longer I stared at it, the more it seemed to dip down into a hollow, rather than fade into the side of the rock.\par
\par
     I glanced around, found hand-holds, and scaled the side of the mountain. It was only two body-lengths up, and I slid onto the other side, landing on the ground with a grunt.\par
\par
     A quick survey of my surroundings stung my hope back to life. This pocket of rock, though close to the ground, had been completely hidden from me as I walked alongside. Perhaps others had missed it - no, I was certain they had. I continued my journey with renewed interest.\par
\par
Yet I found nothing.\par
\par
Truedark fell. I leaned against the stark cliff-side and rubbed my brows. My lashes were cold when my closed eyes introduced them to my cheeks.\par
\par
     I would find a way over the mountains. I had to. But they were not interested in making it easy for me. I sighed and ran a hand over the wall, fingers idly reporting the crags and fissures through the thick layer of my gloves. The sensation was minimal and I\rquote d long since stopped paying attention when my fingers fell through a hole. Startled, I opened my eyes to utter darkness and flapped my hand around the open space until I found its edges. I pushed myself off the rock and ran my hands along the surrounding stone. When the gloves muted even the sharpest edges of the hole I growled and stripped them off.\par
\par
     The cold struck me immediately, needles of pain thrusting through my hands. I ignored them and once again explored the edges of the hole, felt with a gasp the fluting running down the straight side, nicked and broken in places but undeniably familiar. A door jamb? Here?\par
\par
     All around I could feel the crumbled slide of scree and rock obscuring most of the portal. I put my gloves back on and grabbed one of the rocks, tossing it aside. Pebbles I scraped away with my hands curled into claws and I grunted with the effort of moving larger stones. I was soon sweating beneath my heavy winter clothing.\par
\par
     Truedark lifted shortly after the beginning of my excavations, and the hazy outline in the dark only set a-fire the hunger and joy in my heart. I continued.\par
\par
     False dawn\rquote s murky green had just stained the horizon when I stood at last in the hall hidden behind a knee-high mound of rock. It was without doubt a hall; in the poor light I could still see the even walls, and I didn\rquote t need eyes to feel the carved relief embedded in the stone near waist-height.\par
\par
     Oh, the ancients hadn\rquote t come over the mountains at all. They\rquote d come through them. My heart was lighter than a cloud\rquote s, and as the sun woke that hall heard the first Jokku laugh since its founders passed from the World.\par
\par
     I had to tell her. I scrambled over the mound of rocks and out of the sheltered well of stone and ran to Het Noidla, tail a dark flag and my lips agape for the stream of exhilarated gasps and laughter that erupted from my mouth.\par
\par
     I burst into her room to find Ilushet helping her from bed. They both looked up at me, fire-lit countenances holding completely different expressions: the eperu\rquote s surprise, and Dlane\rquote s expectation.\par
\par
     \ldblquote It\rquote s time to go to the Birthwell, ke anadi,\rdblquote  I said.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     In the light of the lantern, Dlane\rquote s gray skin gathered the ambers and sparkle-hazed yellows of fire. She belonged here, in this hall, one hand tracing with reverence the dancing Jokka carved into the walls. I looked over her head at Ilushet, who stood outside.\par
\par
     \ldblquote We\rquote ll be back soon,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote A few weeks, maybe.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Weeks?\rdblquote  Ilushet sounded uncertain. \ldblquote Are you sure, cousin?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I wasn\rquote t sure why I knew, but I did. \ldblquote It won\rquote t take us long to go and return. We can meet in Het Noidla, and then Dlane and I will go back for our House and we can bring them to meet you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Through the winter cold,\rdblquote  Ilushet said, shaking its head. It laughed. \ldblquote I am sure you have a ready answer for that as well.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote It will be done because it must,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote And after this\'85\rdblquote  I smiled with half my mouth. \ldblquote After this, very little will seem difficult or strange.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Indeed.\rdblquote  Ilushet chuckled softly. \ldblquote In a few weeks, then, I will seek you and we will talk of Re\'f1a and your future, cousin. Go with the gods, the both of you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     The eperu began its hike back to town, leaving the two of us in the hall. Dlane smiled at me, her flame-complected eyes glowing. I offered her my hand and she took it, and together we walked deeper into the mountain.\par
\par
     The lantern uncovered more and more of the beautiful relief running on both sides of us. Naked Jokka following one another, their bodies stylized so that each sex was easily distinguished from the other. There was no stiffness to their poses, but no depth either; the relief was shallow, and beneath the long row ran strips of decorative designs, their angularity in sharp contrast to the fluidity of the figures.\par
\par
     \ldblquote It is like the ancestor of our writing,\rdblquote  Dlane said as her fingers trailed over the designs. \ldblquote Almost like the tablets I saw in Het Narel, but not exactly so. Oh, Thenet.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I squeezed her hand.\par
\par
     We gauged the passing of the hours by how often we poured oil into the lamp; we\rquote d brought a small bag of leather sacks, and each held half a day\rquote s oil. We\rquote d traveled an entire day when the passage opened into a small round room with a domed ceiling. As I set down our supplies, Dlane followed the carvings with fingers and eyes.\par
\par
     \ldblquote They\rquote re resting,\rdblquote  she said, and indeed when I looked up the Jokka on the walls were in various states of repose, some sleeping, others sitting or leaning against one another. Nor was there any separation of sexes: anadi, emodo and eperu all mingled in the raised stone.\par
\par
     I chuckled and unpacked our small dinner. We\rquote d brought no wood for fires, and though walking had kept us warm the frigid stone chilled the air. We would make due with blankets, and the fur cape Ilushet had insisted I keep.\par
\par
     By the time she\rquote d exhausted her curiosity, I was ready with dried fruit and roots and a few strips of cured meat. I passed her the water bag and she sat next to me, hips and shoulders touching.\par
\par
     \ldblquote What do you think we\rquote ll find?\rdblquote  she said, her voice hushed and curls of white rising from her lips.\par
\par
     \ldblquote I don\rquote t know,\rdblquote  I said, pulling one of the blankets around us. \ldblquote What I see now\'85 this work\'85.\rdblquote  I shook my head. \ldblquote I am prepared for anything.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I am prepared for wonder,\rdblquote  she said, nibbling on the strip of jerky.\par
\par
     So was I.\par
\par
     That night we slept on top of the soft fur with the blankets piled atop us. Dlane set her back to my chest and I pulled her into my arms. It was not long before we\rquote d grown warm enough to relax, and she was asleep in moments.\par
\par
     My eyes roved over the carvings. I\rquote d always believed that the hands of the Brightness, World and Void cupped my life. This was the first time I\rquote d sensed the presence of my forebears. I tucked my chin atop Dlane\rquote s head and let her regular breathing lull me into a light doze, and the Jokka in the carvings stepped out of the walls and danced and talked in the semi-reality of an eperu\rquote s dreams. I danced with them - the latest in a long chain of individuals leading back to the beginning of time. I was connected to them all, could see distinct faces and colors. They were each a Jokkad.\par
\par
     It seemed important that each one greeted me, had different eyes and a face of his or her or its own. But the meaning of it eluded me.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     We spent days walking through the ancient corridor, spending oil to reveal the beauty of the walls and their not-quite-writing. We spoke little, content to be together as we approached the fruition of the dream that had driven her out of her House and into my life.\par
\par
     On the ninth day the faintest glint of blue-white light competed with our amber lantern glow on the heads of the reliefs. Dlane stopped beside them and ran her fingers along the cracks and we stared at the shadows they cast - not just umber, but with a hint of lavender as well. Our eyes met, and hers were fierce and bright.\par
\par
     We quickened our pace after that. Each hour brought more and more complexity to the colors and shadows in the hall, soft hints of white and gray stained with purple. The air carried a new smell, salty and fresh. Our stop to refuel the lantern chafed at us both, and I nearly spilled the precious fluid from the stiffened leather neck.\par
\par
     When it came it took us both by surprise, for the hall simply ended and the night yawed open. I stumbled over a step and onto the cold ground on the other side, and as I lifted my lantern its glow receded.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Truedark!\rdblquote  Dlane cried. \ldblquote But we just got here!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Hush,\rdblquote  I said, and blew out the light. \ldblquote It will keep for morning. Imagine it, setasha. Dawn on the Birthwell. What would be better?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote But I want to see it!\rdblquote  I heard her feet scrape over the ground and I reached out, caught her as she staggered.\par
\par
     \ldblquote You will soon. But we must sleep first.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Her body molded itself to mine, pliable and warm. I sank to the step leading into the corridor, sloughing off our packs and supplies and engulfing her. As I wrapped my cape around us she touched her mouth to my chin, and I met her kiss with a whole heart. The fire in her mouth, the drip of her honeyed teeth, the utter darkness my open eyes could not pierce\'85 I drew joy from her with my hands, and they were mine: an eperu\rquote s, not an emodo\rquote s. As we sought one another\rquote s rapture, it finally came to me that I was eperu, and that all my life I\rquote d confused unusual and unwonted with unnatural. When her hands slid down to touch the bones above my tail I felt brief misgivings, but did not resist.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Are you sure?\rdblquote  she asked into my mouth.\par
\par
     I kissed her, and let that be my answer. In the truedark in the place where the Jokka had been born, I could not say no\'85 and through the haze of heat she cast on me like a second blanket my ears could hear a heart beating, a heart so large it could only be the World\rquote s.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     She slept in sticky contentment against my chest, our fur cape and blankets holding us together in a warm cocoon. I dozed as well, the shame that had plagued me about eperu passion not touching me in my joy over our arrival. The dawn would open the Birthwell for us at last.\par
\par
     I was startled to wake hours later, for eperu did not sleep so. Sitting up, I found myself alone in the blankets and sought my anadi\'85 and instead saw for the first time the wonder that was the birthplace of the Jokka.\par
\par
     The heart that I had cupped in my ears all night was not my imagination, but the sussurus of a vast body of water. I had never seen so much water; it rolled all the way to the horizon, lost there in a haze of blue-green against the pearl-cream sky. Over and over teal green waves crawled up the ground and back, bringing with them a sizzling froth that bled white and then vanished. These rolling waves repeated themselves across the surface of the water, and in amazement I rose and took a step forward. I would have expected all water to act as it did in a pool - still unless wind-ruffled. But this thing of constant motion, assayed by mysterious means and without seeming pattern, was beyond my comprehension.\par
\par
     I ran to the shore, my feet crunching across the pebbly surface of the ground. Bright white splashes fountained from my toes as I stepped into the tide, and the water tugged at my feathered ankles, matting the black hair into spikes.\par
\par
     Who could encompass such a wonder? My mouth fell open as I scanned the entirety of it. Roika had had a name for it, an ancient-sounding word that seemed to roll out of the very deepest of our memories. Isal. Ocean. Not the paltry thin \lquote sea\rquote  I\rquote d always heard. Ocean - this was the word with which tides and power and passion could be associated.\par
\par
     I brought the ancient token from beneath my blouse, into the light of dawn on the Birthwell\rquote s ocean. Now I could see what it was - a wave. A wave off the sea with its curling cap of foam. I lifted my face to the wind, tasted the heavy scent of salt and moisture and wilder things and closed my eyes.\par
\par
     My body shook: cold, not exhilaration. I had my blouse on, but no pants. Scampering back to the blanket I\rquote d discarded, I pulled on a pair, hooked my long-cloth around my hips and buttoned a vest on before going back out to search for my anadi. My feet crunched across the white beach, but in my distraction I ignored the wonders of the Birthwell. Where was Dlane?\par
\par
     And then I saw her, kneeling on the ground some distance from me. I jogged toward her, relieved.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Ke anadi!\rdblquote\par
\par
     She did not reply, and when I drew near she blew the joy from my heart like a wind does a flame. Bent there against the ground, a tunic untidily spilling to her thighs and her hand clenched, she was tension itself: as if she were trying not to weep. Alarmed, I faced her and crouched. \ldblquote Ke anadi?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Thenet,\rdblquote  she whispered.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Ke anadi!\rdblquote  I exclaimed, horrified at her small, empty voice. This was not the vibrant Dlane that had saved my life - not the bright anadi with whom I\rquote d fallen in love. \ldblquote What is it?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Oh, Thenet,\rdblquote  said she. \ldblquote Look. Look at the beach.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I did, finally; not at the ocean that had first captured my attention, not at her, but at the ground beneath my feet. I gasped, and my hand dropped to it, closed into a fist, and came up full of more wealth than I\rquote d seen ever in my life. This beach\'85 the Birthwell\'85 with wide eyes I glanced from one direction to the next. The Birthwell was mounded high with treasure.\par
\par
     It was a beach of shells.\par
\par
     I had probably crushed more money beneath my feet in the act of walking to her than Re\'f1a had ever earned. I lifted my gaze to hers in shock, and in her eyes\'85 my skin prickled and my mouth dried at the hollow despair there. I had seen her in misery and pain and fear, had seen her soul in her, had felt the fury and power of her struggles. But this emptiness\'85 this was the absence of the desire to struggle.\par
\par
     She held up one spiral shell. Almost as long as my finger, pearly white with a stripe of coral pink along its fringes, it would have bought Re\'f1a\rquote s whole household food for months. \ldblquote Look at this, Thenet.\rdblquote  Her other hand dipped to the ground once more and held up another: an ivory bivalve with a fringe of slate blue almost as broad as my palm. \ldblquote And this. And this. And this.\rdblquote  She pulled up one after another, shells so large and so pristine they would have represented decades\rquote  worth of earnings of a rich House.\par
\par
     \ldblquote What does it mean?\rdblquote  she asked at last. \ldblquote I could pick up a handful of shells anywhere on this beach and we could go back and Re\'f1a would be beyond the power of any House on Ke Bakil.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote So let us!\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote It is the Birthwell\rquote s gift to us!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote But what if others come to the Birthwell, Thenet?\rdblquote  She shook her head. \ldblquote You don\rquote t see, do you.\rdblquote  She let her head drop. \ldblquote Thenet\'85 what is the worth of a shell?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote What?\rdblquote  I said, feeling briefly the same confusion that had been my primary state of mind when I\rquote d first met her, had first heard her litany of incredible questions. \ldblquote Ke anadi\'85 it is money. It represents work and wealth.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Why don\rquote t we use pebbles?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I frowned. \ldblquote There are pebbles everywhere. Anyone could just pick one up and exchange it for goods. They wouldn\rquote t have earned\'85 it\'85.\rdblquote\par
\par
     As I trailed off, a faint and bitter smile touched her lips. \ldblquote Now you understand, don\rquote t you.\rdblquote  When I didn\rquote t reply, her sad, hollow voice continued, \ldblquote It has value only because it\rquote s rare\'85 and for us to know it is rare, we need the context of the world around us. Well look here, now, Thenet. What is the worth of a shell, if there are a hundred thousand?\par
\par
     \ldblquote I tell you what it is. Worthless.\rdblquote  Her soprano hardened on the last word, and then she was walking away, leaving me with a handful of shells.\par
\par
     I understood what she was saying. I thought I understood the source of her despair. She had come to the Birthwell for answers, and it had told her what she didn\rquote t want to know. How exactly her realization related to her situation I couldn\rquote t fathom. When I stood, my knees creaked and I turned my face back to the wind coming off the ocean. I didn\rquote t know how to help her. I didn\rquote t have any reply.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     That evening I drew on some of our stores, too listless to hunt for our meal. I crouched on the wealth of a nation, jaws working on a tough piece of jerky. I finished the short meal with half a flatcake and bundled up some meat and cake for my anadi before seeking her.\par
\par
     The Birthwell at dusk was riddled with tiny, beautiful shadows. The shells threw them off in multiple shades of blue, slivers more complex than any normal landscape. It was eerie and lovely beyond words, and I stopped several times to stare across a sea turned murky-violet with the twilight\rquote s veil.\par
\par
     I had sensed, coming here, the hands of the Trinity. Had sensed the realization that would set my people loose. But I couldn\rquote t grasp it, anymore than I could the relevance of Dlane\rquote s realization. What was I missing? Would I find it in time?\par
\par
     In time for what? I glanced back toward the mouth of the tunnel. I had stumbled onto the entrance to the Birthwell through luck and the gods\rquote  will alone. What danger could find us here?\par
\par
     I resumed my walk, troubled and chilled, and the breeze off the ocean slapped my mane around my neck. By the time I reached the figure slumped on the beach, my skin felt clammy where my clothing exposed it to the touch of the sea and the sky had darkened to a deep purple. I wished for a lantern, to imbue Dlane with an artificial warmth to replace the inner one that no longer animated her face.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Ke anadi?\rdblquote  I asked softly. When she did not reply, I sat next to her. \ldblquote I brought you dinner.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Again she gave no answer. I placed the bundle in front of her and looked for her hands. Finding one, I rolled it between mine to rub some of the cold out of it. \ldblquote Setasha, you must eat.\rdblquote\par
\par
     That, at last, prompted a response in a dull soprano. \ldblquote Why?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Because you\rquote ll sicken without it. We\rquote re far from home, my love. If you grow weak, it will be difficult for me to get you back safely.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Home? What home? Re\'f1a is gone.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Re\'f1a is not gone!\rdblquote  I exclaimed, startled. \ldblquote Dlane! What is wrong with you?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Everything is wrong with me,\rdblquote  Dlane said, looking away from my gaze. Her golden eyes were golden no longer, but a dull, pale brown. \ldblquote The world cannot be fought, Thenet. I am just one Jokkad in an ocean of Jokka. I am nothing, and the unnatural House I built with me.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Each word was a throwing claw, slamming into my breast. I dropped her hand in shock. \ldblquote Ke anadi!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote It\rquote s true,\rdblquote  she said, turning to me. The emptiness in her eyes was a knife in the wound in my heart. \ldblquote You do not know it yet, Thenet, but I am done. I am\'85 I am done.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I stared at her, jaw hanging open and ears splayed. Her body was the body of Dlane: the same golden tail splashed against the broken shells of the beach, the same smooth and lovely nose, the delicate ears, the curved hip and shoulder. But the spirit inside\'85 the spirit inside was not my anadi\rquote s.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Go, Thenet. You have vowed to see me safe wherever I went; to protect me. To see me happy. I took those vows, and I took you for my own, but I was wrong. You are your own person, beholden to no one, and owe no one your oaths. I release you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I stumbled to my feet and backed away from her, sickened; had the presence of mind to take several steps toward the tunnel before I began to run. I ran through the cold dark until my limbs pitched me to the ground, and there I retched, my rejection of her words and her change staining the pristine shells of the Birthwell.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
     Dlane and I withdrew from one another in the days that followed. I did not have the courage to face her, though I watched her leave the edge of the tunnel each day. I couldn\rquote t stand the unclean words, the strange spirit that inhabited her, and dozing next to her at night sent tremors of unease to walk my spine.\par
\par
     One day she rose and shed her clothing, walked naked into the dawn. That night she did not return to sleep with me, and I did not go after her. Some part of me had hoped that if I left her alone she would find herself again, but crouching beside the tunnel and gathering her spice-scented blouse to my nose, I knew, somewhere, that it was over.\par
\par
The days dragged on. I realized that if we were to leave, I would have to make the choice.\par
\par
     But I found the Birthwell entrancing. I sat beside the ocean and watched it breathe, listened to it sing and sigh and rustle. The hiss of the foam against the precious shells filled my ears to their brims. With the glass ornament pressed to my chest beneath my clothing, I stretched my body out beside the tide and gathered the water in my eyes.\par
\par
     On the eleventh morning it struck me suddenly that I had seen no birds in the Birthwell. That absence piqued my curiosity, and I scouted along the beach until well into the afternoon. By the time I stood with my feet in the ocean, I had come to the disturbing realization that not only were there no birds, there were no animals. No insects. No signs of life at all.\par
\par
     My eyes dropped to the ocean, and as in a doze-dream I bent to it and rested my hand on its restless surface. It slopped over my knuckles and I brought them to my lips, tasted, and gagged. And I noticed then, at last, the white crystals growing on the shells nearest the ocean\rquote s touch.\par
\par
     Salt. More salt in water than I\rquote d thought possible to suspend in it. The Birthwell was not just undrinkable\'85 it was lethal. Beauty, power, death and riches: truly this place brooked no weakness of soul, and wondered what weakness it had exploited in my anadi. Perhaps she would regain herself outside the Birthwell. I decided then that we would leave the next morning, and turned to the tunnel.\par
\par
     A figure already stood there, and I caught my breath. Had Dlane returned to herself at last? I jogged toward the tunnel, then ran, eager to see her, to hold her, to tell her it was time to fill our pockets with the Birthwell\rquote s final gift and go.\par
\par
     A long ululation rose from the tunnel, echoing in eerie assonance. I halted, almost losing my balance, and then like a wound bursting the tunnel disgorged the hunting beasts, dozens of \'f1edsu, all wailing and giggling as they darted toward me.\par
\par
     I jerked my head back up to the figure and saw in horror that it was not Dlane at all - not a female figure bundled in thick clothing, but a male. A male with skin like black glass and hair like the clouds over the mountains.\par
\par
     No. Not here. Not here! I didn\rquote t realize I was yelling until her name left my lips. \ldblquote Dlane! DLANE! RUN!\rdblquote\par
\par
     I didn\rquote t know where she was. I couldn\rquote t protect her. I veered as hard as I could and sprinted away from the beasts, but my capture was inevitable. They were fresh and I was not, and they were faster. There were no trees to climb, and the Birthwell\rquote s sheer cliff-faces mocked the very thought of ascending them. Shells sprayed from beneath my feet as I streaked, gasping, alongside the salt-laden waves.\par
\par
     In a fit of inspiration, I splashed into the ocean. The \'f1edsu ran to the edge and milled in frustration, and their moans and gibbering filled me with a fierce determination to live, to be free long enough to find Dlane and drag her away somehow.\par
\par
     I waded out as far as I dared, until I was chest-deep in the cold waves. My teeth chattered. The beasts dared not follow me. I couldn\rquote t leave. Impasse.\par
\par
     The crunch of shells heralded the approach of my adversary\'85 how appropriate that he came, destroying the equivalent of hundreds of months of work beneath his feet! He had chased us from the south, scattered Re\'f1a before him, and now he had cornered us at last, with our backs to the sea and him beneath us and our only exit.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Come from the water, ke Thenet,\rdblquote  Roika said.\par
\par
     \ldblquote N-n-no,\rdblquote  I said, clenching my fangs against the stutter. The water lapped at my ribs, leaching warmth from the marrow of my bones.\par
\par
     \ldblquote You will be sick,\rdblquote  he said, as if he were concerned for my welfare.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Go away, Roika. Leave us. We are not for you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Aren\rquote t you?\rdblquote  He chuckled and twisted, waving. I glanced that way wildly to see several more Jokka marching toward Roika\'85 and in their arms a limp figure, one familiar to my eyes, to my hands, to my body, to my soul.\par
\par
     \ldblquote NO!\rdblquote  I cried, lunging from the water. The \'f1edsu jumped up, eager to resume the chase, but it was Roika who caught my arm and pulled me to his side. The cold of the ocean was nothing to the cold of the air on my wet body, but it didn\rquote t matter. Nothing mattered.\par
\par
     She lifted her dull gold eyes to me. There was nothing in them - no fear, no anger, no will to fight. And seeing it, I sagged.\par
\par
     \ldblquote You see?\rdblquote  he said gently.\par
\par
     Where the rage came from in the face of the obvious end of all our dreams, our plans, I don\rquote t know. But in that moment I yelled and threw myself at the Jokka holding Dlane, and I would tear them from her limb by limb just to get her free. Time began to click toward the Moment\rquote s utter stillness, and even the ocean stopped breathing.\par
\par
     My hands had reached the throat of the first eperu when time shuddered back into motion. Roika\rquote s metal-dark eyes pinned me and I felt the hand on my collar jerking me back like a betrayal. The World had always stopped for me-\par
\par
     And then the blow to the back of my head dropped me, drowned me.\par
\par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     The trip from the Birthwell acquainted me with anadi sleep, for I only woke a few times, and all of those conscious moments were fraught with the weight of my despair and the sour taste of feverwait on my tongue. I knew the drug was used to keep a Jokkad unconscious until its body regained the strength to fight disease, but I\rquote d never heard of it being used this way - to keep someone asleep.\par
\par
     I dreamed. Lurid, disjointed things, full of brazen images of emodo and anadi together, their cries the piercing wails of the \'f1edsu. Montages of my life, of Re\'f1a and Het Narel and the caravan, all pasted together so that they seemed senseless, without context or meaning.\par
\par
     The dreams exhausted me. The rock of the wagon beneath my body when I woke brought bile to my tongue, but I had nothing to vomit in my nausea. I was never awake long, and the Jokkad with the powder was the only other person I saw. It came to me that I should try to escape, but my hands and feet had been bound. Even if they hadn\rquote t been, I would have been too weak to stand.\par
\par
     I endured the waking minutes and slept the journey through.\par
\par
     The end announced itself when food replaced my drug doses, until one day I found the wagon had ceased to sway beneath my body and that I was strong enough to lift my head.\par
\par
     The eperu that entered the wagon helped me to sit upright without untying me, then combed my mane and tail and braided them. Its attentions struck me as ludicrous, but I did not protest as it decorated me with beads, stripped my clothing, ran a warm wet cloth over my sweat-stained body. It enlisted the aid of several fellows when it came time to dress me in clean clothing, but I did not fight them. I need more information on where we were, how many people were guarding us, how badly Dlane had fared on the voyage. I needed to plan.\par
\par
     Two eperu led me from the wagon. My knees and ankles shook beneath my weight, and as I stepped down to the ground they threatened to give way beneath me. Despite the meals I could remember being fed, my muscles trembled and my flesh felt twice as heavy.\par
\par
     I looked up and almost groaned aloud. Not Het Narel\'85 oh, anywhere but here, where once Re\'f1a had stood so proudly. And we were on the northeastern outskirts of town, near the Green but still far enough that I trembled at the thought of having to walk all that way between two eperu, obviously a prisoner.\par
\par
     But where was Dlane? I was the only one standing in the center of Roika\rquote s eperu.\par
\par
     I had no more time to consider it. The eperu took me by the arms and set me on the path into the Green and I followed. To my shame, I found I had to lean on them several times throughout the journey. Several Jokka passed us, but none of them met my frantic gaze. Het Narel! Had they abandoned us so quickly? Did they not remember Re\'f1a?\par
\par
I was panting when we reached our destination, and sweat slicked my sides\'85 but that had no effect on my heart as I stared up, and up, and up at the tall, grand House that stood alone even amid the Green\rquote s luxurious array of tall, grand Houses. All of three stories high on the surface and probably another underground if the doors angled against the ground were any sign, it was made of brick and stone, combined artistically to create a mottled gradient marching from blue-gray at the base to cream-gold at its roof. Stringcourses of silvery rock separated each floor, and the windows had been barred with the clearest glass I\rquote d ever seen. My heart turned in my chest at the sight of such wealth.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Come,\rdblquote  the eperu said to me, and not unkindly. They had allowed me to take my fill of the facade, and perhaps it was part of their orders to well and truly intimidate me after walking me several blocks while weakened. As clear as the strategy was, I was annoyed to discover that it worked. Tired, horrified at how much my body had atrophied in the few weeks I\rquote d been drugged, torn by worry over the absence of Dlane and now presented with this exalted magnificence, my morale plummeted somewhere around my toes.\par
\par
     I followed the eperu into House Edze.\par
\par
     They guided me to a large room and left me there, closing the door. Besides that door, there was another on the wall facing a large window with all that clear, clear glass. I walked to it and spread my fingers across it, my other hand straying to the bump beneath my blouse where the glass token of the Birthwell\rquote s wave sat against my ribs. The eperu dressing me had left my two necklaces alone, for which I was grateful\'85 and angry about that gratitude, since it illustrated how deeply under another\rquote s power I was.\par
\par
     A long sofa ran along one of the walls, but I was too nervous to sit. I paced instead, my shaking legs protesting every step.\par
\par
     The door opened, produced Roika. He\rquote d dressed well, in black pants and a black vest that emphasized the crisp, bright white of his blouse; and the ember-red embroidery lining the buttons and cuffs of his outfit, revisited in the expensive knotwork on his decorative black long-cloth.\par
\par
     I wanted so much to attack him, but had not even the energy to step forward. So instead I let the chill in my voice speak in lieu of action. \ldblquote Where is my anadi?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote In my care, ke eperu. Where she will remain.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote She is not yours. Nor am I. We are more than toys for your House; more than another anadi and another eperu for your use. We were meant for something greater. You know that,\rdblquote  I said.\par
\par
     Roika cleared his throat. \ldblquote I think that moot. Don\rquote t you? It\rquote s a little late for such statements. I have her. I have you. Your feelings are irrelevant. Your destinies even more so.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I will take her from you, emodo,\rdblquote  I said, not even bothering with the honorific. My words burned in my mouth. \ldblquote Look well on us now, for you might have us now but you will not keep us.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Do you truly think you have a chance?\rdblquote  he asked, one brow lifting askance. \ldblquote You\rquote re not thinking clearly, ke Thenet. I thought for certain you had come to your senses on the long ride here. That perhaps you were prepared to join me, to bring our people back to their former glory. Real glory, not this false, unnatural ideology the two of you persist in holding.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Glory! What is glory, riding on the wombs of unwilling anadi?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Now you sound just like your strident little Dlane.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote She is larger than you, Roika,\rdblquote  I hissed.\par
\par
     He bared his teeth. \ldblquote Is she, Thenet? Why then is she willing to sacrifice the greater good of her people to save herself from pain? That speaks more to me of fear and selfishness than it does of a largeness of heart.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote She is wiser than you,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote She knows that no empire can be built without the consent of its members. Otherwise it is no just empire at all, but a collection of slaves serving their master. What good is that?\rdblquote  I turned my back on him, trying to express my disdain without moving much. I felt distinctly queasy. \ldblquote Give her back to me and let me go, or you will rue it. You will not have her.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Not while you are Thenet Re\'f1a-eperu, eh?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I let my silence speak for me.\par
\par
     The door opened behind me, and I heard his low murmur. Then the shuffle of feet as several Jokka filed into the room. Was he planning to take me prisoner again? Lock me up somewhere? Surely he knew better\'85 and too, there were far too many feet for the amount of eperu necessary to subdue me. We both knew just how fragile my health was.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Turn around, ke eperu.\rdblquote  Was there sadness in his rumbling bass? A pain? What could inspire such an emotion in him? Anxious, I turned.\par
\par
     They stood in a line, the perfume of their unbound hair striking me just as the sight of their bound hands did: sweet Witha, head hanging; Degerra and Osa resting against one another. Ne\'f1a and Koise, hands clasped and eyes staring wearily into a distance. Linuthet, Adzani, Sekootet, Madad, Iihaf, they were all there. Even the eperu came, their hands tied behind their backs and their feet hobbled: Fooshet, Larin, and my beloved Seper. All Re\'f1a\rquote s folk, save Dlane and I\'85 and of them, Witha, Ne\'f1a, Koise, Madad and Osa all in various stages of pregnancy. From the curve of Koise\rquote s belly, he\rquote d captured them only a week or two after Dlane and I had left\'85 and though there was still intelligence in their eyes, it had been smothered by resignation.\par
\par
     \ldblquote No,\rdblquote  I said, but my voice could barely produce the sound. My hand lifted, shaking.\par
\par
     Seper said softly, \ldblquote I am sorry, ke eperu. I tried.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote So you see,\rdblquote  Roika said as two of his eperu entered the room, standing on either side of the line of anadi, \ldblquote I have met your challenge, ke eperu. I could not have your Dlane while you were Thenet Re\'f1a-eperu\'85 but Re\'f1a is gone.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote No!\rdblquote  Louder this time, and though I shook to my bones I was running, out the door, away from the vacant stares in the anadi I had once gathered flowers with, cooked for, laughed and protected. It was not Seper who had failed, it was me. I had destroyed Re\'f1a. I should have taken them with us. I should have left them better fortified. I should have done something-\par
\par
     \ldblquote Thenet, stop!\rdblquote\par
\par
     I ignored him, dashed down a flight of stairs. His silhouette appeared at the top. \ldblquote Thenet, you don\rquote t want to go down here.\rdblquote\par
\par
     There were two doors. I choose the one directly in front of me and ran in, hoping it would have one of the exterior doors I\rquote d seen outside the House\'85 and stopped as if gutted.\par
\par
     The room must have encompassed most of the floor, its plain walls unadorned. It held six breeding harnesses, the sloping chairs bolted to the ground, their bizarre combination of leather straps and stone limbs meant to prop up and immobilize an anadi in a position that allowed the emodo ease of use and safety, in case she grew violent.\par
\par
     \ldblquote I told you,\rdblquote  he said from behind me.\par
\par
     I whirled to face him. There was no way out of this chamber of horrors save past him, and when I stopped moving my body resumed quivering and my nausea returned full-force. My chances of escaping were slim.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Roika. Don\rquote t do this to her,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote Don\rquote t make an animal out of her.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Oh, stop it!\rdblquote  he said, and at last irritation rasped in his reply. \ldblquote It\rquote s over, Thenet. I\rquote ve won. And we both know that I must.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote It\rquote s not right,\rdblquote  I said, my love for her welling, drowning me. \ldblquote Can\rquote t you let her be? You\rquote ve already taken Re\'f1a. Isn\rquote t that enough?\rdblquote\par
\par
     He shrugged, the motion rippling through his gray tail. \ldblquote She\rquote ll be happier after she\rquote s been bred a few times.\rdblquote\par
\par
     And it was true - captured and helpless, Dlane would be miserable. Mindlessness would almost be kinder.\par
\par
     What was I thinking?\par
\par
     \ldblquote Let her go,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote You can keep me.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote You\rquote re useless to me unless you want to be here,\rdblquote  Roika said. \ldblquote Dlane can serve me even unwilling.\rdblquote  He canted his head. \ldblquote I thought once that you might be willing. Are you?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Would you let her go?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Maybe.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Maybe!\rdblquote  My hands clenched into fists. \ldblquote Roika! You don\rquote t need to be greedy. You can have the pick of any anadi or eperu or emodo! It\rquote s there in the wealth of this House - you could buy contracts that would beggar every House on this Green. You don\rquote t need us. Don\rquote t need the pittance of a single anadi, a tiny House!\rdblquote\par
\par
     He laughed. \ldblquote You call the smartest anadi I\rquote ve met a pittance? A scrap to be thrown away? You underestimate me, Thenet.\rdblquote  He advanced, and I fell back until my spine struck the foot-rests of one of the harnesses. \ldblquote Face what has happened, eperu. I have absorbed your House. I have taken your anadi. I have taken you. And I will make my empire and save the Jokka. You have a choice now. You can join me, help me rebuild our race, or I will have to discard you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Something in the way he said \lquote discard\rquote  sent a cold shiver down my back. I groped at the harness to stay upright. \ldblquote We wouldn\rquote t oppose you, if you let us go,\rdblquote  I said, hoarse.\par
\par
     \ldblquote You wouldn\rquote t?\rdblquote  He laughed. \ldblquote How na\'efve do you think me, Thenet? Your anadi embraces a philosophy that would give every female the choice of whether or not to breed. How many do you think would choose to do so, given that choice? Enough to keep our race alive? Ha! Look at us, eperu! Look well! We have dwindled since the ancestors arrived from beneath the mountain! Not just in stature, but in numbers! Do you think we will give our race a fighting chance on life again if all the anadi decide that they will not give us children?\rdblquote  He shook his head, touched my chin. \ldblquote Oh, no. No, Thenet. By existing, you oppose the growth of the Jokka. Do you want that on your conscience?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote No,\rdblquote  I whispered.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Then join me!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I can\rquote t!\rdblquote  I cried. \ldblquote Roika, I can\rquote t do it!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Then you are against me.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote No!\rdblquote\par
\par
     He snarled and let go of my face. \ldblquote What is it then? Do you want the Jokka to go forth to their destiny? To grow as a race? Or do you want the anadi to have the freedom of choice? It must be one or the other, eperu. Choose!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I can\rquote t!\rdblquote  I said, and from the turmoil in my heart I couldn\rquote t. Dlane, or the Jokka? The individual, or the species? My love, or my people? How could I make that choice? How could anyone, and stay sane?\par
\par
     Roika\rquote s eyes shuttered instantly, darkened to the roiling gray of the storm. I had never seen him so angry and I leaned away, shaking. \ldblquote Coward! I thought you were strong! I thought there was something in you. But I see now that you are as strong as the other sniveling cowards who have let our race decline to this darkness! Can\rquote t choose, can you? As mindless a follower as any anadi, are you? That\rquote s what you should have been born! Anadi, destined for drooling stupidity! Anadi, mindless follower! Dlane led you by the hand, and now that she\rquote s gone you are unable to make your own decisions! Well, I\rquote ll show you your proper place-\ldblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Roika,\rdblquote  I said, terror gumming up my throat as his hands reached for me. But with the terror came something else - the knowledge that violence would destroy him, that this first step on the path would undo whatever good he intended for the Jokka. I do not know whether it was fear of him, or fear for him, that made me cry out. \ldblquote Roika! No!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I\rquote ll give you what you want! I\rquote ll take your choices from you!\rdblquote  His hands closed on my throat, wrapped around my waist, and he threw me hard onto the stone beneath the harness straps. I writhed desperately, but even healthy I was no match for him. As I struggled, he pushed me down and grabbed my arms, strapping them outstretched to the stone limbs of the sloping chair. A crushing pressure on my back ground the glass token into pieces beneath my chest, and within moments I\rquote d been immobilized with my rump in the air and the blood rushing to my head.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Roika,\rdblquote  I said, \ldblquote Don\rquote t-\ldblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Shut UP!\rdblquote  A hand grabbed my tail and jerked it up, and my clothing ripped from my bottom with a tear like a shriek. I pulled helplessly at the straps, but they\rquote d been made to withstand the bucking of wild anadi and I was weaker than the weakest in my state. \ldblquote I am taking your choices from you, since you deny them. Anadi you deserve to be, and anadi is how you\rquote ll be treated!\rdblquote\par
\par
     I scrabbled at the limbs, trying to pull myself forward, away from the hands that grasped my buttocks and tail, and then I screamed as his staff found me and thrust into me.\par
\par
     I had never imagined, in the pitiful few times Dlane had pressed gently against me with her fingertips, a pain as excruciating as the feel of him jerking in me, all the way until his hips thumped against the bones of my rump. A sob burst from my mouth, surprising me, and I pressed my cheek against the stone as he rocked back and jerked in again, throwing me against the leather straps. \ldblquote Stop! Rrrrroika!\rdblquote\par
\par
     He growled and flexed in me and then the pummeling continued, harder and faster as I wept in anger and pain, writhing against the harness.\par
\par
     When the rapture ran down my back, cold as rain, my nails dug into the stone and I screamed again. There was no more thought. There was heat, and pleasure, and pain, and raw wrath.\par
\par
     And again.\par
\par
     And again.\par
\par
     And his growls, and cries.\par
\par
     And the acrid tang of blood in my mouth, and the scent of it in the air.\par
\par
     And the cut of the leather against my skin.\par
\par
     The cold of the stone. The heat of his body.\par
\par
     And his roar-\par
\par
     And then nothing. Dark, silent, still nothing.\par
\par
 \par
\par
