\par
 \par
\par
     \ldblquote It\rquote s waking,\rdblquote  a voice said from very far away.\par
\par
     \ldblquote I have the tea.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Good. Ke eperu, open your mouth. Yes\'85 yes, good. Drink this, it will heal you-\ldblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote It\rquote s working.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Yes. It needs to sleep. Only nature can fix this\'85.\rdblquote\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     \ldblquote Why is it weeping? It should be asleep.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Did you dose it with the tea?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Yes.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I\rquote m frightened, ke eperu. The fever-\ldblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Hush, hush, ke emodo. The gods saw it into this. The gods will not abandon it now.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I hope so. Because the gods will have much to answer for if it dies.\rdblquote\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     Voices. I knew the voices. They comforted me - they were good voices, safe ones. I floated, barely aware of them. It seemed unreasonably hot; was it summer? Surely not already.\par
\par
     I slept.\par
\par
     I remained in that not-quite-awareness for a timelessness that needed no measure. But one day, inexplicably, I chose to wake, and the sweet tea that barely hid the sour flavor of the feverwait did not depress me into darkness. I was lying in a bed piled high with pillows beside a window-no, it had no glass. This was a wagon, one with half its above-floor space boarded off. My body lay somewhere beneath me, but I could barely feel it.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Hello?\rdblquote  I croaked.\par
\par
     A shape stirred near the edge of the wagon, and I found myself staring into Barit\rquote s brown eyes. He straightened so quickly I thought he would fall. \ldblquote Ke eperu!\rdblquote\par
     \ldblquote Barit?\rdblquote  I said hoarsely. \ldblquote What are you doing here?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Ilushet-Ilushet!\rdblquote  Barit called out the side of the wagon. Then he scrambled to my side and gently touched my shoulder. \ldblquote You know me?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Of course I know you,\rdblquote  I said. My voice refused to stabilize; sometimes a husky rasp, other times a breathy tonelessness no louder than the wind\rquote s whisper. \ldblquote Where am I? What am I doing here?\rdblquote\par
\par
     Ilushet stepped up onto the platform and joined Barit at my side. Its hand drifted over my brow, down to my chest above the blankets. \ldblquote How do you feel?\rdblquote\par
     \ldblquote Tired. Empty,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote Cousin, what happened?\rdblquote\par
\par
     Ilushet\rquote s eyes meeting mine were too calm. I could see the quiver in its chin. \ldblquote When you did not arrive as you promised from the Birthwell, I investigated and found House Edze\rquote s people on the road with wagons. I followed them, suspicious. The day after they arrived to Het Narel, Barit found you outside the town, so close to death we weren\rquote t sure we could take you back from the Void. Barit brought you to me, and we struck into the wilderness to hide from those who wanted you dead. Here you wavered between life and afterlife, and only today have you been able to look at us and speak.\rdblquote\par
\par
     It came crushing back to me. Roika. Dlane. Re\'f1a. \ldblquote No,\rdblquote  I said, sitting up. \ldblquote No, Dlane\'85 we have to go back for her!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote We can\rquote t,\rdblquote  Ilushet said. \ldblquote She is lost, cousin. It is too late.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote It can\rquote t be too late!\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote We can still save her. We have to save her!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote It has been over a month since we found you,\rdblquote  Ilushet said. \ldblquote Thenet, it is too late!\rdblquote\par
     \ldblquote A month?\rdblquote  My jaw gaped open. \ldblquote A month?\rdblquote\par
\par
     Barit whispered, \ldblquote You were near-dead, ke eperu. He had ripped you open from the inside.\rdblquote  His red skin had taken on a gray cast, and he looked shaken. \ldblquote By all rights you should have died that night we found you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Dead?\rdblquote  I asked weakly. \ldblquote I was almost dead?\rdblquote\par
\par
     Gently, Ilushet lifted the covers from me and let me see my wasted body, the bandages around my hips and upper thighs. A smaller strip ran over my chest, and a visceral memory gripped me: shards of glass slashing into my flesh.\par
\par
     Just as Roika had\'85\par
\par
     I turned my face and pressed the back of my hand to my mouth to stop the weeping before it started.\par
\par
     \ldblquote We\rquote ve slathered the parts of you we can touch with root of kema,\rdblquote  Ilushet said, its voice still soft. \ldblquote You shouldn\rquote t feel any pain. But we have not been able to give you food, for fear it would undo the healing.\rdblquote  Its throat pulsed in a sudden swallow. \ldblquote Broth only. And tea. You bled passing even that.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Am I\'85 will I live?\rdblquote\par
\par
     The two exchanged glances. Then Barit said, \ldblquote We think so. But you must not move much. The flesh\'85 the flesh is mending, but we could not salve it or spread ointment on it to speed its healing without further aggravating its condition. You must lie still until it is safe for you to move, and continue drinking the broth we make you until it is safe for you to eat.\rdblquote\par
\par
     But my thoughts were already on Dlane, imagining her immobilized in the horrible sloping chair, the leather straps pinning her down, her struggles as Roika-no. No. \ldblquote How long?\rdblquote\par
\par
     They didn\rquote t answer, and I looked up at them, teeth burning with unshed tears. \ldblquote How long?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Another month, perhaps,\rdblquote  Ilushet said. \ldblquote I\'85 I have never seen anyone recover from wounds as grievous as yours, cousin.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Too long,\rdblquote  I whispered. \ldblquote Oh, Dlane, Dlane, setasha!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote It is too late for her,\rdblquote  Ilushet said, touching my shoulder. \ldblquote We must save you, cousin.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I gulped back my sob.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     From the prison that was my bed I watched the black night cover the land like an emodo an anadi. The nausea that lurched through my stomach had nothing to do with the tea or the drugs. I pressed my hand to my nose, blocking the scent of the soil, of the thin, acid scent of the oil Ilushet used on the wood of its caravans.\par
\par
     Dlane. My beautiful Dlane. She had been right that evening in the Birthwell. She had somehow seen her doom and Re\'f1a\rquote s fall in the shells of the beach. What power of premonition, what gods-touched ability had been triggered by the sight of our ancient birthplace?\par
\par
     And why, oh why, had it crushed her when so little before had daunted her?\par
\par
     I rested my head against the pillow, inhaled the pungent mix of herbs stuffed into it, and was too weary to weep.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Thenet?\rdblquote\par
\par
     His voice sounded so tentative. I could not turn to face him, but remembering the pain of Dlane\rquote s silence, I answered, \ldblquote I am here, Barit.\rdblquote\par
\par
     The stool squeaked as he pulled it next to the bed. \ldblquote I hope I am not intruding.\rdblquote\par
\par
     As he said it I realized he wasn\rquote t, and that his company was welcome. How long had it been since I\rquote d thought of emodo as anything but enemies? Barit had saved my life. \ldblquote No. No, I am glad of the distraction.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I\rquote m sorry about\'85 the way things happened. I\rquote m sorry I didn\rquote t come sooner.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote How could you have known?\rdblquote  I murmured. \ldblquote Re\'f1a was simply gone one day, and without a note.\rdblquote  I turned my face to his, saw in the dark the crimp of his brows and the rigid muscles in his jaw, marks of an anguish that twisted my already wounded heart. \ldblquote But maybe you can tell me something. How did\'85,\rdblquote  I paused, hardened my voice, \ldblquote How did he find them?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote A week after Re\'f1a vanished, Transactions declared it Broken. Since none of the members of the House disputed it, Re\'f1a\rquote s records were closed. After that, he sent parties to search, and found the anadi and eperu in the ruins.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote And then he took them,\rdblquote  I said, baring my teeth.\par
\par
     Barit shook his head. \ldblquote No. He bought their contracts.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote What?\rdblquote  My body shook in rage. How could he do that? Make it legitimate? How dare he attempt to mask his intentions with such subterfuge? The audacity! \ldblquote Surely he didn\rquote t.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote He did. And, Thenet\'85,\rdblquote  Barit paused, glancing down at his hands and ears sagging, \ldblquote Thenet, some of them went willingly.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Willingly? Barit-\ldblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I talked with Seper,\rdblquote  he said, his voice low. \ldblquote Just a few times when this began to fall to. They did not want to be Roika\rquote s trophies\'85 but given the circumstance, some were willing to fight to be the ka\'f1a-befidzu and win a limited freedom from the harness that way. Better a ruler over anadi in a House destined for empire than to live their lives, running, fearful of every shadow.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote But we were going to come back-\ldblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote But you didn\rquote t.\rdblquote  Barit looked up at me, and the faint starlight traced the marks of tears over his chin. \ldblquote Please, ke eperu. They were in extremis.\rdblquote\par
\par
     He asked me to forgive them, despite the anger, despite the helplessness and the guilt I felt for abandoning them to Roika, to the world they could not manage without Dlane\rquote s light\'85 and yet, even Dlane\rquote s light had gone out, leaving only one battered, ugly eperu behind. Who would save the anadi? Who would save the Jokka? Not Roika. He would build his empire, and it would crush our people beneath the weight of it.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Did I tell you how I came to be on this road, ke emodo?\rdblquote  I said at last.\par
\par
     \ldblquote No.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I was a favored eperu within House Mated at home, and all the eperu had gone to the hunt when the matron began to give birth. The child was stopped in her, and the anadi did not have the strength to turn it. By the time they brought me to her, it was too late. I turned the child, but it had died.\rdblquote  I stared into the dark, letting my eyes drift out of focus. \ldblquote They cast me out of Mated for killing the babe, and Dlane saved me. I did not understand why, then.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Do you now?\rdblquote  he asked, his voice so soft I almost lost it to the sound of the insects peeping and the slough of the breeze.\par
\par
     The night had a murky cast, with thick, muddy clouds obscuring the stars; in the uncertainty, I nevertheless knew the lines of the hills, curves fruitful and sweet. The dark did not take them away, though I could not see them.\par
\par
I looked back at him. \ldblquote Yes. Yes, I understand.\rdblquote  I closed my eyes. \ldblquote She saved me then, ke emodo, before I had the power to save myself. Now\'85 now I must save her.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Barit touched my hand and said nothing.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     They had my bag, brought from the Birthwell. I did not have the strength to search its contents, though occasionally I glanced at it. Hunched in a corner, battered and patched, it had seen more of Ke Bakil than most Jokka.\par
\par
     When the constant bed-rest began to chafe, I found the courage to go through the bag. My grasping fingers found a pouch of tools, familiar and soothing. I pulled it into the light and rested it on my lap. From within it, I brought forth a carving knife and a misshapen piece of bone, its edge still jagged.\par
\par
     I touched the corner, felt it prick my skin. I pressed my finger to my teeth and studied the bone piece. The blood in my mouth was sweet.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Dlane,\rdblquote  I whispered. \ldblquote You released me from my vows\'85 but every oath I made to you, I broke. No more. I will take it back.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Then I set the knife to bone, and caressed it, and made plans.\par
\par
 \par
\par
#\par
\par
 \par
\par
     Ilushet was right. It was middle of spring before I was strong enough to walk and to eat real food. A few weeks after I ate my first slice of bread, I could finally pass waste without pain.\par
\par
     From then on I played a game: how much activity could I withstand before exhaustion claimed me? How far could I push my body without hurting myself? Ilushet watched me, I knew, but I ignored the eperu and forced myself to run, to throw the spear and to exercise muscles that had atrophied.\par
\par
     Yes, Ilushet watched\'85 but it was Barit who helped me. He wrestled with me, ran races, danced and fought mock battles, accompanied me on my solitary hunts and dogged my shadow. Perhaps he thought I didn\rquote t see - that as emodo, he was as strong as I, but could not match my endurance, and our bouts and our sessions ended when he tired, not when I did. He kept me from pushing too hard. I welcomed his company anyway.\par
\par
     I found the Void in solitude, and in my thoughts. I danced with my shadow and understood - slowly - that I had become the light. The mandorla was mine now. I could douse it, and choose the way of the World as it was, the Ke Bakil that existed, the empire that Roika would build\'85 or I could take on the mantle that Dlane had left for me, invite the Brightness into my soul.\par
\par
     Pirouetting to meet my shadow with the new throwing claws I had made, I thought that the Trinity made no sense when a Jokkad could move between them thus.\par
\par
     I wrestled with Barit, sought my strength, and felt time\rquote s passage, each day a new wound, each day a new scar that Edze would address.\par
\par
     The caravan was immobile while I recovered. I wondered at this once aloud, and Barit smiled.\par
\par
     \ldblquote The Birthwell,\rdblquote  he said.\par
\par
     Clever Ilushet. It had collected enough shell to make this year\rquote s trade unnecessary. But what did that mean to the towns that depended on its caravan for the goods they otherwise would not have? My heart turned as I thought it, for I knew that Roika would have had the same concern.\par
\par
     Roika. Dlane. Oh, beloved!\par
\par
     I stood in the copper-bright sunlight of a late spring afternoon, a spear in one hand ground into the earth and the other hanging loosely at my side. I wore only a long-cloth, decorated in gold and gray and blue - her colors - and the medallion she\rquote d given me tied to my throat. The wind tousled my loose dark hair, chased it over my shoulders. I took a long breath, and at last felt the strength I had lost.\par
\par
     \ldblquote You\rquote re going back for her now, aren\rquote t you,\rdblquote  Barit said.\par
\par
     I glanced over my shoulder at him as he stepped up to join me. My ears swept back. \ldblquote Ke emodo\'85.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote It\rquote s useless to deny it, Thenet,\rdblquote  he said. He drew abreast of me. \ldblquote You have been pushing and pushing. I have been with you. And when you stop, you always end up staring to the east. Toward Het Narel. It doesn\rquote t take great intelligence.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I have to,\rdblquote  I said. My voice trembled. \ldblquote Barit, I left her there. I abandoned her.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote No, Thenet. You were\'85 were tortured and nearly killed, then left to die. You were ejected from House Edze, with the intent that you wouldn\rquote t live to return. You didn\rquote t abandon her. You were given no choice.\rdblquote\par
\par
     No choice. My fingers curled into a fist, pressed my claws to my palm. \ldblquote I said I would save her, Barit. This time I will keep my promise. Don\rquote t try to stop me.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Stop you!\rdblquote  he exclaimed. \ldblquote Are you blind, ke eperu? I\rquote m not going to stop you, I\rquote m going to go with you!\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote What?\rdblquote  I stared at him. \ldblquote Barit, it won\rquote t be safe. He tried to kill me before, the House is guarded\'85 by the Void, emodo, he took all of Re\'f1a!\rdblquote\par
\par
     He took my arm in his hand and looked at me with level eyes, his jaw tense. \ldblquote You forget, ke eperu. I knew her too.\rdblquote\par
\par
     In his face I saw the truth of it - that Dlane had not only inspired love in her wayward eperu, the one who had failed her, but in others as well. And all of us too blind to see it, to see that she could make an emodo love her and want to see her safe.\par
\par
     That maybe, this is how the empire could work together with Dlane\rquote s vision of freedom\'85 for if the emodo loved the anadi, they would try harder to give them the choices they needed to be full persons.\par
\par
     Was that all it needed? Love, that strange and unspeakable thing? Had we destroyed our people simply by refusing to touch what we could not understand? For I understood now as I never had the vast gulf that separated emodo from anadi from eperu. Understood the gulf - and the bridge that could cross it. The bridge created by the whisper, \lquote setasha\rquote , by a loving touch, by trust\'85 yes, by pleasure.\par
\par
     It seemed a ridiculous notion, that trust and love between sexes might be the key to freedom. Perhaps that\rquote s why it had never been tried.\par
\par
     Your kingdom, Dlane. Your empire. It might work. I would make it work.\par
\par
     Oh, Dlane.\par
\par
     \ldblquote We\rquote ll leave tonight,\rdblquote  I said.\par
\par
     \ldblquote We must tell Ilushet.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote It will try to stop us-\ldblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote It won\rquote t,\rdblquote  Barit said. \ldblquote And we might need its help to get out.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I shivered, remembering through the haze of memory the pain and weakness of my state at Roika\rquote s hands. There was no way we would survive that alone. \ldblquote Very well.\rdblquote\par
\par
     We had a plan a few hours later - Ilushet and its people would await us on the edge of Het Narel and enter it only if we\rquote d been gone for longer than a day. Barit and I, meanwhile, would go to House Edze. The emodo would wait outside to be my safety while I entered and attempted to convince Roika I was ready to join him. At an opportune moment, I would find Dlane, lead her out, and run with Barit for the edge of town.\par
\par
     There would be risk in it. But I could think of no other way to gain entrance to House Edze long enough to locate Dlane. And as much as I desired to free the rest of Re\'f1a, I had to admit the truth: I may not have the time. If I could save them all, I would. But if I could not, it would be Dlane\'85 or myself. This thing would not end without a resolution.\par
\par
     Barit and I set out as the evening settled. Back at last to Het Narel.\par
\par
     Traveling down familiar roads sent shivers down my spine. I appeared unarmed; I could not maintain my fiction with a spear in my hand, though I carried insurance hidden on my body. Barit used his spear as a staff, flanking me as we walked through the clumps of Jokka that wandered the streets, most in search of their homes after a long day\rquote s work.\par
\par
     At the gate to the Green we separated, Barit to circle around behind Edze and I to approach it openly. My heart beat a nervous staccato as I passed the flowered garden, its perfume riding the warm evening\rquote s winds. I had a long time to consider the many possible ways our plan could fail, but I felt no fear. I was coming back for her. One way or another this thing would end\'85 and the running would be over.\par
\par
     The two eperu at the door to Edze called, \ldblquote Halt!\rdblquote  to me as I stepped up to them. I obediently stopped.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Who are you?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I am come with business with the Head of Household,\rdblquote  I said.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Who are you?\rdblquote  the second one repeated.\par
\par
     I took a breath and licked my teeth against the foulness of the words about to leave my mouth. \ldblquote I am Thenet. Thenet Edze-eperu.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Expressionless, one of them walked inside. I waited, forcing myself not to fidget. I\rquote d worn my best: a blouse bleached impeccably white tucked into black breeches, and a vest and matching long-cloth of crimson, gray, black and gold. The Trifold\rquote s medallion nestled against my throat, my earring flat against my mane.\par
\par
The eperu reappeared in the dark doorway. \ldblquote Come in.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I entered. I was guided through the halls, this time to a different room, and left outside the door. \ldblquote Knock,\rdblquote  the eperu said, \ldblquote And wait to enter.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I shrugged, rapped my open hand against the extravagant wooden door.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Come in.\rdblquote\par
\par
     A shiver ran through me at the deep bass - fear, anger, and a slender flame of pleasure. I gritted my teeth and composed myself, then walked inside.\par
\par
     It was a study of sorts; a desk, a pot of ink releasing its nutty odor, expensive wooden tablets with their reusable wax surfaces scattered across its surface. A lamp lit the room, which had two chairs. Decorative rugs in amber, gold, cream and black adorned the walls, proudly displaying the same symbol over and over: Edze\rquote s house sigil, no doubt.\par
\par
     He was seated behind the table, and when I walked in he stood. His face never changed, but in his eyes I could see him true: he hadn\rquote t expected me to live.\par
\par
     I strode to him, pressed one hand against his chest, and then kissed him with an open mouth, and in shock he answered me - shock first, and then hunger. I had to make him believe I was real, and that I wanted him. That I was for him. Ilushet, Barit and I had all agreed this was the fastest way to establish his trust.\par
\par
     I hadn\rquote t anticipated how much it would revolt me, and how deeply I would answer his hunger. The male had almost killed me, but I\rquote d never met a closer match for me. He had been the Void to my World, just as Dlane was my Brightness. I could not deny him that place\'85 and perhaps it was that admission that convinced him, that made him pull me into his arms and drink from me as if he\rquote d been needing it for too long.\par
\par
     But I had ceased to be the World weeks ago. He would learn it soon enough.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Edze-eperu,\rdblquote  he said huskily when we parted mouths. He didn\rquote t let go of me. \ldblquote I thought it was a joke.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote It was a liberty,\rdblquote  I said, grinning with teeth. \ldblquote I wanted you to know my purpose.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I didn\rquote t think,\rdblquote  he started, then stopped. \ldblquote I am surprised to find you here.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Don\rquote t dissemble, ke emodo. You are surprised to find me alive, you wanted to say.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Yes.\rdblquote  His hand crept up to my hair, tangled in my flat black braid, loosened it.\par
\par
     \ldblquote I am tougher than the anadi you put in the harnesses, Roika,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote Didn\rquote t you think of that?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote You came back,\rdblquote  he murmured into my ear. His breath was hot and sweet with the wet of my fangs. \ldblquote I didn\rquote t think\'85 even if you lived, I didn\rquote t think you would come back after what I did to you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote You think I forgive you?\rdblquote  I asked. Truth and falsehood mixed, but they had to for him to believe me. \ldblquote I wish I was emodo, Roika. I wish I could throw you in the harness and use you the way you used me. I would do it with the haft of a spear if I could. But my anger at you is meaningless when compared to the just cause you are championing.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Then-\ldblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I came back because I want the Jokka to have their empire, and if you\rquote re the one I will help you. But not,\rdblquote  and I stared up at him with blazing eyes, \ldblquote Because I like you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     He threw back his head and laughed, his throat bobbing as it bubbled from his mouth. The emodo whirled me around, pressed me with my back to the desk, his strength still more than mine but not so much so as it had been. I snarled at him even as the desire in me burst into flame.\par
\par
     He dropped his head until his lips nearly touched mine and said, \ldblquote You\rquote ll do, ke eperu.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I\rquote ll more than do, ke emodo\'85 and you know it,\rdblquote  I answered, and pulled him onto me, jamming my mouth on his. We squirmed together against the desk, and there was passion and hatred in me, warring, making my struggles more violent. He laughed into my mouth.\par
\par
     Roika drew back. \ldblquote I\rquote ll have one of the eperu show you to your room, my Thenet.\rdblquote\par
\par
     A hot shudder ran down my back but I slid onto my feet and faced him, hands drawn into claws. He didn\rquote t miss it; I didn\rquote t intend him to. He grinned and his eyes narrowed. \ldblquote I think you\rquote ll find it quite agreeable.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I\rquote m sure,\rdblquote  I answered.\par
\par
     He opened the door, and an eperu came as if summoned. \ldblquote Show Thenet Edze-eperu to its room,\rdblquote  he said, glancing at me with a look that spread a slow heat through me. \ldblquote It will be staying.\rdblquote\par
\par
     The eperu nodded.\par
\par
     I found myself installed in a room on the second floor in short order. It had a sumptuous bed, a chest made of expensive wood, and more of the ubiquitous House sigil wall-hangings. As soon as the eperu stepped outside the tremendous wooden door, I started pacing. The hunger in me did not disturb me\'85 my mind was busy with other thoughts.\par
\par
     I had convinced him. I was in Edze. Now how to find Dlane without arousing his suspicions?\par
\par
     A hand struck my door and I looked up. \ldblquote Enter?\rdblquote\par
\par
     A slim figure stepped inside and closed the door behind it with bound hands. The cord around its wrists matched the one tied around its throat.\par
\par
     I rose, my voice a bare whisper. \ldblquote Seper.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Seper cleared its throat. \ldblquote Welcome to House Edze, ke eperu,\rdblquote  it said, its eyes directed at the ground near my feet. \ldblquote You\rquote re to be given a tour of the grounds. I thought,\rdblquote  and it raised its eyes to mine, \ldblquote You might want to start with the nursery first.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I nodded, breathless. This was no cowed servant of the House. There was a fire in Seper\rquote s slate blue eyes that spoke to no master but itself, and the cords that bound it told their own stories. Fractious eperu too often rebelling. I followed it out.\par
\par
     We went upstairs again to the third floor, and Seper led me down a hallway to the end of the corridor. It indicated a door and when I went to push it open, it touched my hand. \ldblquote We are not watched here,\rdblquote  it whispered.\par
\par
     I nodded, stomach clenching, and went inside.\par
\par
     Starlight glowed on the floor of the nursery, setting alight the nests of blankets that held the sleeping children. Some were old enough to approach their first Turning; others were infants yet. But I spared only a quick glance for them before I seized upon the shape of an anadi straightening from the figure of a small boy.\par
\par
     I recognized her. In her nakedness, how could I not? I stepped forward, her name on my lips.\par
\par
     She turned, and my hopes shattered.\par
\par
     Her golden eyes were empty, her belly gently swollen, dragging at her hips. I had come too late for Dlane.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Setasha,\rdblquote  I said. My throat had tightened almost too much for speech.\par
\par
     She cocked her head.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Setasha,\rdblquote  I said again. \ldblquote Dlane.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Her ears swiveled forward. She knew her name, then, but her blank, curious gaze hadn\rquote t changed. She didn\rquote t recognize me. The tears came then, and I reached for her, took her into my arms. She didn\rquote t resist, but it was with the mild puzzlement of an anadi without understanding of the motion. I wept against her golden hair, as silently as I could, but she did not reach up to comfort me, did not hold me, speak my name\'85 and her belly, slight as its swelling was, forced us apart at the hips.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Who are you?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I jumped away from Dlane, wiping at my mouth and looking for the source of the voice. I found it in the boy standing behind the anadi. He was tall enough to be past his first Turning; certainly old enough to be ready for his own room soon. He had dark skin and hair bright red\'85 but the metal-gray eyes of his father. I swallowed. \ldblquote I\rquote m Thenet,\rdblquote  I said.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Thenet Re\'f1a-eperu?\rdblquote  he asked.\par
\par
     Startled, I said, \ldblquote Yes. I am.\rdblquote  And I was, and always would be. My plans were in ruins now, and I was aware of Seper\rquote s silent figure outside the room. How long did we have?\par
\par
     He nodded. \ldblquote Aunt Dlane told me about you when she first came. That was before the problem.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote The problem?\rdblquote  I asked.\par
\par
     The boy glanced at her, ears flattening. \ldblquote She bit open her veins and tried to die. The healers saved her, but there wasn\rquote t much left in her.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I stared at him, shocked. She had tried to escape. My brave, beautiful love! Better to die a free anadi than to live dumb with a babe in the womb\'85 The words echoed in my head and I glanced at what had become of my setasha. There was nothing in her now to make a second try.\par
\par
\ldblquote After that, we started having lots of problems with the other Re\'f1a anadi. They were trying to do it too.\rdblquote  The boy trotted to the end of the room and opened a chest. Dlane and I both stared at him - hers the dumb stare of a curious animal, mine stupified as he chattered on. \ldblquote Father was furious. Ke Seper explained it to me, though. That\rquote s when it got in trouble. It said you would come, just like Aunt Dlane said you would.\rdblquote\par
\par
     The boy returned with a package. Seeing it, Dlane gave a tiny cry of delight. He grinned at her with affection, then handed it to me. \ldblquote This is for you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     With trembling fingers I untied the knot on the top of the bundle. It opened on a note and a sharp object. It was in her strong handwriting; words I could read because of her, because of our life together. They were simple enough.\par
\par
     \lquote Setasha -\par
\par
          I love you, now and always.\par
\par
                    Dlane.\rquote\par
\par
     My hand crumpled on the note and I sobbed aloud. The boy crept forward and set a hand on my knee as my fingers opened on the bundle, spilling it to the ground. Something clunked against the wooden floor and rolled away but I was beyond such things.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Ke eperu? Ke eperu?\rdblquote  The boy patted my leg. \ldblquote Please don\rquote t cry, ke eperu. It happens. She wanted you to have it. Please don\rquote t cry, ke Thenet.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I looked up at him, dripping great strings of tears from my mouth. Slowly, I wiped them from my eyes. \ldblquote What\rquote s your name, ba emodo?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Kaduin,\rdblquote  he said, straightening. \ldblquote I am the oldest son of Edze.\rdblquote\par
\par
     The oldest son of Edze. Something stirred in my mind. \ldblquote I suppose your father\rquote s very proud of you.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote He says so all the time.\rdblquote  Kaduin frowned, tail fidgeting.\par
\par
     \ldblquote This troubles you?\rdblquote  I asked him, touching his shoulder.\par
\par
     \ldblquote I liked Aunt Dlane. And all my other aunts. But he made them very unhappy.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote A House and its family should be happy,\rdblquote  I said quietly.\par
\par
     Kaduin nodded. \ldblquote It would be better that way. It\'85 frightens the children when the anadi seek the Void to avoid their father.\rdblquote  He said it bravely with a lifted chin, as if it could conceal the fear and worry in his eyes.\par
\par
     \ldblquote No children should live thus, I think, Kaduin,\rdblquote  I said, and the plan began to unfold. \ldblquote I know of a better place.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote A better place?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote One where the anadi do not fear the emodo,\rdblquote  I whispered. \ldblquote One where the children need not fear.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote That sounds like a good place,\rdblquote  Kaduin said. \ldblquote I think we should go.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I think so too,\rdblquote  I said. I glanced past him to the nests I\rquote d taken to be full of sleeping children, found instead rows of eyes looking at me. I smiled at them, then gestured for Seper to enter. It did so, hands straining at their binding.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Seper, my Seper,\rdblquote  I said in a low voice. \ldblquote We are taking the children.\rdblquote  My hand touched the small of my back, just above my tail, and pulled from beneath my vest the bone claw nestled there. A flash of white and it had severed the rope at Seper\rquote s throat and hands.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Ke Thenet?\rdblquote\par
\par
     I cupped its cheek. \ldblquote Barit is outside. Find a way to get the children out. Through the window, maybe.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote The window,\rdblquote  Seper repeated, but its uncertainty was fading.\par
\par
     The boy\rquote s voice distracted me. He was reaching up to Dlane, his voice patient but firm. \ldblquote No, Aunt Dlane, you can\rquote t keep that. That\rquote s your gift to ke Thenet, remember?\rdblquote\par
\par
     She was holding a shell in her hand - not any shell, but the first spiral shell she\rquote d shown me in the Birthwell. It had to be the same; I\rquote d never seen one as large. She was stroking it, staring fixedly at its sides with no understanding at all of what it meant, or how much it was worth. My mouth dried instantly as the boy coaxed it from her grasp and she whimpered.\par
\par
     \ldblquote It\rquote s okay, Aunt Dlane. It\rquote s safe. You see, it\rquote s ke Thenet. You told us it would come for us, and it\rquote s here. We have to go now.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Dlane hung her head, and the boy returned to me, handing me the shell. \ldblquote It was in the bundle,\rdblquote  he said.\par
\par
     I suppressed my shiver, tucked the shell into my pouch with the note. \ldblquote Very well, ba Kaduin,\rdblquote  I said. \ldblquote Go with Seper.\rdblquote  I walked past them, stopped only when Seper grabbed my arm.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Where are you going?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I am going to give Roika my scars.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Before Seper could speak, I strode past it. Lowering the children through the window safely and quietly would take time\'85 enough time for me to answer Roika for all the pain and humiliation he had visited upon me and mine, for stealing my Dlane. I went to the second floor, seeking the anadi\rquote s quarters. I passed only one other eperu, who nodded to me as if I belonged there - poor creature! What punishment would its assumption buy it when I had finished here?\par
\par
     I found the anadi behind a door, curled up together as anadi sleep on their soft, plump pillows, arms lazily interlinked, tails still and ears sleek against their heads. Roika\rquote s treasure: the anadi of House Edze, his prize breeders who would bring him his empire. I slid into the room, and now I let the halo of fire in my soul die, exchanged it for the black veil of the Void.\par
\par
     The bone claw I had created so long ago in indecision and doubt I rolled into my palm and clutched so that it protruded from between my knuckles. How tempting to liberate the blood of every Jokka in the room, to bleed Edze dry of life and vigor! How easy it would be to simply slit the throats of every breathing creature in this room and condemn each one to death for having had the ill fortune to be purchased by a mad emodo bent on empire!\par
\par
     But I was no Roika, and I wore the cloak of the Void by choice, and temporarily.\par
\par
     I went to Linuthet first. Touched her cheek and woke her. Her eyes were mazed, not just with sleep but with the seeds of the stupidity that each child would water in her until the weed broke the soil, foul and dark, to choke her living mind.\par
\par
     She lifted her hand to my face, showing me the scars at her wrists.\par
\par
     \ldblquote I have come to free you, Linuthet Re\'f1a-anadi,\rdblquote  I whispered.\par
\par
     After a moment, the reply came back. \ldblquote Free me.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I drew the claw across her throat and opened it, sending her spirit to the afterlife.\par
\par
     One by one, I woke the anadi of Re\'f1a. One by one they chose my knife. I did not realize I was weeping until the blood of the last stained my bone blade, weeping for their courage, their loyalty. I wiped my mouth and stared into the pile of Edze\rquote s anadi. They were truly Edze\rquote s now. None of Re\'f1a remained\'85 save the anadi in the nursery. I would come for her soon enough.\par
\par
     \ldblquote You,\rdblquote  A voice said.\par
\par
     I turned, heard the husky voice. The hair on my spine lifted. Of all the anadi to be spared, that this one should still live and breathe! Magun, her voice still sodden with sleep but her eyes half-lidded and dangerous, rose from the pile.\par
\par
     \ldblquote You,\rdblquote  she said again.\par
\par
     I lifted my chin and bared my teeth. \ldblquote Me,\rdblquote  I agreed.\par
\par
     \ldblquote What\'85 what have you done?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Raped Edze and left him to live with his humiliation.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Magun paused. \ldblquote You mean to take away his power.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote I mean to build my own empire, Magun. One that will make his seem like a candle to the light of the Brightness. When his little dream is collapsing, mine will sweep Ke Bakil.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote You would never.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I grinned with thinned eyes. \ldblquote Watch me.\rdblquote\par
\par
     She lunged for me and I back-handed her, knocking her unconscious. I stared down at her loathsome body. So easy just to trace her jaw with my claw\'85\par
\par
     And then I saw the band of swollen flesh pressing at her hips, and I suppressed the urge to laugh. No, I would let her live, and try to save her clever mind and her fecund body when Roika discovered half his anadi dead. She would be nothing soon enough.\par
\par
     By the time I returned to the nursery, Seper was lowering the last of the infants. It glanced at me, took in the blood scent and the tears on my jaw. Its head lifted, and the ferocity of the pleasure shining in its eyes made me wonder, suddenly, what it had endured at Roika\rquote s hands.\par
\par
     \ldblquote There isn\rquote t much time left,\rdblquote  it said.\par
\par
     \ldblquote This won\rquote t take much time,\rdblquote  I answered. \ldblquote Go. I\rquote ll be down soon.\rdblquote\par
\par
     It nodded and was gone before I could take another breath.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Mmm.\rdblquote\par
\par
     I turned, and she was standing there, hugging her stomach. Nausea swam to my mouth at the sight of her rocking on her heels.\par
\par
     My Dlane.\par
\par
     Flashes of her:\par
\par
     Grasping the limb of a tree, staring down at the \'f1edsu.\par
\par
     Bravely striding alongside me, panting, out of breath, determined to keep up.\par
\par
     Riding in a wagon, staring avidly, greedily at the world as it scrolled past.\par
\par
     Laughing with me.\par
\par
     Teaching me.\par
\par
     Loving me.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Dlane,\rdblquote  I whispered.\par
\par
     She drifted closer, touched the medallion at my throat, and looked into my eyes. A frown furrowed her brow and her mouth worked silently for several heart-beats.\par
\par
     Then - \ldblquote The\'85 Thenet.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Oh, gods.\par
\par
     I kissed her on the forehead, touched her cheek.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Dlane Re\'f1a-anadi,\rdblquote  I whispered. \ldblquote I go to do your work. I cannot ask, but I know your answer.\rdblquote  The bone claw slid into my palm. \ldblquote As I have known you, as I have loved you, I know your answer.\rdblquote\par
\par
     Then the blade that she had shaped drew the blade that it had shaped over her throat. The sweet scent of her blood suffused my senses. Her body sagged and I caught it, easing it to the ground. As her blood poured from her, I lowered my mouth to hers and kissed her, tasted for the last time the wet of her teeth, the heat of her tongue.\par
\par
     When I drew back she was smiling at me.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Good bye, setasha,\rdblquote  I whispered.\par
\par
     Her eyes closed and her smile grew fixed. Her spirit fled where her mind had gone, leaving only her body. Leaving only a shell. I pressed my forehead to hers and took in a long breath, my hands wrapped in her golden mane. I had done what she would have wanted me to do: what she had prepared me to do, with all her conversations, the grave pronouncements of her desire to die rather than to give birth to a chain of children. Never was I more her creature than when I killed her - my most beloved, the light of my living, my Dlane. I could not show my love for her, my willingness to learn from her, in any better way.\par
\par
     But it was hard, so hard.\par
\par
     When I trusted myself to open my eyes, I found them focused on her belly, on the part-formed child that would soon die there. Resting above it, the ring through her navel slit gathered the reluctant light. I touched it, then unfastened it from her flesh. The ring through my ear I likewise unlatched.\par
\par
     I left her delicately arrayed on the ground with the light of the stars on her face, my ear ring positioned just so beside her outstretched hand\'85 left there so Roika would know without question who had done this to him.\par
\par
     Her ring I screwed back together in my ear. It dragged there, heavier and larger than the one it had replaced. It felt right there, to take with me the sign of her worth.   \par
\par
     Barit and Seper met me in the darkness on the ground. I looked around, my spirit still within me. \ldblquote Are we all present?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Yes. Plus the midena.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Midena?\rdblquote  I blinked, glanced at the children and found one of them holding the tether of nine of the small, nimble animals. They were pulling tender grass from the ground, patently unconcerned over the disruption in their routine.\par
\par
     \ldblquote We have a lot of infants here, Thenet, and no anadi back at the caravan to nurse them,\rdblquote  Barit said. \ldblquote We need the milk.\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Fine. Let\rquote s go,\rdblquote  I said, accepting two of the babies.\par
\par
     We slipped silently into the dark, three adults, fourteen children, nine animals - we stole away with the riches of House Edze on our back, and the treasure of House Edze dead in its House. Dlane could not have designed a better way to answer Roika for his arrogance.\par
\par
     The shell pricked through my pouch, and as I led our strange procession through the maze of paths leading out of the Green, my mind wandered to it. What was the worth of a shell without a society to give it context? We had given the shell a meaning: we had made it represent money, work, the labors of an individual, a House. But before the Jokka had taken it, it had lain beside the sea, beyond the touch of Jokku meaning.\par
\par
     But did it need us to give it purpose? To give it a reason to exist? Did anything the gods had put on Ke Bakil require the Jokka to reach its greatest meaning?\par
\par
     We had given shells a worth. But perhaps everything had its own intrinsic value, one that needed no Jokku mind to validate it. Each shell, each tree, each pebble and rock and Jokku soul, for all their multitudes, had its own beauty, its own value outside the ones imposed on them by us. The gods had made it all, and each single one needed us not a whit to have meaning.\par
\par
     The worth of a shell? Everything\'85 in itself.\par
\par
     The Moment came, froze everything, my step mid-motion, the warm wind redolent with spring\rquote s flowers, like one of Linuthet\rquote s perfumes. It was perfection, that heartbeat\rquote s pause, and did not fail me as in my heart bloomed an understanding I hadn\rquote t even realized she\rquote d been preparing me for.\par
\par
     All along, she had been making me ready for this. She had freed me from the Jokka; freed me to see our society clearly for the first time. To engage it with my full faculties, unbound by prejudice or convention. From guilt, from shame, from confusion and fear, Dlane had released me.\par
\par
     I was free.\par
\par
     The world shuddered back to life around me. I looked over my shoulder, but all I could see of Edze were its yellow lights, and those fading quickly.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Ke Thenet,\rdblquote  Kaduin said.\par
\par
     \ldblquote Yes?\rdblquote\par
\par
     \ldblquote Shouldn\rquote t we stop? It\rquote s getting near truedark.\rdblquote\par
\par
     His voice shook, but mine did not. \ldblquote Truedark? Truedark\rquote s the best time to travel.\rdblquote\par
\par
    \par
}
␀